The next morning, over breakfast, Pandey said, “Are you still set on staying at the fort?”  
Byomkesh replied, “Yes, please make the arrangements. At most, we’ll stay two or three days, no more.”  
Pandey said, “I don’t feel good about it—what if there really are snakes?”  
Byomkesh smiled and said, “Even if there are, they won’t be able to harm us. We’re fasting for the snakes.”  
“Very well, I’ll go see Ramkishore Babu and settle everything. —But tell me, what harm would there be in staying at Ramkishore Babu’s house instead of the fort?”  
“That would be too close for comfort; we wouldn’t get the proper perspective. The fort is better.”  
“Alright. I’ll inform my office—if you send word to my munshi, Ataullah, he’ll show you Ishan Babu’s belongings. He has the key to the storeroom.”  

Pandeyji mounted his motorbike and departed. It was only nine o’clock; Pandeyji’s office was in his own house, so there was no rush to examine Ishan Babu’s effects. We lit cigarettes and idly flipped through the newspaper, when a small car pulled up at the door. Byomkesh glanced out the window and said, “Dr. Ghatak. That’s good.”  

Dr. Ghatak wore a slightly apologetic expression. He had realized that we had not betrayed his secret, nor would we in the future. He said, “Yesterday, I didn’t get a proper chance to talk with you, so—”  
Byomkesh, with utmost courtesy, invited him to sit and said, “If you hadn’t come, we would have gone to you. How are you? How are all the old friends? Mohidhar Babu?”  
The doctor replied, “Everyone is well.”  
Byomkesh, eyes twinkling, smiled gently and said, “And Rajani Devi?”  
The doctor’s ears flushed red, then he laughed. “Rajani is well. When she heard you had arrived, she asked if Mrs. Bakshi had come too.”

--- Page 696 ---
Durgarahasya 305

"Satyabati hasn’t come this time. She—" Byomkesh glanced at me.

I explained Satyabati’s situation, saying, "Satyabati has banished us from Calcutta. We’ve vowed not to return until we have good news to bring her."

The doctor smiled and congratulated Byomkesh, but the smile did not sit well on his face. When a hungry man sees another eat, he may smile, but it is never a smile of joy.

Byomkesh, reading the thoughts behind his expression, clapped him on the back and said, "My friend, don’t harbor any resentment. What you have is a fortune few possess. You are savoring, at once, the sweetness of conjugal life and the sharp taste of forbidden love."

I added, "Think of it—Shelley said, ‘O Wind, if Winter comes, can Spring be far behind?’ Let the season of flowers pass; the fruit will come in its own time."

This time, a genuine smile appeared on the doctor’s face. After a few more moments of laughter and banter, Byomkesh said, "Dr. Ghatak, yesterday at Ramkishore Babu’s house I overheard some discussion about property matters. I’m curious to hear the rest, if you don’t mind telling us."

The doctor replied, "Why not? Ramkishore Babu isn’t hiding anything. About a month ago, his health suddenly broke down; his heart was in a very bad state. He’s recovered somewhat now, but he’s afraid that if he dies suddenly, his elder sons will squander the property in lawsuits. They might even try to deprive the younger, underage children. So he wants to divide the property while he’s still alive. The estate will be split into four equal parts; two parts will go to the elder sons, the remaining two will remain under Ramkishore’s control. After his death, Gadai and Tulsi will inherit his share, and the elder sons will get nothing more."

Byomkesh, thoughtful, asked, "I see. What was the quarrel about the fort?"

"The fort remains in Ramkishore Babu’s possession. But both sons covet it."

"Why was there talk of giving the fort to Monilal?"

"The matter is this—Ramkishore Babu has decided to marry Tulsi to Monilal. Monilal had married his elder daughter, as you may know, but she passed away. Yesterday, in conversation, Ramkishore Babu said that after his death, Gadai would get the family home, and Monilal would get the fort. Monilal means Tulsi; nothing is being given to Monilal separately. That’s what started the quarrel between Banshi and Murali."

"Hmm. But Tulsi’s marriage is still some time away. How old is she, anyway?"

"By modern standards, she’s not of marriageable age, but she’s not a child either—thirteen or fourteen, perhaps. Ramkishore Babu will probably marry them off soon. If he dies suddenly, the underage children will need a trustworthy guardian. He has no faith whatsoever in his elder sons."

"From what I’ve seen, he has good reason. What sort of man is Monilal?"

"Cool-headed. That’s why Ramkishore Babu trusts him most. Though, the way he clings to his in-laws’ house, it seems he’s not overly concerned with appearances."

Byomkesh stared into space for a while, then suddenly said, "Dr. Ghatak, you should be careful with your patient."

The doctor started. "Patient? Which patient?"

"Ramkishore Babu. If his heart were to stop suddenly before the deed is registered, someone might stand to gain."

The doctor stared at us, eyes wide.

--- Page 697 ---
Around ten in the morning, after the doctor had taken his leave, we sent word for Munshi Ataullah.

Ataullah was an exceedingly well-turned-out, elderly Muslim gentleman—likely a man of lineage. His lean frame was clad in a spotted achkan, his beard tinged with henna, kohl lining his eyes, and betel leaf ever-present at his lips. As he spoke in his crisp, polished tongue, flecks of betel would spark from his mouth like tiny embers. The man was a thorough gentleman.

Upon learning our intentions, Munshi Ataullah, with the help of two orderlies, brought forth Ishaan Babu’s bed and trunk and presented them for our inspection. The bed was meager—a faded, threadbare mattress wrapped in a worn-out satranji, with an oil-stained pillow. Still, Byomkesh examined it thoroughly. He shook out the mattress and pressed and prodded the pillow, but found no trace of any concealed metallic object within.

Ordering the bed to be removed, Byomkesh said, “Munshiji, there was a mohur—could we see it?”

“Certainly, Janab. If you so desire, I have brought the mohur with me,” Ataullah replied, producing a small wooden box from the pocket of his achkan. The box was inscribed with various cryptic letters and symbols. Inside, wrapped in cotton, lay the mohur.

A solid gold mohur; in size and shape, not unlike a modern silver rupee. Byomkesh turned it over in his hand and asked, “Can you read what’s written here in Urdu?”

Ataullah, his tone slightly wounded, replied, “Not Urdu, Janab—Farsi. It was never the custom to inscribe Urdu on ashrafis. If you wish, I can read it for you—Farsi is my mother tongue.”

Byomkesh, a little abashed, said, “Is that so? Then please, read it and tell me from what era this mohur is.”

Ataullah adjusted his spectacles and read the inscription on the mohur. “There is no date,” he said. “It is written that this mohur was minted during the reign of Nawab Alivardi Khan.”

Byomkesh said, “So it is from Janakirām’s time, not later. Well, Munshiji, thank you very much. You may return to your office. If we need you again, we will send for you.”

“Meherbani,” Ataullah said, and took his leave.

Byomkesh then pulled the professor’s trunk towards him and sat down. The battered tin trunk, however, yielded nothing that might shed light on the cause of his death. The clothing and everyday items suggested that the professor had been of modest means, or perhaps exceedingly frugal. There were two old, tattered books: one, a copy of Kautilya’s Arthashastra edited by Shyamshilpi; the other, an English translation of Shair-i-Mutakharin. From these two volumes, one could glean a hint of the breadth of the professor’s historical knowledge.

Along with the books was an old, leather-bound notebook. The notebook seemed to be thirty years old; its cover had grown limp, and the faded pages were coming loose. The entries within were exceedingly haphazard—here and there a few pages scribbled in pencil, elsewhere a few lines in ink. The handwriting was not elegant, but it was all in the same hand. Those who work with books often keep such a catch-all notebook close at hand, jotting down whatever they wish whenever the mood strikes.

Taking the notebook carefully, we sat at the table. Byomkesh began turning the pages one by one.

The first two or three pages were blank. Then, on one page, was written—

The presiding goddess of history

--- Page 698 ---
The Mystery of the Fort  
307

Had he been able to speak in human tongue, he would have uttered, in the rhythm of the Muharram drums—  
Dhananrjoydham! Dhananrjoydham!

Byomkesh, picking up the cloth, said, “It does sound like the Muharram drums, but what does it mean?”  
I replied, “It seems to say, ‘Earn wealth, earn wealth.’ Your Ishanbabu, I see, was quite the cynic.”  
Byomkesh examined the writing for a while longer, then turned the page. On the next page, only a few dates were noted. Historical dates: when the Hijri calendar began, the date of King Shashanka’s death, and such. Presumably, he had made these notes to teach history to his students. Several more pages were filled with dates, but there’s no need to mention them all.

After that, a few pages lay blank. Then, suddenly, a long composition began. Its opening was thus—  
“I heard the history of his lineage from Rambinod. During the Sepoy Mutiny, it seems the looters could not carry away the accumulated treasures; at least, Rambinod believes so. He has seen the fort. His great hope is that, should he ever become wealthy, he will buy that fort and go live there.”

Then, beginning with Janakirama and ending with Rajaram and Jayaram, the account was written exactly as we had heard from Ramapati’s lips—without a hair’s breadth of deviation. When the reading was done, I said, “Well, at least we can be certain of one thing—Ramapati did not fabricate the story.”

Byomkesh said, “There’s no doubt Ramapati told the story accurately. But what proof is there that he heard it on the night of Ishanbabu’s death? He could have heard it two days earlier.”

“That’s true. Then—?”

“Then nothing. I simply mean that possibility cannot be dismissed. In other words, there’s no proof that Ramapati heard this story that night, and went to Ishanbabu the next morning to hear the rest.”

After turning a few more pages, we came upon one where, like a cry of anguish, a few words were written—  
—Rambinod lives no more.  
My only true friend has departed.  
What a dreadful death!  
That scene clings to my eyes like a nightmare.

Byomkesh kept his gaze fixed on the writing for a while, then said, “A dreadful death. Doesn’t sound like a natural one. Needs to be looked into.” Seeing the question on my face, he recited softly,  
“Wherever you see ashes, sift through, brother—if you’re lucky, you may find hidden gems.”

Here the writing at the front of the notebook ended. It seemed that after Rambinod’s death, the notebook had lain unused for a long time, perhaps even lost. Then, when writing resumed, it was from the reverse side of the notebook.

The first entry was written in ink—on yellowed paper, the ink had faded. At the top of the page was written—

--- Page 699 ---
308  
Byomkesh Samagra

Ramkishore’s eldest son, Banshidhar, has come to the city to attend college.  
After a long time, I have reconnected with their family. Since the death of that Rambinod, I had not kept in touch.  
At the bottom of the page it is written—Banshidhar has been involved in a grave scandal. I am trying to save him. After all, he is Rambinod’s nephew.

Byomkesh said, “We’ll probably learn the details of Banshidhar’s scandal within a few days. But what’s this!”  
We saw that everything written on the remaining pages was in red and blue pencil.  
Byomkesh flipped through the pages several times and said, “Ajit, look under the trunk and see if there’s a red-blue pencil.”  
We didn’t have to search long; we found a double-ended red-blue pencil.  
Byomkesh said, “Well, that explains it. Everything written after this was penned by Ishaan Babu after coming to the fort. These are the final chapters of his life.”  
The first entry was as follows—  
Did not find any secret chamber in the fort.  
Very strange! Where were the fort’s treasures kept?  
I cannot believe they were stored in an open room. Surely  
there must be a secret chamber somewhere. But where? If the sepoys  
had found the secret chamber, it would no longer be secret;  
they would have broken down its door and left it open,  
and then it would have been visible to all. Therefore,  
the sepoys did not find the secret chamber.

Byomkesh said, “The professor’s reasoning isn’t entirely sound. After the sepoys left, Rajaram’s family returned. Perhaps they repaired the broken toshakhana, which is why we can’t detect it now.”  
Turning the page, Byomkesh read—  
Someone is trying to frighten me away from the fort.  
Banshidhar? But I will not leave the fort so easily!  
Dhananjoydham! Dhananjoydham!

I asked, “Why the reference to the Muharram drums again?”  
Byomkesh replied, “Perhaps the scent of gold coins had his nerves on edge.”  
Then, a few pages later, came the last entry in the notebook. We stared at it in astonishment. It was not written in Bengali, but in Urdu or Persian—three lines. Below that, in Bengali script, only two words—  
Who is Mohanlal?

Byomkesh sighed and said, “Indeed, who is Mohanlal? It is not our task to answer that question. Call Munshi Ataullah.”  
Ataullah came and deciphered the script. He said, “Janab, it seems the deceased knew good Persian. Though it’s a bit old-fashioned. He wrote: If I or Jayaram do not survive, all our wealth and treasures—gold and jewels—are entrusted to Mohanlal.”

--- Page 700 ---
Durg Rahasya 309

“In Mohanlal’s custody—!”

“Yes, sir, that’s what’s written here.”

“Hm. Very well, Munshiji, you may take all the things away now. Only this notebook will remain with me.”

The two of us lit cigarettes and sprawled into the depths of the armchairs. In silence, I finished one cigarette, then lit a second from its dying ember and said, “What do you make of the notebook?”

Byomkesh drummed his fingers on the armrests as if playing the tabla, and replied, “It seems to me—Dhananjoydham.”

“No jokes—tell me what you really think.”

“I haven’t understood anything clearly yet. But if someone truly did murder Ishaanbabu, then I can see a motive.”

“What motive?”

“The eternal one—money.”

“All right, but what’s the significance of those lines written in Persian?”

“He didn’t write them himself. That is, the handwriting is his, but the composition isn’t—it's Rajaram’s. He must have found the writing somewhere in the fort, then copied it into his notebook.”

“And then?”

“And then he died.”

5

Pandeyji returned after twelve o’clock. Removing his helmet and wiping the sweat from his brow, he said, “The job’s done, but the old man made trouble at the last moment.”

Byomkesh asked, “What sort of trouble?”

Pandey replied, “It was getting late, let’s discuss everything over lunch. Did you find anything?”

“We’ll talk over lunch.”

Seated at the table, Byomkesh said, “You go first. Yesterday Ramkishorebabu was hesitant, but why did he suddenly dig in his heels today?”

Pandey said, “After we left yesterday, someone told him that you’re a famous detective. That’s what rattled him.”

“What’s there to be rattled about? If he has nothing to hide—”

“That’s exactly what I had to tell him in the end. I said, ‘Even if Byomkeshbabu is a detective, what are you afraid of? Are you trying to hide something?’ Then the old man quickly agreed.”

I said, “So Ramkishorebabu really was trying to hide something.”

Pandey said, “That’s what I suspected. But it’s not about Ishaanbabu’s death. It’s something else. Anyway, I’ve arranged everything—today itself, they’ll make the fort ready for your stay. You can go this afternoon if you wish, or tomorrow morning.”

--- Page 701 ---
310  
Byomkesh Samagra

“We’ll leave this very afternoon.”  
“So be it. But I’ve made another arrangement. My personal orderly, Sitaram, will accompany you.”  
“No, no, what’s the need for that?”  
“There is a need. Sitaram won’t wear his red turban; he’ll go disguised as an ordinary servant. The man is very sharp; besides, he has a remarkable skill—he’s an expert with snakes. Having him with you will be most convenient. Think about it—you’ll need someone for fetching water, washing clothes, cleaning utensils. It’s better not to take their men.”  
Byomkesh agreed. Pandey then said, “Now, please give your statement.”  
Byomkesh, in detail, revealed the mystery of Ishaan Babu’s notebook. After listening, Pandey said, “Hmm, I too would like to know who this Mohanlal fellow was, but a hundred years later, it’s impossible to trace him. Meanwhile, Ishaan Babu is writing about recent events—someone is trying to scare him away from the fort. He suspects Banshidhar. But what’s the real truth? And how is he being frightened?”  
Byomkesh shook his head and said, “We won’t get answers to these questions now. Let’s see if, by going to the fort, we can unravel its mysteries.”

That afternoon, we arrived at the hill-fort in a police van. The three of us, and Sitaram. Pandey-ji would settle us in and then return; Sitaram would stay. Sitaram was thirty-five, lean and tall, with a dusky fair complexion, and a moustache like a hunting cat. His remarkable feature was that, dressed well, he looked every bit a gentleman, but in a loincloth, he could pass for a scullery servant without the slightest hesitation. At present, he wore a cloth down to his knees and a towel over his shoulder—in short, a rough workman’s attire.  
We did not travel light: bedding trunks, rice, lentils, vegetables, provisions, an Emic cooker, and who knows what else. Sitaram and Bulaki Lal began hauling the luggage into the fort. Pandey said, “Come, let’s go meet the master of the house.”

The master sat on the main veranda, his son-in-law Monilal beside him. They greeted us, mildly reproached us for arranging our own meals, and joked that city folk would never find peace in the hills and jungles. Yet his eyes remained sharp and wary.

During these pleasantries, I noticed the other residents of the house had grown quite restless at our arrival. Banshidhar and Muralidhar circled us like hawks, never coming too close. Ramapati poked his head out from inside the house, silently withdrew after seeing us. The steward Chandmohan, at the far end of the veranda, drew on his hookah and skewered us with sidelong glances. Tulsi, peering from behind a jasmine bush like a curious squirrel, darted away; a moment later, I saw her peeking from behind a pillar.

They had clearly learned that Byomkesh was a famous detective and had come to stay at the fort with some grave purpose—and were accordingly agitated. Only Gadadhar’s slow mind, it seemed, had not been roused even by such a shock; I did not see him.

As we rose to leave, Ramkishore Babu said, “Consider that you have come only to stay here—”

--- Page 702 ---
Durg Rahasya 311

“No, please. You are my guests; whenever you need anything, just send word.”

“Of course, of course.” We prepared to leave. The master of the house signaled, and Monilal accompanied us; his purpose was to escort us up to the fort.

As we ascended and descended the stairs, we exchanged a few words with Monilal. Byomkesh asked, “How did Ramkishore Babu come to know that I am a detective?”

Monilal replied, “I told him. I already knew your name; I’ve read books written by him. When I mentioned it, the master became quite agitated. Then, when he heard that you wanted to stay at the fort, he was visibly unsettled.”

“Why?”

“Just the other day, there was an accident—”

“So you fear that what happened to you might happen to us as well. By the way, wasn’t it your wife who also died of a snakebite?”

“Yes, sir.”

“I see there are a lot of snakes in this region.”

“There are, certainly. But I’ve never actually seen one myself.”

We descended to the gateway and began to climb the stairs to the fort. Suddenly, Monilal asked, “Please don’t mind, but since you are police officers, I’m curious—did Ishan Babu really die of a snakebite?”

A quick glance passed between Byomkesh and Pandey. Byomkesh said, “Why do you ask? Do you have any doubts about it?”

Monilal hesitated, “No—but—well, you can never be sure—”

Pandey said, “What else could it be but a snake?”

Monilal replied, “That’s what I can’t figure out. I saw the marks of the snake’s fangs on Ishan Babu’s foot with my own eyes. Just like the ones on my wife’s foot.” Monilal sighed.

We had reached the fort’s gate. Monilal said, “I’ll turn back now. The master isn’t well; I don’t dare leave him alone for long. I’ll come again tomorrow morning.”

With a namaskar, Monilal departed. The sun had set. Above Ramkishore Babu’s house, the slender crescent of the second night of the waxing moon smiled slyly and slipped behind the house. We too passed through the gate and entered the grounds of the fort. Byomkesh said, “This morning I warned Dr. Ghatak about his patient; now I see it was unnecessary. Until the deed of property transfer is registered, the son-in-law Monilal will guard his father-in-law like a yaksha.”

Pandey smiled a little. “Yes—it seems they are suspicious about Ishan Babu’s death. But nothing should be said just yet.”

“No.”

We crossed the courtyard and walked toward the house. In Pandeyji’s hand was a long, pestle-shaped torch; its electric beam could reach far, and if needed, it could serve as a formidable weapon. Pandey switched on the torch and cast its light ahead, saying, “I’ll leave this with you, you might need it. Come, let’s see what arrangements have been made for your stay.”

We saw that the arrangements had been made in that same room bristling with iron spikes. Two iron cots,

--- Page 703 ---
312  
Byomkesh Samagra

The table, chairs, and other pieces of furniture somewhat concealed the stark bareness of the walls. In the meantime, Sitaram had lit the lantern, spread out the bedding, set the rice to cook in the Imeek cooker, and was now heating water for tea on the stove. I was astonished at his industriousness.

Soon, steaming tea arrived before us. Taking a sip, Pandey said, “Sitaram, what did you see?”

Sitaram replied, “I’ve gone all around the fort, huzoor. There are no snakes here.”

A statement made with utter certainty. Pandey let out a sigh and said, “Well, that’s a relief.”

“Anything else?”

“Other than that, there’s no way into the fort except the stairs. Outside the walls is a sheer cliff.”

Byomkesh turned to Pandey and said, “Do you understand what this means?”

“What?”

“If any assailant wishes to enter the fort, he must come by the stairs. That is, he must pass by the gateway. Bulakilal could spot him.”

“Hm, you’re right. Bulakilal must be questioned. But it’s too late today, not tonight. —Sitaram, you needn’t be told much. Look after these gentlemen, and keep your eyes and ears open.”

“Yes, huzoor!”

Pandeyji stood up.

“I’ll come by sometime tomorrow. You all be careful.”

We escorted Pandeyji to the fort’s gate. Byomkesh switched on his torch and cast its beam on the stairs as Pandeyji descended. A little while later, we heard the sound of the police van departing below. Over at Ramkishore Babu’s house, a faint light glimmered.

We returned to the courtyard and stood there. Stars had blossomed in the sky, and a gentle breeze drifted in from the jungle. As if sensing our unspoken desire, Sitaram brought out two chairs and placed them in the open. With a sigh of contentment, we sat down.

Seated in this star-pierced darkness, my mind filled with strange, vivid imaginings. It felt as though we had entered a land of fairy tales. Like those princes or sons of kotwals who, in search of something unknown, arrived in the enchanted city of a sleeping princess—our situation seemed much the same. Of course, there was no sleeping princess here, but who could say whether a jewel glimmered on a serpent’s brow? Who knew what invisible demons or demonesses stood guard over it? Like a pearl hidden within an oyster, what exquisite mystery lay concealed beneath the bony skeleton of this ancient fort?

Byomkesh suddenly struck a match, shattering my romantic reverie. Lighting a cigarette, he said, “Ishan Babu was right—there must be a secret treasury somewhere in the fort.”

I said, “But where? In such a vast fort, is it so easy to dig up and find it?”

“It’s not easy. But I believe Ishan Babu had found it; otherwise, the coins in his grasp make no sense.”

Turning the thought over in my mind, I finally said, “If that’s so, then there must be many more coins there.”

“Possibly. And more importantly, if Ishan Babu could find it, then so can we.”

Byomkesh rose and began pacing in the darkness. After a while, as he walked, he suddenly uttered an “Oof!” and nearly stumbled, barely managing to steady himself. I leapt up—

--- Page 704 ---
Durgo Rahasya 313

I stood up—"What happened!"

"Nothing, just stumbled a little." The torch was in his hand; he switched it on and cast its beam upon the ground.

What had escaped our notice in daylight now became immediately apparent. A square stone had recently been pried up by someone, and then, with clumsy hands, replaced in its original spot. The stone did not sit evenly; one edge jutted up slightly. In the darkness, Byomkesh had caught his foot on that raised edge and stumbled.

Excited by the sight of the loose stone, I exclaimed, "Byomkesh! Is there a secret chamber to the toshakhana beneath this stone?"

Byomkesh shook his head. "No, the stone is, at most, fourteen inches square. Even if there is a hole beneath, a person couldn't possibly fit through it."

"Still—"

"No, it’s not what you’re thinking. There can’t be a secret entrance to the toshakhana in the middle of an open courtyard. In any case, tomorrow morning we’ll have the stone lifted and take a look."

Byomkesh swept the torch around, casting light in all directions, but nowhere else did it seem that the paving stones had been disturbed. Not far off, the cannon lay where it always had, dust and grime thick beneath it, anchoring it to the floor; there, too, no loose earth or stone was visible.

At that moment, Sitaram arrived to announce that dinner was ready. I glanced at my wristwatch—it was a quarter to ten. I had no idea how silently the hours had slipped by.

We went inside and sat down to eat. I had never known that khichuri and meat, cooked in such a rustic manner, could taste so divine. On top of that, Sitaram had fried omelets. It was a veritable feast.

After we finished, Sitaram took his meal on the veranda. Coming to the door, he said, "Huzoor, if you permit, I’d like to take a stroll around."

Byomkesh replied, "Very well. Where will you sleep?"

Sitaram said, "Don’t worry about that, huzoor. I’ll spread my bedding outside the door and sleep there."

Sitaram departed. We dimmed the light and stretched out on our beds. The door remained open; since there were no windows in the room, closing the door would have made it suffocating.

Lying there, I must have drifted into a light doze, when Byomkesh’s voice roused me. "Listen, I don’t like those pegs."

"Pegs? What pegs?"

"Why are there so many pegs in the wall? Pandeyji gave an explanation, but I’m not convinced."

There was little sense in suspecting pegs at such an hour. I glanced at the clock—it was past eleven. Sitaram had not yet returned from his rounds.

"Go to sleep now; think about the pegs tomorrow," I said, turning over on my side.

--- Page 705 ---
314  
Byomkesh Samagra

6

I had fallen into a deep sleep. Suddenly, with a sound like a bomb exploding right by my head, I jolted awake and sat up. For a moment, I could not fathom where I was.

As my sense of time and place returned, I saw Byomkesh shining his torch outside the door, where shards of broken pots and pitchers lay scattered like the remains of some shattered vessel. Then, torch in hand, Byomkesh darted out of the room with great urgency. “Ajit, come—” he called.

I, too, rose in a disheveled hurry and followed him; whether he was pursuing someone or fleeing from danger, I could not tell. Wherever the beam of his torch swept, I ran after it.

At the gateway, Byomkesh cast his light upon the stairs. As I caught up to him, I saw a man rushing up the steps. As he drew near, I recognized him—it was Sitaram.

Byomkesh asked, “Sitaram, did you see anyone coming down the stairs?”

Sitaram replied, “Yes, huzoor, I was coming up when I suddenly collided with someone. I tried to catch him, but he slipped out of my grasp and ran off.”

“Did you recognize him?”

“No, huzoor, I couldn’t see his face in the dark. But as we collided, a foul curse escaped his lips, and from the sound of it, I’d say he was a low-caste Hindustani. But what has happened, huzoor?”

“That I do not yet know for certain. Come and see.”

We returned. In front of the room, the shards of broken pots were strewn about. Byomkesh said, “Look there. I was awake and heard very light footsteps outside. I thought perhaps you had returned. Then, suddenly, the crash—”

Sitaram picked up a piece of broken earthenware and sniffed it. He said, “Huzoor, please, quickly get up onto the bed.”

“Why? What is it?”

“A snake. Someone brought a snake here in a pot covered with a lid and smashed it right here. Give me the torch, I’ll search for it. The snake must be nearby.”

Without delay, we climbed onto the bed, for in the dark of night, bravery is no match for a snake. Sitaram took the torch and began searching outside.

Byomkesh, lighting the lantern, said, “Now I understand what kind of fear they tried to instill in Ishanbabu to drive him away.”

“But who was the man?”

“That is hard to say for now. It could be Bulakilal, it could be Ganpat, or even the Sannisi Thakur.”

At that moment, we heard Sitaram’s sudden burst of laughter. He called out, raising his voice, “Huzoor, come and see over here. There’s nothing to fear.”

Cautiously, we climbed down and went to Sitaram. In one corner of the house, a brown snake lay coiled, writhing. The snake was injured, unable to escape, twisting and turning under the harsh light.

Sitaram laughed and said, “A dhamna snake, huzoor, harmless. Seems someone is trying to—”

--- Page 706 ---
Durg Rahasya 315

“Dillagi is going on.”

Byomkesh said, “Dillagi indeed. But now, what are we to do with the snake?”

“I’ll take care of it.” Sitaram fetched a brass lid with holes for covering food and pressed it down over the snake. “Let it be for tonight. We’ll see about it tomorrow.”

We returned to our room. Sitaram busied himself spreading his bedding before the door. Midnight had just struck.

Byomkesh said, “Sitaram, where were you till so late at night, and what were you doing? Tell me now.”

Sitaram replied, “Huzoor, I went down to the courtyard. There I saw Bulakilal, high on bhang, sleeping in his own quarters. I had hoped to get some information out of him, so I’d befriended him earlier in the evening. I tried shaking him awake, but Bulakilal wouldn’t stir. What to do? I thought I’d go pay my respects to the Sadhu-baba.

“Sadhu-baba was awake, and seemed pleased to see me. He asked me many questions—who you all were, why you’d come here, things like that. I told him you’d come for a change of air.”

Byomkesh said, “Good, good. What else did you talk about?”

Sitaram replied, “A lot of idle chatter, huzoor. Circling around, I finally brought up the subject of Professor Sahib’s death. At that, Sadhu-baba flew into a rage. I saw he harbored a deep grudge against the landlord and the Nayeb-babu. He kept repeating, ‘They’ll be ruined, they’ll be ruined.’”

“Is that so! A most ungrateful sadhu, I see. And then?”

“Then Sadhu-baba packed a chillum of ganja. He offered me some as prasad.”

“You smoked the ganja?”

“Yes, huzoor. One cannot refuse the Sadhu-baba’s prasad.”

“True enough. And then?”

“Then Sadhu-baba spread out his blanket and lay down to sleep. I left. On my way back, I bumped into that man on the stairs.”

Byomkesh said, “Tell me, Sitaram—when you were coming back, did you see Bulakilal?”

Sitaram replied, “No, huzoor, I didn’t see him. But as I passed by the courtyard, I heard his loud snoring coming from his room.”

Byomkesh said, “Well, then it seems whoever brought the snake’s pot, whoever he may be, it was neither Bulakilal nor Sadhu-baba. I hope he won’t return tonight—now get some sleep.”

In the morning, we found the snake under the lid had died during the night; perhaps it had been gravely injured when the pot broke. Sitaram lifted it on the end of a stick and threw it outside the fort walls. We too went up onto the ramparts and made a circuit. We saw that, though the rampart was not entirely unbroken, there was no secret entrance to the fort except for the main gateway. Below the rampart lay a sheer drop into unfathomable depths.

Around eight o’clock, leaving Sitaram at the fort, Byomkesh and I went to Ramkishore-babu’s house. Ramapati greeted us on the main veranda. “Come in. The master will be here any moment—”

--- Page 707 ---
316  
Byomkesh Samagra

“Going out, are you? Heading into the city?”  
“Is that so!” As we hesitated, Ramkishore Babu emerged from inside.  
He wore a silk panjabi, a shawl draped loosely around his neck; seeing us, he said, “Ah! So—how do you like the new place? Spent the night comfortably? No trouble at all?”  
Byomkesh replied, “No trouble at all, I slept very well. Are you going out?”  
“Yes, I have to visit the lawyer’s house, need to get some documents registered. But since you’re here, perhaps I could delay a little—”  
Byomkesh said, “No, no, you go ahead with your work. We’re just out for a stroll, there’s no need.”  
“Well then, all right. Ramapati, serve them some tea. We’ll be back by afternoon.”  
Ramkishore left; his son-in-law, Monilal, followed, carrying a sack full of papers. As they passed us, Monilal greeted us with a cheerful smile and a namaskar.  
Byomkesh said to Ramapati, “No need for tea, we’ve already had some. So, the property partition deed is being registered today, is it?”  
“Yes, sir.”  
“Well, that’s one worry settled. Now, tell me—”  
Ramapati folded his hands and said, “Please, don’t call me ‘apni’, say ‘tumi’ instead.”  
Byomkesh smiled. “Very well, I have much to discuss with you, but that can wait. For now, tell me—where is Ganpat?”  
Ramapati looked a little surprised. “Ganpat—Murali-da’s servant? He must be at home. I haven’t seen him this morning. Shall I call him?”  
Just then, Muralidhar appeared on the verandah, and seeing us, stopped short, startled. His already squinting eyes seemed to squint even more.  
Byomkesh said, “You are Muralidhar Babu, I presume? Namaskar. Could you call your servant Ganpat for us? We need to speak with him.”  
Muralidhar’s face took on a strange expression, a mixture of fear and defiance. In a hoarse voice, he said, “What do you want with Ganpat?”  
“I have a few questions for him.”  
“I—I’ve given him leave. He’s gone home.”  
“Is that so! When did you give him leave?”  
“Yesterday—yesterday afternoon.” Without waiting for further questions, Muralidhar hurried back inside the house.  
We looked at each other. On Ramapati’s face, a nervous excitement appeared. He moved closer to Byomkesh and said in a low voice, “Yesterday afternoon—! But I saw Ganpat at the house even after evening yesterday—”  
Byomkesh nodded. “Quite possible. Because Ganpat didn’t leave the house until midnight. But let that be. The naib, Chandmohan Babu, is at home, isn’t he? We’d like to meet him.”  
Ramapati said, “He’s in his own room—”  
“Good, take us to him.”

Chandmohan’s room was in one corner of the house. As we reached the door, we saw that he was

--- Page 708 ---
Facing the wall, he sat, arranging rows upon rows of tobacco in small clay bowls. Perhaps he preferred to finish the day’s work in the morning itself. Byomkesh waved his hand, dismissing Ramapati. We entered the room; Byomkesh quietly pulled the door to.

Our sudden arrival startled Chandmohan, who sprang to his feet in alarm, a shadow of fear flickering in his cunning eyes. He stammered, “Who—wh-who are you—?”

Byomkesh seated himself at the corner of the takhtposh and said, “Chandmohan Babu, I have a few questions for you.” His tone was not particularly gentle.

Recovering from his initial shock, Chandmohan wiped his hands on his gamchha and asked, “What is it?”

“It’s about something from long ago. How did Rambinod die?”

Chandmohan’s face grew gaunt. He fell silent for a moment, then muttered in a half-whisper, “I can’t say anything—I’m just the steward of this house—”

Byomkesh’s voice turned grave. “Chandmohan Babu, you know my name; it does not go well for those who try to hide things from me. There is proof you were present at the time of Rambinod’s death. Tell me everything—how did he die?”

Chandmohan shot Byomkesh a sharp, furtive glance, then slowly came to sit at one end of the takhtposh. In a dry voice, he said, “Since you insist, I have no choice but to speak. I will tell you what I know.”

He wiped his face with the damp gamchha and began—

“In the winter of 1911, we were in Munger. Rambinod and Ramkishore were in the ghee business then, sending consignments to Calcutta. They had a large warehouse. I was an employee, stationed at the warehouse. The two brothers would come and go.

“Suddenly, one day, plague broke out in Munger. People were dying and fleeing in panic; those who survived abandoned their homes and ran. The city became deserted. Rambinod and Ramkishore were in Munger at the time, and they were in great trouble. There was merchandise worth sixty or seventy thousand rupees in the warehouse—impossible to abandon it; thieves would surely take everything. The three of us consulted and decided to rent a boat on the Ganga and live there, taking turns to go ashore each day to check on the warehouse. Whatever fate had in store, so be it. There was one advantage—the warehouse was not far from the river.

“Rambinod had a childhood friend who taught at a school in Munger—Eshan Majumdar. Eshan died of snakebite some years ago. He too joined us on the boat. There were no boatmen, only the four of us—the boat was quite large; we cooked and lived on it. Sometimes we would anchor at a sandbar in the middle of the Ganga and spend the night there. Our only contact with the city was the daily trip to check on the warehouse.

“Ten or twelve days passed like this. Then, one day, Rambinod caught the plague. He had gone into town and returned with a fever. We took the boat to the sandbar and brought Rambinod ashore. There was no treatment for plague, and in the middle of the river, where would we find medicine, where a doctor? Rambinod died the very next day.”

Byomkesh asked, “What did you do then?”

Chandmohan replied, “We didn’t have the courage to stay any longer. Abandoning the warehouse, we set the boat adrift and fled to Bhagalpur.”

“Did you perform Rambinod’s last rites?”

--- Page 709 ---
Chandmohan wiped his face with a gamchha and said, “There were no means for a cremation; the body was set afloat in the waters of the Ganga.”

“Come, let’s return. Our work here is finished for now.”

As we walked towards the stairs, I asked, “What do you think? Did Chandmohan tell the truth?”

“He lied a little. But it doesn’t matter.”

Just as we were about to descend the stairs, I noticed Tulsi a short distance away, under the shadow of a tree, lost in her own little world of play. So absorbed was she in her solitary game that she did not even notice us. Byomkesh walked over and stood beside her. She looked up at him with wide, startled eyes. Byomkesh smiled gently and said, “Your name is Tulsi, isn’t it? What a sweet little face you have.”

Tulsi continued to stare at him, unblinking. Byomkesh said, “We’re staying in the Durga. Why don’t you come visit us? Come—I’ll tell you many stories.”

Tulsi kept gazing at him, offering no reply. We walked away.

7

Back at the Durga, we took a moment to recover from the fatigue of climbing up and down the stairs. Lighting a cigarette, Byomkesh said, “Ramkishore-babu has gone to register the deed. If it goes through, there’ll be a commotion in their house; Banshi and Murlidhar might want to rent a place in the city. Before that happens, this business needs to be settled once and for all.”

I asked, “Byomkesh, do you understand anything? The more I see, the more tangled it all seems.”

Byomkesh replied, “A vague picture is beginning to form on the screen of my mind. It’s not a small picture; many people, many incidents, many conflicts are woven into its shape. This drama began a hundred years ago, and it still hasn’t ended. —By the way, I’d forgotten about that loose stone from last night. Come, let’s see if there’s a hole beneath it.”

“Let’s go.”

A thin crust of lime and brick dust had formed atop the stone, unlike the smoothness of the surrounding stones. Byomkesh examined it and said, “It looks as though the stone was lifted and then replaced upside down. Come, let’s try to lift it.”

We tried to lift it with our fingers, but the stone wouldn’t budge. Then Sitaram was called. Sitaram, ever resourceful, brought a crowbar and pried the stone up.

Beneath the stone, there was no hole—just solid lime and brick dust. Byomkesh examined the underside of the stone and said, “Look here, there’s Urdu-Farsi script!”

I saw that several lines of foreign script had been engraved on the stone. The carving was not very deep, and dust had settled into the grooves, making the writing almost illegible. Suddenly Byomkesh said, “I think—wait, let me fetch Ishan-babu’s notebook.”

Byomkesh had kept Ishan-babu’s notebook with him. He brought it and began to compare the page with the Persian writing to the inscription on the stone. I looked as well. Though I could not make out the meaning, it was immediately clear that the style of both writings was the same.

--- Page 710 ---
Byomkesh said, “That’s enough. Let’s go.”

We replaced the stone in its original place and returned to the room to sit down. Byomkesh asked, “Do you understand the matter?”

“You explain it clearly,” I replied.

“Recall what happened a hundred years ago. When he heard the sepoys were coming, Rajaram sent his family away. Only he and Jayaram remained in the fort. Then, father and son hid all their treasures.

“But after hiding the gold and jewels, Rajaram grew fearful. If the sepoys killed them, how would their wives and family ever recover the treasure? So he inscribed a coded message on the stone—written in a language not everyone could decipher. Then he smeared it with dust and mud to obscure the writing, so the sepoys wouldn’t easily notice it.

“The sepoys found nothing when they arrived. In their rage, they killed both father and son and left. Later, when Rajaram’s family returned, they too could not discover where he had hidden the treasure. No one noticed the Persian cipher carved into the stone slab.”

I said, “So you believe Rajaram’s treasure is still hidden in the fort?”

“That’s what I think. Unless, of course, the sepoys tortured Rajaram and Jayaram into revealing the hiding place—if so, then there’s nothing left.”

“And then?”

“And then, a hundred years later, Professor Ishan Majumdar arrived. A scholar of history, fluent in Persian; moreover, he had heard the story of the fort from his friend Ram Binode. He began his search—not openly, but in secret. How far his clandestine investigation progressed, I do not know, but he did discover one thing: the cipher carved into the stone. He carefully copied it into his notebook, and then turned the stone over so no one else would see it. After that—after that, what happened is exactly what we must now uncover.”

Byomkesh lay down on the bed, staring up at the ceiling. I too drifted into scattered thoughts. Perhaps Pandeyji would not come at this hour… What news of Satyabati in Calcutta… Why did Byomkesh suddenly speak to Tulsi with such tenderness? The girl is fourteen, but looks no more than ten…

A shadow fell near the door. Turning, I saw Tulsi and Gadadhar. In Tulsi’s eyes was a mixture of fear and curiosity—perhaps she hadn’t dared to come alone, so she had brought Gadadhar with her. Gadadhar, however, showed not the slightest trace of fear or shyness; spinning a top in his hand, his mouth twisted in a mischievous, ear-to-ear grin. Seeing me, he said with a laugh, “He he, Tulsi’s going to marry the son-in-law—he he he—”

With lightning speed, Tulsi turned and slapped him across the face. Gadadhar stood for a moment, cheeks puffed out, then, his face solemn, he left the room, spinning his top as he went. Clearly, he was well accustomed to being slapped by his younger sister.

Byomkesh gently invited Tulsi into the room, but she hesitated, clutching the doorpost. Byomkesh then took her by the hand and seated her on the bed.

Still, Tulsi’s fear did not subside; she sat with the tense posture of a startled fawn. Byomkesh began to speak to her in a soft, friendly tone, as if to an equal. A few words of gentle teasing, stories of girls’ games, doll weddings, and other amusements—gradually, as she listened, Tulsi’s fear began to melt away. At first…

--- Page 711 ---
320  
Byomkesh Samagra

After a few monosyllabic replies—‘hmm’, ‘no’—she began to speak more freely. Within fifteen minutes, an easy camaraderie had blossomed between Tulsi and us. I observed her closely: her mind was guileless, her intelligence keen; only her nerves were not sound. The slightest provocation would send her into a nervous agitation that exceeded all bounds of simplicity. Byomkesh had read her character well, and with gentle affection had drawn her in.

There is no need to recount every detail of our conversation with Tulsi; much of what we learned about their family has already been recorded. I will note down what remains.

Byomkesh asked, “The gentleman who was here, your father’s friend Ishanbabu—did you get along with him?”

Tulsi replied, “Yes. He used to tell me so many stories. He couldn’t sleep at night; many times, in the middle of the night, I’d go to him to hear his tales.”

“Is that so! The night he died—where were you that night?”

“That night, they had locked me in my room.”

“Locked you in your room? What’s this!”

“Yes. I tend to wander off at odd hours, so whenever they get the chance, they lock me in.”

“Who are ‘they’?”

“Everyone. Father, elder brother, second brother, brother-in-law—”

“Who locked you in your room that night?”

“Father.”

“I see. And last night, was it your second brother who locked you in?”

“Yes—how did you know?”

“I know everything. Now, tell me something else. Your elder brother was married, do you remember your sister-in-law?”

“Why wouldn’t I? She was very beautiful. Didi was terribly jealous of her.”

“Really! Then why did your sister-in-law commit suicide?”

“I don’t know. That night, Didi had locked me in my room.”

“Oh—”

Byomkesh exchanged a glance with me. After some further conversation, Byomkesh asked, “Tell me, Tulsi, whom do you love most in this house?”

Without hesitation, with a candid face, Tulsi replied, “Mastermoshai. He loves me very much too.”

“And you don’t love Monilal?”

Tulsi’s eyes suddenly flashed—“No. Why does he envy Mastermoshai? Why does he complain about Mastermoshai to Father? If he marries me, I’ll push him off a cliff!” With that, Tulsi dashed out of the room.

The two of us sat looking at each other. At last, Byomkesh sighed and said, “Poor girl!”

Half an hour later, as I was about to get up for a bath, Ramapati peeked in at the door, his voice hesitant. “Did Tulsi come this way?”

Byomkesh replied, “Yes, she left just a little while ago. Come in—sit down.”

--- Page 712 ---
Durgo Rahasya 321

Ramapati sat down hesitantly.

Byomkesh said, “Ramapati, the first day we came here, you said that now the mystery of Ishan Babu’s death would be solved. That is, you believe there is a problem surrounding Ishan Babu’s death. Is that so?”

Ramapati remained silent. Byomkesh continued, “Let us assume that Ishan Babu’s death is mysterious, that someone has murdered him. Now I will ask you a few questions—answer them directly. Don’t be afraid. Imagine you are giving sworn testimony in court.”

In a faint voice, Ramapati replied, “Ask.”

Byomkesh said, “You know everyone in this house well. Tell me, is there anyone among them who is capable of murder?”

Ramapati, frightened, was silent for a while, then answered in a trembling voice, “I shouldn’t say so, I am dependent on them. But if the situation demanded it, perhaps anyone could commit murder.”

“Anyone? Ramkishore Babu?”

“Yes.”

“Banshidhar?”

“Yes.”

“Murulidhar?”

“Yes. Their natures are rather violent—”

“Nayeb Chandmohan?”

“Perhaps not. But if the master ordered it, he could have someone killed.”

“Monilal?”

Ramapati’s face darkened; clenching his teeth, he said, “He doesn’t have the courage to kill with his own hands. He only causes harm by tattling and intrigue.”

“And you? Could you not commit murder?”

“I—!”

“All right, let it be. —Did you steal the torch?”

With a bitter expression, Ramapati said, “I know I have been maligned. I know who has slandered me. But tell me yourself—if I were to steal, would I steal a mere torch?”

“So you did not steal it. —Fine, do you know that Monilal is to marry Tulsi?”

Ramapati’s face grew hard, but he replied with restraint, “I know. That is the master’s wish.”

“No one else’s wish?”

“No.”

“Not yours either?”

Ramapati stood up—“I am a burden here, what do my wishes or lack thereof matter? But if this marriage takes place, there will be an ugly scene.” Without waiting for our permission, he left the room.

Byomkesh gazed at the door for a while, then said, “The boy has courage!”

--- Page 713 ---
322  
Byomkesh Samagra

8

In the evening, when Sitaram brought in the tea, Byomkesh said to him, “Sitaram, I need you to do something. About two years ago, a group of Bedes pitched their tents here. You must go to Bulakilal and find out who from the house used to visit the Bedes’ camp—gather as much information as you can on this.”

Sitaram replied, “Yes, huzoor. Bulakilal has gone to town with the gentlemen, I’ll ask him when he returns.”

After Sitaram left, I asked, “Why this curiosity about the Bedes?”

Byomkesh replied, “Poison! Don’t you think we need to find out where the snake venom came from?”

“Oh—”

At that moment, Pandeyji arrived. A binocular hung from his shoulder by a leather strap. Byomkesh said, “What’s this? Why the binoculars?”

Pandeyji replied, “Brought them for you, in case you need them. I couldn’t come in the morning, got caught up with work. So I set out early this afternoon. On the way, I saw Ramkishore Babu returning from town. Their engine had broken down, Bulakilal was gripping the wheel, Ramkishore Babu sat inside the car, and his son-in-law, Monilal, was pushing it.”

“And then?”

“Tied a rope and towed the car back.”

“Their court business all settled?”

“No, a bit remains, they’ll go again tomorrow. So, any new developments?”

“There’s plenty of new developments.”

As he listened to the news, Pandeyji grew visibly excited. When the account was finished, he said, “There’s no doubt anymore. Ishan Babu discovered the hidden treasury, and that’s why someone killed him. The question now is, who could have done it?”

Byomkesh said, “Anyone from Ramkishore Babu to Sannyasi Thakur could have done it. But that’s not the only question. There are others.”

“Such as?”

“For instance, where did the poison come from? Snake venom isn’t something you find just anywhere. Then, consider this: to inject snake venom into the body, you’d need a device that leaves marks just like a snake’s fangs.”

“Hypodermic syringe—”

“The mark from an injection needle is very small, it disappears in a few minutes. You saw the marks on Ishan Babu’s leg—do they look like injection marks to you?”

“No. Besides, two marks side by side—”

“That’s nothing. If the patient has already lost consciousness, is it so hard to prick twice in the same spot?”

“That’s true. What other questions?”

“And, if Ishan Babu did find the secret treasury, then where is it?”

“Surely, it’s somewhere in this fort.”

“Not just in the fort, it’s within this very house. The way Murlidhar tried to frighten us with snakes—”

--- Page 714 ---
The Mystery of the Fort 323

“Why is someone trying to drive us away? What could be the reason?”

Pandeyji looked sharply. “Murali Dhar?”

Byomkesh replied, “He may have some other motive. The main point is, before Ishan Babu arrived, no one knew about the toshakhana. After that, someone else found out—besides Ishan Babu—and murdered him. But I believe they couldn’t move the loot.”

“How can you be sure?”

“Look, no one likes that we’re staying in the fort. What does that mean?”

“I understand. So, first we need to find the toshakhana. Have you thought about where it might be?”

Byomkesh smiled slightly and said, “Since yesterday, a suspicion has been circling in my mind—”

I said, “The pegs!”

At that moment, Sitaram came in to clear away the tea things. Byomkesh glanced at him and said, “Bulakilal has returned!”

Sitaram nodded and left with the cups. Pandey asked, “Did you send him somewhere?”

“Yes, I needed something from Bulakilal.”

“Good. Now tell us about your suspicion.”

Byomkesh said, “Those pegs. I suspect their obvious purpose isn’t their only purpose. They’re what you’d call a smokescreen, a decoy.”

Pandey examined the pegs and said, “Hmm. So what can be done?”

“I’d like to take a closer look at them. Since you’re here, it’s best to do whatever needs to be done in your presence. If we find the toshakhana, only the three of us will know for now. Ajit, close the door.”

When I shut the door, the room was plunged into darkness. We lit the lantern and shone our torches, beginning our search. There were fifteen pegs in all. We pulled, pushed, and tugged at each one, trying to lift them. The pegs were rusted but strong as thunderbolts—not a hair’s breadth did they move.

Suddenly Pandey exclaimed, “Here! This one’s moving—just a little—!” 

We rushed to his side. In the middle of the wall facing the door was a peg. Pandey was pushing it inward; whether it was moving or not, we couldn’t tell.

Pandey said, “I can’t do it alone. Byomkesh Babu, help me.”

Byomkesh knelt and began to push the stone with both hands. The rectangular stone slowly slid backward. Beneath it, a dark hole appeared.

I shone my torch inside. The hole was about two cubits long and wide, and inside, a narrow flight of stairs descended.

Pandey, wiping the sweat from his brow in great excitement, said, “Bravo! We’ve found the toshakhana. Byomkesh Babu, you’re the discoverer—you go first.”

Torch in hand, Byomkesh descended first, then Pandeyji, and finally I, carrying the lantern. The room below was as spacious as the one above. Along one wall stood rows of earthen jars. Each jar was capped with a small pot, the pots covered with lids. In another corner stood a large oven, to which a bellows pipe was attached. The leather of the bellows had long since dried and crumbled away, but from the frame, it was recognizable as a bellows. There was nothing else in the room.

--- Page 715 ---
324  
Byomkesh Samagra

Our breaths were coming fast and heavy—who knows what king’s fortune lay hidden in those stout, pot-bellied jars? But first, it was necessary to search the rest of the room. As we swept the light around, something shiny caught my eye in a corner. Rushing over, I saw—a small electric torch. I picked it up and tried to switch it on, but the torch had already been left burning; its cell was spent and it had gone out.

Byomkesh said, “This is irrefutable proof that Ishanbabu had discovered this room.”

Then we opened the jars one by one. Every jar was empty, except for a little powder at the bottom of one, like salt. Byomkesh scooped up a pinch, examined it, then wrapped it in his handkerchief and put it in his pocket. He said, “It could be salt, it could be lime, or it could be something else entirely.”

Next, we examined the vats one after another. But alas, the treasures of seven kings did not reveal themselves. Every vat was empty, not a single coin to be found anywhere.

We stared at each other, bewildered. Then we ransacked the room, but found nothing.

Returning upstairs, the first thing we did was pull the secret door shut. Then we opened the door to the room. Outside, darkness had fallen; Sitaram was pacing up and down by the doorway.

Byomkesh said wearily, “Sitaram, make us another round of tea.”

The three of us took chairs and sat outside. Pandeyji was deflated. He said, “Well, what happened? Where did the goods go?”

Byomkesh replied, “There are three possibilities. One: the sepoys looted everything. Two: whoever killed Ishanbabu took the goods away that very night. Three: Rajaram hid the goods somewhere else.”

“Which possibility seems most likely to you?”

Byomkesh waved his hand and recited the last line of the poem ‘Balaka’: “Not here, somewhere else, somewhere else, in some other place.”

As we drank our tea, Byomkesh asked Sitaram, “Did you see Bulakilal?”

Sitaram replied, “Yes. He was repairing the car’s engine, we exchanged a few words.”

“What did he say?”

“Sahib, Bulakilal is a real character. As soon as evening falls, he gets high on bhang and sleeps like the dead—he knows nothing of what happens at night. But during the day, everyone from the house used to visit the Bedas’ tent. Even Bulakilal went a few times.”

“Why did Bulakilal go?”

“To have his palm read. The Bedeni women are said to be good palmists, can tell past and future. They told Bulakilal he would soon become the driver for the Lat Sahib’s motorcar.”

“Who else from the house used to go?”

“The master, the master’s two elder sons, the son-in-law, the steward—everyone went. And the two little children were always hanging around there.”

“Hm, anything else?”

“Nothing else, sahib.”

As night was falling, Pandeyji rose to leave. Byomkesh handed him the little bundle wrapped in his handkerchief and said, “Have this analyzed chemically. So far, the information we have gathered—”

--- Page 716 ---
Durgarahasya 325

There is no reason to be disheartened by this. At least, I am certain that Ishaan Babu was murdered; the rest of the information will come in due course.”

Pandeyji, as he tucked the little bundle of handkerchiefs into his pocket, said, “Oh, there was a letter for you in the post—I completely forgot to give it to you. Here you are. Well then, I’ll come again tomorrow.”

After seeing Pandeyji off to the stairs, I returned and sat down. Byomkesh opened the letter and read it. I asked, “Is it from Satyabati?”

“No, it’s from Sukumar.”

“What news?”

“Nothing new. But all is well.”

9

The night passed without incident.

The next morning, Byomkesh sat down with Ishaan Babu’s notebook. Sometimes he read, sometimes he lifted his eyes upward and moved his lips silently, but he spoke not a word.

Pandeyji had left the binoculars yesterday; as I fiddled with them, I asked, “What are you investigating?”

Byomkesh replied curtly, “Mohanlal.”

For some reason, at the mention of Mohanlal, I was reminded of the ‘Battle of Plassey’ I had read about long ago. I said, “Once again, the cannons roar… shaking the holy waters of the Ganga—”

Byomkesh shot me a reproachful glance. I continued, “Stand firm, turn back, O foreigner, Mohanlal thunders, death draws near.”

Seeing the reproach in Byomkesh’s eyes turn gradually ferocious, I decided to leave the room. If someone cannot tolerate heroic poetry, it is not right to torment him with it.

Outside, the autumn morning gleamed with golden light. The binoculars were still in my hand, so I climbed up to the parapet. The view all around was delightful. The distant mountain peaks seemed to have drawn near, waves of light played atop the forest canopy. As I scanned the scene, I found myself in front of Ramkishore Babu’s house. I could see every detail. Ramkishore stepped out, ready to go to the city, accompanied by his two sons and his son-in-law. They descended the stairs; a little later, the motorcar drove off… Left behind in the house were Master Ramapati, Gadadhar, and Tulsi.

When I returned to the room, I found Byomkesh still bent over the notebook, reading like a hen drinking water—head dipping down, then lifting up to mutter to himself. I said, “Hey, Ramkishore Babu and his family have gone to the city.”

Byomkesh, paying no heed, continued to drink water like a hen. Then suddenly he said, “Mohanlal was quite the hero, wasn’t he?”

“So I’ve heard.”

Byomkesh said nothing more. From his demeanor, it seemed he would not rise from the notebook today. Thinking the morning would pass in idleness, I said, “Come on, let’s go see the sadhu. Perhaps he knows how to read palms.”

Byomkesh, distracted, looked up and said, “Not now, we’ll go later in the afternoon.”

--- Page 717 ---
326  
Byomkesh Samagra

The afternoon drifted by as I lay half-awake on my bed, drowsy and languid. Ramkishore-babu had spoken the truth; spend two days in such desolation, and your soul will try to flee your body.  

By a quarter to three, I could no longer toss and turn on the mattress. I got up. Byomkesh was not in the room. Stepping outside, I saw him pacing atop the parapet. The sun was not particularly harsh, but I could not fathom the appeal of taking the air on the parapet at this hour. Still, thinking perhaps he had discovered something new, I made my way toward him.  

At the sight of me, he seemed to emerge from the depths of his own mind, and said mechanically, “I need a turpune.”  

“Turpune!” I saw a restless, distracted look in his eyes. That look was not unfamiliar to me—he had found something. I asked, “What did you find?”  

Byomkesh now became fully aware of my presence, and with a hint of embarrassment, replied, “No, nothing. You were sleeping so soundly, I thought I’d come up here and use the binoculars to take in the scenery. But there’s nothing much to see. Here, you look.”  

The binoculars were resting on the parapet’s balustrade. Byomkesh handed them to me and climbed down. I was a little surprised. What was this mood of Byomkesh’s today?  

I let my gaze wander. The outer world shimmered in the sultry air. I raised the binoculars to my eyes; sweeping them this way and that, I finally fixed them on Ramkishore-babu’s house.  

There is a certain resemblance between peering through binoculars and eavesdropping; it is as if one is “spying with the eyes.” Through the binoculars, Ramkishore-babu’s house seemed to have come within ten hands’ reach; I could see everything, while no one could see me.  

No one was at the front of the house, but the door stood open. The binoculars moved upward. A roof bordered by a knee-high balustrade, the staircase at the back. Ramapati sat on the parapet, his chin in his hand—I could see his profile. There was no one else on the roof; Ramapati sat, brow furrowed, lost in thought.  

Suddenly, Ramapati started and looked up. Tulsi came up the stairs, her face alive with secret excitement. She approached Ramapati with quick, light steps, drew her right hand from beneath her anchal, and showed it to him. She held something—a black pencil, or perhaps a fountain pen.  

I watched through the binoculars, but could hear nothing; it was like a silent film from another era. Ramapati, agitated, said something, gesturing with his hands. Tulsi pleaded, clinging to his neck, trying to press the black object into his hand.  

At that moment, several more actors entered the stage. Ramkishore-babu came up the stairs, followed by Banshidhar and Muralidhar; last of all, Monilal.  

All of them were angry; Ramapati stood up, frozen. Banshidhar, his face twisted with rage, rebuked Tulsi and snatched the black object from her hand. Tulsi argued spiritedly for a moment, then, on the verge of tears, went back down the stairs. The others now surrounded Ramapati, berating him with raised voices, while Monilal simply stared with hard, unblinking eyes, his lips pressed tight.  

Suddenly, Banshidhar slapped Ramapati across the face. Ramkishore intervened, then, with a commanding gesture, pointed toward the stairs. They all descended.  

What could this strange scene mean? Without the dialogue, it was impossible to determine. I watched for another ten—

--- Page 718 ---
I waited for several minutes, but no further dramatic developments presented themselves; whatever transpired within the house took place beyond the reach of my eyes.

Descending, I recounted everything to Byomkesh. He listened with deep attention and said,  
“So Ramkishore-babu and the others have returned from the city. —You couldn’t make out what Tulsi was carrying in her hand?”  
“It looked like a fountain pen.”  
“Let’s see, perhaps we’ll have news soon. Ramapati might come.”

But it was not Ramapati who arrived; it was Tulsi. Like a dry leaf swept before a storm, she seemed to flutter and stumble to our doorway, where she halted. Her appearance was wild, her eyes burning red. Catching sight of Byomkesh seated on the bed, she rushed forward and collapsed into his lap, breaking into a wailing cry, “They’ve thrown out my Master-moshai!”

“Thrown him out?”

It was not easy to calm Tulsi’s tears. Nevertheless, under Byomkesh’s gentle consolations, her sobs gradually subsided, and the true story emerged.

Her brother-in-law owned two fountain pens; one was his own, the other he had received as dowry at his wedding... What would he do with two pens? So today, in his absence, Tulsi had taken one from his drawer to give to Master-moshai—Master-moshai had not a single pen of his own—but Master-moshai refused to accept it, grew angry, and ordered her to return it to its place. At that very moment, the whole household arrived, accused Master-moshai of theft... Tulsi insisted that Master-moshai had not stolen anything, but no one listened. In the end, they beat him and drove him from the house.

There was no discrepancy between what I had seen through the telescope and Tulsi’s account. Together, we tried to comfort her, assuring her that the Master would return, there was nothing to worry about; if necessary, we would go and speak to her father.

At the sound of a throat being cleared near the door, I turned sharply and saw the brother-in-law, Monilal, standing there. At the sight of him, Tulsi darted past like an arrow and vanished.

Byomkesh said, “Come in, Monibabu.”

Monilal entered the room and said, “Sir sent me to look for Tulsi. She’s quite a handful—doesn’t trouble you too much, I hope?”

Byomkesh replied, “Not at all. She came to tell us her Master had been thrown out.”

Monilal looked a little uncomfortable and said, “Yes, Ramapati was dismissed by Sir. None of us were at home, and he opened my drawer with a spare key and tried to steal a pen. An expensive pen—”

Byomkesh extended his hand and said, “Is it the pen you have in your breast pocket?”

“Yes.” Monilal handed the pen to Byomkesh.

A Parker pen—an expensive item. Byomkesh, a lover of fine pens, unscrewed the cap, examined the filling mechanism at the back; then, returning the pen, he said, “A fine pen. If one were to steal, this is the sort of pen worth stealing. Is there anyone else in the house who owns a fountain pen?”

Monilal replied, “No one else. There isn’t much of a tradition of study in this house. Only—”

--- Page 719 ---
328  
Byomkesh Samagra

The master writes with ink and pen.'  
'Hmm. Tulsi says he himself took the pen from your drawer—'  
Manilal said with a note of regret, 'Tulsi is lying. Ramapati has always had that flaw. Just the other day, an electric torch—'  
I was about to say, 'But the electric torch—'  
But before I could finish, Byomkesh interjected, 'An electric torch is a trivial thing. Ramapati, whatever else he may be, is an intelligent man—would he really ruin his future by stealing a torch?'  
Manilal stared at Byomkesh for a moment, then said, 'Your words confuse me. Who knows, perhaps he didn't steal the torch after all. But today, my pen—. Then could it really be that Tulsi—'  
I insisted, 'Yes, Tulsi told the truth. I—'  
Once again, Byomkesh cut me off, saying, 'Manilal-babu, we ought not to meddle in your family affairs. We're only here for a couple of days as guests—what need have we to get involved in all this? You have acted as you thought best.'  
'Even so—it's not right to falsely accuse someone—' muttered Manilal as he stepped toward the door.  
Byomkesh asked, 'Are the affairs of your main office settled for today?'  
'Yes, everything was finished early this morning. Only the signatures remained.'  
'Good, then you can be at ease now.'  
'Yes, indeed.'  
Once Manilal had left, Byomkesh peeked out the door, then came back and said, 'You were just about to give everything away!'  
'What! What did I almost give away?'  
'First, you were about to say that the missing torch had been found.'  
'Yes.'  
'Then you were going to mention that you’d seen the rooftop scene through the telescope!'  
'Yes, but what harm would that have done?'  
'Telling Manilal anything is the same as telling the whole house. The lion we’re hunting for, cloaked in donkey’s skin, would have learned that we’ve discovered the toshakhana and have been watching them day and night through the telescope. Wouldn’t the prey have been spooked?'  
I hadn’t thought of that.  

At that moment, Sitaram arrived with tea. A little later, Pandeyji came in. He had brought us a variety of fresh provisions. Sitaram went out to fetch them from the motorcar.  
As we drank our tea, we exchanged news.  
After hearing our report, Pandeyji said, 'The fish is slipping out of the net. Today Ramapati has left, tomorrow Banshidhar and Murlidhar will go. We must draw in the net quickly. Yes, we’ve received news of the misdeed Banshidhar committed at the college hostel.'  
'What misdeed?'  
'He had a quarrel with another boy, though it was patched up. But Banshidhar nursed a grudge; on Dol, he mixed dhutura seeds with siddhi and gave it to the boy. The boy nearly died—barely survived.'

--- Page 720 ---
Durgarahasya 329

Byomkesh was silent for a moment, then said, “Hmm. So, poisoning is a habit of Banshidhar’s.”

“It is,” I replied. “Not just stubbornness—he nurses grudges.”

The clock struck five. Byomkesh said, “Let’s go, let’s have a word with the Sannyasi Thakur today.”

10

As we descended to the gateway, we saw Banshidhar coming down the inner staircase with heavy, purposeful steps. The moment he spotted us, his brisk stride faltered, became oddly disjointed; but he did not stop, simply kept us behind him and strode ahead as if he meant to take the road out to the city.

Byomkesh murmured quietly, “Banshidhar was headed to the Sadhu-baba. Seeing us, he got flustered and changed his route.”

Banshidhar hadn’t gone far when Pandeyji called out, “Banshidhar Babu!”

Banshidhar turned, his brows lowered, and stood still. We approached. Pandeyji, in a tone laced with amusement, asked, “Where are you off to in such a hurry?”

Banshidhar replied curtly, “Going for a walk.”

Pandeyji smiled and said, “You’ve just come back from the city, and you want to go out again?”

The veins in Banshidhar’s temples stood out; he retorted with insolent sharpness, “Yes, I’ll go! You may be the police, but you can’t stop me from taking a walk.”

Pandeyji’s face hardened. In a stern voice, he said, “Oh, but I can. You poisoned someone in your college hostel—the case is still unresolved. Criminal cases never expire. I can arrest you.”

Fear drained the color from Banshidhar’s face, turning it blue. To see arrogance so swiftly dissolve into terror—one would not believe it without witnessing it. Like a trapped animal, his eyes darted about, wild and desperate, then in a flash he vanished back into the house by the same staircase he had come down.

Pandeyji chuckled softly.  
“So much for Banshidhar’s bravado. —Come.”

We went to see the Sadhu-baba. The thick, tangled trees all around had plunged the place into near darkness. Babaji sat before a burning dhuni. At our approach, his face broke into a silent, meaningful smile; with a gesture, he invited us to sit.

Pandeyji began the conversation. Needless to say, it was in Hindi.  
Pandeyji was in his police khaki shirt, and Babaji seemed to speak with him with particular interest.

For a while, the talk was general. We all agreed on the greatness of the ascetic life and the mire of household existence. The cheerful Babaji began to take out ganja from his jhuli and set about preparing it.

Pandeyji asked, “Where do you get ganja here, Baba?”

Babaji glanced heavenward and replied, “The Lord provides, son.”

With tongs, he picked a glowing ember from the dhuni and placed it on the tip of his chillum. At that moment—

--- Page 721 ---
BYOMKESH SAMAGRA

I examined his chimta closely. The sadhus who dwell fearlessly in forests and wilds are not, after all, entirely unarmed. If one knows how to wield it properly, I suspect this chimta could even kill a tiger. Yet with its sharply pointed tips, I had just seen him pick up a tiny ember with the greatest of ease. The sadhus, it seems, accomplish many tasks with this single iron implement.

Be that as it may, Babaji drew deeply on his clay chillum of ganja. The veins and sinews of his neck swelled and stood out. For a full minute he inhaled, then, having exhausted the chillum, he turned it upside down.

Now came the exhalation. Babaji performed this task for nearly three minutes; thin wisps of smoke drifted from his beard and moustache, perfuming the air with their faint aroma.

Babaji murmured, “Bam! Bam Shankar!”

At that moment, hearing footsteps, I turned to see a man approaching. He stopped short at the sight of us. Then I recognized him—Ramkishore Babu! Realizing who we were, he stammered, “Oh—you—!”

Byomkesh said, “Come in.”

Ramkishore, in a slightly disappointed tone, replied, “No, you are speaking with the sadhuji, I see. I only came for a darshan.” With folded hands, he saluted the sadhu and took his leave.

Turning to the sadhu, I saw that strange smile on his lips again. To analyze it—how much was spiritual, how much mischievous—it was hard to say. Perhaps equal parts of both.

Now Byomkesh spoke. “Sadhubaba, you have been here a long time. The other day, a man died here from a snakebite—do you know of it?”

The sadhu replied, “Janta hai. Hum kya nahi janta!”

Byomkesh asked, “Well, did you see anyone go to the fort that night?”

“Yes, I saw.”

Once more, that smile—half mystical, half roguish—appeared on Babaji’s face. Byomkesh, eager, was about to question him further when again footsteps sounded behind us. We turned; Babaji, too, glanced sharply in that direction. But no one appeared; perhaps, realizing our presence, the visitor had turned back from a distance.

Byomkesh was about to question Babaji again when the sadhu pressed a finger to his lips and said, in clear Bengali, “Not now. Come at midnight, then I will speak.”

I stared in astonishment. Byomkesh, however, sprang up at once and said, “Very well, Baba, we shall come. Get up, Ajit.”

Outside the grove, we saw that night had fallen. Byomkesh and Pandeyji cast searching glances all around, but no suspicious figure was to be seen.

Pandeyji said, “I’ll head back from here tonight. If only I could stay till midnight. But there’s no way. I’ll come first thing in the morning.”

Pandeyji departed.

As we walked back to the fort, I asked, “Is Sadhubaba a Bengali?”

Byomkesh replied, “A true Bengali.”

Back at the fort, I glanced at my watch—it was seven o’clock. Under the open sky, I set out a chair and sat down.

--- Page 722 ---
Byomkesh Samagra  
331

The sadhubaba surely knows something. But what does he know? Did he see Ishaanbabu’s murderer entering the fort that night? The staircase leading up from the grove to the fort isn’t visible; especially not on a dark night. Then does the sadhubaba wander near the stairs in the dead of night?... His tongs, after all, are not a trivial weapon—if the tip of those tongs were smeared with poison—

I asked Byomkesh; he only made a sound in his throat, like a suppressed cough.

By ten o’clock, we finished our meal and went out to sit again. We would have to stay awake for another two hours. Sitaram, having eaten, slipped away into the shadows; probably to smoke a bidi or two. The lantern was turned down low in the corner of the room.

The clock’s hands were edging toward eleven. Despite the tension in my mind, I kept yawning—

“Byomkeshbabu!”

A muffled voice startled me out of my stupor. I saw, not far off, a figure standing like a shadow. Byomkesh rose and said, “Ramapati! Come in.”

Taking Ramapati with us, we went inside. Byomkesh turned up the light and examined him from head to toe. “I see you haven’t eaten tonight. —Sitaram!”

Within five minutes, Sitaram brought in a few fried eggs and set them before Ramapati. Without a word, Ramapati began to eat. His face was drawn, his eyes sunken; his half-shirt was torn in several places, and he wore no shoes. As he ate, he said, “So you’ve heard everything? Who told you?”

“Tulsi. Where have you been all this while?”

“In the jungle. Then behind the fort.”

“Did they beat you badly?”

Ramapati lifted his shirt to show his back—red welts stood out in stripes.

Byomkesh’s face hardened.

“Banshidhar?”

Ramapati nodded.

“Why didn’t you go to the city?”

Ramapati didn’t answer, but silently continued eating.

“What’s the point of you staying here any longer?”

In a barely audible voice, Ramapati said, “Tulsi—”

“You love Tulsi?”

Ramapati was silent for a moment, then slowly said, “Everyone torments her, keeps her locked up, no one loves her. If I’m not here, she’ll die.”

When he finished eating, Byomkesh showed him his own bed and said, “Sleep.”

With a weary sigh, Ramapati lay down. Byomkesh gazed at him for a long time, then suddenly began to ask questions.

“Ramapati, do you know who killed Ishaanbabu?”

“No, I don’t know who killed him. But he was murdered.”

“Do you know who killed Haripriya?”

“No, Didi tried to tell me—but she couldn’t.”

“Do you know why Banshidhar’s wife jumped from the mountain?”

After a brief pause, Ramapati said, “I don’t know, but I suspected. Didi couldn’t stand her, Didi’s heart was very spiteful. I think she put on a mask and frightened her with ghosts—”

--- Page 723 ---
332  
Durgar Hasya

“A mask?”  
“Didi had a Japanese mask. The day after that incident, I found the mask at the edge of the jungle; perhaps it had blown there in the wind. I brought it back and showed it to Didi. She snatched it from my hand and tore it up.”  
“Does Banshidhar know about the mask?”  
“I haven’t said anything.”  
“You must have seen the Sadhubaba. What do you think of him?”  
“I don’t feel any reverence. But the master holds him in high regard. He comes straight from the house.”  
“Has Ishanbabu ever told you anything about the Sadhubaba?”  
“No. He never went to see him either. He was rather annoyed with holy men and ascetics.”  
Byomkesh glanced at his watch and said, “It’s midnight. Ramapati, you should get some sleep. We’re stepping out for a bit.”  
Ramapati’s eyes widened. “Where to?”  
“Not far, we’ll be back soon. Come, Ajit.”  
Taking the large torch, we stepped outside.  
Ramkishorebabu’s house was shrouded in darkness. As we passed by the entrance hall, we heard Bulaki Lal snoring thunderously.  
The grove was thick with darkness, broken only by the muffled glow from the ash-covered dhuni. Sadhubaba lay beside the sacred fire; his posture was not quite natural.  
Byomkesh cast a sharp beam of light onto his face, but Babaji did not stir. Byomkesh then shook him by the shoulder and drew in a loud breath, exclaiming, “Ah—!”  
The torchlight swept over Babaji’s body and came to rest upon his feet. There, on the upper part of the heel, were two puncture marks—fangs of a snake.

11

Byomkesh said, “Well, at last, Ram Binod has truly left his body.”  
“Ram Binod!”  
“You look as if you’ve fallen from the sky. Didn’t you realize? Remarkable.”  
Fuel had been thrown into the dhuni, the flames now blazing. Babaji’s corpse lay stiff beside it. The two of us sat a little distance away, facing each other, drawing on our cigarettes.  
Byomkesh said, “Remember, when I first saw Ramkishorebabu, he seemed familiar? In truth, it was Babaji who seemed familiar when I saw him earlier.  
When I saw Ramkishore the second time, I understood.”  
“But Ram Binod died of the plague!”  
“The plague did strike Ram Binod’s household, but before he died, the others abandoned him and fled. I gathered that from Chandmohan’s account. After that, Ram Binod survived. It’s a bit like the Bhawal Sannyasi case.”  
“Where has he been all this time?”  
“That I don’t know. Perhaps at first, he was seized by renunciation, wandering among ascetics and holy men—”

--- Page 724 ---
Byomkesh Samagra 333

He had been wandering. Then, suddenly catching wind of Ramkishore’s whereabouts, he had turned up here. But let that be for now; we must arrange for someone to watch over the corpse. Ajit, I’ll stay here, you take the torch and go fetch Sitaram. And if you can, rouse Bulaki Lal from his sleep and bring him along too. Let the two of them stand guard over the body.’

I said, ‘You’ll stay here alone? That won’t do. If we must stay, we’ll stay together; if we must go, we’ll go together.’

‘Afraid I’ll be bitten by a snake too! This isn’t that kind of snake, you know—it doesn’t strike at people who are awake. Still, since you insist, let’s both go.’

At the entrance, Byomkesh said, ‘Wait, let’s wake Bulaki Lal first.’

After much pushing and shaking, Bulaki Lal finally woke up. Byomkesh gave him the news of Babaji’s death and asked, ‘Did Sadhu Baba have any bhang today?’

‘Yes, he drank a potful.’

‘Who else had bhang?’

‘A servant from the house came and took a potful.’

‘Alright, now go and stand guard over Babaji. I’ll send Sitaram along.’

Bulaki Lal shuffled off, still half-asleep. Even though he was awake, it seemed the haze of intoxication hadn’t left him.

Returning to the quarters, we found Sitaram sitting up, already awake. On hearing the news, his eyes widened once, then he silently went out.

Inside, Ramapati had fallen asleep. I did not wake him. I went outside and sat down. The night was half past twelve.

Byomkesh said, ‘No more sleeping tonight. At least one of us must stay awake. Ajit, why don’t you sleep for a couple of hours, then I’ll wake you and take my turn.’

I was reluctant to get up; my mind was too agitated. I asked, ‘Byomkesh, who killed Babaji?’

‘The same one who killed Ishan Babu.’

‘Who is it?’

‘Why don’t you say? Can’t you guess?’

That very question had been circling in my mind. Slowly, I said, ‘If Babaji was actually Ram Binod, then who could have killed him? There’s Ramkishore Babu—’

‘He would kill his own brother?’

‘He abandoned his dying brother and fled. Now that brother has returned, perhaps to claim his share of the property—’

‘Alright, suppose Ramkishore killed his brother. But why kill Ishan Babu?’

‘Ishan Babu was Ram Binod’s dearest friend. He recognized the sanyasi. Perhaps he threatened Ramkishore, said he’d expose everything if he didn’t get his fair share. Only two people could identify the sanyasi as Ram Binod—Chandmohan and Ishan Babu. Chandmohan is in the master’s pocket; if Ishan Babu is removed, all obstacles are gone—’

Byomkesh suddenly slapped me on the back and said, ‘Ajit! What’s this? You’ve started reasoning things out, step by step! Has enlightenment finally dawned on you? But enough, go get some sleep now. I’ll wake you at exactly three.’

As I was about to leave, he muttered to himself in a low voice, ‘Ramapati is sleeping—or is he?’

--- Page 725 ---
334 Durgo Rahasya

“Did you doze off, Matka?—No matter, I’m wide awake.”

Around eight o’clock, Pandeyji arrived. He had already heard the news of Babaji’s death downstairs, and now he learned the rest. Byomkesh said, “You take the body back. But do come again—and listen—”

Byomkesh took him a little aside and spoke to him in a low voice for a while. Pandeyji replied, “Very well, I’ll be back by ten. Don’t let Ramapati leave his room.”

He departed.

At half past nine, Byomkesh and I went to Ramkishore Babu’s house. In the baithak-khana, Ramkishore sat on the takhtposh, Manilal beside him. Banshidhar and Murlidhar were pacing before the takhtposh; at the sight of us, they vanished in an instant. Perhaps a family council had been in session, now scattered by our arrival. Ramkishore’s cheeks had sunk, his eyes hollowed. Yet outwardly, he remained composed. With a faint smile, he said, “Come—sit.”

We drew up chairs beside the takhtposh and sat. Ramkishore cleared his throat and spoke in his usual tone, “Even the Sannyasi Thakur has been bitten. It’s becoming impossible to live here. Banshi and Murli are leaving in a day or two; the rest of us thought we’d stay. But if the snake menace keeps increasing like this—”

Byomkesh said, “Snakes in winter—remarkable!”

Ramkishore replied, “And on top of that, another nuisance last night. Never before has a thief entered this house—”

Manilal interjected, “This was no ordinary thief.”

Byomkesh asked, “What happened?”

Ramkishore said, “Since I’ve been unwell, Manilal sleeps in my room at night. Last night, around midnight—Manilal, you tell it. When I woke, the thief had already fled.”

Manilal said, “I’m a very light sleeper. Late last night, I suddenly woke, feeling as if there were footsteps outside the door. In this house, no one bolts their doors at night, not even the main entrance is properly shut. It seemed to me that someone was trying, very quietly, to push our door open. My bed is far from the door; I got up silently and tiptoed over. There’s no light in the room; in the darkness, I saw the door was slowly swinging open. At that moment, I made a foolish mistake. Had I waited a bit longer, the thief would have entered, and I could have caught him. Instead, I tried to pull the door open. The thief was alert—he bolted away with a rush.”

Ramkishore said, “That’s when I woke up.”

Byomkesh asked, “The person you took for a thief—could it have been Tulsi? I’ve heard Tulsi has a habit of wandering at night.”

Ramkishore’s expression hardened. He said, “No, not Tulsi. I had locked him in his room last night.”

Byomkesh asked Manilal, “You couldn’t recognize the thief?”

“No. But—”

“But you believe it was someone familiar?”

--- Page 726 ---
Byomkesh Samagra 335

‘Yes.’

Ramkishore said, ‘There’s no need for concealment. You already know I drove Ramapati out yesterday! Manilal believes he came here with some ulterior motive.’

Byomkesh asked, ‘Can you tell us exactly what time this incident occurred?’

Ramkishore replied, ‘Precisely at a quarter to twelve. I keep a watch under my pillow—I checked it.’

Byomkesh cast a meaningful glance at me; I suppressed a smile. I did not mention that at a quarter to twelve, Ramapati had been lying on Byomkesh’s bed.

In a somber, melancholy voice, Ramkishore continued, ‘This morning, I learned something else. Ramapati didn’t take his belongings with him—they were left in his room. I had the room searched this morning. In his battered tin trunk, this item was found.’ He drew a gold hairpin from his pocket and handed it to Byomkesh.

It was the kind of two-pronged iron hairpin women use to fasten their hair, but this one was gold. Slightly larger and thicker than usual, both ends tapered to needle-sharp points. Turning it over in his hands, Byomkesh looked at Ramkishore with a questioning gaze; Ramkishore said, ‘It belonged to my elder daughter, Haripriya. After her death, it disappeared.’

Returning the pin, Byomkesh fixed Ramkishore with a steady gaze and said, ‘Ramkishore-babu, the time has come for a frank reckoning.’

Ramkishore seemed bewildered. ‘A reckoning?’

‘Yes. It’s necessary to clarify who is responsible for your brother Ramvinod-babu’s death.’

Ramkishore’s face turned pale. He opened his mouth to speak, but no words came out. Then, struggling to compose himself, he managed to say in a half-choked voice, ‘My brother—! Whom are you talking about?’

Byomkesh said, ‘You know exactly whom I mean. There’s no use pretending. We also know the supposed suicide was not a real suicide. Your brother was murdered last night!’

Manilal exclaimed, ‘Murdered!’

‘Yes. Did you know, the Sannyasi Thakur is your father-in-law’s elder brother, Ramvinod Singh?’

By now, Ramkishore had regained much of his composure. He replied sharply, ‘Nonsense! My brother died of plague long ago. Where do you get these romantic tales? Can you prove the Sannyasi is my brother? Do you have any witnesses?’

Byomkesh said, ‘There was one witness—Eshan-babu. He too was murdered.’

This time Ramkishore burst out in anger, raising his voice, ‘Lies! Lies! All police trickery. Get out of my house, leave this fortress at once. There’s no place for police spies in my domain.’

At that moment, at the window outside, Murali-dhar’s terrified face appeared—‘Baba! The police have surrounded the house.’ With that, he vanished.

Startled, I turned toward the door and saw Pandeyji entering the room, resplendent in police uniform from his booted feet to his helmeted head, and behind him, Dr. Ghatak, carrying his bag.

--- Page 727 ---
336  
The Mystery of the Fort  
12

Pandey said, “We have a search warrant. We’re going to search your house. Would you like to see the warrant?”

Ramkishore stared at him, his face drained with fear, then replied in a broken voice, “What does this mean?”

Pandey said, “There have been two murders in your area. The police believe the culprit—and the evidence—are here in this house. We want to search and see for ourselves.”

Ramkishore gazed at us for a moment longer, then said, “Very well, do as you wish,” and collapsed onto the bolster.

“Doctor!”

Dr. Ghatak was ready; within two minutes he had given Ramkishore an injection. After checking his pulse, he said, “No need to worry.”

Meanwhile, I peered out the window and saw the police swarming outside; a cluster of constables blocked the stairway, cutting off any escape.

Inspector Dubey entered the room, saluted, and reported, “Everyone is in their own rooms. I’ve forbidden anyone to leave.”

Pandey said, “Good. We need two civilian witnesses. Ajitbabu, Byomkeshbabu, you will be our witnesses. Please observe whether the police do anything unlawful.”

Monilal, who had been standing with us all this time, asked, “Should I stay in my own room as well?”

Turning to him, Pandey said, “Monilalbabu! No, come, let’s search your room first.”

“Come along.”

Pandey, Dubey, Byomkesh, and I followed Monilal into his room. It was quite large, with a bed on one side, and various pieces of furniture—an almirah, drawers, and so on.

Monilal said, “Look wherever you wish.”

Byomkesh replied, “There’s not much to see. You have two fountain pens—just show us those two.”

For a fleeting moment, it was as if a mask slipped from Monilal’s face. Byomkesh had once called him a lion in donkey’s skin; now it seemed the savage beast had shed its harmless disguise. For an instant, I glimpsed a terrifying visage. Then Monilal went to the drawer, swiftly took out the Parker pen, and, with quick fingers, unscrewed both ends. Gripping the pen like a dagger, he raised his eyes to Byomkesh. The hatred and fury in that gaze were indescribable. Monilal bared his teeth and hissed, “Here’s the pen. Want it? Come, take it.”

We stood frozen, like statues. Pandey drew his revolver from his belt. Monilal laughed like a hyena. Then, pressing the nib of the pen into the flesh of his left wrist, he squeezed the ink-filled bulb with his thumb.

Byomkesh said to Ramkishorebabu, “You fed milk and bananas to a venomous snake and kept it as a pet. Fortunately, you had this police spy in your house, or you’d be dead by now. But we’re leaving—we’ve lost our appetite for your hospitality. We’re just taking the vial of snake venom with us.”

Ramkishore gazed around him, bewildered and desperate. Byomkesh walked to the door.

--- Page 728 ---
Byomkesh Samagra  
337

He turned back and said, “Let me say one more thing before I go. Your ancestors hid a great deal of gold. I know where that gold is. But I have no desire to touch what is not mine. Enjoy your ancestral property. —Come, Panditji. Let’s go, Ajit.”

That afternoon, in Panditji’s house, Byomkesh was half-reclined in an easy chair, telling the story. The listeners were myself, Panditji, and Ramapati.

“I’ll be brief. If I leave anything out, ask.”

Panditji said, “Let me say something first. That white powder you asked to have tested—the chemist’s report has arrived. Here, take a look.”

Byomkesh read the report, his brows knitting. “Sodium Tetra Borate—Borax, that is, Sohaga? What is Sohaga used for? I know one thing: gold and Sohaga. Is it used for anything else?”

Panditji replied, “I’m not sure. Perhaps in those days it was used for making medicines.”

Returning the report, Byomkesh said, “Well. Now listen. Manilal appeared to be a decent man on the outside, but his nature was that of a demon—cruel and greedy in equal measure. After his marriage, he resolved to seize his father-in-law’s entire estate. He received no kindness from his brothers-in-law, nor did he love his wife. Only his father-in-law did he win over with gentle words.

“Manilal’s first opportunity came when a group of Bedes set up camp nearby. He secretly obtained snake venom from them.

“It’s not immediately clear why he killed his wife first. Perhaps, in a moment of weakness, he revealed his intentions to her, or perhaps Haripriya herself witnessed the process of filling the pen with snake venom. In any case, Haripriya had to be removed first. But that created a major problem—his connection to his father-in-law would be severed. Yet Manilal had so thoroughly ensnared his father-in-law that he was confident Ramkishore would not break ties with him. His eyes were already on Tulsi.

“In any case, after Haripriya’s death, there was no trouble; the match with Tulsi was all but settled. Manilal waited patiently, planning to remove his brothers-in-law one by one once the relationship was confirmed. Two years passed; Tulsi was nearly of marriageable age when Ishan Babu arrived. Not long after, the holy man appeared. There was, of course, no connection between these two; neither ever realized how close he was to his old friend.

“Ramkishore Babu carried the shame of abandoning his brother to die. Recognizing the ascetic, his heart gave out—he was on the verge of collapse. After regaining some composure, he said to his brother, ‘What’s done is done, but if the truth comes out now, I’ll be utterly disgraced. Go to some pilgrimage site, build a monastery, stay there—I’ll provide whatever funds you need.’ But Ramvinod had his brother in his grasp; he would not budge. He sat beneath a tree, cursing his brother.

“That’s my deduction. But if Ramkishore ever tells the truth, you’ll see I’m not wrong. Manilal, however, was thrown into great difficulty by his father-in-law’s illness; if the old man died suddenly, all his plans would be ruined, and the brothers-in-law would throw him out at once. He began advising his father-in-law to separate the two elder sons. That way, if Ramkishore died of heart failure, as guardian of the minors, Manilal would gain control of half the property.”

--- Page 729 ---
338  
The Mystery of the Fort

After that, he would marry Tulsi, and Gadadhar would die of snakebite.

"Manilal was trusted implicitly by Ramkishore; besides, Ramkishore himself feared that after his death, his two elder sons would deprive the younger siblings. He agreed; discussions with the lawyer began.

"Meanwhile, another incident was unfolding at the fort; Ishanbabu was hunting for hidden treasure. First, he found the Persian inscription carved on the mat. He carefully copied it into his notebook and began his investigation. Then, while fiddling with a spike in his own bedroom, he noticed a stone was loose. It didn’t take him long to realize that beneath that stone lay the fort’s treasury.

"But the stone was massive, heavy as a mountain; Ishanbabu was a frail, ailing old man. How could he move the stone and enter the treasury? Ishanbabu’s conscience was tainted, his character corroded by want. He did not inform Ramkishore, but began searching for an accomplice.

"Two grown men visited him daily—Ramapati and Manilal. Ishanbabu chose Manilal. The reason: Manilal was the stronger brute. And Ishanbabu had sensed that he harbored resentment against the brothers-in-law.

"Perhaps they had agreed to split the spoils fifty-fifty. But Manilal, in his heart, resolved to take it all—why should his father-in-law’s treasure fall into another’s hands? On the appointed night, the two of them shifted the stone and descended into the treasury.

"Before they could search the earthen pots, Ishanbabu spotted a gold coin on the floor. Manilal assumed the pots were filled with coins. He didn’t hesitate; with the torch in his hand, he struck Ishanbabu hard on the neck. Ishanbabu collapsed, unconscious. After that, it was no trouble to jab the pen into his foot.

"But a murderer’s mind is always in haste. Manilal dragged Ishanbabu’s body upstairs. At this moment, I suspect, some disturbance arose from outside. Murlidhar had been trying to scare Ishanbabu away—perhaps he hurled stones or bricks at that very moment. Frightened, Manilal shut the secret door. The pots went unexamined; the torch remained in the treasury.

"I doubt Manilal ever learned that there was no gold or jewels in the treasury. Before the commotion over Ishanbabu’s death had even subsided, we arrived; he had no chance to investigate further. But patience was not lacking in him; he waited, and meanwhile, flattered his father-in-law so that the fort would fall to Tulsi’s share.

"From the very beginning, he suspected that we had not come merely for a change of air, but there was nothing he could do. Then, from yesterday afternoon, a series of incidents occurred that frightened Manilal. Tulsi tried to steal his pen and give it to Ramapati. Whether the pen was poisoned at that time is uncertain, but his obsession with the pen was natural. He could not bear the sight of Ramapati—who can tolerate his beloved’s suitor? Using this pretext, he drove Ramapati away. So far, no great harm was done, but in the evening, another incident took place.

"We were sitting with the holy man, listening to his endless tales—‘Ham kya nahi janta,’ and so on—when Manilal was approaching Babaji; from afar, hearing his declamation, he thought Babaji must have seen him at the fort on the night of Ishanbabu’s death, and would reveal everything to us late at night.

"Manilal realized, disaster! There was a witness to his crime. Babaji, who knew about Ishanbabu’s death—"

--- Page 730 ---
Byomkesh Samagra 339

He could not imagine that Babaji knew nothing; he resolved to finish Babaji off before midnight.

"After we left, Babaji brewed himself a pot of siddhi. Then, I suppose, Monilal went and gave him another pot to drink. Babaji drank without fear, for he had no suspicion of Monilal. After that, he sank into a stupor and drifted into sleep; and at the appointed hour, Monilal came and sent him on to the land of eternal slumber."

I said, "Well then, if the ascetic Thakur knew nothing, why did he summon us at midnight?"

Byomkesh replied, "He called us to tell his own story, to reveal his true identity."

"And another thing. The thief who broke into Ramkishore Babu's room last night—who was he?"

"A phantom thief. When Monilal returned after murdering the holy man, he stumbled while entering the room, which woke Ramkishore Babu. Thus was born the tale of the thief. Ramkishore Babu takes opium; an opium-eater’s sleep is not easily broken, so Monilal was confident. But when he did wake, Monilal quickly spun a story. That cast a new suspicion on Ramapati, and shifted suspicion away from himself. Hiding Haripriya’s gold hairpin in Ramapati’s trunk served the same purpose. One enemy after another. If ever suspicion arose, it would fall on Ramapati."

Byomkesh lit a cigarette.

I asked, "When did you realize Monilal was the culprit?"

Byomkesh exhaled a plume of smoke and said, "At first, I couldn’t figure out what the weapon was. When Tulsi first mentioned the fountain pen, I grew suspicious. Then, when Monilal handed me the fountain pen, everything became clear in an instant. Monilal himself said that no one else in the house had a fountain pen. Such a simple weapon, you see? Always carried openly in the pocket, no one would suspect a thing."

After a moment of silence, Pandeyji said, "But the mystery of the hidden treasure still remains unsolved."

Byomkesh smiled faintly.

A motorcar pulled up and stopped at the front of the house, its horn blaring. Looking out the window, I saw Ramkishore Babu and Dr. Ghatak alight from the car.

Entering the room, Ramkishore, hands folded in supplication, said, "Please forgive me. My foolishness led me to misunderstand everything. Ramapati, I have caused you the most pain, my son. Come back with me."

Ramapati, abashed, bowed to him.

13

Ramkishore Babu was seated with due courtesy. Pandeyji, I believe, went out to order tea.

Dr. Ghatak smiled and said, "Too much excitement is not good for my patient. He insisted on coming, otherwise he should be resting in bed."

--- Page 731 ---
340 The Fortress Mystery

Ramkishore said in a deep voice, “Excitement, you say! After what I’ve been through since this morning, Doctor, I fear nothing now.”

Byomkesh replied, “Truly, there’s nothing left to fear. All danger has passed, and you have a doctor by your side. I know well how skilled Dr. Ghatak is. But tell me one thing—are you still unwilling to call the Sannyasi your brother?”

Ramkishore lowered his head in shame.

“Byomkesh-babu, I am dying of shame as it is—please, do not shame me further. I begged my brother, pleaded at his feet, but he would not return to worldly life. I even offered to build a temple for him in Haridwar, where he could live in comfort as the head priest. He would not listen. Had he agreed, perhaps he would not have met such an untimely end.” He sighed deeply.

Pandeyji returned, the gold mohur we had seen earlier in his hand. He handed it to Ramkishore and said, “This belongs to you. Take it.”

Ramkishore took the coin eagerly, pressed it to his forehead, and said, “An heirloom of my forefathers. They left it all behind, but due to our ill fate, we never received it. Byomkesh-babu, have you truly found the treasure?”

“I believe I have. Though I haven’t seen it with my own eyes.”

“Then—then—!” Ramkishore swallowed hard.

Byomkesh smiled faintly. “It lies within your own estate. Why not search for it?”

“Have I left any stone unturned, Byomkesh-babu? Since buying this fortress, I have searched every inch, every nook and cranny. I found nothing. In despair, I thought the sepoys had looted it all. If you know, tell me. I will not cheat you—you shall have your share. I accept Pandeyji and Dr. Ghatak as arbiters; whatever they deem fair, I will give. You have done me a great service—if you want even half the share—”

Byomkesh replied dryly, “I want no share. But I have two conditions.”

“Conditions? What conditions?”

“The first: you must marry Ramapati to Tulsi. The second: as dowry, you must transfer the fortress to Ramapati.”

I had never suspected that Byomkesh was matchmaking behind the scenes. We all burst out in exclamations. Ramapati slipped away, his face flushed with embarrassment.

Ramkishore paced for a few minutes, deep in thought, then looked up and said, “So be it. I have nothing against Ramapati. I know him—he’s a good boy. If I marry Tulsi elsewhere, perhaps we’ll end up with another ghost or monkey for a son-in-law. I want none of that.”

“And the fortress?”

“I’ll transfer it to Ramapati. You want the ancestral gold and jewels to go to Tulsi and Ramapati, is that it? Very well, so it shall be.”

“You won’t go back on your word?”

Ramkishore replied, his tone firm, “I am the son of Raja Janakirama. I have never gone back on my word.”

“Very well. It’s evening now. We’ll go tomorrow morning.”

The next morning, we returned to the fortress. There were four of us—Byomkesh, myself,

--- Page 732 ---
Byomkesh said, “What you all could not find in years, Ishaan Babu discovered in just two weeks. The reason is, he was an archaeologist—he knew where to look. First, he found a stone inscription. On it was written: ‘If I or Jayaram do not survive, all our wealth—gold and jewels—shall remain in the custody of Mohanlal.’ This inscription was written by Rajaram. But who is Mohanlal? Ishaan Babu could not understand. Had he realized, I think there would have been no trouble at all—he would not have made a futile attempt at theft, but would have informed Ramkishore directly.

“Then Ishaan Babu found the clue to the secret treasury; he thought all the gold and jewels must be there. But as we know, except for a single coin that had rolled away, there was nothing left in the treasury; Rajaram had removed everything else. Let me add, the sepoys never found the treasury; if they had, those earthen pots would not have remained intact.

“Let that be. The question is: who is Mohanlal, in whose custody Rajaram left all his wealth? A little thought reveals that Mohanlal cannot be a person. At that time, in the fort, apart from Rajaram and Jayaram, there was no one else; Rajaram had dismissed everyone. Then in whose custody did he leave the gold and jewels? What kind of being is Mohanlal?

“I too could not grasp it at first. Then one day, Ajit suddenly recited from the Battle of Plassey—‘Again, again the cannon roars—the Mohanlal thunders...’ Cannon—Mohanlal. In those days, cannons were named after great heroes. Like a bolt of lightning, it struck me—who Mohanlal was! In whose custody the gold was kept. There—Mohanlal.”

Byomkesh pointed to the cannon lying on the ground.

We were all excited; Ramkishore, restless, exclaimed, “Eh! Then the gold is buried beneath the cannon!”

“Not beneath the cannon. In those days, everyone used to bury gold in the ground; Rajaram was not so naive. He poured the gold into the barrel of the cannon and sealed its mouth. See there, how a tuft of dry grass is sticking out from the mouth of the cannon? A hundred years ago, the sepoys too saw just such dry grass; they never imagined that inside the belly of that broken, useless cannon, the gold had solidified.”

Ramkishore said in an eager voice, “Then why wait? Come, let’s dig and take out the coins.”

Byomkesh said, “Coins? Where are the coins? There are no coins left, Ramkishore Babu. Rajaram was so clever that even if the sepoys had found the hiding place, they could not have taken the gold away.”

“What do you mean—I don’t understand.”

Byomkesh said, “Panditji, do you remember seeing a furnace and bellows in the treasury? There was also a pot in Sohagaon. Do you understand now? Rajaram melted all his coins and poured the gold into the mouth of the cannon. Inside, there is a solid slab of gold.”

“Then—then—!”

“You may see the gold if you dig away the earth from the mouth. But you won’t be able to take it out.”

“Then what’s the way?”

“The way will come later. You’ll have to bring oxy-acetylene from Calcutta to cut the cannon; three inches of solid iron cannot be cut with a chisel and hammer. For now, you can dig and see.”

--- Page 733 ---
342  
Durgarahasya  
(The Fortress Mystery)

“Is my suspicion correct—Sitaram!”  
In Sitaram’s hands were an iron drill and other tools. At Byomkesh’s command, he mounted the cannon’s back like a cavalryman. We all gathered below, near the cannon’s mouth. Sitaram began digging into the earth with great enthusiasm.  
After cutting nearly a foot, Sitaram said, “Huzoor, I can’t dig any further. It feels solid!”  
Pandeyji said, “Use the drill!”  
Sitaram then inserted the drill into the mouth of the cannon and began to twist it. After a few turns, slivers of gold began to fly out, scattering on the ground. In our excitement, we were all rendered senseless, shouting incoherently.  
Byomkesh raised his hand and said, “Enough, Sitaram, stop now. There’s proof that my suspicion was not unfounded. Ramkishore-babu, install a sturdy door at the entrance to the fort, set up guards; until all the gold is recovered, you and your family should come and live here. Don’t leave so much gold lying about.”  
That day, it was almost one o’clock by the time we returned home. Upon our arrival, we heard a telegram had come for Byomkesh. Our faces went pale. Why a telegram, suddenly? From whom?—Is Satyabati all right?  
Byomkesh’s hand trembled slightly as he tore open the envelope. I stood a little distance away, gazing at him unblinking.  
As he read the telegram, his face changed in a strange way; then he looked up. Clearing his throat, he said, “Gold on that side too.”  
“Gold?”  
“Yes—a son has been born.”  

Six months later, at the beginning of Baisakh, the heat was just beginning to settle over Calcutta. One morning, Byomkesh and I were sharing the newspaper between us, Satyabati sat on the floor with a bowl of milk and the baby, engaged in a wrestling match with her son over feeding him, when a sharp knock sounded at the front door. Satyabati made as if to flee with the child.  
I opened the door to find Ramapati and Tulsi. In Ramapati’s hand was a smart box, he wore a silk panjabi, and his face bore a bashful smile.  
Tulsi was almost unrecognizable. In these few months, she had become a proper young woman. We had been invited to their wedding in Agrahayan, but had not been able to attend. Now, six months later, we saw them again.  
Tulsi swept into the room like a whirlwind. As soon as she was introduced to Satyabati, she snatched the baby from her lap and, showering him with kisses, ran about the room; then, depositing him in Ramapati’s arms, she tugged at Satyabati’s anchal and pulled her into the next room. Their laughter and chatter drifted to us through the curtain.  
It was as if Tulsi’s true nature had been hidden under a stone, now set free. The breaking of a spring’s dream.  
When the room had quieted, Byomkesh asked Ramapati, “What’s in the box? A gramophone?”  
“No. We’ve had something made for you,” replied Ramapati, opening the box. What he drew out held us spellbound. It was a model of the fort, crafted entirely in gold. It weighed nearly two seers, with exquisite workmanship. A perfect replica of the real fort.

--- Page 734 ---
There was not the slightest difference anywhere; even the old cannon stood in its rightful place.

We exclaimed in wonder, “Bravo!”

Then, the day passed in eating, chatting, and playful banter. We learned news of Ramkishore Babu and his family: the master’s health was improving, Banshidhar had built a house on his own estate; Muralidhar had bought a house in the city and was living there; Gadadhar, along with Tulsi and Ramapati, remained with the master at the mountain retreat; Chandmohan had once again taken up the management of the estate. The fort was being completely renovated. Tulsi and Ramapati would live there.

In the afternoon, they took their leave. At the moment of parting, Byomkesh asked, “Tulsi, how is your master?”

Casting a sly, mischievous glance at her master, Tulsi replied, “Terrible.”

Byomkesh said, “Hmm. Do you remember, once you sat on my lap and cried for your master?”

This time Tulsi blushed. Pressing the end of her sari to her mouth, she said, “Oh, hush!”

--- Page 735 ---
The Zoo

One

The incident took place immediately after the Second World War. It was summer. Byomkesh’s brother-in-law, Sukumar, had taken Satyabati and Khoka to Darjeeling. Byomkesh and I were left behind in our Harrison Road lodgings, roasting like prawns.

Work had slowed to a trickle for Byomkesh. This was nothing new for him; but this time, the length and unbroken monotony of inactivity had begun to drive us both to distraction. On top of that, Khoka and Satyabati were away from home. In desperation, we had finally resorted to playing chess.

I knew chess reasonably well; in fact, I had taught Byomkesh how to play. At first, he lost easily; gradually, it became difficult to checkmate him. At last, the day arrived when he defeated me in a major match.

It is said that there is no shame in being bested by one’s son or pupil. Yet, when the person you taught to move the pieces just a few days ago manages to beat you, you cannot help but doubt your own intellect. My heart was no longer at peace.

To make matters worse, the heat had become unbearable. Ever since that morning in mid-Chaitra, when I awoke drenched in sweat, the temperature had only climbed higher over the past month and a half. There had been a shower or two, it’s true, but those only seemed to intensify the oppressive heat, like the black armor of Krishna. Running the fan day and night brought no relief; it felt as if I were sitting, my whole body drenched in syrup from a rasgolla.

In this state of physical and mental despondency, one morning I spread the chessboard on the takhtposh and sat down to play. Byomkesh had almost completed his arrangement for a bishop’s wheel, while I, excessively agitated, was sweating profusely—when suddenly, we were interrupted.

A soft but insistent knock at the door. Not the postman; his knock had a certain reckless urgency. Then who? We looked at each other with eager anticipation.

After all this time, could it be that a new mystery was about to arrive?

Byomkesh quickly slipped on his panjabi and hurried to open the door. Meanwhile, I threw a light shawl over my bare body and composed myself.

Standing outside was a middle-aged gentleman. Of medium build, solidly put together, with a sharp, clean-shaven face and rimless, smoky spectacles. He wore immaculate white pantaloons and a sleeveless silk kurta. No socks on his feet, only braided leather Grecian sandals. His appearance was neat and precise.

In a refined voice, he said, “Byomkesh Babu—?”

--- Page 736 ---
434  
Byomkesh Samagra

On the occasion of collecting his dues, when Bantul arrived, Byomkesh would offer him tea and cigarettes, and engage him in conversation; much news could be gleaned about the machinations of both enemy and ally. Bantul, in these moments, would steer the talk toward business. After the war, the American soldiers had sold off many firearms for a pittance before departing; Bantul had managed to acquire some of these weapons, and now he was trying to sell them to us. He would say, “Buy a rifle and keep it at home, sir. We can’t keep an eye on everything at all times. In troubled times, it’s good to have a weapon at hand.”

I would reply, “No, Bantul, we don’t need a rifle. Something that big can’t be hidden; one day the police will get wind of it and haul us away in chains. If you could manage a pistol or a revolver instead—”

Bantul would say, “It’s hard to get a pistol, babu. Still, I’ll try—”

Bantul came once a month.

That day, as usual, Bantul took his tip, reassured us, and departed. For a while, we sat in a kind of listless gloom, reviewing the temporary state of affairs. How much longer could this go on? How long can one sit with the sword of Damocles hanging overhead? Independence might be on its way, but will we live to enjoy it? Even if we survive the battles ahead, how long can we live on crushed gravel and tamarind seeds? Byomkesh never had much work at the best of times; now, there was none at all. Where open, wholesale murder is being conducted, what use is Byomkesh’s talent for unraveling mysteries?

I said, “Give up on Bharatir and start worshipping Lakshmi instead.”

“What do you mean?”

“I mean, go out at midnight with a knife under your arm—if you can dispatch a few black marketeers, you won’t have to worry anymore. Given the times we’re living in, Bantul should be our role model.”

Byomkesh was silent for a moment, then said, “You’re not wrong; the spirit of the age is the only true law. But you know, that sort of thing needs to be in your blood. Whether it’s murder or black marketeering, without the strength of your ancestors’ blood, it can’t be done. My father was a schoolmaster, taught mathematics at school and studied Sankhya philosophy at home. My mother was a Vaishnav girl, forever immersed in thoughts of Nandagopal. So that sort of work is not for me.”

I knew Byomkesh’s childhood history. When he was seventeen, his father contracted tuberculosis, and his mother died of the same disease. No relatives came to look in on him. After that, Byomkesh crossed the ocean of learning on nothing but water and rice, carving out a new path in life through his own efforts. Perhaps he still has relatives, but Byomkesh keeps no contact with them.

A while passed in melancholy silence. Today, a letter from Satyabati might arrive; inwardly, I was waiting for it.

Suddenly, there was a sharp rapping at the door. I got up and opened it.

It was not the postman. Instead, standing outside was a person whose attire could only be described as that of a woman. But what a woman! Five feet tall, and broad in proportion, a towering, imposing figure; skin the color of polished ebony; hips wide as a water pot, a pair of unmistakable moustaches; her age well past fifty. She looked at me and smiled; it seemed—

--- Page 737 ---
Adimriphu 435

The lid of the harmonium was lifted.

I wondered if she was some superhuman being sprung from the pages of the Ramayana or the Mahabharata. From the harmonium came a deep, grave voice, “Are you Byomkesh Babu?”

I shook my head swiftly in denial. I did not know what business this lady had with Byomkesh, but it was best to make it clear at once that I was not he.

Byomkesh, who had not seen the woman from inside the room, came out upon noticing my predicament. He, too, was momentarily taken aback at the sight of our visitor, but quickly composed himself and said, “I am Byomkesh.”

The lady once again lifted the lid of the harmonium and said, “Namaskar. My name is Miss Nanibala Ray. I have some business with you.”

“Please, come in.”

With the sharp clack of her shoes, Miss Nanibala Ray entered the room; Byomkesh offered her a chair. I thought to myself, with such an appearance, she could never be the mistress of a household. Husband, son, domesticity—these were not for her. The “Miss” before her name was a clear testament against marital fortune. Then what was she? The matron of a zenana gate? No, not quite. A schoolmistress? Perhaps not. A lady doctor? Possible—

At that moment, Nanibala introduced herself. I realized I had not been far wrong. She said, “I was a midwife at Patna Medical College Hospital. Now I am retired and living in Calcutta. I heard your name from someone, got your address as well. So I have come.”

Byomkesh, his face grave, said, “Tell me, what do you need?”

Miss Nanibala’s appearance was formidable, but her manner was not. She carried a black handbag, which she began to open as she said, “I am a poor woman, Byomkesh Babu. I cannot pay you much—”

Byomkesh interrupted, “We can discuss money later. First, tell me what you need.”

Nanibala closed her bag, then suddenly, in a trembling voice, burst out, “My son is in great danger. Please save him, Byomkesh Babu—”

Byomkesh gazed at her for a moment and said, “Your—son?”

Nanibala seemed a little embarrassed. She said, “My son—that is—I raised him. Anadi Babu has adopted him as his own—”

Byomkesh said, “This sounds complicated. Please, tell us everything from the beginning.”

Nanibala then began to tell her story. Her style of narration was poor—sometimes jumping back ten years, sometimes leaping forward twenty, introducing many irrelevant details. But when the knots were untangled, her tale stood thus—

Twenty-two or twenty-three years ago, Miss Nanibala Ray was a midwife at Patna Hospital. One day, a young woman was admitted; her condition was dire, pleurisy with various complications, and she was heavily pregnant. The man who had brought her disappeared as soon as she was admitted. The young woman was not Hindu, probably of some aboriginal tribe, a local Christian. Two days later, she gave birth to a son and died. The man who had vanished never returned.

In such cases, the government usually arranged for the child’s care. But this time, Nanibala took the responsibility herself. She was unmarried, childless, and in the hope that the boy would one day become as her own son, she raised him as such. The child was named Prabhat Ray.

--- Page 738 ---
At that time, Prabhat was three or four years old when Nanibala suddenly received an insured letter. Enclosed with the letter were two hundred-rupee notes. The letter read: I have come to know that my son is with you. Please take care of him. I am sending some money for now; if possible, I will send more. —There was no name or signature on the letter.

After that, there was no further news of Prabhat’s father. The man had probably died. Nanibala was not particularly distressed. She had always feared that the father might one day come and take the boy away. Now she was at peace.

Prabhat began to grow up. Nanibala was busy with her own duties and could not properly look after the boy; Prabhat roamed the streets, playing with the Hindustani boys of the para. He received no education.

There was a Muslim bookbinder’s shop in the neighborhood. When Prabhat was sixteen or seventeen, he began working at the bookbinder’s. True, Prabhat had never studied, but he did not become a wayward or unruly youth. He devoted himself to his work and held his foster mother in deep reverence and affection.

Thus passed three or four years. Towards the end of the Second World War, a gentleman named Anadi Halder arrived in Patna. Anadibabu was a wealthy businessman. He needed many of his business ledgers and account books bound, so he sent for the bookbinder. The bookbinder sent Prabhat to his house. It was not convenient to carry all the books and papers to the shop. Prabhat brought his tools to Anadibabu’s residence and, over several days, bound the covers of his ledgers and account books.

Anadibabu was a bachelor. It seems he took a liking to Prabhat, for one day he visited Nanibala’s house and proposed that he wished to adopt Prabhat as his foster son.

To become the foster son of such a wealthy man was a stroke of fortune; yet Nanibala was not willing to give up Prabhat so easily. Nor did Prabhat wish to leave his mother. So it was settled that Nanibala would also live with Prabhat in Anadibabu’s household, and in that womanless household, Nanibala would manage the home.

Nanibala retired from her job at the hospital. Anadi Halder, too, had almost retired from his professional life, and the three of them came to Calcutta. This was about a year and a half ago. Since then, they have been living on the second floor of a rented house in Bowbazar. In the post-war market, it was difficult to find a good house. But Anadibabu had bought an old house right next to his own and was having it demolished to build a new one. As soon as the house was ready, they would move into it.

Anadibabu had an elder brother who lived in the ancestral house in Calcutta. There was no affection between the brothers; in fact, there was no relationship at all. The brother had died about ten years ago, but he had two sons—Nimai and Nitai. When Anadibabu came to live in Calcutta, they somehow found out and began visiting him.

According to Nanibala, Nimai and Nitai were consummate scoundrels, sly as foxes, ogres who would devour a child. If their uncle adopted a foster son, their uncle’s immense wealth would slip from their grasp; so they tried to manipulate their uncle into rejecting the idea of adoption. At first, Anadibabu found some amusement in seeing through his nephews’ schemes, but gradually he became irritated. A few months ago, he told his nephews plainly not to set foot in his house again.

--- Page 739 ---
Adimriphu 437

Nimai and Nitai had indeed stopped coming to Kaka’s house, but they had not given up hope. Anadibabu had set Prabhat up with a bookshop—a tiny little place tucked into a corner of College Street. Prabhat had never truly learned his letters, but he loved books; that shop was his very soul. Every day he would go there, selling books with his own hands. Nitai and Nimai began frequenting his shop. They never bought anything, only stood there, eyes wide, staring at Prabhat; then, wordlessly, they would slip out of the shop.

The look in their eyes was as terrifying as a tiger’s glare. They never spoke, but Prabhat had no doubt about their intentions. Prabhat was a good-natured boy, and, frightened, he came and told Nonibala; Nonibala, in turn, told Anadibabu. Anadibabu hired a Gurkha—so long as the shop remained open, the Gurkha would stand guard, khukuri in hand. The cousins’ visits to the shop ceased. Yet, even so, the fear that haunted Prabhat and Nonibala did not abate. It was as if two pairs of invisible eyes were always watching them, tracking their every move.

Besides, another matter had brought unrest to the household. Prabhat had taken a liking to a girl; she was the daughter of a refugee family from East Bengal, skilled in music, and quite beautiful. At some gathering, Prabhat had heard her sing and had spoken of her to Nonibala. Anadibabu had been searching for a bride for Prabhat; hearing of this girl from Nonibala, he said he would go see her himself, and if he approved, he would arrange the marriage.

Anadibabu went to see the girl and declared that Prabhat could not marry her. He offered no explanation, but Nonibala was convinced that Nimai and Nitai had a hand in the matter. Be that as it may, from that point on, a new kind of turmoil seemed to fester beneath the surface. Nonibala grew fearful. In these turbulent times, what if some misfortune befell Prabhat? What if a goonda stabbed him? There was nothing Nimai and Nitai would not dare. Now, Byomkesh-babu, you must, by any means, save Prabhat’s life.

Two

Byomkesh listened with closed eyes to Nonibala’s rambling, wordy tale; when she finished, he opened his eyes. Suppressing his irritation, he said as politely as possible, “Miss Ray, what can I do in such a matter? Even if your suspicions are correct, I cannot follow your son around like an armed guard. I think, under the circumstances, it would be best to go to the police.”

Nonibala replied, “I mentioned the police to Anadibabu, and he flew into a rage; he said—there’s no need to stir up trouble over this, and if you’re so afraid for your lives, go back to Patna.”

Byomkesh said, “Then what else do you suggest should be done?”

Nonibala’s voice grew tremulous, on the verge of tears. “What can I say, Byomkesh-babu? Please, find some way. I have no one but Prabhat—I'm a helpless woman—” and with that, she dabbed at her eyes with the end of her sari.

Though no one, seeing Nonibala’s appearance, would ever mistake her for a helpless woman, still, her...

--- Page 740 ---
438  
Byomkesh Samagra

It must be admitted that the heart in question is that of a helpless woman. She loves her adopted son as dearly as if he were her own flesh and blood, and, fearing misfortune for him, she has become excessively anxious. Perhaps her fears are unfounded, but even so, they cannot be dismissed.

For a while, Byomkesh watched Nanibala’s tearful outpouring with a look of irritation. Suddenly, in a harsh voice, he asked, “Where do the two nephews stay?”

Nanibala lifted her hopeful eyes from the edge of her sari. “They live under the lemon tree. Are you—?”

“What’s the address? What number?”

“That I don’t know—Prabhat knows. Are you going to see them, Byomkesh Babu? If you scold them properly, they’ll be frightened—”

“If I go to scold them, they might end up scolding me instead. I just want to see them once. If I see them, I’ll be able to guess if they’re hiding something. Prabhat knows their address? What’s Prabhat’s address—meaning, your house address?”

“House number 172/2, Bowbazar Street. But there—it's better if you don’t go to the house. Anadibabu—”

“Anadibabu might not like it. Very well, then. Tell me the address of Prabhat’s shop.”

“Prabhat’s shop—I don’t know the address—but the name is Jiban-Prabhat. It’s near Gol Dighi, there’s a big signboard hanging over the door—”

Byomkesh stood up, and in a tired, dry voice said, “I understand. You may go now. If there’s any news, you’ll hear of it.”

Nanibala, perhaps a little hurt, took her leave. Byomkesh glanced up at the rafters and murmured, “How strange you are, O world, how endlessly strange your forms!”

That evening, Byomkesh was idly leafing through a Puja annual when, all at once, he tossed the magazine aside and said, “Let’s go out for a bit.”

Since the outbreak of the great war, we had stopped going out after dusk, unless absolutely necessary. I asked, “Where do you want to go?”

He replied, “In search of Jiban-Prabhat.”

We had two stout sticks ready; taking them in hand, the two of us set out. However discontented Byomkesh might be with Nanibala, her story had, in truth, piqued his interest.

Gol Dighi was not far from our lodgings. After a single circuit of the pavement, the enormous signboard caught our eye. The shop itself, however, was rather small in comparison, almost dwarfed beneath the sign. It was a room by the roadside, with a little cubicle behind. By the main door stood a squat, sharp-eyed Gurkha.

We entered the shop; the Gurkha cast us a sidelong glance but said nothing. I saw that the walls were crammed with books up to the rafters, making the room seem even narrower. On the shelves, ten or twelve copies of the same book were stacked side by side. Books from various publishers, but seemingly none from their own press. Even two or three of my own books were there.

But the shopkeeper was nowhere to be seen; there was no one at the counter.

Behind the counter, the door to the cubicle stood slightly ajar. Through the gap, as much as could be seen—

--- Page 741 ---
Adimriphu 439

I saw that inside, a small cot had been laid out, and upon it sat a young man, his head bowed, binding the cover of a book. Above his head, a bare electric bulb glowed; all around, papers, pieces of cardboard, pots of glue, and a fearsome-looking paper-cutting knife lay scattered. Amidst this chaos, the young man sat, utterly absorbed in his work.

Byomkesh cleared his throat a little loudly. The young man raised his head, wiped the glue from his fingers with a torn rag, and came to stand behind the counter; he asked no questions, only gazed at us with an inquiring look.

Now I observed him properly. In appearance, there was nothing to distinguish him from the countless ordinary young men of Bengal. His height was about five and a half feet, his skin a dusky, grimy brown; his face and frame a little lean. The line of a moustache above his lip had not yet filled out; his teeth were good to look at, but their shape was somehow wild, perhaps a trace of some ancient maternal blood. There was a hint of distraction in his gaze, but this was not an expression of mind—rather, a peculiarity in the structure of his eyes. His hair was somewhat rough and unkempt, showing no sign of care. He wore a loose, open-collared panjabi with sleeves rolled up. Altogether, the picture he presented was utterly commonplace and unremarkable.

Byomkesh said, "Are you Prabhat Kumar Ray, the proprietor of Jiban-Prabhat?"

The young man replied, "My name is Prabhat Haldar."

"Oh—yes—that’s right. You are Anadi Babu’s—" Byomkesh hesitated a little.

"Adopted son," Prabhat completed Byomkesh’s unfinished sentence in a detached tone, then asked Byomkesh, "And you are?"

"My name is Byomkesh Bakshi."

At last, Prabhat seemed a little more animated, studying Byomkesh, then turned his gaze to me.

"Then you must be Ajit Babu?"

"Yes."

Ignoring Byomkesh entirely, Prabhat came and stood before me, and with respectful eagerness said, "Namaskar. I will come to see you sometime."

"To see me?"

"Yes. I have some need. Your address—?"

I gave him my address and said, "Come if you wish. But I can’t imagine what business you might have with me."

"I’ll tell you then. —Now, what do you want? Besides new books, I have some excellent old ones too; I bind and sell old books. You won’t find those in any other shop."

Byomkesh said, "For now, we’ve come to get the address of Nimai and Nitai from you."

Prabhat turned to Byomkesh, blinking several times as if to take in this new subject; then he said, "Nimai and Nitai’s address? They live—" Prabhat gave the address, a number on Madhu Baral Lane. But he showed no curiosity as to why we wanted Nimai and Nitai’s address.

"Thank you."

"Do come. But I will come to see you one day."

"Please do."

--- Page 742 ---
We stepped out of the shop. There was still plenty of daylight left; the winter evening smoldered slowly, like a fire made from coconut husks—reluctant to die out. Byomkesh said, “Come, let’s go see Nimai and Nitai. They’re close by.” After we had walked a while, he remarked, “Prabhat binds his own books, can’t let go of his old trade! The boy seems rather lackluster—there’s no spark in him at all.”

I said, “I wonder why he needs me?”

Byomkesh cocked an eyebrow at me and replied, “Haven’t you figured it out yet? He wants to publish your book. Seems none of the established writers have given him their work. Now you’re his hope.”

I said, “Not ‘prothitjyasha’—it’s ‘prathitjyasha’.”

He stifled a laugh; I realized the mistake was deliberate. I continued, “Anyway, he does seem eager to publish books. Even if he’s not well-read, he appreciates literature. That’s no small thing.”

Byomkesh fell silent for a while, then, almost absentmindedly, muttered, “The owl calls to the she-owl—your screech is a treat.”

Lately, Byomkesh had taken to uttering such disconnected phrases that made no sense at all.

We arrived at Madhu Baral’s lane. This alley was no recent addition; it must have dated back to the time of Job Charnock. The houses on either side were brick-built, pressed close together, somehow managing to stand upright by leaning on each other.

I spotted the house by the number above the door. It was dilapidated, but like an old man with dyed hair and polished teeth, it tried to maintain an air of dignity. The main door was slightly ajar, revealing a narrow passageway inside, like a lane within a lane. There was no one in sight.

Byomkesh gestured for me to follow and stepped inside. The tunnel-like corridor ended at a door on the right. We stopped in front of it.

A dimly lit room. It was crammed with furniture—almirahs, tables, chairs, sofas, a takhtposh—there was barely room to move. All the furniture was old, not a single piece less than fifty years; the room looked like a warehouse of antiques. In the middle, on a faded rug spread over the takhtposh, two men were cleaning guns. Double-barreled shotguns, their butts adorned with all manner of strange, intricate carvings—these guns, too, seemed relics of a bygone era. One man was oiling the mechanism, the other cleaning the barrel with a wad of cloth.

The two men looked almost identical—the same age, the same build, the same manner. Describe one, and you’d have described them both. Around thirty, stocky and broad, with round, cherubic faces, dusky complexions, and a ring of fat around their eyes that gave them a vaguely Mongolian look. Their hair was cropped short and even. They wore lungis and fatuas. There were a few minor differences, but nothing significant. There could be no doubt—these were Nimai and Nitai.

As soon as we reached the doorway, both of them looked up at us in unison. The glare of those two pairs of fierce eyes seemed to shove us back. Then, together, they demanded, “What do you want?”

Their tone was harsh, with not a trace of courtesy. I looked helplessly at Byomkesh. Byomkesh, with easy politeness, asked, “Is this Anadi Halder’s house?”

For a moment, the brothers seemed bewildered. Exchanging questioning glances, they replied in chorus, “No.”

--- Page 743 ---
Adimriphu 441

Byomkesh asked again, “Does Anadi Babu not live here?”
A curt reply—“No.”
Byomkesh, as if embarrassed, said, “It seems I have the wrong address. Does any relative of Anadi Babu live in this house? Are you—”
The two brothers exchanged glances once more. One of them said, “Why do you need to know that?”
“It’s only that, if you are his relatives, you might be able to give me his address.”
The answer came, “Nothing will come of it here. You can leave.”
Byomkesh gazed at them for a moment with steady eyes, then said in a slightly sardonic tone,
“I see you have guns. I hope you have licenses for them.”
We turned to leave. The unwavering gaze of the two brothers followed us as we went.
Once outside, I breathed a sigh of relief—“What uncivilized men.”
As we made our way back home, Byomkesh said, “Not uncivilized—cautious. There’s a type of people here, old residents of Calcutta; once they were prominent, now their fortunes have declined; they have no means of earning, so they cling to whatever their ancestors left behind. They try to maintain the old ways with rotting houses, broken furniture, and tattered quilts. Their caution knows no bounds; they don’t mix with outsiders, lest someone slip away with a scrap of their ragged quilt. Except for a few old friends and relatives, they keep no relations with anyone; if someone tries to make acquaintance, they grow suspicious, thinking there must be some ulterior motive. So their behavior towards strangers is naturally harsh. They are at once timid and sharp-tongued, greedy and restrained. They are strange creatures.”
I asked, “What did you make of those two brothers?”
Byomkesh replied, “Nonibala Devi did not lie. A pair of cats; but not dry cats, wet ones.”
I asked, “Do you think they could harm Prabhat?”
Byomkesh said, “A house cat, if it goes to the wild, becomes a wildcat. If their interests are threatened, they too can show their true colors.”
Evening was thickening, the streetlights had come on. We hurried on towards home.

Three

The next morning, after finishing the newspaper, Byomkesh paced restlessly for a while, then said, “Nothing to do, so let’s roast some puffed rice. Come, let’s go pay Anadi Halder a visit. We met the nephews, but not the uncle—that wouldn’t look good.”
I said, “You asked the nephews for the uncle’s address. What will you ask the uncle?”
Byomkesh smiled, “Something will come to mind.”
Around half past nine, we set out. I hadn’t known the numbering of Bowbazar ran from one end to the other, so we were heading towards Sealdah, checking the numbers as we went. After a while, we ran into Batul Sardar on the footpath. Byomkesh asked, “Well, Batul, does this neighborhood fall under your territory too?”
Batul only grinned with his oily face, then shot back with a question of his own, “What brings you gentlemen to this part of town?”

--- Page 744 ---
442  
Byomkesh Samagra

“Karta? Do you need something?”  
Byomkesh replied, “Yes. —Can you tell me where 172/2 is?”  
A sudden flicker of caution flashed in Batul’s eyes. Then, regaining his composure, he said, “172/2? That’s right next to the new house under construction.”  
We resumed our walk. After a short distance, I glanced back and saw Batul still standing on the footpath, staring intently at us. When he saw me turn, he quickly set off in the opposite direction.  
I said, “Hey Byomkesh, Batul—”  
He replied, “I noticed. He probably knows them.”  
A little further on, we found ourselves in front of the new house. Bamboo scaffolding encircled the structure, masons busy at their masonry. The roof of the first floor was already poured; the walls of the second were rising, brick by brick. A massive signboard out front bore the contractor’s name: Gurudutt Singh. Most likely a Sikh.  
Beyond the house, a narrow brick-paved lane. Across it stood 172/2—a two-storied house, with a slender strip of veranda beside the main door, above which ran a similar balcony, hemmed in by a railing. On the lower veranda sat a frail, silver-haired old man, drawing on a battered hookah. As we approached, he glanced at us sidelong, lips never leaving the stem.  
Byomkesh asked, “Is this Anadi Halder’s residence?”  
The old man pulled his lips from the hookah’s mouthpiece and snapped, “Who’s Anadi Halder? How should I know! This is my house—I live on the ground floor.”  
Byomkesh said politely, “And upstairs?”  
The old man, still irritable, replied, “How should I know! Go look for yourself. Anadi Halder! Always—” He clamped his lips back on the hookah.  
Why the old man had suddenly grown so surly, I could not fathom. We wasted no more words and stepped inside. The room was long and narrow, with a door on one side—likely the entrance to the ground floor—and on the other, a staircase rising upward.  
We hesitated at the foot of the stairs, uncertain whether to ascend, when suddenly heavy footsteps thudded above. Looking up, we saw a towering, broad-shouldered Sardarji rounding the bend and descending. Our suspicions about Anadi Halder’s residence were somewhat allayed—this could only be contractor Gurudutt Singh. He wore velvet corduroy trousers and a gabardine coat, his beard plaited, a tight-fitting turban on his head. He swung his arms like clubs as he came down, his eyes rolling. As he drew closer, the words muffled in his beard and mustache reached our ears. The language was not Bengali, but the meaning was clear enough—“These Bengalis won’t pay me! Let’s see how great this Anadi Halder is, I’ll wring his neck and get my money. I’m a Punjabi too, no one can mess with me—” The Sardarji swept past and was gone.  
Byomkesh glanced at me and smiled faintly. In a low voice, he said, “It seems Anadi Babu is not a popular man. Come, let’s see.”  
At the top of the stairs was a door, bolted from within. Byomkesh knocked.  
After a moment, the door opened a crack, and a face appeared in the gap. A face like a bhetki fish, eyes bloodshot, the lower lids sagging and loose.

--- Page 745 ---
Adimriphu 443

Between the slack lips, the lower row of teeth was visible.  
Had I encountered this face at night, in such a state, I cannot say what I would have done; but now, for a moment, I started and stood still. Byomkesh said, “Anadibabu—?”

A smile broke out on the face, exposing even more of the many teeth in the jaw. In a broken, guttural voice, the bhekti fish said, “No, I am not Anadibabu, I am Kestobabu. Are you creditors too?”

“No, we have some business with Anadibabu.”

At this moment, another quick voice sounded from behind the bhekti fish—“Kestobabu, move aside, move aside—”

Kestobabu’s head withdrew, and in his place, a young man appeared at the doorway.  
A thin, sickly figure, with a long, pointed chin, and tightly curled hair that seemed to spread out on either side of his head like wings. There was a certain sharpness in his face.

“What do you want?”

“We wish to see Anadibabu.”

“Is there some business? Anadibabu does not meet anyone without reason.”

“There is indeed business. The house being built next door, I believe it belongs to him. We have some questions about that house. And you are—?”

“I am Anadibabu’s secretary. Please wait a moment, I’ll inform him. This way, come inside.”

We climbed the stairs and entered the room. The young man left.

The room was bare, furnished only with a rough wooden bench. We sat on the bench and looked around. Besides the stair door, there were a few other doors in the room; through one, the front balcony was visible, the other two led further inside.

After waiting a while, I saw one of the inner doors open slightly and a woman peered out. There was no mistaking her—Nonibala Devi. She must have been cooking, and, hearing the commotion of visitors, had come to check, ladle in hand.  
Seeing us, her eyes widened in alarm. Byomkesh put a finger to his lips to reassure her. Nonibala slowly withdrew.

The young man emerged from another door.

“Come.”

Following the young man, we entered further inside. He pushed open the door to a room and said, “Anadibabu is here, please go in.”

At first, on entering, I could make out nothing. The room was dimly lit, with no furniture except a mattress in one corner, covered with a frayed rug. Propped against a pillow on the rug sat a man, staring at us. In the half-light, it seemed a python lay coiled, watching its prey with unblinking eyes.

As my eyes adjusted, I realized—this was indeed Anadi Halder; the resemblance to his nephews was striking. About fifty years old, short and sturdy, with a Mongoloid curve to his eyes, heavy with fat. He wore a purple dressing gown.

We remained standing by the door. Anadibabu gazed at us with dull eyes. After a while, Byomkesh spoke first, “We have come on a small matter. This is Ajit Kumar Bandyopadhyay, recently arrived from East Bengal, looking to buy a house and settle in Calcutta.”

Anadibabu spoke at last. He did not ask us to sit, and in his naturally harsh voice—

--- Page 746 ---
444  
Byomkesh was asked, “Who are you?”  
Byomkesh’s eyes sharpened, but he replied with perfect composure, “I am his agent. I heard you are building a house next door, so I thought perhaps you might be willing to sell. That’s why—”  
Anadi Babu said, “I am having the house built for myself, not for sale.”  
Byomkesh said, “Of course. Still, you are a businessman; I thought if there was profit in it, you might consider letting it go.”  
“I don’t do business through brokers.”  
“Very well, then. Please speak to Ajit Babu, I’ll take my leave.”  
“No, I do not wish to discuss the house with anyone. I will not sell. You may go.”  
There was no point in lingering further. I found the company of this thoroughly discourteous man unbearable, but Byomkesh, his face impassive, said, “Please don’t mind, but is there any objection to telling us how much it will cost to build your house?”  
Anadi Babu’s rough voice grew even harsher. “There is an objection. Napa! Napa! Show them out, get them out—”  
The secretary’s name, it seemed, was Napa. He had been standing just outside the door. He poked his head in and said hastily, “Come, please, this way—”  
With a sense of having been forcibly ejected, we stepped outside. The young man escorted us to the head of the stairs and said in a slightly embarrassed undertone, “Please don’t mind, sir, the master is in a foul mood today.”  
Byomkesh replied with disdain, “It’s nothing. Come, Ajit.”  
We emerged onto the street. However much Byomkesh might feign indifference, one look at his face revealed the heat simmering within. After walking in silence for some time, he glanced at me with a weary smile and said, “There are two kinds of small men—uncouth small men and wicked small men. The wicked ones harm others; the uncouth ones harm themselves.”  
I asked, “Which kind is Anadi Halder?”  
“Both uncouth and wicked.”  

That afternoon, after lunch, I was debating whether to indulge in a short siesta when there was a knock at the door. I opened it to find Nanibala Devi.  
Nanibala entered the room, her face anxious. Byomkesh was dozing in the easy chair, but he sat up at once. Nanibala said, “When I saw you both at that house today, the blood in my veins ran cold. If Anadi Babu finds out that I—”  
Byomkesh said, “Please sit. There’s no chance of Anadi Babu finding out. We went there pretending to be buyers for his new house. But let that be. Now, tell me, what sort of man is Anadi Halder? Speak plainly, there’s no need to hide anything.”  
For a moment, Nanibala stared at Byomkesh with her large, watery eyes. Then, like a dam bursting, a torrent of words poured from her lips—“What can I say to you, Byomkesh Babu—he’s a chamar! A chamar! No skin on his face, no control over his tongue. Not a sweet word ever passes his lips—”

--- Page 747 ---
Adimriphu 445

Not a word escapes his lips, not a single paisa escapes his clutches. He’s obsessed with money, but not a drop of water would slip through his fingers. And if even a pinch of flour goes missing from the kitchen, there’s no end to the trouble—he’ll grind it between his teeth. There used to be a Brahmin cook before, but after I arrived, he dismissed him; for the past year and a half, I’ve been slaving over the stove, and my body is worn to the bone. What a cursed day it was when I let Prabhat become his precious son-in-law! If I had any means, I’d stuff pebbles in that wretch’s mouth and return to Patna. Having said this much, Nanibala began to pant like a battle-weary soldier.

Byomkesh said, “That’s more or less what I’d guessed. How does he treat Prabhat?”

Nanibala paused a moment, then replied, “He doesn’t bother Prabhat much. Besides, how long is Prabhat even at home? He leaves for the shop at eight in the morning, comes back for half an hour at noon to eat, then returns home only at nine at night. He hardly ever sees the old man.”

“The old man doesn’t want to look at the shop’s accounts?”

“No, he doesn’t have the face to ask—after all, the shop is in Prabhat’s name. At first, the old man put on a great show of decency. He asked Prabhat, ‘What will you do?’ Prabhat said, ‘I’ll open a bookshop.’ Immediately, the old man gave him five thousand rupees and set up the shop.”

“Hm. And Napa? The old man’s secretary?”

Nanibala curled her lip and said, “Secretary? He’s more than that—a kind of market manager. He can rattle off English, that’s why the old man keeps him. The old man himself doesn’t know a word of English, so he has Napa handle all the business dealings, write letters. Besides that, he does the marketing, massages his hands and feet. Napa does everything.”

“Seems like a very useful fellow.”

“Very cunning, more like—he’s feathering his own nest. Just because he knows a bit of English.”

I understood—Prabhat doesn’t know English, Napa does, and that’s why Nanibala is not kindly disposed toward him.

Byomkesh asked, “And Kestobabu? Who is he?”

“No one knows who he is. He’s not a relative of the old man, not even the same caste. He’s been living here since before we arrived. A drunkard, always drinking.”

“Is that so? Has he any money of his own?”

“Not a paisa. The old man gives him shoes and clothes to wear.”

“Then where does he get his liquor?”

“The old man pays for that too.”

“Strange! And yet you say not a drop of water would slip through his fingers—”

“I don’t know, Byomkeshbabu, I can’t make sense of any of it. Sometimes I think the old man is afraid. Sometimes, when Kestobabu is drunk, he throws his weight around, but the old man never says a word.”

“Is that so.” Byomkesh seemed about to lapse into deep thought.

Nanibala then asked eagerly, “But what happened on the other side, Byomkeshbabu? Did you go to see Nimai and Nitai?”

Byomkesh replied, “I did. They’re not good people. The uncle and nephews—each pointing the finger at the other. Bitter roots, bitter fruit.”

Nanibala said in a frightened voice, “Then what will happen? What if they—do something to Prabhat?”

Byomkesh said quietly, “They don’t love Prabhat, and they could well wish him harm. But even in these lawless times, it’s not so easy to murder a man, unless—”

--- Page 748 ---
446  
Byomkesh Samagra

No one commits murder unless murder is thrust upon their head. Looking at Nimai and Nitai, they seem cautious men—not the sort to risk the hangman’s noose for a killing. Consider another point. If Anadi Halder is truly determined to adopt a foster son, what would he gain by killing one of them? If one is gone, Anadi Halder can simply take another foster son, or will away his property as he pleases. In such a situation, resorting to murder would be utter foolishness. Rather—'

Nanibala’s eyes widened as she asked, ‘Rather what?’

Byomkesh replied, ‘Rather, if something were to happen to Anadi Babu himself, many problems would be solved.’

Nanibala fell silent for a moment, lost in thought. It seemed she had never considered this possibility before. Then, raising her eager face, she asked, ‘Then Prabhat has nothing to fear?’

‘You may rest assured, Nimai and Nitai are not foolish enough to do something like that. Still, caution never hurts. I have a plan in mind—I’ll put the fear of God into them so thoroughly that even if they wanted to, they wouldn’t dare try anything.’

‘What—what plan have you devised, Byomkesh Babu?’

‘There’s no need for you to know that. You should go home for today. If you hear anything important, let me know.’

Nanibala, her face overflowing with gratitude, thanked Byomkesh profusely and took her leave. Anadi Halder had a habit of taking a midday nap, and Nanibala had slipped out during that interval; if the old man awoke and discovered she had gone out, she would have a hard time explaining herself.

Afterwards, I asked Byomkesh, ‘What plan have you made? You haven’t told me anything.’

Byomkesh said, ‘Do we have any postcards at home?’

‘Yes.’

‘Good. Take a postcard and write a letter. No need to write “Shriharir Sharanam” or any salutation. Just write—“I am watching you.” That’s all. Now, send the letter to either Nimai or Nitai Halder’s address.’

Four

A few days passed.

Nanibala did not return. The matter concerning Prabhat seemed to have withered away before it could take root. Only Batul from the sadar came by once. Batul had brought a revolver, hoping to sell it to us. Had the price been reasonable, we might have considered it, but we had no desire to buy trouble for six hundred rupees. Byomkesh offered Batul a cigarette and steered the conversation elsewhere.

‘Do you know anyone at 172/2 Bowbazar Street, Batul?’

‘Yes, sir, I do.’

‘Do you know Halidar?’

‘Yes, sir.’

‘Is he—well—one of your clients?’

--- Page 749 ---
Adimriphu 447

Batul smiled faintly, carefully extinguished his half-burnt cigarette and tucked it into his pocket, then spoke in a graver tone, “Anadi Halder used to pay his dues, but for the past few months he has stopped. Now, if anything good or bad happens to him, we bear no responsibility. —But how did you come to know him? Did you have any prior acquaintance?”

“No, we met only recently.”

Batul did not press his curiosity any further. Instead, he left us with an irrelevant proverb, delivered in a slow, deliberate manner as he took his leave—“It is not wise, sir, to quarrel with a crocodile while living in the water.”

The day of Kali Puja arrived. From early morning, the air was thick with the thunderous sound of drums. Whether these were the rhythms of festivity or the war-drums of a looming battle, we could not say for certain, and so we stayed indoors.

After dusk, the city gleamed with rows of lamps. In every street and alley, fireworks began to burst—tubri, atashbaji, fanush, colored torches, and the relentless crackle of Chinese firecrackers and dodoma. Crowds of people poured out to witness the city’s splendor—some on foot, others in carriages or motorcars. The sword of communal riots hung overhead, but who cared for that? Life is short; why not laugh for a day or two?

We did not step outside, but watched the festivities from our window. If anyone were to mock us as cowards for this, we would not object. But to dance in foolish joy with a garland around our necks like a sacrificial goat—that we could never accept.

Night deepened. At midnight, Kali Puja would reach its peak; the festivities were in full swing. We were not worshippers of Shakti, but of intellect—yet we had no wish to incur the displeasure of Mother Kali. That night, after partaking of mahaprasad with polao, we retired to bed.

Little did we know that before the night ended, the matter that had been lying in wait would rear its head again, like a serpent.

I awoke abruptly at half past three. The world was silent, a chill breeze drifted in through the window. I pulled the quilt up from the foot of the bed and wrapped myself, preparing to drift off once more, when a violent noise shattered my drowsiness.

Someone was banging furiously at the door. Sitting up in bed, I thought—the battlefront has reached our very doorstep; there is no escape tonight. The heavy stick stood ready in the corner of the room; I gripped it tightly and stepped out of the bedroom. If I must die, let me die fighting.

Byomkesh, too, emerged from his room, stick in hand. The front door was sturdy, but it would not hold much longer; it would give way at any moment. We stood on either side of the door, sticks raised.

The pounding stopped for a moment, and in that brief pause, we heard an urgent voice—“O Byomkesh Babu—please open the door—just once—”

We stared at each other, eyes wide. A man’s voice, oddly familiar.

Byomkesh called out, “Who are you? State your name.”

The reply came—“It’s me—Kesto Das—please open the door quickly—”

Kesto Das! For a moment, the name eluded me, then I remembered. Kesto Babu from Anadi Halder’s house!

Byomkesh asked, “What brings you here at this hour? Who is with you?”

“No one, I am alone—”

--- Page 750 ---
Only one man was making so much noise. My suspicion was not dispelled. Byomkesh asked again,

“What brings you here so late at night?”

“Anadi Halder has been murdered. Please, open the door. I am in great trouble.”

Stunned, I exchanged another glance with Byomkesh. Anadi Halder—!

Byomkesh hesitated no longer; he opened the door. Kestobabu staggered into the room. His appearance was disheveled, his face anxious like a startled bhetki fish. On top of that, his breath reeked sharply of liquor. He collapsed heavily into a chair and, panting, said, “Someone shot Anadi dead. I swear I know nothing about it. I wasn’t at home—”

Byomkesh raised a hand. “We’ll get to that later. First, answer my question. How did you know me? Where did you get my address?”

Kestobabu sat hunched for a while, a damp-cat look about him. At last, in a slurred voice, he said, “That day you came to our house, seeing you made me suspicious. So, when you left, I followed you. Here, at the hotel downstairs, I learned your identity.”

Byomkesh regarded him with steady eyes for a moment, then said, “Hmm. You’re a sharp fellow, I see. Why do you cling to Anadi Halder’s coattails?”

Kestobabu replied, “Anadi and I are childhood friends—fallen on hard times—that’s why—”

“So Anadi Halder fed you, clothed you, even supplied you with money for drink. That’s what I’d call a deep friendship. Well, tell me what happened tonight. Start from the beginning.”

Kestobabu stared at Byomkesh, unblinking, then said in a slightly plaintive tone, “You seem to know everything already. But I swear I didn’t kill Anadi. This evening—well, yesterday evening, I quarreled with Anadi. I told him, today is Kali Puja, you must give me fifty rupees. That started a furious argument. Anadi gave me ten rupees and said—take this and get out, and don’t ever set foot in my house again.”

“Who heard you quarrel?”

“Nanibala and Napa were at home. Shasthibabu from downstairs heard it too. He was sitting on the verandah, smoking. As I was coming down, he said loudly enough for me to hear—‘Day and night the battle of Nishumbha rages overhead—who knows when this sin will be washed away.’”

“Go on.”

“Then, around one in the morning, I came back. And saw—”

“Where were you until one in the morning?”

“No use hiding it from you—I was drinking at the liquor shop—played cards at a gambling adda and won thirty rupees—then wandered here and there a bit—”

“Hmm. What did you see when you returned home?”

“The first thing I saw was Shasthibabu downstairs, pacing in the stairwell with his hookah in hand. When he saw me, he said—‘The wheel of dharma turns in the wind.’ I didn’t understand a thing. I went up the stairs—found the stair door broken!

“Inside, I saw Prabhat and Napa sitting on the bench, Nanibala leaning against the wall, sitting on the floor. When they saw me, all three stared wide-eyed, as if they’d never before…”

--- Page 751 ---
Adimriphu 449

Hadn’t seen. I was astonished. I said—What’s this, why are you all sitting here? Not a word from anyone’s mouth. Then, suddenly, Napa sprang up, pointed a finger at me and cried—Kestobabu, this is your doing. You have killed the master.

“Murder! My head spun. I asked—Who? Where? Why? No one answered. At last, Prabhat said—Go look in the bathroom.

“I peered into the bathroom by the roadside. Anadi was lying there, a bloody mess. Shot in the chest. The sight nearly made me faint; I sank down onto the floor. Everything in my head began to whirl.

“I don’t know how long passed after that. The three of them spoke in hushed voices, arguing about what should be done. From their words I gathered that none of them had been home after evening; Anadi had been alone in the house. Around midnight, when they returned and knocked on the door, there was no response. After knocking for a long time, they grew afraid—perhaps something had happened. Then they broke down the door and entered, and found Anadi dead in the bathroom.

“When my head cleared a little, I said—Why are you blaming me? Why would I kill Anadi? Anadi was my benefactor, my friend— Napa leapt up and said—Don’t pretend. I’m going to inform the police. With that, he went down the stairs.

“I was terrified. The police would come and arrest me. They’d testify that I’d quarreled with Anadi. I couldn’t stay there any longer; I got up and fled. I had no idea where to go; out on the street, I thought of you.”

For a while, there was silence; Kestobabu seemed to be dozing off. But I noticed that even in his drowsiness, his half-closed eyes kept darting to Byomkesh’s face.

Suddenly, Byomkesh said, “So, you did not kill Anadi Haldar.”

Kestobabu started, his eyes wide with shock. “Eh! No, Byomkeshbabu, I did not kill him. Just think—what would I gain by murdering Anadi?”

Byomkesh said, “Anadi Haldar had thrown you out of the house.”

Kestobabu replied, “That was just something he said in anger. Anadi didn’t really have the courage to throw me out.”

“No courage! Meaning, you know some grave secret about Anadi Haldar’s life.”

Kestobabu was silent for a while, then slowly said, “I know all of Anadi’s secrets; I could have sent him to the gallows. But let’s leave that for now—if it’s necessary, I’ll tell you later, Byomkeshbabu. For now, please find a way to save me from the police.”

Byomkesh thought for a moment, then said, “If I am to save you, then we must find out who really committed the murder. We’ll have to go to the scene.”

Kestobabu grew anxious, his voice faltering, “Must I go too?”

“Of course you must. On what pretext will I go if you don’t come?”

“But—the police must have arrived by now—”

Byomkesh said sharply, “If you haven’t committed the murder, what are you afraid of?—Ajit, get ready, the three of us are going.”

Kestobabu sat there, bewildered, while we dressed and prepared ourselves. When we returned to the baithak-khana, Kestobabu rose from his chair with difficulty and said,

--- Page 752 ---
‘Byomkesh Babu, do you—heh heh—have any liquor at your house? A little whisky or brandy, perhaps? I feel as if the strength has left my limbs.’

Byomkesh replied with irritation, ‘I don’t keep liquor at home. Come along.’

Five

When we reached Anadi Haldar’s residence, it was half past four in the night. Calcutta, after its raucous revelry until midnight, now slumbered in the depths of the late hours.

The main door downstairs was open. There was no one in the stairwell. Sasthi Babu, it seemed, had retired to bed, exhausted. Ascending the stairs, we found the latch on the door broken; the door itself was intact, but the latch had been snapped off and flung aside. We entered the room with Byomkesh leading the way.

At our entrance, a commotion seemed to ripple through the room. Yet there were only three people present: Nonibala, Prabhat, and Napa. All three sprang up in alarm. Napa cried out, ‘Who? Who is it? What do you want?’ Then, catching sight of Kesto Babu behind us, she fell silent. Nonibala, her fleshy face agape, involuntarily exclaimed in a loud voice, ‘Ah, Byomkesh Babu.’ She did not seem particularly pleased to see us. Prabhat stared on, vacant as a simpleton.

Byomkesh cast his gaze around the room and addressed Nonibala, ‘Kesto Babu brought me here. The police haven’t arrived yet?’

Nonibala shook her head. When Byomkesh turned his eyes to Napa, she stammered in confusion, ‘You—Byomkesh Babu, that is—’

Byomkesh said, ‘Yes. This is my friend Ajit Bandyopadhyay. Perhaps you remember we came the other day. You went to call the police, didn’t you? What happened?’

Napa seemed bewildered, then startled, she replied, ‘The police—yes, I went to the thana. There was no one there, just a constable with his feet up on the table, fast asleep. When I told him, he got angry and said, “Go, go, a Hindu has died, what’s all this fuss about? Throw the body out on the street.” I was leaving when he called me back and said, “Leave the address, when the inspector comes in the morning, we’ll let him know.” So I gave Anadi Babu’s name and address and came back.’

There was nothing new in the police’s habitual indifference in some cases and excessive zeal in others; in fact, out of habit, I had hoped the police would come running as soon as they heard the news. Byomkesh stood for a moment, brows furrowed, then looked up and said, ‘You all suspect Kesto Babu of murdering Anadi Babu. On his behalf, I wish to investigate this matter. Does anyone object?’

No one replied; all averted their eyes, avoiding Byomkesh’s gaze. Then Byomkesh asked, ‘The body is on the balcony—has anyone touched it?’

Everyone shook their heads in denial.

We then entered the balcony. An electric lamp glowed against the wall, and in its unwavering light we saw Anadi Haldar’s corpse lying on its side on the floor, face turned toward the street. He wore a white thermal vest, over which was a dressing gown. Across his chest—

--- Page 753 ---
Adimriphu 451

The quilt had slipped off; there was a hole in the vest, and through that hole, dark blood had oozed out and dripped onto the floor. Upon the dead man’s face, a grotesque distortion had settled, like the mockery of a demonic smile.

Byomkesh bent down and pulled the quilt away from the back. I saw that on this side too, there was a neat, round hole in the vest. Here, the blood had not flowed as much—only the area around the hole was damp. The bullet had passed clean through the body.

Leaving the corpse, Byomkesh stood up and gazed absentmindedly out toward the street. In a low voice, I asked, “What do you think?”

Byomkesh replied, distracted, “This is the same man who behaved so rudely with me the other day, isn’t it strange?... The body is beginning to stiffen... Perhaps Anadi Halder was standing by the railing, watching the fireworks on the street—” Byomkesh looked toward the large house across the road. “But where did the bullet go? It isn’t in the body, it passed right through—”

If Byomkesh’s assumption was correct, the bullet should have lodged in the balcony wall. But we found no sign of a bullet or bullet mark on the balcony wall, the ceiling, or the floor. Sometimes, when a bullet exits, it takes an oblique path; or perhaps Anadi Halder was standing at an angle, and the bullet went out through a gap at the side of the balcony. But from the way the body had fallen, it seemed Anadi Halder had been facing the street, took the bullet in his chest and sat down right there, then slumped to the side.

Directly across the street stood that house. In between, a gap of seventy or eighty feet. Perhaps the shot had come from a window on the second or third floor of that house.

Finding no trace of the bullet on the balcony, Byomkesh bent down again to examine the corpse. When the quilt was pulled away, I saw that the knot of the dhoti at the waist had loosened, and around the waist was a thick black thread, like a waistband. Hanging from the thread was a key.

Byomkesh fiddled with the key, then carefully removed it and, covering the corpse again with the quilt, said, “Come, we’re done here.”

Outside, the darkness of night had not yet lifted. Along the road, lorries loaded with greens and vegetables had begun to roll by. The city of Calcutta was preparing to satisfy its enormous hunger.

Returning to the room, we found the four people who had been inside still standing just as before, none had moved. Byomkesh held up the key and said, “This was at the dead man’s waist. Whose key is it?”

I looked at each of their faces in turn. All stared fixedly at the key, except for the napha, whose face was shadowed with fear. At last, Nanibala said, “There’s an iron safe in Anadi Babu’s bedroom, that’s its key.”

“What’s in the iron safe? Money?”

They all shook their heads; no one knew. Nanibala said, “How would we know? Did Anadi Babu ever let anyone touch the safe? If you even went near it, he’d start barking—” She stopped, glancing at Prabhat’s eyes.

The napha licked his lips and said, “I don’t think there was money in the safe. Master kept his money in the bank.”

Byomkesh slipped the key into his pocket and said, “We’ll see what’s in the safe later. For now, I have a few questions for you. —How many ways are there to enter or leave the house?”

--- Page 754 ---
452  
Byomkesh Samagra

Everyone pointed toward the broken door. “Only that one.”  
“No other doors?”  
“No.”  
Byomkesh sat down on one end of the bench and said, “Alright. That means, when Anadi Babu died, there was no one else in the house—the shot came from outside. Prabhat Babu, tell me, when did you leave the house?”  
Prabhat kept his eyes fixed on the ground, ran a hand through his disheveled hair for a moment, then looked up and replied, “I left with my mother—around half past eight, I think.”  
“Oh, you both left together?”  
“Yes, Mother went to see a film.”  
“Is that so?” Byomkesh glanced at Nanibala.  
Nanibala said, “I hardly ever get to see a film—once in six or nine months, if at all. Yesterday, near Sealdah, there’s that cinema—they were showing ‘Jai Ma Kali’, so I went. Dinner in this house is always over by eight, so we went to the night show. Prabhat said—”  
Byomkesh interrupted her, “When you left, who was in the house?”  
Prabhat said, “Only Anadi Babu was here. Nripen Babu had left just after eight.”  
Byomkesh turned toward Napa, but where was Napa? She had been standing by an inner door all this time, but now, unnoticed, she had vanished.  
Byomkesh looked at Nanibala in surprise, turning his palms upward in question. Nanibala silently pointed—Napa had disappeared through that very door. Byomkesh, moving with feline steps, headed in that direction; I followed him.  
A narrow passage, almost like a lane, then a room. The light was on. We peered in—Napa was at one corner, drawer of a table open, her hand plunged inside, searching frantically for something. Seeing us at the door, she straightened abruptly and slammed the drawer shut.  
We entered. Byomkesh, displeasure in his voice, asked, “Is this your room?”  
Napa stared blankly for a moment, then said, “Yes, my room.”  
“Why did you leave without saying anything? What are you doing?”  
Napa tried to force a wan smile. “Nothing—I just came to get a cigarette—can’t seem to find one—”  
It was hardly possible not to find it—the cigarette packet lay on one corner of the table.  
Byomkesh said, “What’s that? Looks like a cigarette packet to me.”  
Napa seemed to startle. “Ah—oh yes—the matches—I can’t find the matches—”  
Byomkesh gave her a long, searching look, then took out a matchbox from his own pocket and handed it to her. “Here.” Napa, with trembling hands, struck a match and lit her cigarette.  
I glanced around the room. A small space: a bed on a cot, a table with drawers, and a chair beside it. There was a window with iron bars.  
The window was open. Byomkesh went and stood before it; I joined him.  
The sky was growing pale. Through the window, the half-finished new house could be seen. In the middle—

--- Page 755 ---
Adimriphu 453

The alley stretched ahead like a deep chasm.

“Nripenbabu, where is your home?”

At this sudden question from Byomkesh, Nripen almost leapt up. He had been leaning against the edge of the table, taking a long drag from his cigarette. Eyes wide, he replied, “Home—?”

“Yes, your native place. Where do you live? Which district?”

Nripen, caught off guard, stammered, “Native place? Khajurhat, on the Diamond Harbour line, in the district of Twenty-Four Parganas.”

Byomkesh turned from the window and fixed his gaze on Nripen. “Khajurhat! Do you know Ramesh Mallik of Khajurhat?”

Nripen, as if his voice was choked by smoke, dropped the burnt-out cigarette and said, “Yes. He lives in our para.”

“Who do you have in Khajurhat?”

“My uncle.”

“Your father is no longer alive?”

“No.”

“Very well, what is your full name?”

“Nripen Dutta.”

Byomkesh stepped closer to Nripen, his tone taking on a note of intimacy. “Nripenbabu, you strike me as a capable man. How long have you been working as Anadi Babu’s secretary?”

Nripen thought for a moment. “Almost four years.”

“Four years? You lasted that long?”

Nripen remained silent.

“You must know if Anadi Babu had any enemies?”

Nripen lifted his face helplessly. “Whose name should I mention? He was at odds with everyone he knew. Quarreling was his nature.”

“He quarreled with everyone in the house as well?”

“He would abuse everyone. But we were his subordinates, we had to keep quiet. Only Kestobabu would sometimes—”

“Did Anadi Babu abuse Prabhat as well?”

“Not exactly abuse, but he would needle him whenever he got the chance. Prabhatbabu never took it to heart, though.”

“All right, let that be. Tell me, when did you leave the house last night?”

“I left just after eight.”

“When did you return?”

“Around one, I think. When I came back, I saw Nonibala Devi and Prabhatbabu pushing at the door.”

“Where were you from eight to one?”

“I went to see a movie.”

“You too went to see ‘Jai Ma Kali’?”

“No, I went to see an English film.”

“Oh! How did you return so late at night?”

“I walked.”

--- Page 756 ---
I noticed that, as Byomkesh continued his questioning, Nripen had grown considerably calmer; the earlier signs of fear and agitation had vanished. Byomkesh said, “Come, let’s go to the other room now.”

Six

The three of us returned to the adjoining room and found Kestobabu and Prabhat seated at opposite ends of the bench. Kestobabu was yawning and casting sidelong glances at Prabhat, who sat with his chin resting on his palm, lost in thought. Nanibala had sprawled on the floor, her legs stretched out, dozing against the wall. At our entrance, everyone straightened up. Prabhat rose from the bench and murmured, almost inaudibly, “Please, sit.”

Byomkesh replied, “No, I won’t sit. Dawn is breaking; it’s time for us to return home. The police may arrive at any moment. If they see us here, it might sour their mood. Let me give you all a word of advice—remember it well. Do not try to shift the blame onto anyone else; it will only harm you. The police might just round everyone up and throw you all into lockup.”

Everyone fell silent.

“Prabhatbabu, now it’s your turn to speak. Yesterday, you accompanied your mother to the cinema, but you didn’t watch the film yourself?”

Prabhat replied, “No. I bought the tickets, seated my mother inside, and then went to my shop.”

“Oh. You went to the shop after half past eight at night?”

“Yes. On Diwali night, I had decorated the shop with lights.”

“And then?”

“Then, at about quarter to twelve, I closed the shop and went back to the cinema, brought my mother home from there.”

“So, from roughly nine to quarter to twelve, you were at the shop. Was anyone else there?”

“Gurung was there, standing guard at the door.”

“Gurung—the Gurkha doorman. Did any customers come by?”

“No.”

“What did you do all that time in the shop?”

“Nothing. I sat in the back room and bound some books.”

“All right, let’s leave that for now. —Did you get along with Anadibabu?”

Prabhat lifted his eyes, brimming with resentment. “No. He took me in as a foster son, treated me well at first. Then—gradually—”

“Gradually, his attitude changed?” Byomkesh said. “Tell me, why did he take you in as a foster son?”

“I don’t know.”

“At first he treated you well, then his mood changed; was there any particular reason for that?”

“Perhaps there was. But to my knowledge, I did nothing wrong.”

Prabhat, exhausted, sat down again on the bench. Byomkesh regarded him with a gentle gaze and said, “You should go and lie down for a while. Once the police arrive, there will be no more rest.”

--- Page 757 ---
Adimriphu 455

‘Whether you’ll get it or not, I doubt.’

But Prabhat merely shook his head. Byomkesh then turned to Nanibala and said,

‘You didn’t have a cordial relationship with Anadi Babu either, did you?’

Nanibala’s mouth and cow-like eyes widened at once, and she almost broke into tears,

‘I’ve told you everything, Byomkesh Babu. I was a thorn in the old man’s side. He loved Prabhat, but he couldn’t stand the sight of me. Day and night he’d look for excuses; the moment he found something, he’d start gnashing his teeth. Such a vile heart—’ Nanibala stopped abruptly.

Anadi Halder was indeed dead, but perhaps the sudden memory that his corpse still lay nearby made her regain her composure. Moreover, she realized—albeit a little late—that it was best not to dwell on her discord with Anadi Babu.

Kestobabu hinted at the same, letting out a hiccup-like chuckle and saying,

‘So it wasn’t just with me that Anadi quarreled?’

Prabhat turned his head and glanced at him once. Byomkesh said, ‘That line of thought leads nowhere. It appears Anadi Halder quarreled with everyone; that proves nothing. To commit murder, one must not only have the desire, but also the opportunity.’

Byomkesh turned to Nanibala. ‘How was the cinema yesterday?’

Nanibala stared at him, mouth agape.

‘Eh—the cinema—!’

‘Did you watch the film till the end?’

At last, Nanibala seemed to grasp the meaning of the question. She said, ‘Oh, but of course I did! I watched it from start to finish, only left after the film ended. I came out and stood outside, and then Prabhat came. I returned home with him. When we arrived—’

Byomkesh sighed and said, ‘I know. Now let’s go to Anadi Halder’s bedroom. We need to look at the iron almirah.’

The six of us gathered and made our way to Anadi Halder’s sleeping quarters. It was the room adjacent to the one where we had met Anadi Halder a few days ago. Nripen pressed the switch by the door and turned on the light.

The room was similar in size and shape to Nripen’s, but at the other end of the house. A barred window stood open. Apart from a bed and a steel almirah at its head, there was nothing else in the room.

Once we all entered, the room felt cramped. Byomkesh said, ‘Not everyone needs to be in here. Kestobabu, perhaps you could stay in the other room. The stairway door is broken; the police might arrive any moment.’

Kestobabu was surely as curious as the rest of us to know what was inside the almirah, but he said, ‘No matter, I’ll keep watch over the corpse. But at a time like this, a cup of hot tea would be most welcome.’ With that, he began to rub his hands together eagerly.

Byomkesh said, ‘Tea wouldn’t be amiss,’ and turned a questioning gaze towards Nanibala.

Nanibala replied reluctantly, ‘I can make tea. But there’s no milk.’

Byomkesh said, ‘Lemon juice will do instead of milk.’

Kestobabu said in a deep voice, ‘Ginger! Ginger! Have tea with ginger juice, it’ll invigorate you.’

Byomkesh said, ‘Ginger juice will do as well.’

When Nanibala and Kestobabu left, Nripen hesitated a little and said, ‘Will you need me—’

--- Page 758 ---
456  
Byomkesh Samagra

“What?”  
Byomkesh said, “It’s you I need. Prabhatbabu, you’d better go to your own room and get some rest.”  
Prabhat hesitated for a moment, then, without a word, slowly left the room.  
Now only the two of us and Nripen remained.  

There was nothing much of note in the room. The bed was made; the sheets were clean, unused since last night. On the wall, a fresh dhoti was neatly folded on a peg. In one corner stood a water jug, covered with a glass. Byomkesh glanced around, then took a key from his pocket.  

The almirah was new. Its varnished wood gleamed, tall and slender, built to last. Byomkesh turned the key and swung open the double doors. Nripen and I leaned in eagerly to peer inside.  

There were four shelves within. The topmost shelf held a row of books running from one end to the other, with occasional gaps like missing teeth. On the spines of some, names were inscribed in gold—Krittibasi Ramayan, Mahajan Padabali. Byomkesh took out a few more volumes; most were cheap Bat-tala editions, though well-bound. Perhaps Prabhat had them rebound.  

Byomkesh asked Nripen, “Did Anadi Halder read much?”  
Nripen replied dryly, “Never saw him read.”  
“Does anyone else in the house read?”  
“Prabhatbabu does. I read when I get the chance. But I’ve never seen the books in the master’s almirah before.”  
“And yet, from the gaps in the row, it looks like a few books have been taken out. Where did they go?”  
Nripen glanced around the room and said, “I couldn’t say. They’re not here. Should I ask Prabhatbabu?”  
Byomkesh said, “Let it be for now, it’s nothing urgent. —Tell me, outside the house, where did Anadi Halder go most often?”  
Nripen replied, “The master rarely left home. When he did, it was either to meet the solicitor or to go to the bank. Nowhere else, really.”  

Byomkesh lowered his gaze to the second shelf.  

On the second shelf were many bottles and vials. The vials contained patent medicines, the bottles foreign liquor. One bottle was nearly empty, the others still sealed.  
Byomkesh asked, “Did Anadi Halder drink?”  
Nripen said, “He wasn’t a drunkard. But he drank. I’ve caught the smell from time to time.”  

Examining the medicine vials, we found most were tonics—imported concoctions promising to restore lost youth. Byomkesh asked, “Did Anadi Halder have a habit of going out in the evenings?”  
Nripen replied, “Not much, maybe two or three times a month.”  
“Ah! Anadi Halder’s whole character is coming into focus. What a fascinating character!” Byomkesh turned his attention to the third shelf of the almirah.  

On the third shelf were several thick notebooks and a few files. The notebooks, bound in cardboard...

--- Page 759 ---
Adimriphu  
457

It was sturdily bound. Upon opening it, we found it was a ledger of business accounts. To truly understand the nature and methods of the business, one would have to study these books thoroughly; but there was no time for that. Byomkesh turned to Nupen and asked, “Do you know what business Anadi Halder was engaged in?”

Nupen replied, “I don’t know what he did before; he never spoke about himself to anyone. But at the beginning of the war, he bought up Japanese goods. The kind of machinery needed for cotton mills, that sort of thing. He bought them cheap—”

“And then sold them at black market rates. I see.” Byomkesh picked up a file, opened the cover, and began to examine it.

Inside the file were various documents: the deed for the new house, letters from the solicitor, rent receipts, and so on. As he lightly skimmed over the papers, turning the pages, Byomkesh suddenly stopped at one spot. On a ruled sheet of paper, a few lines were written, and beneath them, a signature over a stamp.

Byomkesh took the paper out of the file, held it up to his face, and began to read it with close attention. I craned my neck to see—a handnote. Anadi Halder had lent five thousand rupees to a certain Dayalhari Majumdar, as attested by his own handwriting.

Byomkesh looked up from the handnote and asked, “Who is Dayalhari Majumdar?”

Nupen stared for a moment, then said, “Dayalhari—ah, I remember now—” He stepped a little closer and, in a lowered voice, said, “Prabhatbabu wanted to marry Dayalharibabu’s daughter, but the master saw the girl and disapproved—”

“The girl must have been unattractive?”

“None of us ever saw her.”

“But then, why lend five thousand rupees?”

“I don’t know; perhaps for that very reason—”

“For what reason?”

“Perhaps, the person to whom he lent the money, the master didn’t want Prabhatbabu to marry his daughter.”

“Possible. Did Anadi Halder usually deal in moneylending?”

“No. I’ve never seen him lend money to anyone.”

“The date on the handnote is 11/9/1946, that is, about a month ago. When did Anadi Halder go to see the girl?”

“Around that time. I don’t remember the exact date.”

“Do you know anything about Dayalhari Majumdar?”

“Nothing at all. I’ve heard from others that the girl is supposed to sing very well, has already made quite a name for herself. They’re from East Bengal, only recently arrived in Calcutta.”

“Is that so! Ajit, remember Dayalhari Majumdar’s address—” He read from the handnote—“13/3, Ramtanu Lane, Shyambazar.”

I silently repeated the address to myself several times. Byomkesh began to inspect the lowest shelf of the almirah.

On the bottom shelf, there was only a single wooden hand-box, nothing else. The box was locked. Byomkesh turned the key and lifted the lid. Inside were a bundle of ten-rupee notes, some loose change, and a cheque book.

Byomkesh counted the notes. Two hundred and sixty rupees. The cheque book was quite thick, a book of a hundred cheques; more than half had already been used. Byomkesh began to examine the used cheques.

--- Page 760 ---
458  
Byomkesh Samagra

Flipping through the passbooks, Byomkesh asked, “Did Anadi Halder keep money in any bank other than Bharat Bank?”

Nripen replied, “I don’t know which bank he kept his money in.”

“Strange! A new house is being built—how did he pay the contractor?”

“He paid in cash. I know, because I used to prepare the draft receipts and get the contractor’s signature. On the days payment was due, master would leave the house at nine in the morning and return at eleven. Then he’d pay the contractor.”

“So, he went to fetch money from the bank?”

“That’s what I think.”

“Hm. Do you know how much has been paid to the contractor for the house?”

Nripen did a quick calculation in his head and said, “About thirty thousand rupees have been paid. The receipts should be in the files. If you want to check—”

Byomkesh put down the checkbook and muttered half to himself, “How odd!—No, there’s no need for meticulous accounting. Come, Ajit, we’ve seen all there is to see in this room.” With that, he carefully closed the almirah.

At that moment, Nanibala entered the room carrying a large tray with four cups of tea. “Here you are. Prabhat is lying down in his room; I’ve given him his tea.”

Nripen turned off the light. Through the window, we could see that outside, the morning had cleared up nicely.

The three of us took our teacups and moved to the outer room. Nanibala followed us, carrying Kestobabu’s cup on the tray.

Kestobabu was stretched out on the bench, fast asleep. His snoring filled the room with a guttural rumble.

Peeking out onto the balcony, I saw the morning light falling across Anadi Halder’s dead face. The flies, drawn by the scent, had begun to gather.

Seven

We had just finished our tea and set down our cups when the sound of several footsteps echoed on the stairs. At last, I thought, the police must be arriving.

But I was mistaken—the police were still lost in sleep. The newcomers were three in number: an unfamiliar elderly gentleman, accompanied by Nimai and Nitai. Just as vultures sense a carcass from afar, Nimai and Nitai had caught the scent of their uncle’s great departure.

The footsteps woke Kestobabu, who sat up, rubbing his eyes. Prabhat entered from the inner rooms.

For a moment, both parties exchanged silent glances. Nimai and Nitai stood on either side of the elderly gentleman, their eyes moving from one of us to the next, finally coming to rest on Byomkesh; suspicion flickered in their gaze. Perhaps they recognized him.

Byomkesh was the first to speak. “What do you want?”

Nimai and Nitai immediately leaned in and whispered into the elderly gentleman’s ears.

--- Page 761 ---
Adimriphu 459

The middle-aged gentleman’s unshaven face bristled with a salt-and-pepper beard and mustache; perhaps his temper, too, was not at its best, having been roused at such an untimely hour. For a moment, he regarded Byomkesh with eyes full of irritation, then asked in a distorted voice, “Who are you?”

Byomkesh replied, “You may call me a family friend. My name is Byomkesh Bakshi.”

A sudden alertness flashed in the eyes of all three men. The middle-aged gentleman drew a breath and asked, “Detective?”

Byomkesh said, “Satyanweshi.”

The gentleman made a dismissive sound in his throat, then, turning to Prabhat, said, “We have received news that Anadi Halder has passed away. These two brothers, Nimai and Nitai Halder, are his nephews and heirs. They have come to take possession of the property. You will have to vacate this house.”

Prabhat stared blankly for a while, then turned his gaze to Byomkesh. Byomkesh said, “Is that so? We have to leave the house! But you haven’t told us who you are.”

The gentleman said, “I am their lawyer, Kaminikanta Mustafi.”

Byomkesh said, “A lawyer. Then you should know that Anadi Halder’s nephews are not his legal heirs. He had adopted a son.”

Lawyer Kaminikanta made a sound through his nose, regarding Byomkesh with utter contempt, and said, “Since you claim to be a family friend, you should know that Anadi Halder did not adopt a son. One cannot adopt a son with mere words. There must be a registered deed, rituals must be performed. Anadi Halder did none of these. —You will have to leave here with only the clothes on your back, not a stick more. Everything in this house belongs to my clients.”

For a moment, Byomkesh seemed taken aback, staring at Prabhat; then he composed himself. With a crooked smile, he said, “Indeed? You think you can seize Anadi Halder’s property with threats. It’s not so easy, Lawyer-babu. If the adoption was not lawful, you’ll have to prove it in court, obtain a succession certificate—only then can you take possession. Do you understand?”

The lawyer said, “If you do not vacate the house immediately, I will call the police.”

Byomkesh replied, “No need to call the police—the police are already on their way. —By the way, how did you learn of Anadi-babu’s death so quickly? It hasn’t even been two hours—”

Suddenly, one of the brothers, Nimai or Nitai, blurted out, “Two hours. Uncle died at eleven last night—” and stopped short, as if catching himself.

Byomkesh said sweetly, “At eleven, you say? How did you know? Were you present at the time of death? With a gun in your hand?”

Nimai or Nitai turned utterly pale. The lawyer snapped at him, “You two keep quiet, let me do the talking. So, you refuse to vacate. Very well, the court will see to it.” With that, he grasped his clients by the arms and turned toward the stairs.

Byomkesh said, “Leaving so soon? Won’t you wait a little longer? The police will surely want to hear the nephews’ statements. Where were you all at eleven o’clock last night—”

Before Byomkesh could finish, the pair of nephews hurried past the lawyer and made for the stairs.

--- Page 762 ---
460  
Byomkesh Samagra

He disappeared down the stairs. Advocate Kaminikanta Mustafi cast a heated, smoldering glance at Byomkesh before following after them.

When the sound of their footsteps had faded below, Byomkesh turned to Prabhat and asked,  
“You never told me before that you are not, by law, Anadibabu’s adopted son. Why?”

Prabhat stood there, scratching his neck with a look of irritation. Now, Nanibala Devi stepped forward. Until now, I had not noticed how her face had shriveled and sunk, her eyes wide and desperate. She cried out,  
“Byomkeshbabu, is what they said true? Prabhat is not Anadibabu’s adopted son?”

Byomkesh replied,  
“That’s exactly what I’m trying to find out. Prabhatbabu—?”

Prabhat licked his lips and muttered,  
“I—I don’t know the law. When I first came to Calcutta, Anadibabu took me to a solicitor’s office. There I heard that to adopt a son, you have to register a deed, perform a homa-yajna. But none of that ever happened.”

“So you knew you were not Anadibabu’s heir?”

“Yes, I knew. But I thought—”

“You thought that before his death, Anadibabu would register the deed and make you his adopted son?”

“Yes.”

A moment of silence. Then Nanibala let out a long, trembling sigh and said,  
“Then—then—Prabhat will get nothing. Everything will go to that Nimai and Nitai!” Nanibala’s ample frame seemed to suddenly collapse; she sank down onto the floor.

Prabhat hurried to her side, sat down, and said in a low, deep voice,  
“Why are you worrying, Ma? We have the shop. That will be enough for the two of us.”

Nanibala clung to Prabhat’s neck and broke into sobs. At least, after the death of Anadi Halder, someone was finally seen to weep.

Byomkesh narrowed his eyes, watching them, then let his gaze wander around the room before suddenly asking,  
“Where is Nripenbabu?”

All this time, no one had paid any attention to Nripen; he had silently vanished again. Byomkesh signaled to me with his eyes. I had just started towards Nripen’s room when he returned of his own accord.  
“Here I am,” he said.

Byomkesh asked,  
“Where had you gone?”

“I—I went up to the roof for a moment.” From the look on Nripen’s face, it seemed he was relieved about something.

Byomkesh said,  
“The roof? The roof on the third floor?”

“No, there’s a roof on the second floor.”

“Is that so? Come, let’s have a look at this roof.”

“Is that so? Come, let’s have a look at this roof.”

At the far end of the corridor that led to Nripen’s room was a door; beyond it, the roof. A small space, checkered like a chessboard, enclosed by an iron railing. At the back, the wall of another house; to the side, across the alley, Anadi Halder’s new house.

Standing on the roof, the frame of the new house was clearly visible; in fact, if one had a habit of long leaps, it was possible to cross from this house to that one. The wall of the new house rose up to the level of the second-floor roof, bamboo scaffolding lashed all around.

--- Page 763 ---
Adimriphu 461

Circling around the edge of the terrace, Byomkesh asked, “Is the door to the roof left open at night?”

Nripen replied, “It’s not supposed to be. Every night, before going to bed, the master would close the door himself.”

“Was it closed last night?”

“That I don’t know.”

“When you came up a little while ago, was it open or closed?”

Nripen scratched his throat, gazing up at the sky, and finally said, “I can’t recall. My mind was elsewhere—”

“Hm.”

We returned to the room. Nanibala Devi still sat on the floor, legs outstretched in utter desolation, while Prabhat tried to comfort her in a gentle voice. Kestobabu was just finishing his belated cup of tea, setting the empty cup aside.

Byomkesh said, “Still no sign of the police. We should be going now. —Come, Ajit, before we leave, we must put the key back where it belongs, or the police might make a fuss.”

We went out to the balcony. The flies had already swarmed the body. Bending down, Byomkesh slipped the key into the waistband of the dead man’s dhoti and said, “Ajit, look here.”

I leaned in and saw, near the string at the waist, a mark—reddish, about the size of a half-rupee coin. I asked, “What kind of mark is this?”

Byomkesh ran his finger over it and said, “Looks like a bloodstain, but it isn’t. It’s jadu—betel juice.”

We covered the corpse and came away. Byomkesh said, “We’re leaving now. Answer whatever questions the police ask, but don’t volunteer anything extra. There’s no need to mention that I opened the almirah. If Nimai or Nitai come, don’t let them into the house. —Kestobabu, please come by our place later today.”

Kestobabu nodded in agreement, tilting his head. We descended the stairs. The sun had risen, and the city’s bustle had already begun.

Eight

As we reached the bottom, we saw old Shasthibabu wandering about the stairwell, broom and dustpan in hand. He shot us a sidelong, sardonic glance. The fierce expression I’d seen on our first meeting was gone; now, his crumpled face seemed almost softened by a keen curiosity.

Byomkesh paused and asked, “Your name is Shasthibabu?”

He scrutinized Byomkesh carefully, then replied, “Yes. And you—who are you—?”

Byomkesh offered no introduction, only said briefly, “Don’t ask, my friend. I was owed money by Anadi Halder, and it seems that money is lost for good. You’ve probably heard the man is dead.”

The suspicion and caution faded from Shasthibabu’s face. With great satisfaction, he said, “I’ve heard. Last night—”

--- Page 764 ---
462  
Byomkesh Samagra

—I’ve been hearing since then. “How did he die?” This last question he asked, craning his neck so that his words were almost whispered into Byomkesh’s ear.

Byomkesh replied, “Haven’t you heard? Someone murdered him. I heard you were sitting out on the veranda until quite late last night—”

With a gesture of irritation, Shasthibabu said, “What could I do? The boys from the para started bursting firecrackers right in front of the house. Just look there, how many spent tubri shells are lying around. And not just tubri! The din from those Chinese firecrackers was enough to split my ears. I thought, there’s no hope of sleep, might as well watch the fireworks. So, how was he killed? Was it with a knife?”

Byomkesh sidestepped the question and said, “So, you were sitting on the veranda from evening till late at night. Did anyone come to see Anadi Halder during that time?”

“No one. Not until after midnight, when that boy and his mother arrived and started banging on the door. Then Napa came. Then Kesto Das.”

“No one else in between?”

“No one entered the house. Though—I did see one of Anadi Halder’s nephews loitering by the footpath, near the hotel.”

“Is that so? And then?”

“Didn’t see him after that. At least, he didn’t come into this house.”

“What time did you see him?”

“I didn’t really note the time. But early on, the babus were still upstairs in the hotel, sitting by the window, playing dice. Must have been around ten, maybe half past ten. So, have you found out who killed him?”

Byomkesh lowered his head and thought for a moment, then suddenly asked, “Did you have any quarrel with Anadi Halder?”

Shasthibabu started, “Eh! Quarrel? Well, it’s not as if there wasn’t any.”

“You didn’t go upstairs last night?”

“Me! Why would I go upstairs? You’re quite a character, aren’t you? What are you getting at?” Shasthibabu was beginning to bristle.

“You don’t know who killed Anadi Halder?”

“How would I know! The one who killed him knows, how would I? You’re a dangerous man, sir! I’m an old man, keep to myself, and now you want to frame me?”

Byomkesh laughed. “I don’t want to frame you. You’re doing that yourself. You’re so pleased at Anadi Halder’s death, you can’t even hide it. Come, Ajit, let’s go have another cup of tea at that hotel.”

Shasthibabu was left speechless. We stepped down onto the footpath. Across the street, above the hotel, hung a large signboard: Srikanta Panthnibas. Srikanta was probably the owner’s name. On the ground floor, the tea-thirsty crowd had already settled in the restaurant; on the second floor, a row of windows, a few open. Byomkesh stepped forward to cross the road, then suddenly stopped and said, “Wait, let’s take a look down the alley.”

“What’s there to see in the alley?”

“Come on.”

We entered the alley that ran between Anadi Halder’s house and the new building. The alley was already terribly narrow, and now, with scattered bricks and rubble from the new construction, and the bamboo scaffolding poles—

--- Page 765 ---
Adimriphu 463

…have only made it more impenetrable! Byomkesh, eyes fixed on the ground, advanced slowly.

The lane was a blind alley, it did not go very far. Reaching its end, Byomkesh turned back, once more walking with his gaze intent on the earth. Then, upon reaching the side of Anadi Halder’s house, he suddenly bent down and picked something up.

I asked, “What did you find?”

He opened his fist to show me—a shiny, new key. I said, “A key! But to what?”

Byomkesh glanced once at the window above, slipped the key into his pocket, and said, “I can’t swear to it, but I suspect it’s the key to Anadi Halder’s almirah.”

“But—”

“I had a hunch we’d find something in the lane. Now come, let’s have some tea.”

“But, the almirah key—”

“Is at Anadi Halder’s waist. Yes, it is. But what’s to stop there being another key?”

“But how did the key end up in the lane?”

“Through the window. —Come.” Byomkesh took my hand and pulled me along.

We entered Srikanta Panthnibas and sat down at a table. A servant brought tea and biscuits. On questioning him, we learned that the hotel’s proprietor, Srikanta Goswami, was in a room nearby. Finishing our tea and biscuits, we entered the designated room.

It was Srikanta Babu’s office; a table in the center, a few chairs. Srikanta Babu was a middle-aged man, round-faced, clean-shaven; his countenance bore the tranquil air of a Vaishnava. He was having tea with last night’s leftover fowl cutlet, and seemed a little flustered at our sudden appearance.

Byomkesh said politely, “Excuse me, are you the proprietor, Srikanta Goswami?”

Goswami Mahashay’s mouth was full of fowl cutlet; he took a sip of tea and managed to swallow, then said, “Come in. You are—?”

Byomkesh replied, “We’ve come on a small matter. Perhaps you’ve heard—a murder took place last night in the house across the street?”

“Murder!” Srikanta Babu pushed aside his plate of fowl cutlet. “Who was murdered?”

“Anadi Halder, who lived at 172/2.”

Srikanta Babu’s eyes widened in astonishment. “Anadi Halder murdered! Is that so!”

“You knew him?”

“Of course I did. He lived on the second floor of the house opposite, was building a new house. He’d often come to my hotel for chop and cutlet. —Why, I saw him just last night.”

“Really! Where did you see him?”

“He was standing on his balcony, watching the fireworks on the street. Every time I looked out the window, I saw him standing there on the balcony.”

Byomkesh said, “Please tell me everything you saw—when, from where, what exactly. I am investigating Anadi Halder’s murder. My name is Byomkesh Bakshi.”

Srikanta Babu gazed at him in astonishment and said, “You’re Byomkesh Babu! What good fortune.” He called the servant and ordered tea and fowl cutlet for us. We…

--- Page 766 ---
Just now, even after having tea and biscuits, there was no escape.

Then Shrikant Babu said, “I live in two rooms on the second floor of my hotel; in the remaining three rooms, a few gentlemen keep a mess. Altogether, there are eleven of us. Of these, three have gone home for the Kali Puja holidays, the remaining eight are at home. After evening yesterday, the gentlemen in rooms 1 and 3 locked their doors and went out to see the city lights. In room 2, Jamini Babu and his two companions stayed in. They are very fond of playing dice. I play too. After seven in the evening, they called me, and the four of us sat down on Jamini Babu’s cot to play dice. Jamini Babu’s cot is right by the window facing the street. While playing, whenever I glanced outside, I saw Anadi Halder standing on the balcony, watching the fireworks. We played three rounds, and the game went on till about half past ten.”

“After that, you didn’t see Anadi Halder again?”

“No, after that we had dinner and went to bed. I didn’t see Anadi Halder again.”

“When did the gentlemen who went out to see the lights return?”

“Two of them came back at midnight; the others haven’t returned yet.”

“They are still out enjoying the lights.”

Shrikant Babu pursed his lips and let out a small sigh; perhaps he was silently expressing his regret about the inherent weaknesses of humankind.

Byomkesh chewed his cutlet absentmindedly for a while, then said, “Look, Anadi Halder’s body was found right there on the balcony, a bullet wound in his chest, the shot passing through his back. From that, it can be surmised that someone fired the gun at Anadi Halder from your hotel—”

Shrikant Babu’s eyes widened again. “From my hotel! What are you saying! Who would kill him?”

Byomkesh said, “It’s only a supposition. You’re saying that from seven in the evening, apart from the four of you, there was no one else on the second floor. Are you certain of this?”

Shrikant Babu replied, “I am certain that none of the other residents were there. However—wait. There is a servant who works on the second floor, he would know. Harish! Someone, call Harish!”

A short while later, Harish appeared—a middle-aged man in a printed fatua. Shrikant Babu asked, “Where were you yesterday evening?”

Harish replied, “Sahib, I was upstairs, sitting at the foot of the stairs the whole time. When you all started playing dice—”

“How long did you stay there?”

“Sahib, when Dhiru Babu and Manik Babu returned at midnight, I spread my blanket by the stairs and lay down. I didn’t go anywhere, sahib!”

Shrikant Babu looked at Byomkesh. Byomkesh asked Harish, “From the time the gentlemen started playing dice until midnight, you sat by the stairs the whole time, didn’t go anywhere, not even once?”

Harish replied, “Just once, for five minutes, I went downstairs to fetch a dokta for Jamini Babu.”

Shrikant Babu said, “Yes, yes, Jamini Babu did send him to fetch a dokta once.”

--- Page 767 ---
Adimriphu 465

‘Indeed.’
‘When was that? At what time?’
‘Sir, it must have been around nine at night.’
‘Hmm. From nine at night till midnight, did anyone come up to the second floor?’
‘No one came to the second floor, babu. Around ten o’clock, the tenant from the third floor came, but he didn’t stop on the second floor—he went straight up to the third.’
Byomkesh widened his eyes and looked at Shrikantbabu. He said, ‘Oh, I forgot to mention the third-floor tenant. There’s a small room up there, you could call it an attic. A gentleman has rented it. He doesn’t stay there permanently, doesn’t eat or drink there. But every morning and evening he comes, shuts himself inside, does who knows what, then locks up and leaves again. A rather peculiar fellow.’
‘What’s the gentleman’s name?’
‘His name? Wait, let me see—’ Shrikantbabu opened a bound ledger—‘Nityananda Ghoshal.’
‘Nityananda Ghoshal.’ Byomkesh glanced sidelong at me—‘If he comes twice a day, he must be a Calcutta man. How long has he been here?’
‘Almost six months. Pays rent regularly, never any trouble.’
‘What does he look like?’
‘Short and stout, round-faced.’
Byomkesh again shot me a sidelong glance and smiled faintly—‘Seems familiar—’ Then to Harish, he said, ‘Nityanandababu came around ten? Did you speak to him?’
Harish replied, ‘No, sir, he doesn’t talk. Just went straight up to the third floor with his bag.’
‘Bag?’
‘Yes, sir. He always carries a leather bag when he comes.’
‘Is that so! What sort of bag?’
‘A longish bag, sir; like the kind you keep a shehnai in.’
‘Like a clarinet case? Does the gentleman come to the third-floor room to practice his flute in solitude?’
‘Sir, I’ve never heard him play.’
Byomkesh remained lost in deep thought for a while. Then he looked up and asked, ‘When did he leave last night?’
‘After about an hour. He hurried down the stairs, looking very agitated.’
‘Oh!—Well, you may go now.’ When Harish had left with the empty cups and plates, Byomkesh said to Shrikantbabu, ‘I’d like to take a look at the upper floors. Do you mind?’
‘Not at all, why should I? Come along.’ Shrikantbabu led us upstairs.
On the second floor were five large rooms side by side, with a long veranda in front. The first two rooms at the top of the stairs belonged to Shrikantbabu. The doors were locked. Byomkesh asked, ‘Do you live alone?’
Shrikantbabu replied, ‘For now, yes. I’ve sent my wife and children to her father’s house. Times being what they are.’

--- Page 768 ---
466  
Byomkesh Samagra

‘You did well.’

The first room was locked; the gentlemen had not yet returned. In the second room, three gentlemen were present. One sat on the floor, polishing his shoes; the second was shaving; the third lay sprawled on the bed by the open window, reading the newspaper. Through the window, one could see Anadi Halder’s house directly across the street. I tried to peer into the balcony, but the dense cast-iron railings allowed nothing to be seen.

In the third room, Dhirubabu and Manikbabu had only just climbed onto their beds and were yawning with a snap of their fingers. Shrikanthababu said with a smile, ‘Well, are you awake now?’

Both men stretched their arms overhead and broke their stiffness.

Byomkesh did not question anyone. After inspecting the second floor, he turned towards the stairs. The same staircase led up to the third floor, and he began to ascend. Shrikanthababu and I followed behind.

On the third floor, there was only one room; the rest was an open terrace. The door to the room was locked.

Byomkesh asked Shrikanthababu, ‘Do you have the key?’

‘No. But—’ He took out a bunch of keys from his pocket and said, ‘See if any of these fit. It may not be proper to open a tenant’s room in his absence, but under the circumstances—’

Byomkesh tried a few keys from the bunch. The lock was cheap; it didn’t take much effort. With a click, it opened.

We entered the room. There was only one window, open to the street. The furnishings consisted of a bare wooden cot and an iron chair. Nothing else.

Without glancing around, Byomkesh went straight to the window and stood before it. Below, the broad street teemed with the flow of people and vehicles. Across the way, among the row of houses, was Anadi Halder’s balcony.

Gazing in that direction, Byomkesh spoke, almost to himself, ‘Last night, around eleven... boys were bursting crackers in the street... the air was filled with bangs and booms—Anadi Halder stood on his balcony, watching the fireworks... At that moment, would it have been so difficult to shoot him from this window? Even if the gunshot was heard, it would be mistaken for the sound of a firecracker.’

Shrikanthababu said, ‘That’s true. But is it so easy to smuggle a gun into the hotel, right under everyone’s nose?’

‘Your tenants come in carrying bags. A pistol or revolver could easily be brought in that way.’

‘But could a rifle or shotgun be brought in? Forgive me, I am a son of the Advaita lineage—I know nothing of guns, pistols, or such matters. Still, it seems to me, killing a man from this distance with a pistol or revolver is no easy task.’

Byomkesh only made a sound in his throat by way of reply. Then, casting his eyes around the bare room, he said, ‘Let’s go, I’ve troubled you enough—’ He broke off mid-sentence. I saw his gaze fixed on a spot on the wall.

Just opposite the window, near the ceiling, a patch of lime plaster had crumbled from the wall. Below it, on the floor, the fallen lime dust lay scattered. Byomkesh hurried over and examined the debris. ‘It looks freshly fallen. Shrikanthababu, is this room swept every day?’

--- Page 769 ---
Adimriphu 467

“Is it so?”
Shrikant Babu replied, “No. The room is never left unlocked—”
Byomkesh stepped aside, gazing upward.
“Can you tell when this plaster and lime fell off the wall?”
“No. All I can say is, when I rented out the room six months ago, the plaster was intact.”
“Hm. Ajit, hold the cot for me, let’s have a look—”
The two of us dragged the cot up against the wall; placing the iron chair atop it, Byomkesh climbed up. From there, he could reach the damaged spot on the wall. He felt around with his fingers, then came down holding a small object. It was a metallic piece, elongated like a pencil cap, with the spiral grooves of a rifle etched along its body.
A rifle bullet. Turning it over in his hand, Byomkesh mused, “How did this get here? When did it arrive? Did someone fire a rifle inside the room? Or—” Byomkesh looked toward the window, “If Anadi Halder fired from the balcony, aiming through the window, then the bullet could have struck the wall right there. Or else—”

Nine

We returned home late. From half past three in the night till half past eight in the morning, I had no sense of how the hours slipped by.
As soon as we got back, Byomkesh sat down with the newspaper. I tried several times to bring up the subject of Anadi, but he paid me no mind. Once, distracted, he glanced up and asked, “Does the sky really have a tangy smell?”
I fell silent, annoyed. In an ill-fated moment, I had bought Khoka a book of nonsense rhymes. Byomkesh had memorized it and now recited lines at the most inopportune moments.
Last night’s sleep had been cut short, so I made up for it in the afternoon. Over evening tea, Byomkesh himself broached the topic, “Kestobabu still hasn’t shown up. Seems everyone has gone to ground.”
I said, “When Kestobabu had a fishbone stuck in his throat, he came running. Now that it’s out, perhaps he’s gone into hiding.”
“That must be it. But if they don’t come, what can I do? The case is quite mysterious—”
“Haven’t you figured out who the murderer is yet?”
“No. But whoever it is, they planned it thoroughly, left nothing to chance. Kali Puja night, the sound of bombs bursting everywhere, and amidst that, a single gunshot. Such coordination doesn’t happen unless the murder was meticulously planned.”
“Who could plan something like that?”
“Who couldn’t? Everyone has a motive, everyone had the opportunity.”
“Who do you mean by everyone?”
“Let’s take them one by one. First, Nimai and Nitai. If the uncle’s foster sons inherit, the uncle’s property is lost to them—”

--- Page 770 ---
468  
Byomkesh Samagra

So, before Khuro could formally adopt the boy, he had to be removed. One of the Nimai-Nitai twins took up position on the roof of the Srikanta Hotel, lying in wait with a rifle. On the night of Kali Puja, between ten and eleven, a shot rang out. Khuro was finished—job done.

“So, the nephews committed the murder. There’s no reason to suspect anyone else.”

“There’s plenty of reason. Where did the bullet fired from the Srikanta Hotel’s third-floor room come from? It’s only an assumption that the shot was fired from that room, not an unavoidable conclusion. Think about it: where Anadi Haldar stood on the balcony, there was a door right behind him. If someone crept up from behind and shot him, the bullet would pass through his body, enter the third-floor room of the Srikanta Hotel through the window, and lodge in the wall.”

“That’s possible. But there’s a flaw at the very start. There was no one else in Anadi Haldar’s house, and the door was locked from the inside. Besides, another thing—did the bullet enter Anadi Haldar’s chest and exit through his back, or did it enter from the back and exit through the chest?”

“That won’t be known until the post-mortem is done. But whichever way the bullet entered, it wasn’t found on the balcony. From that, it’s not unreasonable to infer that Anadi Haldar was shot from inside the house.”

“All right, for the sake of argument, let’s accept that someone fired from inside the house. But who was it?”

“That’s the real question. Let’s see who had a motive. At first glance, Kesto Das doesn’t seem to have any. But the man is extremely cunning and sly—perhaps he rushed to me late at night just to cover his tracks. So he can’t be ruled out. The second is Nanibala Devi.”

“Nanibala!”

“Nanibala Devi is a formidable woman. Her affection for her adopted son is in no way less than true maternal love. She didn’t know there was a legal flaw in Prabhat’s adoption. So she might have thought that if Anadi Haldar could be removed, Prabhat would inherit the property, and no one else would try to harm him. I don’t know if you remember, but when Nanibala came to see me for the second time, I said that many would benefit from Anadi Haldar’s death. Perhaps that very thought took root in Nanibala’s heart.”

“But—a woman, firing a gun?”

“Why not? What’s so difficult about firing a gun? Just as pressing a harmonium key produces a note, pressing a trigger fires a bullet. Cooking kumro-chenchki is far more complicated.”

“But Nanibala was watching ‘Jai Ma Kali’.”

“She went to see ‘Jai Ma Kali’, but what proof is there that she was in the theatre the whole time? She didn’t know anyone there; perhaps after the film started, she slipped out of the dark hall, did what she had to do, and returned to her seat.”

“Where did she get a gun?”

“Oh, you fool! When people like Bontul Sardar are running around smuggling stolen guns, is there any shortage of firearms? For five rupees, you can rent a gun.”

--- Page 771 ---
Adimriphu 469

“Hm. And then?”

“And then, Prabhat. Prabhat, of course, knew he was not Anadi Halder’s foster son, but he might have had other motives. He owns his own shop; even if Anadi Halder died, he would not lack for food or clothing. He might have thought that after Anadi Halder’s death, his nephews would no longer try to harm him. Perhaps, to save his own life from the nephews, he killed Anadi Halder.”

“You consider that a strong motive?”

“It may not be a very strong motive, but a mountain can grow from a molehill. Prabhat wanted to marry a girl, and Anadi Halder broke off the match. That’s no small motive either.”

I smiled. Byomkesh said, “Don’t laugh. What seems trivial to you may be a mountain to someone else. You’ve never been in love, you don’t know what love is. For love, a man can kill, can go to the gallows, can lose everything—”

“All right, all right, I admit, Prabhat could be the murderer too.”

“But there’s one thing. Prabhat was in his shop the whole time, and there was a Gurkha guard at the door. If his alibi is solid—”

“It’s likely to be solid. Prabhat wouldn’t tell a lie that could be so easily exposed. Go on.”

“Then there’s Napa.” Byomkesh took out the key he’d found in the pocket, turning it over and over in his hand. “Two things we need to know for certain: is this Anadi Halder’s key, and who threw it into the alley?”

I said, “So your suspicion falls on Napa, doesn’t it? Let’s suppose this is Anadi Halder’s almirah key and Napa threw it into the alley. What does that prove?”

“It may prove nothing, but it does cast suspicion on Napa. There might have been a lot of cash in the almirah—”

This was a new possibility. I asked, “So where do we stand? Who’s the culprit? Nimai-Nitai? Kestobabu? Nanibala? Prabhat? Napa? Or someone else?”

“There could be one more.”

“Who else?”

“Batul Sadri.”

“Batul! Why would he want to kill Anadi Halder?”

“Extorting protection money is Batul’s profession. Anadi Halder had stopped paying. If, seeing him, the others stopped paying too? That’s why Anadi Halder needed to be punished—let everyone see the consequences and fall in line.”

Putiram came in and took away the tea cups. Byomkesh, lighting a cigarette, said, “A Dom is blind in the bamboo grove. Whom to keep, whom to cast aside—like Sriradhi and Chandravali.”

We both smoked in silence. When the clock struck a quarter past four, there was a knock at the door.

I opened it to find Kestobabu. At last, Kestobabu had arrived. But this was not the frightened, drunken Kestobabu of the morning—this was a brisk, smart Kestobabu. He wore freshly pressed clothes, and a self-assured, gentle smile played on his toothy face. The man seemed completely transformed.

He took the chair opposite Byomkesh. Byomkesh, still absorbed in examining the key in his hand, looked up and asked, “Any news? Did the police come?”

--- Page 772 ---
470  
Byomkesh Samagra

Kestobabu examined the key, but not a flicker of reaction crossed his face.

In response to the question, he clicked his tongue and said, “He came at eleven o’clock. What a kingdom of Ram we live in.”

Byomkesh pocketed the key and asked, “And then what happened?”

“What else? The daroga threatened everyone, opened Anadi’s almirah, found a bundle of notes and stuffed them into his pocket, then took the corpse away and left.”

Byomkesh sat silent for a while, then asked, “Didn’t he question any of you?”

“He only asked where we were last night, nothing else. Didn’t even bother to write a single line. Came in with a bang, left with a bang.”

Byomkesh sighed and said, “Well, Anadi Halder has found his peace. Who killed him will never be known, and I doubt we’ll even get a post-mortem report. Perhaps it’s for the best—you all won’t have to suffer.”

Kestobabu said, “Those who were meant to benefit have benefited—what good has it done me, Byomkeshbabu? I won’t be able to stay there much longer.”

“Why?”

“Nonibala is after me, wants to throw me out. Now that Anadi is gone, the woman’s boldness has grown. Just see—when I was leaving, I asked her for a cup of tea. She snapped at me, said, ‘No tea now, go have tea at the shop.’”

After a moment’s silence, Byomkesh asked, “So what do you plan to do now?”

“I’ll have to make some arrangement. At this age, work won’t suit me anymore.” With that, Kestobabu bared two rows of teeth in a grin.

Byomkesh said, “You’re not so old—still fit to work.”

“I’ve lost the habit, Byomkeshbabu. Ha ha—well, I’ll take your leave for today.”

He rose to go.

“Sit, sit, have some tea before you leave.”

Kestobabu sat back down. Byomkesh called out to Putiram to bring tea and some snacks.

Kestobabu said cheerfully, “You’re a gentleman, you understand compassion. Does everyone? The world is selfish—unless you squeeze their throats, no one gives you anything. Anadi has left me completely high and dry—” He glanced sidelong at Byomkesh. “Tea is a fine thing, but you know, I’ve picked up a bad habit—by evening, tea alone doesn’t intoxicate me anymore.” He laughed, a dry, throaty laugh.

Byomkesh ignored the hint. “Did anyone else come besides the police? Nimai, Nitai?”

Kestobabu replied, “Nimai and Nitai didn’t come. But Gurudutt Singh showed up and made quite a racket.”

“Gurudutt Singh, the contractor—”

“Yes. He arrived just after the police left. Started shouting, ‘I did fifty thousand rupees’ worth of work, got only thirty thousand, today Anadi Halder was to give me ten thousand, now he’s dead—who’ll pay me now?’ I said, ‘Sir, how should we know who’ll pay you? Go to Anadi’s heirs, go to the thana, go to the court, but leave here.’”

--- Page 773 ---
Adimriphu 471

...to go. Does he want to leave? With great difficulty, I managed to bid him farewell.'

Byomkesh, lost in thought, said, 'Anadi Halder told the contractor he would give him ten thousand rupees today... Yesterday was a bank holiday, which means he must have withdrawn the money from the bank the day before yesterday, that is—'

Kestobabu interjected, 'From the bank?'

'Yes, where else would he get that much money if not from the bank?'

Kestobabu changed his tone and said, 'Of course, of course. I know nothing of these matters! Just a humble trader, ha ha—'

Byomkesh said, 'Let that be. You are a man of the house, you know the pulse of the family—can you not guess who committed the murder?'

Kestobabu kept his head bowed for a while, then looked up. 'Let me tell you the truth—no one from the family did this.'

'You have no suspicions about anyone?'

'Suspicions? I suspect everyone, but I cannot believe it. This is the work of those two nephews. Think about it—what would the family gain by killing Anadi? All of them lived off Anadi’s bounty. Now things have come to such a pass that in a couple of days, there won’t even be food on the stove.'

'Why wouldn’t there be food? Nripen was a salaried servant—he’ll find work elsewhere. And Prabhat? He has his shop.'

'Will the shop remain? The nephews will file a suit and seize it.'

'Even if they do, they won’t starve. Prabhat, if nothing else, can work as a stationer and feed himself.'

'Stationer’s work!' Kestobabu shot a sharp glance at Byomkesh and stared at him.

'You didn’t know? Prabhat knows the stationer’s trade—he learned it as a boy, working in a stationer’s shop.'

Putiram arrived with tea and refreshments. Kestobabu picked up a plate and busied himself with the food, but his eyes remained deeply thoughtful. Only once did he mutter under his breath, 'How strange! I had no idea.'

Byomkesh said, 'What’s so strange about not knowing? Stationer’s work isn’t such a noble profession that one would go about beating the drums about it.'

Kestobabu looked up with sly eyes and said, 'That’s true.'

When the meal was over, Byomkesh offered him a cigarette and said, 'This morning, you said you knew all of Anadi Halder’s secrets, that if you wished, you could have him swinging from the gallows—'

Kestobabu sprang to his feet. 'Secrets! No, no, how would I know Anadi’s secrets? What does it matter what I said under the influence of drink? Well, I’ll be going now, thank you very much.' He moved toward the door.

Byomkesh laughed. 'Listen, Kestobabu—' He stopped at the door and turned. 'If you don’t wish to speak of secrets, don’t. I’m not overly interested. But there’s no harm in having dinner here tonight, is there? You might find it difficult to get a meal at your place tonight—'

Kestobabu eagerly took two steps forward. 'Dinner—!'

'Yes. For your sake, we might even arrange for a little liquid refreshment.'

'Are you serious? So you indulge as well! Provided Modda Didimoni doesn’t find out—'

--- Page 774 ---
“How is it? Yes, yes. Tell me, what time should I come?”

“Come after evening. I may have to step out for a while. But don’t worry, if I’m late returning, the servant will seat you.”

“Very well, I’ll come after evening.” With a tusked, grotesque grin, he took his leave.

Byomkesh flicked his eyes at me and said, “Kesto Das won’t say a word to us in broad daylight. —Ajit, you go to the liquor shop and buy a bottle of five-star. Nasik whisky will do. Meanwhile, I’ll instruct Putiram.”

Ten

At five o’clock, the two of us set out.

Putiram had been briefed. On the table in the sitting room, a bottle, corkscrew, and glass had been arranged. At a knock on the outer door, Putiram would appear and open it, and with a face like a startled bhetki fish, he’d say, “Come in, babu, the masters have stepped out, they’ll be back soon.” He’d seat the bhetki-faced guest at the table, bring him a plate of fried eggs, and then vanish himself. After that—

Stepping onto the footpath, I asked, “Where are we headed?”

Byomkesh smiled and said, “No fixed destination. Kesto Das will come, polish off the bottle, and then we’ll return.”

“I see. But what shall we do until then?”

“Until then, let’s take the air at Gol Dighi.”

We went to Gol Dighi and began to circle it. Not much conversation passed between us; at one point, Byomkesh remarked, “Kesto Das didn’t drop the key in the alley.”

After a while, I noticed a crowd entering the University Institute—there must be some event. We’d grown weary from our aimless wandering, so I said to Byomkesh, “Come on, let’s see what’s happening there.”

Byomkesh agreed, “Let’s go. Probably a memorial gathering for some famous person.”

As we entered the University Institute, we ran into Indubabu. He was from the cinema world, and a musician as well—he’d come to attend the event. Byomkesh’s guess was correct: the film fraternity was mourning the death of one of their stalwarts with music and dance. I introduced Byomkesh to Indubabu. He took us to a row near the front and seated us, then sat down beside us himself.

On stage, I recognized a few familiar faces from the theatre, and some I didn’t know. The chairman was an elderly, silver-haired actor.

Among those on stage, one woman’s face drew my particular attention. Unfamiliar, not beautiful, but striking. Not slender, but full-figured; her complexion could be called fair, and a mass of hair tumbled in curls at her neck. What is called sexual allure—this young woman possessed it in abundance. A burly young man sat pressed close beside her, occasionally leaning in to whisper in her ear.

--- Page 775 ---
Adimriphu 473

The song that was playing came to an end. The president, holding a slip of paper, stood up and announced, “Now Kumari Shiuli Majumdar will sing—Kotha Jao Phire Chao Durer Pathik.”

The young woman I had been observing was indeed named Shiuli Majumdar. With a composed, measured step, she came forward and took her seat. The burly young man sat beside her with his tabla. The song began. Her voice was sweet, rounded, bewitchingly melodious. I closed my eyes and listened. Then, jolted by Byomkesh’s elbow, my reverie was broken. Byomkesh whispered in my ear, “Hey, look to your left.”

I turned my gaze cautiously to the left. Except for a few empty chairs, Prabhat was sitting in the front row. His face bore an absorbed, meditative expression, his unwavering gaze fixed on the singer. Prabhat, it seemed, had not noticed us; had he done so, he could not have appeared so engrossed. I turned to glance at Byomkesh—he was listening to the song with a faint, crooked smile on his lips.

A flash of lightning seemed to streak through my mind. Shiuli Majumdar—the very woman Prabhat had wished to marry—was this her?...

Shiuli Majumdar’s song ended. After that, a few others performed. I noticed that once Shiuli’s song was over, Prabhat quietly slipped out.

Before the gathering concluded, we too rose to leave. Indubabu accompanied us to the door. Byomkesh asked him, “That girl named Shiuli Majumdar—she sings beautifully! Is she from the movies?”

Indubabu replied, “No, not yet. But now that she’s got Gadananda, it won’t be long.”

“Gadananda?”

“The man who was playing the tabla. He’s a film agent. His profession is to teach music to girls from respectable families, but if he finds a suitable one, he drags her into the movies.”

“Is that so! Is his real name Gadananda?”

“His name is Jagadananda. In the film world, everyone calls him Gadananda. He’s ruined many a girl.”

“Do you know Shiuli’s father’s name?”

“I think I heard it—yes, Dayalhari Majumdar. Recently arrived from East Bengal.”

We returned home at seven o’clock.

The door was ajar; entering, we found Kestobabu kneeling on the takhtposh, his right index finger transformed into a gun, aiming steadily at the corner of the room. The liquor bottle lay on its side, empty. Kestobabu did not notice our arrival; he fired at the upper corner of the room—“Gudum—Phiss.”

Of course, he made the sound with his mouth.

Byomkesh asked, “Kestobabu, what’s going on?”

Kestobabu replied, “Shh, the bird will fly away. —Gudum—Phiss.”

Byomkesh burst out laughing, “Oh, you’re hunting birds. So, how many have you bagged?”

Kestobabu lowered his gun-hand and replied matter-of-factly, “I’ve shot three hotel doves.” A faint smile of satisfaction played on his slack face.

Byomkesh said, “Very good, very good. But why ‘Gudum—Phiss’? I understand ‘Gudum’ for the gunshot, but what’s ‘Phiss’?”

Kestobabu replied, “Don’t you get it? ‘Gudum’ is the sound of the gun, and ‘Phiss’ is the bird’s soul leaving its body.”

--- Page 776 ---
