Byomkesh Samagra

“No, I haven’t heard. What happened?”  
“Someone stabbed him last night.”  
In a tone of indifference, Prabhat said, “Oh—”  
Byomkesh said, “Let that be. Surely you know that Nimai Nitai has filed a complaint against Dayalhari Babu over a promissory note for five thousand rupees.”  
Prabhat’s face twisted with disgust. He said, “I know. But let’s leave that too, Byomkesh Babu. My heart is poisoned from witnessing the inhumanity of men. I came to tell you that I can no longer bear to stay here. I’ll be leaving soon.”  
“What? Where will you go?”  
“I haven’t decided yet. I might return to Patna. Wherever I go, I’ll manage to scrape by. But no more Calcutta.”  
“But—your shop?”  
“I’ll sell it—” Prabhat’s face grew pained. Turning to me, he said, “Ajit Babu, do you know anyone who might want to buy a bookshop? I’m not asking for much. If I get three thousand—even two and a half—I’ll sell it.”  
I began to ponder—who among my acquaintances could possibly want to buy a bookshop?  
Suddenly, Byomkesh said something quite unexpected. “We could buy it. Ajit and I have been discussing opening a bookshop for some time. Ajit is a writer himself, he could run it. If we could get your shop, that would be ideal.”  
A flicker of life returned to Prabhat’s face. He said, “You would take it? What could be better? If you buy it, I’ll have no regrets in selling. Then—”  
Byomkesh asked, “How much stock do you have in your shop?”  
Prabhat replied, “I can’t say without checking the accounts, but it won’t be less than four thousand rupees’ worth.”  
“Very well. Tomorrow morning, we’ll come and look over your books and accounts. There’s no mortgage on the shop, is there?”  
“No, sir.”  
“Then it’s settled. Tomorrow we’ll review your papers, tally the stock. You’ll get whatever is the fair price. But one thing—you must hand over possession to us on the morning of August 15th. We want to begin business at the dawn of independence.”  
“So be it. You can have it whenever you wish. I’ll take my leave now, I must get the papers in order.”  
“All right. By the way, is Nripen Babu still with you?”  
“Yes. I’ve already told him I won’t be keeping the shop. He’s looking for another job—he’ll leave as soon as he finds one. Will you keep him on?”  
“We might. Could you send him over here once?”  
“I’ll send him as soon as I get to the shop. Well then, namaskar.”  
No sooner had Prabhat stepped outside than Byomkesh sprang up and caught hold of Bikash, and in a rapid, hushed tone, whispered something in his ear while pressing a few notes into his hand. I only caught the last words—“Remember, until midnight tonight, you’re not to take a minute’s rest.”  
Bikash nodded firmly once, then shot out the door like an arrow released from the bowstring.

--- Page 797 ---
Adimriphu 495

When the room was empty, I asked, “What’s going on?”

Byomkesh lit a cigarette, stretched out in his chair, exhaled a plume of smoke, and said, “A tremendous opportunity has come our way, Ajit. We shouldn’t let it slip by.”

“What opportunity are you talking about?”

Byomkesh replied, “Take this bookshop, for example. If we can get it, should we let it go? The book trade is a profitable business; you’ll also find work to your liking. These days, writing books alone leads nowhere. You see, the clever writers among you have quietly stepped into business, and now they’re living comfortably, with bread and butter aplenty.”

It was true. There was money in the book trade, especially if one could corner the market for school textbooks. Still, I raised a token objection, “But in these troubled times, is it wise to suddenly raise such a large sum?”

He said, “If we split it between the two of us, it won’t be a burden. You’ll be the working partner, and I—the sleeping partner.”

Half an hour later, Nripen arrived. He said, “Prabhat Babu sent me. Did you call for me?”

“Yes, sit in that chair.” Byomkesh fixed him with a hard gaze for a moment, then said, “I know all about your exploits. Ramesh Mallik is my friend.”

Napa started, turning to stone. Byomkesh continued, “You made a duplicate key for Anadi Haldar’s almirah. There was a lot of money in that almirah—where did it go? If I inform the police, they’ll want to know. What will you tell them?”

Napa licked his lips. Byomkesh said, “I can keep this from the police—if you do something for me.”

A broken, trembling voice emerged from Napa’s throat, “What do you want?”

“You’ll have to make another key.”

Sixteen

Poet Hemchandra once wrote, ‘Dawn breaks on the thirty-first night of August.’ How many years have passed since then; not a soul remains alive who remembers that first day of municipal self-rule. Now, another August night has dawned. Once again, there are celebrations in every home, once again, the tenants and the widows and the courtesans make merry in their quarters. Only now, the stage is broader, stretching from the seas to the Himalayas, across all of Bharatvarsha.

That morning, as I awoke, I sat down to ponder. Now that India is free, how much credit do I deserve? I didn’t even wave a flag to help. (Byomkesh had gone to Delhi and worked for seven months.) There are hundreds of thousands like me who did nothing, yet will enjoy the fruits of freedom. One man pulls the boat’s rope, ten cross the river. If this is the way of the world, then where is the connection between action and its reward?

I shared my spiritual dilemma with Byomkesh. He said, “We’ve gained freedom through others’ efforts, but we must make it meaningful through our own. The work isn’t finished yet.”

At half past nine, Byomkesh said, “Come, let’s go. We’ll stop by Prabhat’s house, then head to his shop.”

I asked, “Why do we need to go to Prabhat’s house?”

--- Page 798 ---
Byomkesh smiled faintly and said, “I have a strong desire to see Nanibala Devi.”

On the ground floor of the Bowbazar house, the inevitable Shasthibabu sat enthroned, hookah in hand. At the sight of us, he started and removed the pipe from his lips. Byomkesh asked in his sweetest voice, “No more trouble with the upstairs folks these days, I hope?”

Shasthibabu looked at us with anxious eyes and replied, “No—well—no, there’s never been any trouble on my part. I’m an old man, I keep to myself, don’t meddle in anyone’s affairs—”

Byomkesh smiled. We climbed the stairs.

A maid opened the door for us. Seeing two unfamiliar men, she stepped aside, and we entered. The room that once contained nothing but a hard wooden bench was now furnished with several comfortable chairs; a Ravi Varma painting adorned the wall. Nanibala Devi sat in a large chair, spectacles perched on her nose, perusing a well-known English weekly magazine; a pencil in her hand.

Nanibala Devi’s attire was striking. Over a glossy jute sari, she wore a blouse embroidered with vines and leaves. Both arms were adorned with thick bangles and bracelets—gold, perhaps, though gilt was not out of the question. Her face bore the dignified gravity of a householder. There could be no doubt that Nanibala, freed from the shadow of Anadi Halder, had reclaimed her true self.

Nanibala seemed a little flustered at the sight of us, but then she closed the harmonium lid and greeted us, “Come in, come in. How are you?—Chinibas, bring two cups of tea. Byomkeshbabu, a little sweet for you—?”

“No, no, nothing for us. We came looking for Prabhatbabu.”

“Prabhat? He left for the shop at eight. —Won’t you sit for a while?”

We sat, barely touching the edge of the chairs. Not just the maid, but a servant named Chinibas was present as well; perhaps even a cook had been hired. Such sudden prosperity is rarely seen unless Jupiter’s favor is at its peak.

Byomkesh asked, “What are you working on?”

Nanibala replied, “Breaking the crossword puzzle. You know, I won the first prize—twenty-one thousand rupees.” The notes of the harmonium seemed to echo in her voice.

So the jewelry wasn’t gilt after all. We, too, had tried our hand at solving crosswords for a while, but luck was not on our side; we never managed to crack the puzzles.

Offering his congratulations, Byomkesh said, “Then we’ll take our leave for today. Has Nripenbabu gone to the shop?”

Nanibala replied in a displeased tone, “No. I don’t know what’s come over him since yesterday—he’s locked himself in his room. Who knows what he’s up to, not even eating properly, not going to the shop—can’t rely on him anymore.”

We took our leave. As we walked down the street, Byomkesh said, “I don’t think Nanibala knows that Prabhat is selling the shop.”

Arriving in front of the shop, Byomkesh glanced around and then said, “You go on inside. I’ll be along. There’s a nail in my shoe.”

Across the street, pressed against the wall of Goldighi, a young cobbler sat with his tools. Byomkesh went over to him to have his shoe repaired. I entered the shop.

Prabhat was shuffling through the account books. He looked up and said, “Ah! Byomkeshbabu didn’t come?”

--- Page 799 ---
Adimriphu 497

“He’s coming. Have you finished the calculations?”
“Yes. Here, take a look.”
I sat down to examine the accounts. After a while, Byomkesh joined us; by the time we finished checking the figures, it was already afternoon. We rose. Byomkesh said, “We’ll pay three thousand rupees. Tomorrow morning at eight you’ll receive the cheque, and you must hand over possession immediately.”
“Yes, sir.”

That afternoon, Byomkesh said, “Why don’t you telephone Indubabu, see if there’s any recent news of Gadananda?”
I said, “But Gadananda has run away—how would Indubabu know where to find him?”
Byomkesh replied, “Gadananda has eloped with Shiuli, but he isn’t a fugitive. Shiuli is of age; if she’s left her father’s house with someone, it’s not a criminal matter. Most likely, Gadananda has taken her to his own home.”
“All right, let me see—”
I called Indubabu. Hearing my question, he said, “Of course I know about Gadananda. The whole cinema world is abuzz with him right now. Didn’t I mention it to you the other day? Gadananda ran off with Shiuli, then married her at the Registry Office. That makes it three times for Gadananda.”
“Three times! Three times what?”
“Three marriages.”
“You don’t say—he had two other wives?”
“Not anymore. The first wife was very beautiful, but she didn’t do well in cinema; her looks didn’t come out well on camera. Suddenly, one day, she died of heart failure. Then Gadananda lured another girl and married her. This one acted fairly well, but she had no personality—turned out she couldn’t play the heroine’s part. That didn’t last long either.”
“What a disaster! Do you think Gadananda—did something to his wives—eh!”
“God knows. Shiuli at least has a good singing voice, that’s her only saving grace.”
I relayed the news to Byomkesh. He began to chuckle softly to himself, then said, “I’d like to know Gadananda’s lineage. Such things don’t happen in a single generation.”
Evening descended. The city, adorned for Deepavali, evoked memories of another night of lights. In every home, every shop, the thunderous voices of the radio drowned out all other sounds. Every ear was turned toward Delhi. Within a few hours, the proclamation of independence would be made there.
At seven, as we finished our meal, Nupen arrived, handed Byomkesh a gleaming key at the door, and vanished like Aladdin’s genie.
At ten, we finished dinner.
At half past eleven, Byomkesh said to Putiram, “We’re going out now, not sure when we’ll return. Stay awake. Prepare a brazier with charcoal in another room. When we come back, light the fire.”
Putiram replied, “Yes, sir,” and left. I asked, “What’s the charcoal fire for—”

--- Page 800 ---
498  
Byomkesh Samagra

“Will it be so?”  
He replied, “The past must be reduced to ashes.”  
A little before midnight, we stepped out. Conch shells were sounding from house to house—  
But the shops around Gol Dighi were all closed. The shopkeepers, I suppose, had retreated to their homes, clutching their radio sets. At such a late hour, the streets in this part of town had grown deserted.  
Beneath the shadow of a lamppost, a man was standing, smoking a bidi. As we drew near, he stepped forward. I saw that it was Bikash.  
Byomkesh said, “Any news?”  
Bikash replied, “No. Prabhat Babu closed his shop and left at half past nine.”  
“Did he have anything in his hands?”  
“No.”  
“No one else came after that?”  
“No.”  
“All right, then. Let’s go.”  
The three of us crossed the street and stopped in front of Prabhat’s shop. Byomkesh took out a key and unlocked the door; the lock yielded easily. Then, handing the key to Bikash, Byomkesh said, “The two of us are going inside. You lock up after us. I can’t say how long you’ll have to wait. Stay just as you were. If anyone opens the door and comes in, you needn’t do a thing.”  
“All right, sir.”  
We entered the dark shop. Byomkesh had an electric torch in his pocket, which he switched on and swept around the room. The rows of books seemed to bare their teeth and grin silently. We went into the back room and sat on the edge of the low cot, leaving the middle door half open. Byomkesh said, “There are no books in this room. I doubt anyone will come in here.”  
I asked, “Byomkesh, may I know what exactly we’re doing in Prabhat’s shop at midnight?”  
Byomkesh whispered in my ear, “Creeping, creeping, creeping along—Uncle Ghost has drawn his sword.”  

A bookshop has its own scent, the smell of new books. Usually, one doesn’t notice it, but if you’re shut inside a shop late at night, you begin to feel it, bit by bit. It’s a sharp smell, it tickles the nose, makes you want to sneeze.  
On top of that, there was the carbon dioxide from our own breathing. After waiting for about an hour, I felt the air in the room growing heavy. The heat made me restless.  
I said, “Byomkesh—”  
Byomkesh gripped my hand in a vise, and a muffled hiss escaped his throat, “Sss—.”  
Another sound reached our ears—someone was unlocking the door with a key. The door opened a crack, and the light from outside slowly spread like a thick curtain. A shadowy figure slipped inside and closed the door behind them. We watched, breathless, from within the little room.  
Suddenly, in the middle of the shop, a torch flashed on. The beam of light swept upward.

--- Page 801 ---
Adimriphu 499

The beam, like a searchlight, swept up along the wall. The man behind the torch was nowhere to be seen.

Torch in hand, the man vaulted up onto the counter. Silently, we crept to the doorway of the cubicle and peered in. The torch’s beam fell upon the highest shelf of books. The man reached out and pulled down a book—large and thick, much like a ‘Chalantika’. Then he took out another, and another. In this way, he gathered five books, then leapt down from the counter; placing the burning torch atop it, he began stuffing the books into a market bag.

Just as he finished packing the books into the bag, Byomkesh stepped forward and laid a hand on the man’s shoulder, saying, “Give me the bag.”

A rasping, saw-like gasp escaped the man’s throat. Then Byomkesh shone his own torch directly onto the man’s face.

Though fear and astonishment had contorted his features, there was no mistaking him—it was Prabhat.

The whites of his eyes showed starkly. He stared for a minute, then, in a stunned voice, said, “Byomkesh-babu!”

“Yes, it’s me. And Ajit. Give me the bag.”

Prabhat hesitated a moment, then handed the bag to Byomkesh.

Byomkesh passed the bag to me. “Ajit, keep this. These books are very valuable. —Prabhat-babu, now let’s go.”

Prabhat stared a few moments longer, then asked, “Where are we going? To the police station?”

Byomkesh replied, “No, for now, to my house. First, we must see to these books.”

The three of us stepped out of the shop. At Byomkesh’s signal, Prabhat locked the door. Turning, I saw Bikash had silently appeared behind us. Byomkesh said, “Bikash-babu, many thanks. You may go now. Please come to my house tomorrow morning.”

“Yes, sir—” Bikash melted away into the night. Byomkesh and I took Prabhat between us and set off for home.

Seventeen

The three of us sat in our baithak-khana. Prabhat and I took the chairs; Byomkesh sat cross-legged on the taktaposh, the bag of books beside him. It was nearly two in the morning; outside, the city’s murmur had faded into silence.

Byomkesh’s face was grave, tinged with melancholy. He glanced up at Prabhat with a look of stern reproach; but Prabhat’s face bore no trace of guilt. The shock and fear that had overwhelmed him at the moment of his capture had vanished. Now he seemed entirely composed, ready for whatever might come.

Byomkesh drew the books out of the bag, one by one. Bound in brown board, they were not much to look at from the outside. But when Byomkesh opened their pages, a sudden thrill nearly took my breath away. Every single page of every book was a one-hundred-rupee note.

Byomkesh examined each book in turn, then set them aside, and asked Prabhat,

--- Page 802 ---
'How much are all the books worth in total?'

Prabhat replied, 'About two lakhs. I have spent some of it.'

'Apart from the money you gave Dayalhari Majumdar, did you spend anything else?'

A flicker of surprise flashed in Prabhat’s eyes; the question of how Byomkesh knew so much seemed to peer out from his gaze. But he asked nothing, merely said, 'There were some other expenses, altogether fourteen or fifteen thousand.'

Byomkesh then placed his hand upon the books and said in a calm voice, 'Prabhatbabu, was it for these that you killed Anadi Halder?'

Prabhat shook his head firmly. 'No, Byomkeshbabu.'

'Then will you tell me why you did it?'

Prabhat opened his mouth as if to speak, then closed it again without a word.

Byomkesh said, 'If you won’t say it, then I shall. —Anadi Halder broke off your engagement to Shiuli and wanted to marry her himself. That was the reason—am I right?'

For a while, Prabhat sat with his head buried in his chest. When he finally looked up, the veins in his temples stood out. The savagery I had noticed in the set of his teeth became starkly visible as he spoke, his voice choked, 'Yes. Anadi Halder bribed Shiuli’s father with five thousand rupees to make him agree—' He broke off there, sitting in silence, as if swelling with the fire within.

Byomkesh said, 'So my guess was correct. —But why did you try to kill Kestobabu?'

Forgetting his anger, Prabhat looked at Byomkesh in astonishment. 'What! Kestobabu? I know nothing about him!'

Byomkesh pierced Prabhat with a gaze bristling with suspicion. 'You didn’t kill Kesto Das?'

Prabhat replied, 'No, Byomkeshbabu. In the past eight months, Kestobabu took eight thousand rupees from me. When I heard he was dead, I was glad; but I did not kill him. Believe me, if I had, I would not deny it to you now.'

Byomkesh’s face gradually brightened, the gloom that had shrouded his mind like a mist seemed to lift. He said, 'But then, who killed Kesto Das?'

'I don’t know. But—' Prabhat hesitated.

'But?'

Prabhat said a little awkwardly, 'Ten or twelve days ago, Batul Sardar came to see me. You probably don’t know Batul—'

'I know him well. In fact, I know the nature of your connection with him too. Go on.'

'Batul started asking me about Kestobabu; who he was, what he knew about Anadibabu’s death, all that. I told Batul everything. After that—'

Byomkesh broke into a smile and said, 'Ah, now I understand. Kesto Das’s hunger for money was not satisfied by blackmailing you, so he tried to blackmail Batul as well. Greed destroys the weaver.' Byomkesh called out, 'Putiram!'

Putiram came and stood near the inner doorway. Byomkesh said, 'Putiram, can you bring three cups of tea?'

--- Page 803 ---
Adimriphu 501

Putiram said, “Sir, there’s no milk.”

Byomkesh replied, “No matter. Brew the tea with ginger, then. And have you set the coal tongs right?”

“Yes, sir.”

“Good. Now you may light the fire.”

When Putiram had left, Byomkesh said, “Prabhatbabu, your mother—Nonibala Devi—she doesn’t know anything, does she?”

“No, sir.” Prabhat gazed at Byomkesh for a moment, his eyes wide with a mixture of awe and confusion, then said, “Have you found out everything, Byomkeshbabu?”

Byomkesh paused for a moment before replying, “I believe I have. But one can never be sure—there may be some errors. For instance, I had placed the blame for Kesto Das’s death upon your shoulders. I should have realized, the knife is not your weapon.”

I said, “Byomkesh, tell us how you figured it all out, will you? I still don’t understand a thing.”

Byomkesh said, “Very well, I’ll tell you. I had understood who killed Anadi Halder even before I left for Patna. But at the time I thought, since no one seems to care about Anadi Halder’s death, why should I trouble myself over it? But when I returned and saw that Kesto Das had also been murdered, I could no longer remain silent. A man who solves all his problems by killing others must be stopped. However, now I see I was mistaken—Prabhatbabu did not kill Kesto Das. I am relieved of a grave duty. Now, listen to the story. Prabhatbabu, if I make any mistakes, please correct me.”

Byomkesh began to recount the tale of Anadi Halder. With astonishment, I realized today’s situation was entirely new. Byomkesh was sitting in his own baithak-khana, narrating the story of the murder to the killer himself, as if to a friend—such a thing had never happened before.

“During the last war, Anadi Halder made a great deal of money on the black market. Perhaps two and a half, maybe three lakhs. Prabhatbabu, how many books did you bind?”

Prabhat replied, “Two. Each had four hundred notes.”

“So, two lakh forty thousand. Let us assume Anadi Halder made close to three lakhs in black money. The question arose—where would he keep it? He couldn’t deposit it in a bank, or the income tax bloodhounds would be at his throat. Anadi Halder devised a plan.

“Anadi Halder was as cunning as he was unscrupulous. Over the years, many tricks have been invented to outwit the income tax men—I do not know them all. But the scheme Anadi Halder devised was not a bad one. First, he converted all the money into hundred-rupee notes. He didn’t keep it all in one place; some in Calcutta, some in Delhi, some in Patna—so as not to arouse suspicion.

“There may have been other reasons for going to Patna. In any case, there he sought out a bookbinder; Prabhatbabu came to his house to bind books. Seeing a young Bengali like Prabhatbabu, Anadi Halder took a liking to him. He had been looking for just such a bookbinder, so he told Prabhatbabu the real story; he also said he wished to adopt him as his heir. The reason for this was, after learning such a great secret, he could not let Prabhatbabu out of his sight.

“Prabhatbabu bound the books. The proposal to adopt him as heir was settled. Anadi Halder—”

--- Page 804 ---
Byomkesh Samagra

Prabhat and Nanibala Devi returned to Calcutta. The notebooks, along with the other books, were placed in the cupboard. A steel almirah, with its only key always hanging from Anadi Halder’s waist. So, there was no possibility of anyone opening the cupboard. Even if, by some means, someone did open it, what would they see? Just a few books—Mahabharata, Ramayana, and the like. There was very little money. No one would think to open the books and examine their pages. Besides, to throw outsiders off the scent, several thousand rupees were also kept in the bank.

There were two other men living in Anadi Halder’s Calcutta house—Kesto Das and Nripen. Nripen was his secretary. Anadi Halder was not well-educated, so he had kept Nripen to manage his business affairs. And Kesto Das had forced himself onto Anadi’s shoulders. Kesto Das was Anadi Halder’s childhood friend, knew of many of Anadi’s misdeeds, and had been his accomplice in quite a few of them.

At the age of seventeen or eighteen, Anadi Halder had beaten his own father so severely that the old man died the very next day. The seed of patricide was in Anadi’s blood. In the world of living beings, the relationship between father and son is one of primal enmity; that ancient savagery was in Anadi Halder’s veins. After murdering his father, he vanished. The relatives, fearful of scandal, hushed up the affair.

Much later, Anadi met Kesto Das again; together, they went to rob a Marwari’s house. Anadi murdered the Marwari and fled with the money; Kesto Das got nothing from the loot.

Now, twenty years later, Kesto Das reappeared in Anadi’s life. By then, Anadi had settled in a house in Bowbazar; Kesto Das told him, “You’re a murderer—if you don’t support me, I’ll turn you over to the police.” Helpless, Anadi began to provide for Kesto Das.

Meanwhile, Anadi Halder’s two nephews, Nimai and Nitai, had heard that their uncle had become a wealthy man and settled in Calcutta. They began to visit Anadi. But Anadi was cunning; he saw through their intentions, played along for a while, and then one day drove them away. Nimai and Nitai realized that their uncle’s property would slip from their grasp, so they tried to frighten his prospective adopted son into leaving. But that, too, came to nothing. Seeing the Gurkha doorman, they stopped going to Prabhat Babu’s shop.

Yet, they could not let go of their greed for so much money. With no other recourse, they rented a room in a hotel opposite Anadi Halder’s house and began to keep watch over the place day and night. Of course, this was of no real use, but when a man finds no way forward, he will do something—anything—to calm his mind. Nimai and Nitai took turns coming to the hotel, sitting by the window with binoculars trained on the house. Ajit, you may remember, when Nanibala first arrived, she said it always felt as if invisible eyes were watching them. Those invisible eyes belonged to Nimai and Nitai.

So the days passed. Anadi Halder bought land and built a house. He brought Prabhat Babu here with the promise of adopting him, and at first treated him well. He gave him five thousand rupees to set up a shop; went to the attorney to learn the legalities of adoption. But he was not too eager to get entangled in formalities, and kept delaying the paperwork. Prabhat Babu, with his shop, was content; Nanibala Devi did not know that legal adoption required documentation. So no one raised any questions.

Then, something happened. Prabhat Babu saw Shiuli Majumdar and, upon hearing her sing, was enchanted.

--- Page 805 ---
Adimriphu 503

He began frequenting the Shiulis’ house. Dayalhari Majumdar was a shrewd man; after making some inquiries, he discovered that Prabhatbabu was the pampered son of a wealthy man. Dayalhari had no objection to marrying his daughter to Prabhatbabu. Dayalhari Majumdar was destitute; he thought, if he could manage to get his daughter married off in this way, what harm could there be?

Prabhatbabu spoke to Nanibala Devi about Shiuli. Nanibala spoke to Anadi Halder. Anadi Halder had no objection to marrying off Prabhatbabu; he said, “If I like the girl after seeing her, I’ll agree to the marriage.”

Up to that point, Anadi Halder harbored no ill intentions; he went to see the girl purely in the role of the groom’s guardian. But upon seeing Shiuli, he lost his composure. There was not a single vice absent from Anadi Halder’s character. He decided he would marry Shiuli himself.

Returning home, he declared that he did not like the girl. Then, secretly, he began matchmaking for himself. Dayalhari Majumdar saw his chance to strike; he seized the opportunity. He told Anadi Halder, “You are old—why should I marry my daughter to you? Unless, of course, you give ten thousand rupees—”

Thus began a period of negotiation, and finally it was settled: Anadi Halder would lend five thousand rupees on a handnote. After the marriage, the handnote would be torn up.

Having secured the marriage arrangements, Anadi Halder began to ponder how to get rid of Prabhatbabu. He had never been particularly eager to take in a pampered son; now it was simply impossible for him to keep Prabhatbabu in the house. His behavior toward Prabhatbabu grew harsh. Yet he could not simply throw him out. If Prabhatbabu revealed the matter of the bound notes to the police, Anadi Halder would end up in jail for income tax evasion.

Prabhatbabu knew nothing of these inner workings. The breaking off of the engagement left him deeply dejected, but then he resolved to marry Shiuli against Anadibabu’s wishes, come what may. He went to Dayalhari’s house. Dayalhari insulted him and threw him out, declaring that Shiuli was to be married to Anadi Halder.

At this point, Byomkesh paused. As he took a cigarette from the tin, he said, “This is the background to Anadi Halder’s death. There is some conjecture here, but I don’t believe I am mistaken. What do you say, Prabhatbabu?”

Prabhat replied, “You are not mistaken. At least, as far as my knowledge goes, you are not.”

Puntiram entered with the tea.

Eighteen

The three of us sat in silence, sipping the ginger-scented tea. The night was drawing to a close. Lighting a cigarette, Byomkesh began again, “When Nanibala Devi first came to me, I viewed the whole affair from the opposite angle. The question was whether there was any danger to Prabhatbabu’s life. From what Nanibala told me, I saw no cause for fear. Still, one can never be sure. These are troubled times; people’s scruples regarding murder have greatly diminished. A primitive savagery seems to have settled over our minds—”

--- Page 806 ---
Byomkesh Samagra

I set out to investigate.

I saw Prabhatbabu; I saw Nimai, Nitai, Anadi Halder, Nripen, Kesto Das—all of them. Nanibala came again; I told her, “No one stands to gain by killing Prabhatbabu. On the other hand, there is profit in killing Anadi Halder.” And then, on the night of Kali Puja, Anadi Halder was indeed murdered.

In the last hours of the night, Kesto Das came and fetched me. Everyone believed Kesto Das was the murderer. But when I went and observed everything, I realized—this was not a crime of passion, but a murder carefully planned. If Kesto Das had committed the murder, he would not have quarreled with Anadi Halder before killing him. Besides, no matter how heated the quarrel, Kesto Das is not such a fool as to kill the goose that lays golden eggs.

However, there is one thing. If Kesto Das could get his hands on a large sum of money by killing Anadi Halder, he might well commit murder. But this logic applies equally to the other members of the household. If we accept this reasoning, we must also accept that Anadi Halder kept a large amount of cash in his house.

If Anadi Halder kept large sums at home, he would have kept them in his steel almirah. The key to the almirah was always at his waist. When I opened the almirah, I found only about two hundred and fifty rupees. Did Anadi Halder buy a steel almirah just to keep such a paltry sum?

No money was found in the almirah, but it was noticed that several books were missing from the shelf inside. The remaining books were the usual Ramayana, Mahabharata, and the like. The question arises: what is the point of keeping such ordinary books in a steel almirah?

There was a bank checkbook in the almirah, from which it was clear that Anadi Halder had given his new house’s contractor, Gurudutt Singh, more money than he had withdrawn from the bank. Where did the rest of the money come from? Surely, Anadi Halder had earned black money and kept it in the almirah. Now that the money is missing, it must have been taken by the murderer.

The motive for the murder was found. But who was the murderer? And how did he enter the house? At the time of death, Anadi Halder was alone in the house, and the door was locked from the inside.

Anadi Halder was shot while standing on the main balcony. From the window of the Srikanta Hotel, it would have been easy to shoot him, but not to steal the money from his almirah. Therefore, there was no benefit in shooting from the Srikanta Hotel.

When Nimai and Nitai arrived with a lawyer and claimed that they were Anadi Halder’s heirs, that Prabhatbabu was not his legal adopted son, another motive emerged. If Anadi Halder could be removed before he formally adopted Prabhatbabu, all property would go to the nephews. Anadi Halder had certainly not made a will. In this country, the uneducated and half-educated rarely make wills.

It is not at all unbelievable that Nimai and Nitai could arrange for their uncle’s Ganga-yatra. Let us now consider their actions. Six months before the murder, they rented a room at the Srikanta Hotel and frequented it regularly. The hotel staff had come to recognize their faces. For those plotting to murder their uncle, is such openness normal? As I have already said, this was a planned murder; the killer had decided to strike on the night of Kali Puja, so that the sound of firecrackers would muffle the gunshot. If so, what was the point of renting a room six months in advance? Besides, how could they be sure that on the night of Kali Puja, their uncle would step out onto the balcony?

--- Page 807 ---
Adimriphu 505

But what certainty is there? No one makes a plan based on such uncertainty. And then, the bullet had pierced through Anadi Halder’s body, yet it was not found on the balcony. That too is worth considering.

“So, the proposition that Nimai or Nitai Khuro fired from the window of the Srikanta Hotel does not hold water. Whoever did it, shot from inside the house. Let us see if it was possible to shoot from within.

“The main door was locked. But the door at the back of the house, leading to the roof, used to remain open—Anadi Halder would close it himself before going to bed at night. Besides, the latch on that door was not very strong; with a few shakes, it would come undone. Let us suppose, that night, around eleven o’clock, someone quietly entered Anadi Halder’s new house. The roof of the new house had been completed up to the first floor, with scaffolding all around. The murderer climbed onto the roof; between the two houses was a narrow alley, and from the scaffolding, the murderer took a long plank and built a bridge between the two houses, then crossed over to the old house using that bridge. The roof door would be open, since Anadi Halder had not yet gone to bed.

“It seems, then, that for a nimble person, entering the house would not be a difficult task. But who is this nimble person? Not Nimai or Nitai, for they could not have known about the large sum of black money in the almirah; only someone from the household could know or guess such a thing.

“There were four people in the house—Nonibala, Kesto Das, Nripen, and Prabhatbabu. One among them killed Anadi Halder. If you say Nimai or Nitai entered the house, committed the murder, and absconded with the loot from the almirah, then why did they try to seize the house at dawn? It would have been natural for them to lie low. In due course, they would have gained possession through the court. It was precisely because they did not commit the murder that they hurried to take possession, so that the household could not spirit away the contents of the almirah.

“So, we are left with the four in the house. All of them were, of course, outside at the time, but none has a solid alibi. Nonibala Devi can be excluded, for she is a stout woman, hardly nimble. It would be impossible for her to cross the alley on a plank.

“That leaves Kesto Das, Prabhatbabu, and Nripen. At first, suspicion fell most heavily on Nripen—his behavior was highly suspicious. He was the most likely to know about the large sum in the almirah, being Anadi Halder’s secretary and keeper of his accounts. But when I learned that he had made a duplicate key for the almirah, I had to rule him out. If he had intended to murder Anadi Halder, why would he bother making a key? The key was always at Anadi Halder’s waist.

“Think about it. Nripen’s nature is that of a petty thief. He made the key, intending to pilfer a few notes when Anadi Halder was away from home. But it seems he never got the chance. He had left the key in the drawer of his desk. That night, when he returned from the cinema and found Anadi Halder murdered, the key slipped entirely from his mind. Later, when I took the key from Anadi Halder’s waist and showed it to everyone, Nripen suddenly remembered. Disaster! If the police found the duplicate key in his drawer, they would brand him the murderer. He desperately tried to get rid of it, and finally, in a moment of opportunity, he threw the key out the window into the alley.

“I found the key in the alley the next morning. From that moment, I knew Nripen was not the murderer. Then, after receiving my friend Ramesh Mallik’s letter, everything became clear. Nripen—

--- Page 808 ---
Chhichuke is a petty thief; he does not have the courage to commit murder.

“That leaves Kesto Das and Prabhatbabu.

“That evening, Kesto Das came here. At night, after plying him with drink, I learned the old history of Anadi Halder. Kesto Das, too, learned something from me that night. In the course of conversation, I mentioned that Prabhatbabu knows the work of a clerk. He hadn’t known that before.

“Well, a few days passed after that. I noticed that Nripen and Kesto Das were still staying at the old house. If they had stolen the money, why were they still clinging to the old place? They had every excuse to leave; after Anadi Halder’s death, there was no longer any pretext for them to remain in the house. And where would they have kept the money? Surely not in a bank, nor would they entrust it to anyone else. Then where?

“They have no other hideout in Calcutta where they could stash the money. But Prabhatbabu does have a place—his shop. If he had committed the murder and taken the money, he would have no trouble hiding it.

“The shop—a bookshop. Like a flash of lightning, the whole affair blazed in my mind. Prabhatbabu hadn’t bound account ledgers in Patna; he had bound hundred-rupee notes—Anadi Halder had kept those bound books mixed in with the Ramayana and Mahabharata in his almirah—after killing Anadi Halder, Prabhatbabu had taken the keys from his waist, removed the books of notes from the almirah, and brought them to his own shop—amongst the thousands of books in the shop, the books of notes were openly displayed—no one, seeing them from the outside, would ever suspect—

“The entire plan rose before my eyes, whole and complete.

“But—

“Would Prabhatbabu do such a thing for money? From what I had understood of his character, he did not seem greedy. On the contrary, Anadi Halder’s death meant loss for him; had Halder lived, he would have adopted him as a son, with the prospect of inheriting all his property. Would he throw away that possibility for the lure of ready cash?

“Was the money secondary, then? Was there a greater reason? Anadi Halder had broken off Prabhatbabu’s marriage to Shiuli; but was that such a grave crime as to warrant murder? The answer to that question came to me late. On my way back from Dayalhari Majumdar’s house, the real truth suddenly flashed in my mind.

“Anadi Halder had done something that could drive even the most harmless man to murder. He had tried to bribe Dayalhari with five thousand rupees to marry Shiuli himself. Prabhatbabu’s blood caught fire. That fire was not strange; the spark had always lain smoldering in his veins.

“And yet, he possessed a cold, icy cunning as well, inherited by birth. He resolved to kill Anadi Halder with all his wealth. He already knew Bantul Sardar; hiring a rifle was not difficult. On the night of Kali Puja, the old goat was made ready for sacrifice.

“That night, Prabhatbabu dropped Nanibala Devi off at the cinema and went to his shop. He lit up the shop, and at half past ten, he left again, this time with a cloth pouch in his pocket. The shop remained open, the Gurkha doorman stood guard at the door.

--- Page 809 ---
Adimriphu 507

‘As he approached his house, Prabhatbabu saw that fireworks were being set off in front of it. No one paid him any attention; he slipped quietly into the new building. Inside, Batul Sardar was waiting with a rifle. Batul was not pleased with Anadi Halder, so it was only natural that he should be eager to take part in this affair.

‘Prabhatbabu entered the house by laying a plank across the roof. The roof door was probably already open; even if it hadn’t been, it wouldn’t have mattered—he rattled the door a few times and unlatched it. Anadi Halder was standing on the balcony, watching the fireworks. At the sound behind him, he turned around. Instantly, Prabhatbabu fired. The bullet pierced Anadi Halder’s body, flew across the street, entered through the window of the Srikanta Hotel, and lodged itself in the wall. It was a high-velocity military rifle—had Nimai or Nitai been standing in its path, the bullet would have torn through them as well.

‘Then Prabhatbabu took the keys from the dead man’s waist and opened the almirah. He stuffed the account books into a bag, replaced the keys where he’d found them, and retraced his steps. Batul was waiting, then vanished with the rifle. Prabhatbabu returned to the shop and arranged the books on a high shelf. Afterwards, at the appointed time, he went to the cinema, brought his mother home, and returned to the house.

‘The doorman, Griha, knew that Prabhatbabu had not spent the entire night in the shop. When I went looking for Griha, he had already quit his job and returned to his village.

‘That day, Ajit and I were on our way to Prabhatbabu’s shop when we saw Batul walking ahead of us. As he was about to enter the shop, he noticed us behind him and decided not to go in, walking straight past instead. When we entered the shop, we found Prabhatbabu down with fever—a fever of terror. I took him to a doctor I knew. The doctor examined Prabhatbabu and privately shared the results with me. Then, all doubt vanished.

‘Someone else had realized before me that Prabhatbabu had murdered Anadi Halder—that was Kesto Das. Like a snake recognizing a sneeze, Kesto Das knew there was black money in Anadi Halder’s almirah; so when he heard from me that Prabhatbabu was familiar with the duties of a clerk, he quickly pieced the whole matter together. He began to exploit Prabhatbabu. Ajit, do you remember that day when we saw one hundred-rupee notes, one after another, until our eyes ached? Even at night, when we went to eat at the hotel, there was no respite—Kesto Das produced a hundred-rupee note there as well. Most of those notes had come from Anadi Halder’s bound account books.

‘Anyway, before leaving for Patna, I had almost managed to erase the Anadi Halder affair from my mind. I only asked Bikash Dutta to gather information about Dayalhari Majumdar.

‘Then, after returning from Patna, I found a new situation—Kesto Das had been murdered. Kesto Das had been blackmailing Prabhatbabu, so I was convinced that he was the killer. Once again, I prepared to catch the culprit. But it wasn’t enough to catch the criminal; the money had to be recovered as well.

‘To recover the money easily, I had to resort to a little cunning. Otherwise, searching the entire shop for the account books would have been difficult. Perhaps Prabhatbabu wouldn’t have allowed a search; the police would have to be called, and everything would slip from my grasp. So when Prabhatbabu mentioned selling the shop, it was a stroke of luck. I said, we would buy the shop. With that...

--- Page 810 ---
508  
Byomkesh Samagra

I sent Bikash along to keep an eye on things, to see if Prabhatbabu would remove anything from the shop.  
“The arrangements for buying the shop were finalized; on the morning of Independence Day, we were to take possession. I knew Prabhatbabu would remove the books at some point before that. Bikash reported that he hadn’t taken anything during the day. So, at night, we entered the shop and waited. Napa had made a duplicate key for us—”

Suddenly, a tremendous wave of sound crashed against our eardrums from outside—the awakening blare of a radio. We started and looked toward the window. The first light of day was beginning to seep in.

Nineteen

Byomkesh shifted and sat up straight.  
“Putiram!”  
Putiram poked his head in through the door.  
“Bring the fire tongs.”  
I said, “I’ve been hearing about these tongs for a long time, but I still don’t know what they’re for. Are you going to perform a homa or something?”  
Byomkesh replied, “Yes, I am. These notes will be our offering to the fire.”  
“What do you mean?”  
“I mean, I’m going to burn the notes.”  
I cried out in anguish, “What! You’re going to burn two lakh rupees?”  
“Yes. These notes are black money, cursed money; they have no rightful owner. On this auspicious day, this will be our offering at the feet of Mother India.”  
“But—but if you burn them, will Mother India get anything? Wouldn’t it be better to give the money to our new government—”  
“It’s the same thing, Ajit. Even if we burn them, it’s the state that will benefit. Think about it—the notes aren’t real money, they’re just government hand-notes. If we burn the hand-notes, the government won’t have to redeem them; it’ll be two lakh rupees gained. But if we try to return them now, there’ll be all sorts of complications. The government will want to know where the money came from, and in digging for earthworms, we might unearth snakes. What’s the point? Better to offer them to the fire, where the gods will receive them. What do you say, Prabhatbabu?”

Prabhat sat staring blankly, as if his wits had deserted him. With effort, he managed to say, “I have nothing to say. Do as you think best.”

Putiram brought the fire tongs and placed them before Byomkesh. Byomkesh told him, “You can go get some sleep now.”  
Putiram left. Byomkesh looked at our faces and smiled. Then he began tearing pages from the book and feeding them to the fire. In a deep voice, he intoned, “Swaha, swaha, swaha—”

I could not sit and watch any longer; I got up and stood by the window.  
Byomkesh is my friend; I love him, I respect him. But today, I saw a new side to his character. What he did, I could never have done—burning two lakh rupees with his own hands.

--- Page 811 ---
Adimriphu 509

I could never have brought myself to burn the money.
‘Swaha, swaha—’
An hour later, Byomkesh and Prabhat came to stand beside me. The sun had risen, and all around, the auspicious wedding drums were beating. I turned to look back—the ring was now encircled by heaps of burnt paper ash. Black money, turned to black ash.

The three of us stood by the window for a while in silence. It was Prabhat who spoke first, his voice trembling. “Byomkesh-babu, I—about myself—if you hand me over for murder, I will not deny it.”

Byomkesh turned to him, his voice softened by compassion. “I will not hand you over. In every civilized country, there is a tradition—on festival days, prisoners are set free. Today, you too are free. We had told you we would buy your shop—if you wish to sell and leave, we will take it. Or, if you want to sell us half and become our partner, we have no objection to that either.”

Prabhat broke down, sobbing uncontrollably. At last, wiping his eyes, he said, “Byomkesh-babu, this is beyond anything I could have imagined.”

Byomkesh replied, “The times we live in are themselves beyond imagination. Who could have dreamt that we would live to see India’s independence? But let that be. You have escaped the death penalty, but you will not be entirely free. Some punishment you must still bear. In this world, the fruits of action cannot be wholly escaped.”

Prabhat said, “Tell me what punishment, whatever you decree, I will accept with bowed head.”

Byomkesh said, “You must learn your own identity.”

Prabhat’s eyes widened—“My own identity!”

“Yes. Do you know who you are?—Your father’s name?”

Prabhat shook his head. “No. My mother told me I was born in a hospital. I know nothing more.”

“I know. Your father’s name was Anadi Halder.”

I will not attempt to describe Prabhat’s reaction to this revelation, for I myself was dumbfounded. At last, regaining my composure, I said, “Byomkesh! What are you saying? Do you have any proof?”

Byomkesh replied, “Of course. The proof is on Prabhat-babu’s own body.”

“On his body!”

“Yes. Prabhat-babu has a red birthmark on his waist, like a half-rupee coin. Prabhat-babu, may I see it?”

Like an automaton, Prabhat lifted his shirt. Near the seam of his garment on the right, the birthmark was visible.

Byomkesh said to me, “You remember where you’ve seen one exactly like this before, don’t you?”

I did remember. When Byomkesh had been fastening the key to the waist of the dead Anadi Halder, he had shown it to me. Yet my amazement did not abate; overwhelmed, I asked, “But how did you know that Prabhat-babu had such a birthmark on his waist?”

“The day I took Prabhat-babu to Dr. Talukdar, I asked the doctor to examine his waist.”

Still, my mind was not at ease. I said, “But can this really be called proof?”

Byomkesh replied, “If you refuse to call it proof, then call it a reasonable inference, a legitimate—”

--- Page 812 ---
510  
Byomkesh Samagra

Inference must be drawn. Why did Anadi Halder suddenly go to Patna? Why did he want to adopt the office assistant as his foster son? What need was there to give Prabhatbabu five thousand rupees to set up a shop? Consider all these together, and you will have no doubts left.”

Prabhat, staggering, went and collapsed onto the armchair, lying there for a long while with his face buried in his hands.

I began to reflect—the pieces were indeed falling into place. Prabhat, born of Anadi Halder, raised by Nanibala. Having earned a fortune in the black market, he had secretly gone to see his son. When he saw the boy working as a clerk, perhaps that was when the idea struck him—to bind the notes into books and hide them away. In Anadi Halder’s cunning mind, adopting Prabhat as a foster son seemed far more prudent than acknowledging him as his own child… If not for his reckless impulses wrecking everything midway, the secret of Prabhat’s birth might have remained forever unknown.

Prabhat raised his face, ashen as a corpse, and sat up. In a broken voice, he said, “Byomkeshbabu, why didn’t you just have me hanged? That would have been far better than bearing this stain of blood.”

Byomkesh placed a firm hand on his shoulder and said resolutely, “Take heart, Prabhatbabu. Who among us is free from the stain of blood? Never forget that in the veins of mankind flows the blood of beasts. Through long penance, man has managed to subdue some of the savagery in his blood; he has become civilized, gentle, human. With effort, it is not impossible to overcome the influence of blood. Forget the past—the chains of the past have broken. Today, you are a new man of a new India, free in spirit and in life.”

Blindly, Prabhat reached out and touched Byomkesh’s feet. “Bless me,” he whispered.

--- Page 813 ---
Bohni-Patanga

One

We spent ten or twelve days in Patna in relative peace. Then, one day, we happened to run into Purandar Pandey. Pandeyji had been transferred to Patna about a year ago. It was during the Durga mystery that we first came into contact with him, and since then, we had not met again. Pandeyji was delighted, and we were no less pleased ourselves. Pandeyji is a harbinger of deathly mysteries; scarcely two days after our reunion, a mysterious death presented itself, and—

The very death-mystery I mentioned in Adim Ripu, I now set down here in detail.

One evening, after dusk, we were gathered for adda at Pandeyji’s house. No outsiders were present—only Byomkesh, Pandeyji, and myself. With tea, bowls of Kabulichana, laddus from Monere, and Gaya’s tobacco, we were ruminating on old memories. The servant would come in from time to time to change the chillum on the hookah.

Ever since we had met Pandeyji, our addas had become a near-daily affair—sometimes at our place, sometimes at his. Tonight, the gathering was at Pandeyji’s house. He had invited us for dinner the next evening and promised us Kashmiri chicken korma. Our idle exile in Patna was turning sweet indeed.

Pandeyji’s transfer to Patna had brought him a promotion, but I saw no joy in his heart. Though the flames of the great war had died down, the air and sky were still heavy with the ashes of the pyre, and now, the birth-pangs of independence. Our reminiscences soon descended from history into the present. Pandeyji recounted to us several recent, hair-raising true incidents. At last, he said—

“Since the time of the war, the number of thugs, swindlers, murderers, and scoundrels in the world has increased, and so has the work of the police. Crimes we could never have imagined before are now daily occurrences. Foreign soldiers have come and taught us all sorts of alien villainy. So many kinds of intoxicants, so many poisons have entered the country—there’s no keeping track. Just the other day, a most ordinary petty thief in Patna was found with a vial of medicine; on testing, it turned out to be a deadly poison, native to South America.”

Byomkesh, removing the hookah pipe from his lips, asked lazily, “What poison? Curare?”

“Yes. I see you know the name. Such a terrible poison that a single drop mixed with blood brings instant death. The vial we found could wipe out the entire city of Patna. Imagine how many such vials have been smuggled in.”

Byomkesh asked, “Have you found any evidence that the poison has actually been used anywhere?”

--- Page 814 ---
Byomkesh Babu, do you think the police ever hear of every case of poisoning in our country? You don’t even need a doctor’s certificate to cremate a corpse. Perhaps if someone of importance is poisoned, there’s a bit of an uproar. Even then, the family hushes it up. Yet I believe the number of poisonings in this country is not at all insignificant.”

Byomkesh, drawing deeply on his hookah, said after a pause, “Tell me, all these poisons and narcotics you people seize—where do they end up?”

Pandeyji replied, “Where else would they go? They stay with us for a while, then are sent to the head office, and they take care of it. But that’s just a fraction. Most of it is out there, circulating in the black market. Whoever needs it, buys it and uses it.” Pandeyji sighed—“War and revolution strip civilized men of their civility. Then the mask of conscience and reason falls away, and the raw, ravenous beast emerges. How fragile our civilization is! At heart, we are barbarians.”

Byomkesh seemed to weigh the words, then said, “Indeed, we are barbarians. But when we regress from civilization to barbarism, we take with us at least one trait of civilization. The mask doesn’t fall so easily, Pandeyji; it takes time to uncover the beast within. Outwardly, a calm, gentle, harmless creature—inside, sharp claws and fangs. That is the most terrifying thing of all.”

The clock struck eight. It was a winter night, but we felt no particular urge to return home; so when Pandeyji proposed another round of tea, we did not object.

At that moment, the servant entered and announced, “Inspector Chowdhury has arrived.”

Pandeyji said, “Who—Ratikanta? Bring him in. And prepare four cups of tea.”

The servant departed. A moment later, a young man in police uniform entered. Tall and sturdy, with a ruddy complexion, sharp features, and blue eyes—at first glance, one might mistake him for a sahib. He was about thirty. He came in, raised his right hand in a salute, and stood beside Pandeyji.

Pandeyji asked, “What news, Ratikanta?”

Ratikanta replied, “Sir, there’s an invitation letter.” Saying this, he produced an envelope from his overcoat pocket. Ratikanta’s speech was pure Hindi of northern India, not the adulterated dialect of Bihar.

Pandeyji smiled and said, “What’s the invitation for? Your wedding, perhaps?”

Ratikanta made a woeful face and replied, “Who would marry me, sir? It’s an invitation from Dipanarayan Singh.”

As Pandeyji opened the envelope, he said, “But why did you bring Dipanarayan Singh’s invitation?”

Ratikanta, with a playful, embarrassed smile, said, “What can I do, sir? He’s a big man, a relative—might become a minister someday, so I have to keep up appearances. I drop by now and then to pay my respects. Went today, and he handed me all the invitation letters for the police officers to deliver.”

Pandeyji drew out a glossy card embossed in gold and read it. “Hmm, looks like a serious affair. A proper dinner. But what’s the occasion?”

Ratikanta replied, “He’s recovered after a long illness, so he’s treating his friends. He’s invited all the city’s notables.”

Pandeyji, slipping the card back into the envelope, said, “The invitation is for tomorrow night. But—”

--- Page 815 ---
Bahni-Patanga 513

“I won’t be able to come, Ratikanta.”

“Why, sir? Are you going out for inspection tomorrow?”

“No. These two gentlemen have come from Calcutta, I’ve invited them for dinner tomorrow night.”

Byomkesh murmured softly, “Kashmiri chicken korma.”

Ratikanta glanced at us with a sudden smile. All this while, he had been casting curious glances our way; perhaps he was intrigued to see us, unfamiliar faces, sitting with Panditji and drawing on the hookah, but he had not let his curiosity show. Now, smiling, he raised his right hand to his forehead in a salute. Then he said to Panditji, “Huzoor, if I’d known about the Kashmiri korma, I’d have come to your house tomorrow to join the adda myself. But now there’s no way. Well, I’ll take your leave for today.”

Panditji said, “Sit, have some tea before you go.”

Ratikanta replied, “Tea can wait for another day, huzoor. And if you serve Kashmiri korma, there’s nothing more to say. But I really can’t stay today. I still have two or three letters left to deliver. Besides, I have to go report to Dipnarayanji. You saw the RSVP written on the invitation, didn’t you?”

“All right, then, go on.”

With a gentle smile, Ratikanta saluted all of us together and took his leave.

Byomkesh exclaimed, “Ah, what a striking young man! Like a prince!”

Panditji said, “You’re not far from the truth. His ancestors were great taluqdars of Pratapgarh. Almost royalty. Now their fortunes have fallen so low that Ratikanta had to take up a job. He’s a clever boy; educated himself through his own efforts, passed his B.Sc.”

Byomkesh said, “These days, it’s a good sign that sons of noble houses are joining the police.”

The tea arrived. After some conversation on other topics, Byomkesh asked, “Who is Dipnarayan Singh?”

Panditji replied, “You haven’t heard of Dipnarayan Singh? A formidable zamindar of Bihar, annual income of a million rupees, and on top of that, he deals in moneylending. But he’s a good man. He supported the political movement with funds. Now he’s getting on in years, close to fifty—” He took a few puffs on the hookah and continued, “In his old age, he’s made one mistake: he’s taken a young bride.”

“Is the former wife still living?”

“No, not that. The former wife passed away a few years ago, and after that, the young bride arrived. One can’t really blame the gentleman—no children, only a nephew who shares the estate, but he’s utterly useless. Someone must enjoy all this royal splendor, after all.”

“Then what’s the mistake in Dipnarayan Singh marrying again? The family line will be preserved.”

“The line isn’t preserved yet. But that’s not the main point. The real issue is that Dipnarayan Singh, smitten by beauty, married outside his caste. A civil marriage.”

“The young bride is beautiful, I suppose?”

“Beautiful and accomplished. Skilled in the arts, she can sing and dance, she can paint, and on top of that, she’s a brilliant student from Allahabad University—first class first in her B.A.”

“Dipnarayan Singh seems both fortunate and progressive.”

--- Page 816 ---
514  
Byomkesh Samagra

“He wasn’t always this progressive. Until recently, the women in his household lived behind the purdah. Now, the veil has been lifted entirely.”

“Well, that’s good, isn’t it? What’s wrong with that?”

“There’s nothing wrong. But the unfamiliarity stings, like a drop of acid on the forehead. The people of Bihar still haven’t been able to cast off the purdah from their hearts. So, the moment they see women with a bit of freedom, they start whispering, exchanging knowing glances—”

Our conversation, following the path of women’s emancipation, soon veered into the realm of politics; the hands of the clock were inching steadily towards nine. If I returned home too late at night, Satyabati would raise a ruckus. So, with some reluctance, we prepared to take our leave.

Pandeyji said, “Come, let me drop you off in my car.” He used to have a motorcycle; now he had acquired a small motorcar.

We had just risen to our feet when, from the corner of the room, the telephone began to ring. Pandeyji went to answer—“Hello… Yes, this is Purandar Pandey… You wish to speak to Dipnarayan Singh?… Namaste, namaste… I very much wanted to attend your party, but… Bring my friends along as well?… Well—they’re still here, let me ask them—”

Covering the mouthpiece with his hand, Pandeyji turned to us. “Dipnarayan Singh is inviting you both to his party as well. What do you say?”

Byomkesh glanced at me, then said, “Why not! It’ll be something new. Your Kashmiri korma will have to wait for now.”

Smiling, Pandeyji spoke into the telephone, “Very well, they’ll come… Please send their cards to me… Yes, we’ll meet again tomorrow. Namaste.”

Replacing the receiver, Pandeyji said, “Come, let me drop you home now.”

TWO

The following evening, around seven, Pandeyji arrived and bundled us into his car, taking us to Dipnarayan Singh’s house.

Dipnarayan Singh’s residence was in the old quarter of the city—a sprawling, two-storied house from a bygone era, surrounded by high walls like a prison. When we arrived, we saw the house and garden aglow with Japanese lanterns, the faint strains of a shehnai drifting through the air, and a crowd of guests already assembled.

The main gathering was in the large hall on the ground floor, though guests were seated in the surrounding rooms as well. In one room, a bridge adda was underway; in another, elderly, official-looking guests had formed their own exclusive circle and were deep in conversation. Liveried servants, their uniforms adorned with badges, circulated with trays of tea, coffee, and stronger drinks.

The hall itself was vast, furnished in the English style with sofa sets arranged here and there. Each sofa set hosted its own little group. In the center of the room, facing the main door, was a divan-like seat. Reclining against its cushions sat a middle-aged man—this was our host, Dipnarayan Singh. He wore a long, warm coat and a woolen scarf around his neck. His features were pleasant; at fifty, he had not yet succumbed to the stiffness of age, though the pallor and gauntness of his face suggested he was only recently recovering from a long illness. With utmost courtesy, he rose and shook our hands in both of his.

--- Page 817 ---
Bohni - Patanga 515

Pandeyji said, “Congratulations on your recovery.”

Dipnarayan smiled sweetly, his face still gaunt. “Many thanks. I had no hope of surviving, Pandeyji. If it weren’t for Dr. Palit, I wouldn’t have made it this time.” He gestured toward a corner of the room.

In the corner, seated alone on a sofa, was a gentleman in coat and trousers—of medium build, his attire lacking any particular elegance, about fifty years old by estimate. Noticing the gesture, he came over and stood before us. Introductions were made. Dipnarayan Singh said, “It is thanks to him that I have been granted a new lease on life.”

Dr. Palit seemed a little embarrassed. He was a man of grave disposition. After a brief silence, he said, “I did nothing more than what is required of a doctor. Besides, though I treated him, all the eminent doctors of the city examined him as well. Mr. Tridib—”

Pandeyji asked, “What was the illness?”

From the symptoms Dr. Palit described, in the manner of Western medical science, I gathered that a variety of malignant germs had conspired with the liver to cause anemia and were on the verge of injuring the heart. It had taken injections and other infernal treatments to bring them under control. Now, of course, the patient’s condition was much improved, though he still required careful monitoring.

At that moment, a sound like someone blowing his nose startled us from behind. Turning, I saw a young man standing there, making a noise through his nose as if to express disdain. His face was sharp as a jackal’s, his body clad in the latest Western fashion, his expression mocking and arrogant. Dipnarayan introduced him—“This is Dr. Jagannath Prasad, a young Bihari doctor.” Dr. Jagannath nodded at us with contempt, and from the few words he spoke, it was clear that he held nothing but scorn for senior doctors, especially if they happened to be Bengali. Had the responsibility for Dipnarayan Singh’s treatment been entrusted to him instead of to a handful of elderly Bengali doctors, he would have cured the illness in five days. Dipnarayan Singh listened to him with a wry, gentle smile. Dr. Palit, annoyed, returned to his seat. Dr. Jagannath, after a few more moments of oration, spotted a servant carrying drinks and, neighing like a horse, hurried off in that direction.

Dipnarayan Singh, in a voice tinged with both shame and resentment, said, “These are the Biharis of the new age. They have no respect for merit; under the guise of communalism, they only look out for themselves. Today, Bengalis are losing their standing in Bihar, and it is they who are to blame.”

Byomkesh said, “Perhaps the Bengalis are at fault too.”

Dipnarayan replied, “Perhaps. But the irony is, when they fall ill, when their lives hang in the balance, it is to the Bengali doctors that they run.”

This unpleasant provincial topic having arisen, we all felt a little awkward, but Pandeyji smoothed things over, changed the subject with a few words, and led us over to where Dr. Palit was seated.

Once we were settled, Dr. Palit smiled faintly and said, “What else did Horse Jagannath have to say?”

Pandeyji burst out laughing. “So his name is Horse Jagannath? Splendid name, suits him perfectly. But don’t pay any heed to their words, Doctor. Who takes them seriously anyway?”

Palit said, “But how can one not? They go about in groups, spreading their propaganda everywhere.”

--- Page 818 ---
BYOMKESH SAMAGRA

The clever ones may not pay them any heed, but ordinary folk listen to their every word.’

Our conversation might have continued a while longer, but suddenly it was interrupted by a strange kind of laughter from nearby. Turning my head, I saw that, not far from us, three men had settled themselves onto another sofa set. Among them, the one who was laughing so uproariously was so immense in size that he alone seemed to occupy the entire sofa. A barrel-chested, elephant-shouldered youth, with rolls of fat cascading from chin to hip in undulating waves. The bizarre laughter that issued from his throat was so peculiar, it was hard to believe that such a cacophony could emerge from a single human being—unless one witnessed it firsthand. Imagine a dozen jackals howling in unison, and, mingled with that, the wailing of several newborns snatched from their mothers—perhaps then you might begin to grasp the tumult of sound he produced.

The other two men sat silently, smirking. Curiously, the fat youth was as corpulent as his two companions were thin. If the flesh and fat of these three were distributed evenly, you might have ended up with three healthy, ordinary men. Needless to say, the thunderous laughter had drawn the startled gaze of everyone in the room.

A frail, elderly man, wearing a silk turban, appeared from somewhere and hurried towards them.

Byomkesh leaned over and whispered to Dr. Palit, ‘Who is that behemoth?’

Dr. Palit suppressed a smile and replied, ‘That’s Debnarayan, nephew of Dipnarayan Babu. An absolute—’ He left the sentence unfinished, but the unspoken noun was all too clear. It was about him that Pandeyji had remarked yesterday—“Not only is he a hopeless fool, but his wits are as bloated as his body.” The turbaned old man approached the two thin youths and whispered something in their ears, seemingly a gentle rebuke. The two thin men looked thoroughly chastened, lowering their eyes like scolded, drenched cats. The behemoth’s laughter had not yet ceased, though it was now subsiding. The old man stood behind him, bent close to his ear, and murmured something. Suddenly, like a car slamming on its brakes, the laughter came to an abrupt, jarring halt.

Byomkesh, as before, quietly asked Dr. Palit, ‘Who are the two thin fellows?’

Palit replied, ‘The one with the curly hair and crab-like moustache is Debnarayan’s buffoon—his “yar,” you could say. Name’s Beniprasad. The other is Liladhar Banshi—assistant manager of Dipnarayan Babu’s estate, and assistant buffoon to Debnarayan.’

‘And the old man?’

‘That’s Gangadhar Banshi, Liladhar’s father, the chief manager of the estate. A deep-water fish, that one.’

The deep-water fish glanced at us once, his eyes lifting, and with a gentle, honeyed smile, he anointed us before departing elsewhere. Debnarayan, meanwhile, drew from the pocket of his gleaming sharkskin bandhgala coat a massive betel nut box, stuffed several betel leaves into his mouth, and began to chew with a grave, ponderous expression. It was hard to believe this was the same man who, only moments ago, had been raising such a ruckus with his laughter.

Byomkesh asked, ‘Did you catch what was so funny?’

Palit replied, ‘I suppose the buffoons must have said something amusing, hence all that laughter.’

A servant appeared, bearing a silver tray laden with betel leaves wrapped in golden foil and cigarettes. We lit our cigarettes. Byomkesh glanced about and said to Pandeyji, ‘Inspector—’

--- Page 819 ---
I do not see Ratikanta.’

Pandeyji said, ‘Perhaps he is in another room. Or maybe he’s been detained at the thana. He will surely come. Please, you all sit. I’ll just have a word with the Commissioner Sahib and return.’

Pandeyji got up and left. The three of us sat there, drawing on our cigarettes, our eyes wandering over the scattered guests in the room. The majority were men, though there were one or two women among them.

At that moment, a lady entered through a door at the far end of the room. The room was already well-lit, yet it seemed that with her arrival, the brightness increased. I could not tell what color sari she wore, nor what ornaments adorned her; all I saw was a moving source of radiance slowly advancing towards us. Everyone in the room became alert at once; some even rose to their feet and greeted her with folded hands. The lady, smiling and graceful, greeted everyone as she made her way toward us.

Dr. Palit stubbed out his cigarette in the ashtray and murmured, ‘Mrs. Dipnarayan—Shakuntala.’

A true beauty. She could not be less than twenty-four or twenty-five, yet the intoxicating bloom of youth seemed to burst forth from her every limb. In the northwestern regions of our country, one might see two or four such golden-skinned, radiant women, but here they are rare. Even her name—Shakuntala—seemed to harmonize with her beauty. Shakuntala—the celestial nymph, the very one who made Dushyanta forget himself. It was not hard to understand why Dipnarayan Singh, a man of mature years, had chosen to marry outside his caste.

Shakuntala came and stood before us, and we rose respectfully. Dr. Palit introduced us. Shakuntala spoke a few words of cordial welcome in a sweet voice, her manner blending the courtesy of a mistress of the house with the modesty of a young woman. Then she turned away.

At that moment, I noticed that Shakuntala was not alone; behind her stood another young woman. Just as the morning star is lost in the sun’s brilliance, so had this young woman been hidden until now. Now I saw that she carried a child of about a year and a half in her arms. In fact, it was the child’s sudden wail that drew our attention to her. The young woman was perhaps a year or two younger than Shakuntala; fair and comely, with a plump, loose figure, she seemed almost weighed down by her costly clothes and heavy jewelry. There was abundance in her attire, but no finesse. Moreover, it seemed she was not accustomed to mingling openly with men; the veil of seclusion had not yet lifted from her.

As the child began to cry, Shakuntala turned and glanced back. A shadow of displeasure crossed her face. She said, ‘Chandni, why have you brought the boy here? Go, leave him with the nurse.’

Like a loyal dog chastised by its master, the young woman looked at Shakuntala, then, bowing her head meekly, retraced her steps with the child in her arms.

From a distance, Dipnarayan called to his wife—‘Shakuntala!’—some distinguished guests had arrived.

Shakuntala went that way. Glancing sideways, I saw Debnarayan, in his arms...

--- Page 820 ---
518  
Byomkesh Samagra

With bulging, frog-like eyes, he stared at Shakuntala.  
We lit another cigarette. Byomkesh asked, “Who was the second lady?”  
Dr. Palit replied absentmindedly, “Debnarayan’s wife. The boy is Debnarayan’s as well.”  
I noticed a faint furrow on Dr. Palit’s brow. His gaze, too, was following Shakuntala.  

We sat for a long while, passing the time in a haze of smoke. Dr. Palit remained distracted, his eyes trailing Shakuntala with a hint of unease.  

At half past eight, the call for dinner arrived.  
In another hall, a table had been laid for the meal. The arrangements were regal. Cooks and servers had come from some British hotel in Calcutta. By the time we finished eating and rose from the table, it was a quarter to ten.  

Returning to the outer hall, we busied ourselves with paan and cigarettes. Dr. Palit let out a satisfied sigh and said, “Not bad at all. Well, I’ll take my leave now—might have to go out to see a patient tonight. I’ll be back tomorrow morning to give Dipnarayan Babu his injection.”  

Byomkesh asked, “He’s still getting injections?”  
Palit replied, “Yes, one for the liver every week. I’ll stop after two more. Well then—good night. You’re still here, so we’ll surely meet again—”  

He had just started to leave when I saw Inspector Ratikanta Chowdhury enter through the main door. He wore the police uniform, only missing his cap. There was a trace of urgency about him. Standing by the door, he glanced around the room, then, spotting Dr. Palit, quickly came over to us.  

“Dr. Palit, I have some bad news. There’s been a theft at your dispensary.”  
“A theft!”  
Ratikanta said, “Yes. Around nine o’clock, I was leaving the station to come here when I noticed the dispensary door was open. Going closer, I saw the lock was broken. Inside, your desk drawer was open—the thief broke it and made off with the cash. I’ve left a constable there. You should go. Was there any money in the drawer?”  
Palit, bewildered, said, “Money! There’s never much at night, at most two or four rupees.”  
“Still, you should go. If anything besides money is missing, you’ll be able to tell.”  
“I’ll go at once.”  
“And if anything else is missing, send a report to the station tonight.”  

Shakuntala and Pandeyji, who had been conversing at a distance, noticed our agitation and came over. Pandeyji asked, “What’s happened?”  
Dr. Palit did not stop; he hurried away. Ratikanta explained about the theft.  
Then, turning to Shakuntala, he said, “I’m terribly late—will I still get dinner?”  
Shakuntala smiled slightly. “Of course. Come with me.”  

The master of the house had already retired for the night. We took our leave of Shakuntala and left the house.

--- Page 821 ---
Bohni - Patanga 519  
We returned.

Three

The next morning, at around nine o’clock, a motorcar arrived and stopped in front of our house. Byomkesh, lifting his face from the newspaper, furrowed his brow. “Pandeyji—so early!”

A moment later, Pandeyji entered our baithak-khana. Dressed in police uniform, his face grave. In response to Byomkesh’s questioning gaze, he said, “Dipnarayan Singh has died.”

We stared at him, dumbfounded, as if the words had not quite sunk in.

“Died?”

“Ratikanta just telephoned. This morning, Dr. Palit had come to give Dipnarayan Singh an injection. The moment the injection was administered, he died. I am going there now. Will you come?”

Without a word, Byomkesh threw his shawl over his shoulder. I too rose.

“Let’s go.”

As the motorcar rattled along, the scenes of last night replayed in my mind. I had seen Dipnarayan Singh only once, but had taken a liking to him; a courteous, smiling gentleman, who seemed to be recovering from his illness. What had happened, so suddenly? And Shakuntala—

Shakuntala was now a widow... Somehow, I could not bring myself to accept this cruel truth.

We reached our destination. Three or so cars stood parked by the gate. Pandeyji halted the car and got down. Passing through the gateway, we arrived at the main entrance of the house. The garden was deserted; a tense hush seemed to hang over everything.

At the main door, Inspector Ratikanta saluted Pandeyji with a grave face. Seeing us, his eyebrows rose slightly, but without a word, he led us all inside.

Before the door of the hall, the divan-like seat stood as before, and upon it lay the body of Dipnarayan Singh. Seated beside the corpse, Dr. Palit stared fixedly at the dead man’s face. There was no one else in the room; only the furniture remained arranged as it had been the previous night.

We tiptoed to the side of the divan. Dipnarayan Singh looked just as he had last night; death’s touch had wrought no change in his form. Eyes closed, the muscles of his face relaxed; as if he had simply fallen asleep.

Dr. Palit was so absorbed in gazing at the dead man’s face that he did not seem to notice our arrival. It was only at Pandeyji’s gentle touch that he started from his reverie. Rising, he looked at each of us in turn, then said, “A post-mortem is necessary. And—keep this vial.” Near his hand was a small brown bottle with a rubber stopper; he handed it to Pandeyji. Pandeyji held the vial up to the light—there was still about half of the liquid left inside. He handed the vial to Ratikanta and said quietly to the doctor, “Come, let’s go sit over there.”

Dr. Palit picked up his handbag from the divan. We all went and sat on a nearby sofa set. Ratikanta remained standing. Pandeyji asked—

--- Page 822 ---
Byomkesh Samagra

He asked, “Where is everyone else in the house?”

Ratikanta replied, “I’ve sent them all upstairs. Miss Manna is with Shakuntala Devi.”

“Miss Manna? The lady doctor?”

Palit said, “Yes. She’s the regular doctor for this house as well. Seeing Shakuntala’s condition, I telephoned her and had her brought over.”

“You did the right thing. Any news of Debnarayan?”

“Debnarayan is an idiot—he’s bawling like a child. Dewan Gangadhar Banshi is with him. Poor Chandni is the one in real trouble, she’s weeping herself, running to her husband one moment, then rushing to Shakuntala the next.” He sighed deeply.

Pandeyji remained silent for a while, then said, “Doctor Palit, please tell us everything from the beginning.”

The doctor set his bag down from his lap and said, “There isn’t much to tell. Around eight o’clock, I arrived and found Dipnarayan Babu sitting on that couch, waiting for me. Seeing me, he smiled and said—‘Didn’t expect you to come so early in this cold, have some tea.’ I said—‘Alright, but let me give you the injection first.’ Chandni was present, Shakuntala wasn’t there today. I checked Dipnarayan Babu’s pulse—it was quite good. Then I filled the syringe with liver extract and gave him the injection in his arm. It was an intramuscular injection, nothing complicated. But Dipnarayan Babu slowly slumped over. I saw his eyelids growing heavy, closing; he tried to speak but couldn’t. Immediately, I gave him adrenaline, then began artificial respiration. But it was no use—within three or four minutes, his lungs ceased to function.”

The doctor ran his fingers over his chest and fell silent. He was an experienced physician; sudden death was nothing new to him. Yet the shock he had suffered within broke through his stern composure.

Pandeyji asked, “You couldn’t determine the cause of death?”

The doctor replied, “From the symptoms, I thought—anaphylactic shock. But now I see that’s not it.”

“Then what could it be?”

“I can’t say for sure. Perhaps some kind of poison.”

Pandeyji turned his gaze to Byomkesh. Byomkesh said, “Could it have been curare poison?”

The doctor stared at him, eyes wide, then, half to himself, muttered, “Curare! It’s possible. But until there’s a post-mortem, nothing can be said for certain.”

“If he died from curare poisoning, will the post-mortem reveal it?”

“It will. It can be detected in the kidneys.”

Byomkesh asked, “Doctor, the vial you just handed to Pandeyji—what is it?”

The doctor replied, “That’s the liver extract vial. It contains ten doses of the medicine, the mouth of the vial is sealed with rubber. The syringe needle is inserted through the rubber to draw out as much medicine as needed. Today, I took the dose from that very vial and gave the injection.”

Byomkesh said, “Since death occurred immediately after the injection, it can be assumed that—”

--- Page 823 ---
Bahni - Page 521

“It’s possible that the injection itself was the cause of death. So, was there poison in that vial?”

The doctor replied, “What else could it be? Yet—just yesterday evening, I gave an injection to a patient from that very vial, and he’s perfectly fine.”

“Does the vial always stay in your bag?”

“Yes. When one runs out, I replace it with a new vial.”

“All right, tell me, where was your bag last night?”

“It was in the dispensary.”

“When you get a call at night, what do you do? Do you take the bag from the dispensary to visit the patient?”

“No, I keep another bag at home. If there’s a call at night, I take that one.”

“I see. So, when the thief broke into your dispensary last night, this bag was there?”

“Yes.” The doctor started, surprised—“Last night, around seven o’clock, I returned to the dispensary after seeing a patient. There was no time to go home, so I left the bag, told the compounder to lock up, and came straight here.”

“Oh—” Byomkesh pondered for a moment. “Do you know when the compounder locked up the dispensary and left?”

“Of course I do. After hearing about the theft last night, I came back here and sent for the compounder. He said he locked up the clinic and went home right after seven.”

“Very well, have you found out if anything else was stolen from the dispensary?”

“Nothing else was taken. Just a few rupees and some small change from the desk drawer.”

Byomkesh turned his gaze from Pandeyji to Ratikanta and said in a calm voice, “Then the real purpose of the theft is clear.”

Ratikanta had been listening to Byomkesh’s questions and answers with furrowed brow. Perhaps he did not like that an outsider was asking the very questions the police ought to ask. But since Byomkesh was Pandeyji’s friend, he had remained silent. Now, in a rather dry tone, he said, “What purpose is clear?”

Byomkesh replied, “Don’t you see? The thief didn’t come to steal money. He came to switch the liver extract vial.”

Pandeyji said, “Switched it?”

“Or perhaps injected a few drops of liquid curare into the doctor’s vial with a syringe. The result is the same. The thief knew that this morning, Dipnarayan Singh would be given an injection. —Now do you understand?”

For a moment, we all fell silent. Then Pandeyji asked, “Who knew that the injection would be given this morning?”

The doctor said, “Everyone in the house knew. On Sunday mornings, the injection is always given. I give it to him first, then go out to see my patients.”

Byomkesh said, “I knew as well, last night. The doctor had mentioned it. So, no one can be ruled out on that count.”

Inspector Ratikanta spoke up, leaning over the back of Pandeyji’s chair from behind, “Sir—”

--- Page 824 ---
BYOMKESH SAMAGRA

At first, I thought it might have been an accidental death, that Dr. Palit had mistakenly injected the wrong medicine. But now it seems— I want to take charge of this case.”

Pandeyji said, “Of course, it’s your jurisdiction! You take charge. Send the body for post-mortem immediately. And send that vial of medicine to the laboratory for analysis. This matter must be resolved.”

Ratikanta’s face hardened. “Rest assured, sir, I will see it through. Dipnarayanbabu was my elder, my kinsman. Whoever murdered him will not escape my hands.”

Though his words had a touch of theatrics, there was genuine emotion beneath them. Saluting, he was about to leave when Pandeyji said, “Ratikanta, perhaps you don’t know my friend, Shri Byomkesh Bakshi. He’s a famous man, one of our own. He’ll assist you as well.”

Ratikanta looked at Byomkesh. There was a glimmer of surprise in his eyes regarding Byomkesh; now, having learned the truth, it was clear he was not altogether pleased. Slowly, he said, “You are the famous Byomkesh Bakshi? I have read some of your stories, translated into Hindi. Well, if you are willing to take up the investigation—”

Byomkesh quickly interjected, “No, no, you will conduct the investigation. If you need my advice, I’ll help as best I can—nothing more.”

Ratikanta said, “Thank you. To have your help is a matter of fortune. Well, sir, I must go now, arrangements for the body must be made.” Saluting, Ratikanta departed.

We too rose. There was no point in lingering here. Dr. Palit, hesitating, said, “I’ll just go see Shakuntala once. Of course, Miss Manna is with her—”

At that moment, a woman entered from the inner part of the house. Tall, clad in a tightly draped sari, spectacles on her eyes, close to forty, her demeanor radiated firmness of character. Seeing her, Dr. Palit moved towards her. The two began speaking in low voices.

Byomkesh glanced at Pandeyji, who murmured, “Miss Manna.”

After a brief conversation, Miss Manna went back inside, and Dr. Palit returned to us. I noticed a deep furrow of worry on his brow.

Pandeyji asked, “Is there any news? Anything new?”

The doctor replied, “There is news, but it’s not new. I suspected it last night.”

“What did you suspect?”

After a moment’s silence, the doctor said, “Shakuntala is pregnant.”

FOUR

Leaving the house and walking towards the gate, last night’s events came to mind. Dr. Palit’s anxious, searching eyes had followed Shakuntala. He was an experienced doctor; what escaped others’ notice, he had observed. But why had I seen such a shadow of worry and doubt in his eyes? What was he anxious about?

Outside the gate, the doctor was about to get into his car, then—

--- Page 825 ---
He returned to us, thoughtful, and said to Pandeyji, “Dipnarayan Babu died by my hand. If you wish to arrest me, I have nothing to say. Now I must go to see my patients. Summon me to the thana whenever you wish, I will present myself.”

Pandeyji said nothing, only smiled a little. The doctor nodded, climbed into his motorcar, and, starting the engine, departed.

Pandeyji glanced at his wristwatch and said, “It’s not yet half past ten. Come, let us go to my house.”

We were about to get into the car when another motorcar pulled up and stopped. An old, rattling Morris, from which alighted the young Dr. Jagannath Prasad. Seeing us, he made a snorting sound, then, frowning at Pandeyji, said, “You here so early in the morning?”

Jagannath spoke lightly, “I was passing this way to see a patient, thought I’d drop in to see Dipnarayanji. How is he?”

Pandeyji replied in an icy, hard voice, “You know very well how he is. Is there any need for this pretense?”

For a moment, Dr. Jagannath was taken aback, then, baring his teeth in an uncivil grin, said, “So what I heard is true—Pannalal Palit gave Dip Babu an injection and killed him.”

Pandeyji, with great effort to control himself, answered in a measured tone, “Dipnarayan Babu is dead. How he died is none of your concern, you are not this house’s doctor. The house is now under police control. Do not attempt to enter without Inspector Ratikanta Chowdhury’s permission.”

Jagannath cast a bold glance at us and said, “I see you too have joined the ranks of the Bengalis. Join if you must, but if you fall ill, don’t go to a Bengali doctor. Remember Dip Babu’s example.”

Before Pandeyji could reply, Jagannath climbed into his own car and, with a rattle and roar, drove away.

I had never seen Pandeyji angry before; now I saw his fair face flush with rage. He made a strangled sound in his throat and got into the car. We followed.

Within ten minutes we reached Pandeyji’s house. He ordered tea, for in the western chill there is no fixed hour for tea-drinking. Then we went to the baithak-khana and took our seats. Pandeyji asked, “What do you think?”

Byomkesh said, “It is murder, certainly—not an accident. The person who did this is extremely cunning. Now the question is, who profits from Dipnarayan Singh’s death?”

Pandeyji replied, “Only Debnarayan stands to gain. Dipnarayan has died without a son, so all the property now goes to him.”

Byomkesh said, “Whether he died without a son is not yet certain—Shakuntala Devi’s child may be his. But perhaps Debnarayan did not know this.”

Pandeyji said, “It is quite possible he did not. Only shortly before his death did Dipnarayan Singh himself perhaps learn the truth.”

--- Page 826 ---
Byomkesh shook his head and said, “If he had known, would he have kept quiet? Anyway, let’s suppose he didn’t know, that Shakuntala didn’t tell her husband. Then what does that mean? Debnarayan murdered his uncle out of greed for the entire property. He didn’t do it with his own hands—he doesn’t have the brains for it.”

Pandeyji said, “Last night, when we went to Dipnarayan’s house at a quarter past seven, Debnarayan was at home.”

Byomkesh replied, “With a body like an elephant’s, he surely didn’t go to the doctor’s chamber himself. But someone else could have gone, at the master’s bidding. His cronies—”

Tea arrived. Byomkesh took a small sip from his cup, lit a cigarette, and, his tone tinged with speculative musing, said, “But if Debnarayan didn’t send his uncle on a journey to the Ganges, then who could have done it? Who stands to gain?”

Pandeyji said, “I can’t see anyone else profiting. But that rascal Ghoda Jagannath—there’s nothing he can’t do. They’d do anything to disgrace Bengali doctors.”

Byomkesh smiled. “You seem to have a real grudge against Ghoda Jagannath. They’re all rats and owls, no courage for murder among them. The one who did this has a different nature—bold to the point of recklessness, yet cunning; educated, yet ruthless; versed in science, with some knowledge of medicine—”

Pandeyji said, “Your description fits Ghoda Jagannath to a tee.”

Byomkesh replied, “Ghoda Jagannath’s motive isn’t strong enough. Of course, if he had some other motive, that’s a different matter. Tell me, if you don’t mind—Shakuntala Devi is beautiful and modern, surely she has admirers in Patna?”

Pandeyji said, “Oh, she does. I’ve heard that every evening, two or three wealthy, modern young men would gather for adda at Dipnarayan’s house. Bridge, tea and cake, laughter, songs—that sort of thing. Ghoda Jagannath loves mingling with the rich, he was part of their circle too. But about six months ago, when Dipnarayan fell ill, their gatherings broke up. One or two would drop by now and then to check in. Narmadashankar—”

“Narmadashankar? Who’s he?”

“A rich man’s good-for-nothing son. From Allahabad. Has a zamindari in Bihar. Not just a wastrel—a scoundrel. His name’s in the police records. Once, on a hunting trip, he ran off with a village girl. The affair became quite a scandal, but then he paid off the girl’s father and had the case quashed—”

“Narmadashankar used to visit Dipnarayan Singh’s house?”

“Yes, Narmadashankar is very dapper and polished on the outside, good-looking, smooth talker. But at heart, a thorough rogue.” Pandeyji made a gesture of distaste. “Female independence is all very well, but the trouble is, it’s hard to keep these well-dressed scoundrels at bay.”

“Hmm. Was Shakuntala Devi very close with these people?”

“She was. But I never heard any real scandal about her. Those who couldn’t reach so high would joke and gossip among themselves, make snide remarks—that’s all.”

“It’s our nature—the grapes are always sour, bitter with envy.” Byomkesh drained his teacup and stood up. “Well, let’s be going, then. Are you headed that way again?”

“I’ll go in the afternoon. If you come too—”

--- Page 827 ---
Bohni-Patanga 525

"Of course, I shall go. It’s necessary to prod the household a bit, see how they react."

Five

Before the clock struck four in the afternoon, Pandeyji arrived with the car. He said, "Let’s go, we’ll stop by the thana first. The post-mortem report might have come in."

The three of us reached the police station. It stood in the heart of the city. Ratikanta was present, and with due courtesy, he led us in and seated us in his office. He said, "I’ve just received the post-mortem report—curare has been found. There’s no doubt about the cause of death."

Pandeyji glanced over the report and asked, "And the report on the medicines?"

"That hasn’t come yet. I’ve sent an urgent reminder. Most likely, we’ll have it tonight. Without the medicine report, we can’t begin a proper investigation. Still, I’ve set men to inquire if anyone is smuggling curare."

Pandeyji nodded. "You’ve done right. The thief from whom a vial of curare was once seized—he’s still in jail, isn’t he? If we press him, perhaps we’ll learn who’s running the curare racket."

"Yes, sir. I’ve checked—he’s not in Patna jail now, but in Buxar jail. I’m arranging a meeting with him. Meanwhile, I’ve interrogated Dr. Palit’s compounder."

"Did you get anything?"

"Nothing at all. On the other front, I’ve ordered everyone in Dipnarayanji’s house to remain at home. No outsiders are allowed in, and no one is to leave, except for the manager Gangadhar and his son Liladhar."

Pandeyji said, "We’re heading there now. Will you join us?"

Ratikanta hesitated a moment, then said, "You go ahead. I have an urgent matter to attend to." Then, smiling at Byomkesh, he asked, "Have you formed any impression?"

Byomkesh replied, "No. But it seems to me that no one in the house can be excluded from suspicion."

Ratikanta said, "Not just the family—even the State’s employees cannot be ruled out. Everyone must be examined under a microscope."

Byomkesh asked quietly, "What do you make of Dr. Palit?"

Ratikanta shot a glance at Byomkesh. "Dr. Palit! But he—if he had any motive, would he have done it with his own hands?"

Byomkesh smiled faintly. "Precisely because he did it with his own hands, suspicion will fall on him the least."

We returned to the car and took our seats. Byomkesh asked, "Is Dr. Palit’s dispensary nearby?"

Pandeyji replied, "Just a little way off, on this very road. Shall we stop there?"

"Let’s. We should see the real scene of the crime."

Within two or three minutes, we reached Dr. Palit’s clinic. It, too, stood on the main road, surrounded by shops—no residences nearby. On a winter’s night, by eight o’clock, all the shops are shuttered. Then, for a thief, breaking a lock and slipping inside would be no trouble at all.

--- Page 828 ---
The doctor’s chamber was utterly unremarkable. Two rooms, one in front and one behind; in the front, patients would come and sit, while the doctor sat in the inner room. The compounder prepared medicines in that same inner room.

Both the compounder and the doctor were present, and a few patients were seated in the outer room. When we arrived, we saw the doctor in the inner room, pressing the stomach of a patient who lay stretched out on a long, narrow table. Turning his head and seeing us, he smiled slightly. “What is it, have you come to arrest me?”

Pandeyji replied, “No, no, just came to have a look.”

“Please, sit.”

We gathered around the doctor’s table and took our seats. The doctor finished examining the patient, then sat at the table and wrote out a prescription, which he handed to the compounder. In the meantime, we observed the compounder—a thin, hollow-cheeked Bihari youth. Though his name was Khublal, his skin was very dark. At the sight of Pandeyji in his uniform, the darkness of his face seemed to deepen further.

The doctor said, “What would you like to see?”

Pandeyji glanced at Byomkesh, who said, “Where is the lock that the thief broke to get in?”

The doctor said, “Khublal, bring the lock.”

Khublal had been standing at one end of the room before a shelf crowded with bottles and vials, preparing medicines. We could see only his back, but even without seeing his face, it was clear from his posture that he was listening intently to our conversation. At the doctor’s command, he came over and, with trembling hands, placed the lock on the table, then returned to his medicines.

The lock was cheap and ordinary; one could break it instantly by inserting an iron rod and twisting—no great strength required. That’s exactly what had happened—the hinge of the lock had snapped and come off. Byomkesh turned the lock over in his hands, examined it, then set it down.

“They broke the lock on your desk drawer as well, didn’t they?”

“There was no need to break the drawer; it’s always left open. The key’s been lost for ages.”

Palit opened the drawer to show us—there was nothing inside but a few scraps of paper. Palit said, “A thief grows wiser after a theft. I’m being careful now—someone will sleep here in the dispensary every night from now on. I’ve thrown out all the old medicines and brought in new stock. You never know.”

Pandeyji nodded in approval. Byomkesh said, “May I ask Khublal a few questions?”

The doctor replied, “Go ahead. Though he’s already been questioned once—Inspector Chowdhury grilled him thoroughly. Khublal!”

Khublal came and stood nearby. Licking his lips, he said in a broken voice, “Huzoor, I am innocent.”

Byomkesh spoke in a reassuring tone, “Why are you afraid? If you’ve done nothing wrong, what is there to fear? No one will harm you.”

Khublal said, “Sir, I am a poor man—”

Byomkesh asked, “How much salary do you get?”

Khublal cast a furtive glance at the doctor and replied, “Sixty rupees, sir. And a ten-rupee allowance.”

“Nothing extra?”

--- Page 829 ---
Bohni - Patanga 527

Khublal, wide-eyed with fear, replied, “No, sir.”

“Who lives in your house?”

“My wife and a child.”

“How much rent do you pay?”

“Twelve and a half rupees.”

“Seventy rupees is enough for you to get by?”

Khublal cast a furtive glance at the doctor again. “We manage to get by, huzoor. Doctorbabu has said he’ll raise my pay by five rupees from January.”

Byomkesh looked at the doctor; the doctor nodded. Byomkesh then said, “Alright, let’s leave that aside. Last night, at what time did you close the dispensary?”

“Sir, I didn’t check the clock. Doctorbabu finished seeing patients and returned, left his bag, and went out again. That must have been around seven. Five or ten minutes later, I locked up the dispensary and went home.”

“Was there any patient here at that time?”

“No, huzoor.”

“Alright. Last night, do you know how much money was in the drawer?”

A shadow of anxiety crossed Khublal’s face once more. He said, “I didn’t hear, huzoor, but I think there were about three rupees and a few annas. While Doctorbabu was away, a few patients came with old prescriptions. I gave them medicine, took the money, and put it in the drawer.”

“Did you lock the door properly?”

“Yes, sir.”

“Do you keep the key with you at night?”

“Yes, sir. In the morning, I come first and open the dispensary.”

“Do you know Dr. Jagannath Prasad?”

Khublal faltered, then finally replied in a faint voice, “Yes, sir.”

Byomkesh gazed at him, eyebrows raised, for a while. “Are you close to Jagannath?”

Khublal cried out in distress, “No, sir. I am a poor man, he is a doctor. But—but—”

“But what?”

“He called me to his house a few days ago—”

“And then?”

“He—he told me to leave this job.”

The doctor said in surprise, “This is the first I’m hearing of it. Why didn’t you tell me?”

Khublal remained silent, like a guilty man.

Byomkesh said, “So why didn’t you quit? Dr. Jagannath would have given you another job.”

Khublal said, “He didn’t say he’d give me another job, sir. He just told me to leave this one. I refused, and then he threatened me, said—if I didn’t resign, I’d land in trouble.”

“Still you didn’t quit?”

With tearful eyes and a choked voice, Khublal replied, “Huzoor, Doctor Palit is like my father and mother. As long as he keeps me, I won’t leave him. Such a kind man—” Khublal’s eyes...

--- Page 830 ---
528  
Byomkesh Samagra

began to wipe his eyes. Byomkesh said kindly, “All right, you may go now, get on with your work.”  
We stood up. Dr. Palit accompanied us to the car, talking as he walked.  
“He’s a good boy, Khublal. But—now and then he’ll pilfer a few coins, slip a couple of vitamin tablets or a strip or two of quinine into his pocket to take home; that’s nothing, all compounders do it. I don’t think he’s involved in anything serious like this.”  
As Byomkesh settled into the car, he suddenly leaned out and asked, “Doctorbabu, how many months pregnant is Shakuntala Devi?”  
Dr. Palit looked at Byomkesh with steady eyes and replied, “Three months.”  
Byomkesh said, “You were very surprised.”  
“It’s only natural to be surprised,” he said, and turned to go.

Six

From the doctor’s chamber to Dipnarayan Singh’s house was a five-minute drive by motor. In those five minutes, not a word passed between us. Each of us remained deep in thought.  
We stopped the car outside the gate and got down. In the gateway, a constable was sitting on a stool; he sprang up and saluted Pandeyji.  
Byomkesh said, “Come, let’s take a look around the outside of the compound.”  
As I’ve mentioned before, the house was surrounded on all sides by high walls, like a jail. We walked once around the perimeter, close to the wall. In front was the main road; on both sides and at the back, mango and jackfruit orchards. In this area, mango and jackfruit orchards were plentiful, and all of them belonged to Dipnarayan.  
Perhaps once there had been a neighborhood here, but Dipnarayan’s ancestors had gradually acquired the entire para and turned it into fruit orchards. Now, Dipnarayan’s house was the only one left in the area. Yet there was no lack of modern, scientific conveniences; electric and telephone wires crossed over the wall and entered the house. There was even a red mailbox, standing guard at one corner of the wall like a sepoy in a red tunic. If you wanted to send a letter, you didn’t have to go far.  
I don’t know what Byomkesh saw as we circled the wall; there was nothing of note to my eyes. On the sides and at the back, the mango and jackfruit trees pressed right up to the wall, and along the edge of the field ran a narrow footpath. From the direction of the mailbox, if you went along the side, there was a small wicket gate—probably the servants’ entrance. Other than this, there was no other way through the side or back walls.  
The wicket gate was open, and we entered through it. As Byomkesh went in, he examined the door carefully. It was an old-fashioned, squat, sturdy door, with thick planks studded with heavy nails; yet for all that, the two leaves of the door had become rickety. A chain hung near the top, probably to lock the gate at night.  
Byomkesh’s curiosity about the wicket gate struck me as a little odd; I couldn’t quite fathom the direction of his thoughts. In any case, once inside, we saw a row of rooms built right up against the wall. These were the offices, where the zamindar’s clerks sat and did the serestha’s work. Seeing us, a man emerged from inside and

--- Page 831 ---
Bohni - Patanga 529

He arrived. I had seen the man last night—the manager, Gangadhar Banshi, with a turban wound around his head.

He advanced swiftly, stopping near the back door, and introductions were made.

Pandeyji said, “I’m just taking a look around the place.”

Though a question flickered in the experienced eyes of the manager, he merely said, “Very well, very well. Come, let me show you.”

Byomkesh pointed toward the back door and asked, “Tell me, is this door always left open?”

The manager looked a little uncomfortable, scratched his neck, and replied, “Eh—can’t say for sure. I think it’s kept shut at night. But why do you ask?”

Byomkesh said, “Pure curiosity.”

At this moment, a servant was seen at the back of the house. The manager raised his hand and called out to him. When the servant approached, he asked, “Bishun, is the back door kept shut at night?”

Bishun also scratched his neck. “I don’t really know, huzoor! I think the chain is put up. The chowkidar would know.”

“Call the chowkidar.” Bishun went off to fetch the chowkidar.

Byomkesh asked, “Does the chowkidar keep watch over the house at night?”

The manager replied, “Yes, sir. There’s a darwan at the main gate, and two chowkidars take turns keeping watch.”

Soon after, Bishun returned with a chowkidar in tow. The chowkidar looked like a skeleton in a palm-leaf uniform, but with his enormous mustache and sideburns, he tried to lend his gaunt face the fierce air typical of his kind. His eyes, perhaps from sleeplessness or the effects of ganja, were red as cranberries. The manager questioned him, “Gajadhar Singh, is the back door kept open at night, or is it shut?”

Gajadhar replied in a broken voice, “Sahib, sometimes it’s open, sometimes the chain is fastened.”

Byomkesh asked, “Is it ever locked?”

Gajadhar said, “No, huzoor, there used to be a lock long ago, but now it’s broken. But there’s nothing to fear—we two brothers keep such a watch that not even a mouse could sneak in.”

“Really! And how do you keep watch?”

“From ten at night, huzoor, the watch begins. From ten to two, one of us keeps guard, and from two to six, the other. There’s a bell in the gateway; when it rings, we get up and make a round, then the bell rings again and we make another round. In this way, we keep circling the house all night, sahib.”

“Then, between the ringing of the bells, if someone were to go out from inside, or come in from outside, you wouldn’t know?”

“Who would come in from outside, huzoor? Who has ten heads on his shoulders?”

“I see. You may go now.”

When Gajadhar had left, manager Gangadhar Banshi said in a tone of justification, “There’s no need for very strict security in this house; the thieves and ruffians know there’s a darwan and chowkidar here—if they’re caught, there’s no escape. Not a twig has ever been stolen.”

Byomkesh said, “I wasn’t thinking about theft. Anyway, let’s go and take a look over there.”

--- Page 832 ---
Afterwards, Gangadhar Banshi took us around, showing us every corner. In between, he expressed his condolences to the late master; without being asked, he tried to find out the cause of death. But we did not indulge his curiosity, instead conducting a thorough, silent inspection with deep concentration.

In front of the house was a flower garden, and behind it, a patch of leafy vegetables. The house itself was two-storied, with a polished, gleaming exterior, standing on nearly seven or eight kathas of land. On either side of the house, two iron spiral staircases led to the upper floor. The sweepers and cleaners used these stairs to keep the upper story tidy, for Patna had not yet been blessed with proper drains.

Our inspection over, we returned to the main hall and found that Inspector Ratikanta had arrived in the meantime. He glanced at Byomkesh with a slight smile and asked, “What were you looking at in the garden? Did you find anything?”

Byomkesh replied, “Nothing much. Only that it’s clear anyone from the house could open the back door at night and let an outsider in.”

Ratikanta stared at him for a moment. “But—does that have any connection to the present affair?”

“It might, or it might not. Come, let’s go meet the members of the household—”

We were about to enter the house when, at the sound of a sputtering engine, we turned to see a motorbike approaching from the gate. The rider was a stranger; his features were handsome, his age about thirty-five. He wore white flannel trousers, a deep blue woolen cricket coat, a red woolen muffler at his throat, and a blood-red cricket cap on his head. The very image of a sportsman—one would think he had just returned from the cricket field.

The gleaming new Sunbeam came to a halt before us, and the rider dismounted with practiced ease. Seeing the deep frown on the foreheads of Pandeyji and Ratikanta, I guessed that, however great a sportsman he might be, he was not a favorite of the police. The next moment, hearing Pandeyji’s greeting, I had no doubt that this was the notorious woman-stealer, Narmada Shankar.

Pandeyji said, “Narmada Shankar Babu, what brings you here?”

Narmada Shankar offered a polite namaskar and replied, “I heard on the cricket field that Dipnarayan Babu has died suddenly. I was told it was not a natural death. I couldn’t stay away after hearing that. I rushed over. What happened, Mr. Pandey?”

Pandeyji answered in a dry voice, “Forgive me, but there can be no discussion with you on this matter. But you haven’t said what your business is here.”

Narmada Shankar’s face grew mournful as he said, “What business could I have, except to inquire after a friend in his hour of misfortune? I can well imagine what a terrible shock Shakuntala must have suffered. Just last night I saw her, the very image of happiness! Who could have thought— May I see her, just once?”

“Why do you want to meet her?”

“To offer my condolences, to say a few words of comfort—nothing more. Surely you know I am quite close to Shakuntala.” At the mention of Shakuntala’s name, a glint flickered in Narmada Shankar’s eyes; none of us missed it.

Pandeyji, his voice tight with suppressed irritation, said, “Forgive me, but no one will be allowed to see Shakuntala Devi. This is not the time for such formalities. —Ratikanta, tell the constable at the gate not to let anyone in without our permission.”

--- Page 833 ---
Bohni - Patanga 531

Pandeji’s hint was so unmistakable that a different kind of glint flickered in Narmadashankar’s eyes—a glint of crooked rage. Yet, with utmost politeness, he said, “Very well, then. You will convey my condolences to Shakuntala. I shall take my leave now. Namaste.”

Narmadashankar’s motorbike sputtered away into the distance. Ratikanta, gazing after his receding figure with a look of deep aversion, muttered under his breath, “Sly devil!” Then he strode off to issue orders to the constable at the gate.

Byomkesh turned to the manager. “Did you happen to notice what time Narmadashankar Babu arrived for dinner last night?”

The manager replied, “I can’t say exactly when he arrived, but at half past six, I saw him sitting with Shakuntala Devi, chatting. No other guests had arrived by then.”

“Excuse me, where do you live?”

The manager pointed across the road, indicating, “There’s a house in that mango grove, the estate’s house—I stay there.”

“Are there any other houses in the nearby mango groves?”

“No, sir. There are no other houses in this area.”

“Very well. Did you see Dipnarayan Babu this morning before his death?”

Manager Gangadhar Banshi shook his head with some irritation. “No, sir. The doctor had arrived before me. On Sundays, the office is closed, so I come a bit late. When I arrived, everything was already over.”

Seven

When Ratikanta returned, we all entered the house together. The hall was shrouded in shadowy gloom, not a soul in sight. The five of us stepped inside and looked at one another’s faces.

Byomkesh said, “Manager Babu, we’ve detained you for quite some time. Surely you have other work—”

The manager shook his head. “I have no work today. It’s Sunday, the office is closed. I came only out of habit.”

It was clear he would not leave our company. He listened to our conversation with deep concentration, striving to grasp its significance. His eyes, dark and honeyed like bumblebees, flitted over our faces. Yet he himself was sparing with words. A fish from deep waters.

Pandeji cast a sidelong glance at Byomkesh and said, “By the way, Banshi-ji, are the estate’s accounts all in order? We might need to examine them.”

Banshi-ji responded at once, “All the accounts are in order. You may inspect them whenever you wish.” Then, hesitating a little, he added, “Except for one transaction that hasn’t been settled—”

“What transaction?”

The manager replied, “Eight or ten days ago, Dipnarayanji summoned me and ordered that twelve thousand rupees be given to Dr. Palit. The money was handed over to the doctor, but the receipt—”

--- Page 834 ---
532  
Byomkesh Samagra

‘No receipt was taken.’

‘Why wasn’t a receipt taken?’

‘The doctor had asked for the money as a loan, but Dipnarayanji had decided to reward the doctor with the money, so he forbade taking a receipt.’

‘Oh—’ Byomkesh furrowed his brow and remained silent for a while. Then he said to Ratikanta, ‘Now let’s question everyone in the house. Where are they?’

Ratikanta replied, ‘They’re all upstairs. All the bedrooms are above. Please sit, I’ll call them down one by one. Whom should I call first—Shakuntala Devi?’

Byomkesh said, ‘There’s no need to trouble Shakuntala Devi, we’ll go upstairs ourselves. We only have a few ordinary questions to ask. Debnarayan Babu is probably upstairs too?’

‘Yes. Chandni Devi is there as well.’

‘Then let’s go.’  
A staircase led upwards from a small room beside us. We ascended the stairs.

At the top was a room, with two doors on either side. The upper floor was divided into two parts. As we reached the landing, Ratikanta said, ‘Which way will you go? This side is Debnarayan Babu’s quarters, that side is Dipnarayan Babu’s.’

Byomkesh hesitated, undecided about which way to go, when suddenly the door to Debnarayan’s side opened and Chandni emerged. In her hand was a bowl of milk; her face was swollen from crying. Seeing us, she stood still, embarrassed, instinctively reaching to adjust her veil, then remembering the new customs of the house, she stopped. Spotting the manager, Gangadhar Banshi, among us, she addressed him in a trembling voice, ‘Chachiji hasn’t taken a drop of water all day… so I’m going to try once more, maybe I can get her to drink a little milk. Chachaji is gone, and if she too dies without eating, what will become of us?’ With that, she broke down into tears.

We were taken aback. None of us seemed prepared to witness this image of intimate, domestic care. Gangadhar Banshi, visibly unsettled, cleared his throat and said, ‘Go, child, try to get her to eat something first. How can she go on without food?’

Chandni, still wiping her eyes, left with the milk. Byomkesh said, ‘Let’s go see Debnarayan Babu first.’

We entered Debnarayan’s quarters, the manager leading the way.

Room after room, all crammed with furniture from home and abroad; but nothing was in order, everything was a jumble, chaotic. At last, we reached the far end of the house, before a door veiled with a curtain.

We had not yet seen who was inside, but our collective footsteps drew someone’s attention—a man pulled aside the curtain and peeked out, then quickly vanished. We entered the room. It was quite large, with windows on three sides. Half the floor was covered with thick bedding, over which lay a Persian rug, and on that, several large cushions. Debnarayan sat in the middle, surrounded by cushions, and just behind him, a little to the side, was Beni Prasad, the jester, with curly hair and a curled moustache.

Seeing us, Beni Prasad stood up.

The manager addressed Debnarayan, ‘They have come to see you.’ Debnarayan said nothing, but stared at us, bewildered, like a stunned frog.

--- Page 835 ---
Bohni - Patanga 533

The manager asked us to sit. Byomkesh and I took our seats beside the bed. The others remained standing.

Byomkesh glanced around and said, “There was someone else in this room—the one who peeked through the curtain—where did he go?”

Beniprasad was visibly flustered. “He—you mean Liladhar—” He shot a quick, nervous glance at the manager and finished, “He’s gone to the next room.”

Byomkesh inquired, with the innocence of a good man, “What’s in the next room?”

Beniprasad replied, “That is—the bathroom!”

Byomkesh chuckled softly. “I see. There’s a spiral iron staircase attached to the bathroom, and Mr. Liladhar has left the house through that way. Am I right?”

Beniprasad did not answer. He scratched his hip, casting a sidelong glance at the manager.

We had learned last night that Liladhar was the son of manager Gangadhar Banshi and the assistant jester to Debnarayan. I noticed Gangadhar Banshi’s face had turned dark with anger, but he restrained his rising emotion as best he could and asked Beniprasad, “What are you all doing here?”

Abandoning his hip, Beniprasad raised one hand and began scratching his armpit. He said, “Well—the young master was feeling low, so we were just—”

At the mention of feeling low, perhaps Debnarayan was reminded of his uncle’s death. Suddenly, he burst into tears with a wild, animalistic wail. The man, as huge as an elephant, began to sob, his mouth agape as if to swallow the sky and the earth.

Yesterday, we had heard Debnarayan’s laughter; today, we heard his weeping. The sound was nearly the same, as though a pack of jackals were howling.

After five minutes, the crying ceased as abruptly as it had begun. Dabbing his eyes with a handkerchief, Debnarayan scooped a fistful of betel leaves from the silver box and began to chew. Byomkesh, who had been calmly studying the Ravi Varma painting on the wall, spoke in a casual tone once the sobbing stopped, “Debnarayanbabu, do you drink alcohol?”

Debnarayanbabu replied, “No. I take bhang.”

“Then what’s that under your pillow?” Byomkesh pointed.

Meanwhile, Beniprasad had been edging diagonally toward the bathroom door; now, he slipped away in a flash. I looked where Byomkesh had indicated and, turning over the pillow, found a corked bottle beneath it, filled with a milky white liquid.

With a foolish expression, Debnarayan glanced at the bottle and said, “Oh, that’s tadi. Liladhar and Beniprasad were drinking it.”

Tadi in a bottle! I had never seen such a thing before. Byomkesh said, “So—they were drinking tadi to cheer you up! Well, let that be. Tell me, besides bhang, do you indulge in any other intoxicants?”

With a mouthful of tobacco, Debnarayan replied, “Nothing else.”

“Cocaine?”

“Cocaine? No.”

“Ganja?”

“No. Gajadhar smokes ganja.”

--- Page 836 ---
534  
Byomkesh Samagra

"All right, let him go. —You must have many friends, I suppose?"  
"Friends—I have them. Friends by the millions."  
"Is that so? Name a few, will you?"  
"Names? Liladhar—Beniprasad—Gajadhar Singh—"  
"Which Gajadhar Singh?"  
"The chowkidar. He’s very good at grinding bhang."  
"And who else?"  
"And Badrilal. He massages my feet every day."  
Byomkesh said, "I see. You’re not friends with Dr. Palit?"  
Debnarayan’s massive body gave a sudden shudder; in a dazed voice he said, "Dr. Palit. I won’t keep him, I’ll throw him out. He killed uncle."  
Byomkesh sat for a while, brows furrowed, eyes closed, then opened his eyes and asked,  
"After your uncle’s death, you’ve inherited the entire property. What will you do now?"  
"What will I do?"—Debnarayan looked around as if the thought had never occurred to him before. I was astonished, wondering, is Debnarayan truly such a simpleton?  
Byomkesh stood up and said, "Let’s go, there’s nothing more to learn from him."  
As we turned toward the door, I saw Chandni had come and was standing by the curtain, her face etched with anxiety and apprehension. The moment we looked at her, she slipped away in a flash.  
We exchanged glances. Ratikanta asked Pandeyji in a low voice, "Shall we question Chandni Devi?"  
Pandeyji looked at Byomkesh. Byomkesh thought for a moment and said, "We’ll see later. For now, let’s go to Shakuntala Devi’s quarters."

Eight

On the way from Debnarayan’s quarters to Shakuntala’s, the manager Gangadhar Banshi suddenly took his leave of us. His mind, perhaps, was troubled about his son Liladhar, otherwise he would not have left us so easily. He said, "It’s time for my evening prayers, I must go now. You carry on."  
He went down the stairs. We entered Shakuntala Devi’s quarters. Evening darkness had begun to thicken; Ratikanta, flicking switches and lighting lamps as he went, led the way.  
First, a medium-sized room. In the Bengali style, a mattress spread over a low cot, a few cushioned, low chairs, in the corner a tall table with a silver vase of flowers. On the wall, a painting by Jamini Roy. Here, the family could spend the evening in adda, or seat intimate friends if they came.  
The room was empty. We passed through it and entered the adjoining room. This was a rather large chamber, two beds set against opposite walls. There was a big wardrobe, and on a dressing table with a mirror, several medicine bottles. It seemed this had been Dipnarayan’s bedroom.  
Now, both beds were covered with sujani quilts. This room too was empty. Byomkesh, in a low voice,

--- Page 837 ---
Bohni - Patanga 535

He said, “This must have been Dipnarayan Babu’s bedroom. But why are there two beds?”

Ratikanta hesitated a little before replying, “When Dipnarayanji’s illness was at its worst, a nurse used to stay here at night.”

“Of course, I should have guessed.”

After that, we entered the third room and, looking around, were struck with wonder. This room was even larger, illuminated by a bluish neon light. On the back wall, three windows stood at equal intervals, and between them hung exquisitely painted, costly Egyptian carpets. On one side of the room was a fireplace, and around it, various musical instruments hung on the wall. On the other side were all sorts of painting supplies, and on the wall, a wide oil painting. The floor was covered with a thick velvet carpet, and at its center, like a princess with regal hips, lay a tanpura. It took no time to realize this was the artistic sanctuary of Shakuntala, the mistress of the house. The sight was soothing to the eyes. The difference in taste and sense of beauty between the two wings of the same house was so stark, one would not believe it without seeing for oneself.

Byomkesh’s gaze was immediately drawn to the oil painting on the wall. Without looking anywhere else, he walked up and stood before it. The painting was three feet high, five feet wide. The subject was not new: Shakuntala, clad in bark, was watering plants in the forest, while Dushyanta, hidden behind the trunk of a tree, watched her in secret. The style of the painting was good—Shakuntala’s arms and legs were not stick-thin, nor did Dushyanta resemble the villain Dushasan from a jatra troupe. The atmosphere of the painting was ancient, but the two figures were timeless. The painting filled the heart with satisfaction.

Turning my eyes to Byomkesh, I saw him absorbed in the painting. Following his example, Ratikanta and Pandeyji came and stood beside us. Byomkesh then turned to them, his voice brimming with enthusiasm, and said, “A marvelous painting. Who painted it?”

Pandeyji looked at Ratikanta, who replied hesitantly, “I think it was painted by Shakuntala Devi herself. I can’t say for certain.”

Byomkesh turned again to the painting and said, “That must be it. The Shakuntala of our time has painted the Shakuntala of another age. Do you see, Ajit, the serene simplicity on the face of the hermitage maiden Shakuntala, the enchanted longing in Dushyanta’s eyes, the vibrant greenery of the forest glade? Together, they create a rare harmony of worldly life and ascetic retreat. If only it were possible to take this painting away with me.”

I was a little surprised. Byomkesh might have an appreciation for art, but I had never seen him so moved. Seeing me staring at him, eyes wide, he collected himself; turning away from the painting, he cast his eyes around the room. Remembering Shakuntala Devi’s present condition, he said in a slightly pained voice, “This must be Shakuntala Devi’s room for music, painting… a cultivated garden… tools for forgetting—” He sighed and said, “Let’s go.”

After that, we passed through another empty room and a veranda, and arrived before Shakuntala’s bedroom. The door was ajar; when Ratikanta knocked, a middle-aged maid opened it. Ratikanta craned his neck inside and said in a hesitant voice, “We’ve come from the police to ask a few questions.”

A moment later, a muffled voice came from within, “Come in.”

We entered the room, somewhat self-conscious. Ratikanta nodded to the maid, who silently withdrew.

--- Page 838 ---
I shall not attempt to describe Shakuntala Devi’s bedchamber. It was the very thing that is born when impeccable taste is wedded to inexhaustible wealth. Shakuntala was seated upon the bed; as we entered, she drew a cream-colored Kashmiri shawl about her shoulders. Only her face remained uncovered. Her complexion was pale as wax, dark circles shadowed her eyes. Her hair was loose and disheveled—like dew-soaked, fallen shefali blossoms.

“Please sit,” Shakuntala lifted her weary, submissive eyes to us for a moment.

There were a few low, leather-cushioned stools in the room; Byomkesh and I dragged two of them close to the bed and sat down. Ratikanta and Pandeyji remained standing, hands resting on the bed’s edge. Byomkesh glanced at Pandeyji, silently seeking permission; Pandeyji gave a slight nod. Then, in a voice of utmost gentleness, Byomkesh addressed Shakuntala, “Forgive us for disturbing you at such a time. No one knows when calamity will strike in a man’s life, and so there is no way to be prepared beforehand. I met your husband only once, but it is clear to me what a good man he was. We assure you, whoever is responsible for his death will not escape justice.”

Shakuntala did not reply; she lifted her anguished eyes and silently thanked Byomkesh.

Byomkesh continued, “I must ask you a few questions—only out of necessity, not to trouble you. But before I come to the main point, allow me a trivial query: the painting of Dushyanta and Shakuntala on the wall of the other room—did you paint it?”

A flash of surprise lit Shakuntala’s eyes; she merely inclined her head—yes, the painting was her own work.

Byomkesh said, “It’s a marvelous piece—you have genuine artistic talent. But let us move on. Do you know if Dipnarayan Babu left a will?”

This time, Shakuntala gazed at him for a moment with uncomprehending eyes, then replied in a faint voice, “I know nothing of such matters. He never spoke to me about property or wealth.”

“Do you have any property of your own?”

“I don’t know that either. But—”

“But what?”

“After our marriage, my husband deposited five lakh rupees in the bank in my name.”

“Is that so? Where is that money now?”

“It’s still in the bank. I have never touched it.”

Byomkesh fell silent for a moment, deep in thought.

“Then this five lakh is your own stridhan. If you bear a son, he will inherit half the joint property.”

Shakuntala did not raise her eyes, remaining bowed. It seemed to me her face had grown even more bloodless, drained of all color.

Byomkesh asked, “Tell me, did your husband know that you are with child?”

Shakuntala’s downcast lips trembled slightly. “He knew. I told him last night.”

“Last night? Before dinner, or after?”

“After. He had already gone to bed.”

“He must have been very happy to hear the news!”

“He was overjoyed—he was beside himself with happiness—”

--- Page 839 ---
So far, Shakuntala’s demeanor suddenly changed. Until now, she had been speaking in a weary, subdued manner; now, in a wave of terror-stricken bewilderment, she looked at each of our faces in turn, then uttered a strangled, plaintive cry and collapsed in a faint.

For a moment, we were all struck dumb. None of us had noticed that Chandni had come and was standing by the door. Now she rushed forward, cradled Shakuntala’s head in her lap, and, casting an angry glance at us, said, “What sort of people are you, do you want to kill her? Go, leave this room at once. Have you no pity or compassion in your hearts? Send for Miss Manna immediately.”

We found no way to escape fast enough. As we descended the stairs, we heard Chandni calling out in a loud voice to the maid, “Somoria, where have you gone—bring water, quickly—”

Downstairs, Pandeyji was the first to telephone Miss Manna. “Please come at once. We’ll wait here until you arrive.”

After that, we sat in the hall, waiting. Night had fallen; the clock struck seven.

Pandeyji said, “Ratikanta, go see if Shakuntala Devi has regained consciousness.”

Ratikanta left. Byomkesh sat with a troubled face; raising his eyes, he said, “Pandeyji, it’s necessary for Miss Manna to stay with Shakuntala Devi for a few days. Please make the arrangements. She must remain with Shakuntala at all times, not leave her side for a moment.”

“Very well.”

At that moment, the manager, Gangadhar, returned and, hearing of Shakuntala’s fainting spell, expressed his concern. Pandeyji said, “Arrange for Miss Manna to stay here for a few days. Shakuntala Devi is pregnant, and now this calamity has befallen her. She needs a doctor by her side at all hours.”

The manager’s face changed in a peculiar way. Then, composing himself, he replied, “Of course, of course.”

Miss Manna arrived, a medical bag in hand. When everything was explained to her briefly, she said, “Very well, I’ll stay. I’ll just have to have some of my things brought over.”

She quickly went upstairs.

Ten minutes later, Ratikanta came down and said, “She’s regained consciousness. Doctor Manna says there’s nothing to worry about.”

Pandeyji stood up.

“We’ll take our leave now. Ratikanta, finish up here and then come by my house.”

Turning to us, he said, “Come, have some tea at my place.”

Nine

As we drove in the motorcar, the scene in Shakuntala’s bedroom floated before my eyes. It felt as though I had just witnessed a profound moment from a heart-rending drama. If Shakuntala had not fainted, and if Chandni had not entered and broken the spell—

Why did Shakuntala suddenly lose consciousness? Of course, in such circumstances, a fainting spell at any moment is not unusual, but was the intensity of her grief the only cause?

I looked at Byomkesh; he seemed lost in the depths of thought. I asked,

--- Page 840 ---
“Are you thinking about Shakuntala’s swoon?”

He roused himself and replied, “The swoon? No—I was thinking about the letterbox.”

In surprise, I said, “You were thinking about the letterbox!”

He said, “Yes, the letterbox at the corner of Dipnarayan’s house. It’s in a very peculiar spot. When you look at it, it seems like a plump little sepoy in a red coat, standing guard at the street corner. But that’s not what it really is.”

“What is it, then?”

“In truth, it’s the messenger of Shri Radhika.”

“I don’t follow. Stop being cryptic and speak plainly.”

But Byomkesh did not speak plainly. With a crooked smile, half to himself, he murmured, “The idea of a clandestine rendezvous is rather sweet, of course, if the woman is someone else’s wife. If it’s your own, perhaps it’s not so sweet.”

“What do you mean?”

“I mean, ‘Ratisukhasare gatamabhisare madana-manohara-vesham.’”

“What nonsense are you spouting!”

Byomkesh replied gravely, “It’s not nonsense, it’s from the Gita Govinda. If you want nonsense, I can oblige—the rhythm is the same. ‘Baburam the snake-charmer, where are you off to, my good man—’”

Pandeyji, driving the motorcar, burst out laughing. Disappointed, I decided to rein in my curiosity for the time being.

When we reached Pandeyji’s house, we found tea was ready. Alongside it, there were steaming hot beguni, pakora, and spicy dal fritters. Byomkesh, without a word of protest, sat down. We joined him.

After we had helped ourselves generously to the fare, Byomkesh, in a tone of deep satisfaction, said, “I hadn’t realized it before, but my soul had been yearning for these very things.”

Pandeyji smiled and said, “Now that your wait is over, tell us what you saw and heard.”

Byomkesh took a long sip from his teacup, set it down with care, drew a few foundational pulls from the hookah, and then, in a slow, thoughtful voice, said, “I saw and heard many things, but the end is not yet in sight.”

Pandeyji asked, “Still?”

Byomkesh said, “Two motives are emerging. One—money, two—the poison of passion. I can’t yet tell which outweighs the other. It’s possible the two motives have become entangled.”

I said, “Whatever the motive, who is the man?”

Byomkesh replied, a shade impatiently, “How can I say? The person who mixed the poison with the medicine could be a hired hand. The one who hired him is the one we’re after.”

Pandeyji said, “Among those we know, who could have done the hiring? There’s Debnarayan. But would he—”

Byomkesh said, “Very well, let’s start with Debnarayan. He appears to be a thorough simpleton, but that could be a disguise. Perhaps he’s the one who arranged for someone to kill the old man. He has no shortage of loyal followers—Liladhar, Banshi, or Beniprasad—any of them might kill for the promise of a reward. Here, the motive is sole control of the property.”

--- Page 841 ---
Bohni - Patanga 539

I said, “But—”

Byomkesh raised his hand to stop me. “Then let’s consider—Chandni.”

“Chandni!”

“Yes, Chandni. Her excessive concern for Shakuntala doesn’t seem quite natural; in fact, it feels a bit overdone. Perhaps, deep down, she envies her, wishes to diminish her authority. After Dipnarayan’s death, Shakuntala will no longer be the mistress of the household—Chandni will. If Debnarayan truly is as meek and simple as he appears, he’ll be entirely in Chandni’s grasp, and Chandni will become the sole sovereign of a vast fortune—”

“But—”

Byomkesh again raised his hand, silencing me.

“Then consider—the manager, Gangadhar Banshi. According to Dr. Palit, he’s a fish from deep waters. That’s not so surprising; you can’t become the manager of such a large estate without being a creature of the depths. But if he’s a crocodile, then there’s real cause for worry. Think about it—Dipnarayan Singh was a shrewd man, kept a close watch on his property. While he was alive, large-scale theft was impossible—perhaps a little pilfering here and there, but nothing more. But if he dies, the entire estate passes to Debnarayan. Then, theft with both hands would be possible. So, we must admit that manager Gangadhar Banshi, too, has a motive.”

Byomkesh drew on the hookah for a while; we remained silent. Then he handed me the stem and said, “Finally, consider—Shakuntala Devi.”

Having said this much, he fell silent. We waited in anticipation. He shifted in his seat, then began to speak slowly, “It is not the business of a gentleman to discuss a lady’s character, but when a murder has taken place, there is no choice but to do so. Shakuntala Devi is three months pregnant, yet three months ago Dipnarayan Singh was bedridden, passing through a critical phase of his long illness… Today, Shakuntala told us she had informed her husband last night of her pregnancy, and that Dipnarayan Singh, upon hearing the news, was overwhelmed with joy… I suspect that is not true.”

I asked, “Why do you think it’s not true?”

Byomkesh said, “If Dipnarayan Singh had truly been overwhelmed with joy, why did he not share this wonderful news with anyone? If not at night, he could have told Dr. Palit in the morning, or called Miss Manna to confirm the good news… Shakuntala did not tell her husband, because it was not something she could tell him. Had Dipnarayan Singh found out, he would have killed Shakuntala, or at the very least, cast her out of the house. That is why, before the truth could come out, it became necessary to remove Dipnarayan Singh.”

I said, “But suppose Dr. Palit made a mistake?”

Byomkesh replied in a dry tone, “If both Dr. Palit and Miss Manna are mistaken, if Shakuntala is innocent, then she has no motive to kill Dipnarayan. But neither Dr. Palit nor Miss Manna are irresponsible children; they have not made a mistake. Nor have they deliberately lied—they are not the sort to tell a lie that would be immediately exposed.”

I said, “That’s not what I mean. There’s no doubt Shakuntala is pregnant. But Dipnarayan—”

“I understand what you’re trying to say. But on that front, the whole household is a witness, and the doctor—”

--- Page 842 ---
Palit will not get away by telling lies.” Byomkesh took the hookah’s stem from my hand and began to draw on it again.

I said, “Very well, for the sake of argument, let’s accept that Shakuntala has a Dushyanta. But who is this man?”

Byomkesh glanced at me, a little startled, and replied in a half-murmur, “Shakuntala’s Dushyanta! Well said. That is precisely the Dushyanta we are searching for. Who else but this Dushyanta could have left poison in Dr. Palit’s bag instead of medicine?”

“Then who is this Dushyanta?”

“That depends on Shakuntala’s taste. She is a modern woman of refined sensibilities, so Dushyanta is likely to be an educated, modern man as well. It could be Narmada Shankar, or someone else from their circle. Or it could be someone who does not openly frequent that house.”

Pandeyji sat for a while, his hand on his cheek, deep in thought. Then he said, “Or suppose it is someone who has abandoned Shakuntala after putting her in danger.”

Byomkesh said, “That is quite natural for Dushyantas. In that case, Shakuntala would have to try something else—she would need to find another accomplice.”

“And where would she find such an accomplice?”

“Why, what scarcity of accomplices is there? There is Gangadhar Banshi himself, his son Liladhar, Beniprasad—if the fee is right, any of them would agree. Even Dr. Palit and Miss Manna cannot be ruled out. The whole village is full of tricksters.” We fell silent.

For a while, there was no sound except the bubbling of Byomkesh’s hookah. Then, quite irrelevantly, he said, “That painting on the wall keeps coming to mind. I feel it is not just a picture—something deeply personal to the artist is hidden within it. I must look at it again, properly, in daylight.”

A servant entered and announced that Inspector Chowdhury had arrived.

Ten

Ratikanta entered the room and said, “The chemical analysis report has just come in. No poison was found in the medicine.”

We stared at him, mouths agape. We had been so certain that curare would be found in the liver vial that the news did not register at first.

“No poison was found?”

“No. Here is the report.” Ratikanta handed a slip of paper to Byomkesh.

There was no mention of any poison in the report—just an ordinary, standard liver extract. Byomkesh cast a furrowed glance at Pandeyji and Ratikanta.

“Most astonishing.”

Ratikanta cleared his throat and said, “Byomkesh Babu, what do you make of this?”

Byomkesh replied, “First, tell me—what do you make of it?”

It seemed to me that Ratikanta was secretly pleased. He sat down on the edge of a chair, gazed intently into space for a while, then slowly said, “There is no doubt that Dipnarayanji died of curare poisoning. The post-mortem confirmed the presence of poison. The toxin entered his body—”

--- Page 843 ---
Bohi - Page 541

“How was it done? The poison cannot enter except by injection. Yet, the vial from which the injection was administered—no trace of poison was found in it.” Ratikanta hesitated a moment. “From this, the only conclusion is that the vial Dr. Palit used to give the injection was not the one he handed over to us—he gave us a different vial.”

Pandeyji said, “But why? What would he gain from that?”

Ratikanta replied, a touch of anxiety in his voice, “The gain would be that we would believe the death was not caused by the injection.”

“Could it have been someone other than the doctor? After Dipnarayan’s death, many people entered the room. In the commotion, perhaps someone else switched the vial.”

“Not impossible, but—”

Byomkesh spoke slowly, “You believe Dr. Palit is the true culprit?”

Ratikanta fell silent for a moment, then said, “The hint you gave at the station today about Dr. Palit was already on my mind. Then, after receiving the analysis report, I thought—if Dr. Palit is innocent, why is he not acting straightforwardly? In these circumstances, it is only natural to suspect him. Of course, Dr. Palit has no personal gain in Dipnarayanji’s death. But those who do stand to gain could have bribed him to do their bidding. Perhaps they paid him fifty thousand, or even a lakh of rupees. There is nothing a man won’t do for money.”

Byomkesh nodded slowly. “True enough, there is nothing a man won’t do for money. If Dr. Palit committed this act for money, then it isn’t enough to apprehend only him—we must also catch the one who paid him. Have you any idea who that might be?”

“At first glance, it seems it could be none other than Debanarayan.”

“That does seem likely for now, but where is the proof? Has any evidence been found?”

“No evidence has been found yet.”

Ratikanta looked at Pandeyji and said, “Tonight, I’m taking the one o’clock train to Buxar. If I can interrogate the prisoner and find out whether Dr. Palit purchased curare—”

Pandeyji said, “That could resolve much of this. When will you return?”

“I should be back by tomorrow evening. I’m leaving Sub-Inspector Tiwari in charge at the station.”

“Good. What arrangements have you made for the house?”

“I’m posting four constables under a head constable at Dipnarayanji’s house. They’ll keep watch round the clock. And you’ve already asked Miss Manna to stay with Shakuntala Devi tonight.”

Pandeyji said, “Yes, Miss Manna will stay with Shakuntala for a few days. You know, Shakuntala is expecting.”

After a moment’s silence, Ratikanta spoke in a slightly deeper tone, “I’ve heard. Dipnarayanji was desperate for a child. He never got to see it.”

The clock struck eight with a metallic clang. Ratikanta stood up. “I must go, I have to get ready. Please keep an eye on things here.” With a smile and a salute, Ratikanta departed.

I noticed that Ratikanta’s manner had become much more relaxed and natural this evening. At first, he had been a little stiff. The arrival of Byomkesh in his territory—he had not liked it, deep down.

--- Page 844 ---
Byomkesh Samagra

It seems now he understands that Byomkesh does not wish to share in his glory; thus, he has grown reassured.

Byomkesh sat with his eyes closed for a while, then said, “Everything is getting tangled. There is no consistency in Dr. Palit's behavior. He was the first to say that the cause of death was curare, and that it was his injection that killed him. Then why did he switch the medicine vial?” Byomkesh closed his eyes once more.

The sound of a motorcar came from outside. The servant entered and announced, “Dr. Palit has arrived.”

Byomkesh’s trance was abruptly broken. In a soft voice, he said to Pandey, “There’s no use telling the doctor about all this.”

When Dr. Palit entered, Pandeyji seated him with due courtesy.

The doctor spoke wearily, “There is no peace in my soul, Pandeyji. After closing the dispensary, I thought I’d come by to see if there was any news.”

Pandeyji cast a sidelong glance at Byomkesh. Byomkesh said, “We too are searching for news, Doctor, but where is it to be found? If what you said about Shakuntala Devi is true—”

The doctor’s face grew a shade displeased. “If it’s true, any other doctor could examine Shakuntala and know for themselves.”

Byomkesh quickly interjected, “No, no, that’s not what I meant. Shakuntala herself has admitted as much. We are thinking that at that time, Dipnarayan Singh was on his deathbed—”

The doctor said, “There is ample proof of that. Three months ago, Dipnarayan Singh’s condition was very bad. Many doctors in the city saw him; they can attest to it. Besides, a nurse was with him day and night. She can tell you.”

“Is that so? What is the nurse’s name?”

“Miss Lambert. She lives near Medical College.”

Byomkesh asked Pandeyji, “Do you know her?”

Pandeyji replied, “I don’t, but I have seen her house.”

Byomkesh sat in thought for a moment, then turned to Dr. Palit and said, “Doctor, may I ask you a personal question? Please don’t take offense. Why did you borrow twelve thousand rupees from Dipnarayan Singh’s estate?”

Dr. Palit was thunderstruck, his eyes wide with astonishment. “I borrowed money? What—who told you that?”

“I heard it from the manager, Gangadhar Banshi. Is it not true, then?”

“Utterly false. Twelve thousand rupees! That Gangadhar Banshi is a dangerous man. Now that Dipnarayanbabu is dead, he wants to pocket twelve thousand rupees in the confusion. Wait, I’ll show him—I’ll go right now and wring his neck. To slander me with such a lie, such audacity!”

Dr. Palit made as if to rise, but Byomkesh said, “Sit, sit. You can settle things with the manager later. But if there’s no truth to it, how did such a rumor arise?”

The doctor thought for a moment, then said, “I think I understand how. About two weeks ago, one morning I went to give Dipnarayanbabu his injection. Seeing my car, he said—‘Doctor, your car is looking rather battered, you should replace it.’ I replied, ‘Nowadays, a new car costs ten or twelve thousand rupees—where would I get that kind of money?’”

--- Page 845 ---
Bahni-Patanga 543

I could never save a single paisa; whatever I earned, I spent it all. Hearing this, he said nothing more, only smiled a little. I am certain he had decided to give me that money—perhaps he had even told the manager so. Then, when he died so suddenly, the manager’s mind hatched a clever scheme: here was his chance to pocket twelve thousand rupees. Just wait, I’ll expose his tricks—I won’t let him outsmart me.

Dr. Palit was a gentle, serious man by nature, but I saw that he had flared up in anger. It was impossible to restrain him any longer; he stood up and declared he would settle matters tonight, and departed. Byomkesh listened intently—the sound of Dr. Palit’s motorcar faded into the night. Then he sprang to his feet and said to Pandeyji, “Come, we must see Miss Lambert now.”

Astonished, Pandeyji exclaimed, “Now—at this hour of the night?”

Byomkesh replied, “If we must go, it has to be tonight. The way Dr. Palit rushed off, I can’t tell if he’s gone to warn Miss Lambert. Come.”

Pandeyji said, “Let’s go.”

Miss Lambert was an Anglo-Indian lady. She was advanced in years. Her features were distinctly English—sharp eyes, fair complexion. But her heart, I suspected, was Indian. After dinner, she sat listening to the radio, her lips reddened from chewing paan. On learning who we were, she welcomed us warmly into her drawing room. It was a small house, a modest drawing room, but neat and tidy. The lady herself was much the same. It did not seem that Dr. Palit had come this way.

Miss Lambert smiled and said, “So late at night—how shall I entertain you? Have some paan.” She opened the betel box before us. We each took a paan. Pandeyji asked, “You don’t have to go out anywhere tonight, do you?”

Miss Lambert replied, “No, I am free tonight.”

Pandeyji said, “We have come to ask you something. Have you heard that Dipnarayan Singh has died?”

Miss Lambert’s face grew grave. “Yes, I’ve heard. It’s unimaginable that such a thing could happen at Dr. Palit’s hands.”

“From whom did you hear?”

“From Dr. Jagannath Prasad. Then I heard it from the other doctors as well. So sad. Tell me, what can I do?”

Pandeyji then succinctly explained the matter at hand. Miss Lambert listened with deep attention and shook her head firmly. “Impossible. I nursed Mr. Dipnarayan for a month and a half, and in all that time, I never took my eyes off the patient for even ten minutes.”

“You alone cared for him?”

“No, I had an assistant—Miss Dastur. She was there during the day, and I at night. In our absence, no one was allowed near the patient—not even the servants.”

“Hm. From when to when did you attend him?”

“One moment, I’ll show you my diary.”

Miss Lambert brought her diary from the next room and handed it to Pandeyji. Each day’s schedule was meticulously recorded. For the month and a half that Dipnarayan Singh’s life…

--- Page 846 ---
BYOMKESH SAMAGRA  
544  
There is an account of how, between Yama and man, a tug-of-war was waged over his life.  
Reading the description of the patient’s condition, there can be no doubt that for that month and a half, his state was extremely precarious. His vitality had ebbed so low that he no longer had the strength to even sit up in bed. Comparing the dates, it became clear that Miss Lambert’s period of nursing had begun four months ago and ended two and a half months before today. Even after that, Dipnarayan Singh had remained unwell, but his life was no longer in danger.  
Returning Miss Lambert’s diary and expressing our heartfelt gratitude, we took our leave.  
It was already half past nine at night. The market shops were shuttered. As I walked home, I kept thinking about the scolding I would receive from Satyabati for returning so late. Pandeyji dropped us off.  

Eleven  

The next morning, upon waking, we learned that it had rained during the night. The sky was shrouded in mist; the sun, it seemed, lay curled up beneath a blanket. There was, therefore, no reason for us to rise from bed either.  
At half past eight, Satyabati brought us tea and said, “Today is Amavasya again. No one is to step outside the house today.”  
Who would want to go out on such a day? But Pandeyji paid no heed. At precisely nine o’clock, he arrived, dressed in police attire. Shivering, we emerged from beneath our quilts. Pandeyji, seeing our condition, laughed.  
He said, “Something happened last night.”  
Byomkesh wanted to know what had happened. Pandeyji recounted the incident succinctly—  
From midnight, mist had begun to gather in the sky, and soon after, a light drizzle set in. Pandeyji, being a late sleeper, was preparing for bed around half past one when the telephone rang. The call was from Dipnarayan’s house—the jamadar whom Ratikanta had stationed there with four constables was calling.  
The jamadar reported that a short while ago, two men had tried to enter through the back door, but the sepoys were alert, and upon being spotted, the intruders fled. One sepoy had shone his torch at them from a distance—both were gentlemen in coat and trousers, but could not be identified. It seemed they had come on a motorbike, for a little later, the distant puttering of a bike was heard.  
Pandeyji had done nothing further that night, merely instructing the jamadar to keep a vigilant watch before hanging up. This morning, he had made inquiries and learned that there had been no further disturbances during the night.  
Byomkesh raised his brows and gazed at Pandeyji for a while.  
I said, “Narmada Shankar.”  
Byomkesh turned his head toward me and said, “I’m wondering who the other man was. If Narmada Shankar is indeed Dushyanta, would he venture into Shakuntala’s grove with a companion?—Pandeyji, what do you think?”

--- Page 847 ---
PANDITJI said, “I can’t make head or tail of this. I’ve brought two warrants with me—no names on them, but we can fill those in if necessary.”

Byomkesh replied, “Then let’s go. Let’s raid Narmadashankar’s house. If we catch him off guard, he might blurt out the truth.”

Within five minutes, we were ready and stepped outside. Satyabati said nothing, only stared at us with wide, unblinking eyes.

As we climbed into the car, I saw a burly sub-inspector already seated inside. Panditji introduced him—Sub-Inspector Tiwari.

Tiwari’s appearance was straight out of the old days—a true daroga. He bared his worm-eaten teeth in a salute. I gathered that Ratikanta had left him in charge of the station.

Meanwhile, the sky’s tears were slowly receding. The newly awakened sun was slicing through the clouds with a sharpened blade, scattering them into fragments. What had lain heavy and oppressive overhead now began to dissolve like rings of smoke. By the time we reached Narmadashankar’s house, the world was ablaze in raw, golden sunlight.

Narmadashankar’s house stood in the city’s new quarter, surrounded by a cast-iron railing, a tennis court in front. We left the car outside and entered as quietly as possible. From the look of things, the house had not yet fully woken. On the front veranda, a drowsy servant sat cross-legged, brushing several pairs of shoes. At the sight of us, he gaped, mouth agape, for a moment.

Byomkesh walked over, picked up a pair of shoes, and turned them over in his hands. He asked the servant, “Whose shoes are these?”

The servant, still slack-jawed, replied, “Master’s.”

Byomkesh showed us the soles. They were caked with mud. There could be no doubt these shoes had been worn after midnight.

Just then, from inside the house, a high-ranking, liveried bearer emerged. He too was startled at the sight of two police officers. Panditji, in a stern tone, demanded, “Where is Narmadashankar Babu?”

The bearer, frightened, replied, “Sir, he is at home.”

“Take us to him.”

The bearer hesitated a moment, then led the way. As far as I could see, the interior of the house was tastefully decorated. The bearer brought us to a door, drew aside the curtain, and stood aside. We entered the room.

The windows and doors were shut, electric lights burning. The room could only be described as a hunting lodge. Tiger and deer skins were spread across the floor, tiger and deer heads mounted on the walls. In a glass cabinet, rifles, guns, and pistols were neatly arranged. In the center of the room stood a round table, surrounded by several cushioned armchairs.

As we entered, we saw two men sitting opposite each other, each holding a glass of colored liquid. On a side table stood bottles of soda and whisky. It was not hard to guess what filled their glasses. Evidently, the midnight libations had not yet ceased.

The man facing us was Ghoda Jagannath. At the sight of us, his bleary eyes widened, his entire body jolted as if struck by lightning; the glass in his hand—

--- Page 848 ---
546  
Byomkesh Samagra

Liquid sloshed out of the glass. At that, Narmadashankar turned his head to look. A frown appeared on his flushed face. In a harsh voice, he said, “What do you want?”

Alcohol has its strange effects; once it enters the belly, a man’s character transforms. Some weep, some sing, some grow belligerent. Narmadashankar’s meek, compliant manner was gone; now he stared at us with fierce, defiant eyes.

Pandeyji strode over to them, his voice taking on the sternness of the police. “There is a warrant out for both of you.”

Narmadashankar rose to his feet, glass in hand, and exclaimed in insolent surprise, “A warrant? For me? What warrant?”

Pandeyji replied, “Last night at one o’clock, you both trespassed at Dipnarayan Singh’s house.”

“Do you have proof?”

Unmoved, Pandeyji said, “We do. Police officers saw you.”

A sly, wicked glint flickered in Narmadashankar’s bloodshot eyes. Curling his lips in a crooked sneer, he said, “And if I say Shakuntala called me—would that still be trespassing?”

“You can tell that to the court. —Sub-Inspector Tiwari—” Pandeyji gestured to Tiwari, who produced two pairs of handcuffs from his pocket.

At the sight of the handcuffs, Ghoda Jagannath began to wail. Until then, he had been silent, not even making a sound to clear his nose. Now, setting his glass on the table, he seized Pandeyji’s hands in both of his, pleading desperately, “Pandeyji, for mercy’s sake, don’t put the handcuffs on us. We haven’t truly done anything wrong, I’ll tell you everything—no, no, Narmadashankar, you keep quiet, don’t be stubborn—if this scandal gets out, we’ll never be able to show our faces in the city again. Pandeyji, listen to my statement—”

Pandeyji said, “If you tell the truth, I’m willing to listen.”

“I’ll tell the truth, I’ll hide nothing.”

“Very well, if after hearing you I believe you had no ill intent, perhaps I won’t arrest you. —Narmadashankarbabu, you go now, you’ve had enough to drink, go lie down on your bed. We’ll call you if needed.”

By now, Narmadashankar had regained some of his senses; he cast a smoldering, impotent glare in our direction, then picked up the bottle and left.

We sat down then. Ghoda Jagannath gulped down the rest of his drink with a series of snorts, and the account he gave was as follows—

Ghoda Jagannath’s friendship with Narmadashankar was not particularly deep; but at Narmadashankar’s house, imported liquor was available for free, so Jagannath maintained a superficial camaraderie with him. Last night, Jagannath had come here with a few other friends, and after dining together, the others departed. Jagannath and Narmadashankar then came into this room and began drinking. Narmadashankar had been deeply aggrieved because the police had not allowed him to meet Shakuntala that evening; as they drank, this was the subject of their conversation. Midnight came, and rain began to fall. Suddenly, Narmadashankar declared that, come what may, he would see Shakuntala tonight. Jagannath tried to dissuade him, but he would not listen. So the two of them set out on a motorbike, Jagannath sitting pillion. Nearing Dipnarayan’s house, they—

--- Page 849 ---
He hid the motorbike amidst the mango grove, then entered through the postern gate. But the police were on watch; scarcely had they crossed the threshold when the beam of an electric torch fell upon the two intruders. Without a moment’s hesitation, both turned and fled, mounting the motorbike and making their escape. Since then, up to the present moment, they have been sitting here, drinking. They harbored no unlawful intent—drunkenness led them to a foolish act. Now, considering all this, let Pandeyji, in his wisdom, forgive them.

When Horse Jagannath’s pleading statement came to an end, Pandeyji raised his brows at Byomkesh. Byomkesh asked, “What exactly is the nature of the relationship between Shakuntala Devi and Narmadashankar Babu?”

Jagannath, visibly alarmed, replied, “Look, please don’t ask me about such matters. I mean—”

“You mean—you won’t say?”

Jagannath grew even more agitated. “No, no, why wouldn’t I say? But I don’t meddle in such affairs—I am a respectable doctor—what business do I have poking my nose into others’ pots?”

“Indeed! You don’t meddle in others’ affairs! Yet you threatened Doctor Palit’s compounder, Khublal, to make him quit his job.”

At the mention of Khublal, Horse Jagannath shrank like a worm—“I—I mean, I—”

Byomkesh said, “Let that be. You don’t know how close Shakuntala and Narmadashankar have grown?”

“I swear, I know nothing of their secret dealings.”

“Didn’t Narmadashankar say anything last night?”

“Narmadashankar is a terrible liar. He thinks every woman in the world is drowning in love for him. One cannot trust a word he says.”

“So he did say something. What did he say?”

“He claimed he’s been in love with Shakuntala for a long time. That they studied at the same college in Allahabad, and their romance began back then.”

Byomkesh slowly rose to his feet and said, in a flat voice, “Hmm! You are free to go today. But later, you may have to testify in court. Do not attempt to flee the city, or you’ll find yourself in handcuffs. Let’s go, Pandeyji.”

***

Arriving at the house of Dipnarayan Singh, Pandeyji said to Tiwari, “You may return to the station now; you’re no longer needed here.” When Tiwari had departed, he turned to Byomkesh and asked, “And now?”

Byomkesh smiled faintly. “Come, let’s go towards the serestah. It seemed to me that Manager Gangadhar Banshi, seeing us from a distance, slipped quickly into the office.”

Crossing the portico, we made our way towards the serestah. On the way, we encountered the jamadar, who saluted Pandeyji and reported that all was well.

Yesterday, we had seen the serestah rooms from outside. Three or so rooms in a row;

--- Page 850 ---
Every room had a jute carpet spread over the takhtposh. A few clerks sat, busy with their work.

When Manager Gangadhar realized he could no longer avoid us, he emerged from the serestah. In his hand was a bundle of outgoing letters. He wore a practiced smile, as if he had only just noticed us, and said, “Ah, there you are!”

Byomkesh glanced at the letters and asked, “Dewanji, how many letters do you send out daily from your serestah?”

Dewanji handed the letters to a peon, who took them and slipped out through the side door; there was no doubt he was going to drop them in the mailbox at the corner of the house. Dewanji replied, “About twenty or twenty-five. We have to write to many people—lawyers, agents, clients, tenants—”

Byomkesh asked, “Are all the letters dropped into that mailbox at the corner of the house?”

Gangadhar replied, “Yes, indeed. We arranged with the postal department to have that box installed there. It’s convenient to have a mailbox close at hand.”

“Of course. How many times is it cleared?”

“Once at seven in the morning, once at four in the afternoon. But why do you ask? Does your investigation have anything to do with the mailbox?”

“It might. Dewanji, in our language there’s a saying—wherever you see ashes, stir them up, you might find hidden treasure. But let’s leave that for now. Any news of Eric?”

Gangadhar turned his palms up and said, “I know nothing. I haven’t even learned the true cause of the master’s death. Was there really poison in the injection?”

Byomkesh replied, “That’s what the doctors say. By the way, did you meet with Dr. Palit?”

Gangadhar Banshi’s face suddenly seemed to collapse, his eyes retreating deep into their sockets. He was silent for a moment, then said in a slightly faltering voice, “Yes, I met him. He denies taking any money.”

“Did you give him the money yourself?”

Gangadhar was silent again for a while, then finally said, “No, the assistant manager gave it to him.”

“By assistant manager, you mean—your son Liladhar Banshi?”

Gangadhar cleared his tightening throat and said, “Yes. There’s a problem—we didn’t get a receipt. Who could have imagined Dr. Palit would do such a thing—”

“Indeed—not something one would expect. So, where is Liladhar Babu now?”

“He—he’s gone to his in-laws’ house.”

“Really! He was here until yesterday evening, drinking toddy in Debnarayan’s room, and today he’s off to his in-laws’.”

Gangadhar replied in a vague, faltering tone, “His wife is ill… he left suddenly when he got the news.”

“Hmm—” A mischievous glint danced in Byomkesh’s eyes. He spoke in a slow, thoughtful manner, “It’s not a small sum—twelve thousand. If so much of the State’s money is to be lost, Dewanji, you ought to inform the police. Even without a receipt, the police can uncover whether Dr. Palit took the money. What do you say, Pandeyji?”

Pandeyji replied firmly, “Certainly. Manager Sahib, if you say so, we’ll begin the investigation at once.”

--- Page 851 ---
Bahni-Patanga 549

We will. We’ll examine every document in the office; if there’s any discrepancy, it will be caught. We’ll interrogate Dr. Palit and Liladhar as well, search them—’

Byomkesh and Pandeyji, together, were slowly edging the Manager Sahib toward the brink of some precipice—at least, so it seemed to him, a man well-versed in the deep waters of intrigue. He shook his head with a weary air and said, ‘No, since Dr. Palit is denying it, I’ll pay the money myself. Misfortune dogs me—my life has been spent paying for others’ losses.’ With that, he let out a deep sigh.

Byomkesh suppressed a smile. Pandeyji made a noise in his throat, but it was not a sympathetic sound.

Leaving the Dewanji in the office, we made our way to the main entrance of the house. In the outer hall, a sepoy stood on guard; upon questioning him, we learned that everyone was upstairs. We ascended the staircase.

At the top of the stairs stood Chandni; her eyes were bloodshot, her hair disheveled. If her features were not naturally sweet and gentle, one might have called her a warrior goddess. The moment she saw us, without any preamble, she began, ‘So you’ve forbidden me from seeing Chachiji! What do you think of me? That I’d poison her?’

This sudden attack left us momentarily stunned. Byomkesh looked helplessly at Pandeyji, who scratched his head and replied awkwardly, ‘Look, you’re not the only one—no one is allowed to see her right now. In a few days, when our work is done, you’ll all be able to visit her again.’

Chandni said passionately, ‘But why? Who can care for her as I can? Why shouldn’t I be allowed to see her? She’s ill, she’s suffered such a terrible shock—’

Tears began streaming down Chandni’s face. Now it was Pandeyji’s turn to look helplessly at Byomkesh.

Byomkesh had by now regained his composure. In a calm voice, he said, ‘Perhaps you don’t know, Shakuntala Devi is pregnant. And now, after such a blow—her physical condition is very fragile. That’s why Miss Mann is staying with her. You are her own people—if you keep going to her, her mind will be even more disturbed, which could harm her health. So it’s best for you all to stay away from her for a few days.’

As soon as Byomkesh began speaking, Chandni became transfixed, her eyes fixed and unblinking, as if hypnotized. When Byomkesh finished, she murmured in a dazed voice, ‘Pregnant—’ Then, still in that trance-like state, she turned toward her own quarters.

Byomkesh called after her, ‘Listen. I have something to ask you—’ Chandni turned back. ‘Were you present when Dipnarayan Babu was given the injection?’

It’s hard to say whether Chandni heard the question in full; she replied vaguely, ‘I was.’

‘Was anyone else there?’

‘I don’t know. I didn’t notice.’

‘Please listen carefully to my question. Try to remember what the doctor did.’

‘As soon as the doctor gave the injection, Chachaji collapsed. Then the doctor quickly gave another injection. I ran to inform Chachiji. When I came back, I saw everyone—’

--- Page 852 ---
550  
Byomkesh Samagra

“It’s over.”

“Did you see anyone else there when you returned?”

“I don’t remember. Perhaps Dewanji was there, but I didn’t notice anyone else.” Chandni, without waiting for further questions, retreated to her own quarters.

Byomkesh stood for a while, gazing at the ground, then finally raised his head and said, “Let’s go. It’s time we visited Shakuntala Devi’s room.”

Byomkesh led the way; we followed behind. The sitting room was empty, a fine layer of dust settling over the furniture. The next room was the same. At the third chamber—Shakuntala’s music room—Byomkesh paused before the door and said, “Wait, let me look at the painting once more.”

Byomkesh entered the room. We lingered by the doorway, not particularly eager to examine the painting ourselves.

Byomkesh disappeared toward the wall where the painting of Dushyanta and Shakuntala’s first meeting was hung. Five minutes passed, and there was still no sign of him. I craned my neck through the doorway and saw him, utterly absorbed in contemplation of the painting. With a hint of mockery, I called out, “Well, what’s this? You seem completely entranced! What are you seeing in there?”

Byomkesh turned around slowly. I noticed something strange in his eyes—a look of profound, almost stunned astonishment. He didn’t answer me. Instead, he sat down on the velvet bed, wrapped his arms around his raised knees, and stared into space.

Seeing his demeanor, both Pandeyji and I entered the room. Pandeyji, a touch anxious, asked, “Byomkesh Babu, what is it? What did you see in the painting?” Byomkesh still did not reply; he took a cigarette from his pocket, lit it with great care, and, after a long drag, began to exhale the smoke slowly, deliberately.

I exchanged a glance with Pandeyji, and together we went to stand before the painting. It looked no different in the daylight than it had yesterday. Shakuntala was still watering the roots of the trees, Dushyanta still peering from behind the foliage. But why, then, had Byomkesh suddenly fallen silent?

We returned and sat down in front of Byomkesh, fixing our eyes on him, waiting. He finished his cigarette, flicked it out the window, then took Pandeyji’s hand and said in a deep voice, “I must ask you for a favor.”

“What favor?”

“I want to question Shakuntala alone in her room. No one else must be present.”

“All right. But what have you discovered?”

Byomkesh stood up. “I have discovered everything. You saw the painting too—did you notice nothing?”

Pandeyji shook his head in frustration. “No, nothing. I saw the painting last night, I saw it again today, but I haven’t found the key to the mystery.”

Byomkesh said, “Last night you saw it in the blue glow of the neon light, but today you saw it in daylight. There’s no reason you shouldn’t have seen it today. In any case, please wait in the front room. I’ll be with you in half an hour.”

Byomkesh went to Shakuntala’s door and knocked. Miss Manna opened it and stepped out. Byomkesh whispered something to her; she nodded and came over to us. Byomkesh entered Shakuntala’s room and closed the door behind him.

--- Page 853 ---
We three went and sat in the front room. Miss Manna looked at us with anxious eyes. What could we say? We ourselves knew nothing. Turning our faces away, we began to gaze at the Jamini Roy painting.

Twenty-five minutes later, Byomkesh arrived. His face and eyes bore the marks of harsh exhaustion, as though he had emerged victorious from a fierce battle of wits, wounded and weary. Sitting beside Miss Manna, he spoke to her in a low voice, giving her instructions. The essence of his words: until a quarter past ten tonight, she must not let Shakuntala out of her sight for even a moment, nor allow anyone else to speak privately with her. After a quarter past ten, Miss Manna would be free to go home. Hearing these instructions, Miss Manna cast a questioning glance at Pandeyji, who nodded his assent. Miss Manna then went off to Shakuntala’s room.

Byomkesh looked in turn at Pandeyji’s and my face, and gave a dry smile. “Come, let’s go now.”

Pandeyji said, “But—”

Byomkesh raised his hand. “Not here. I’ll tell you everything on the way home.”

That evening, just before we left the house at dusk, Byomkesh, nibbling at his snack, glanced sidelong at Satyabati and said, “We’ll be a little late coming back tonight.”

Satyabati, her face darkening, replied, “Of course you will. It’s amavasya tonight, and on top of that, I told you not to go out. If you’re not late tonight, when will you ever be?”

Byomkesh said, “Is it amavasya today? Ah, then the atmosphere is perfect.”

Satyabati said, “Is it? Good.”

Byomkesh said, “Ajit is a poet at heart—ask him, the night of the new moon is the best for an assignation.”

“So will your assignation last all night?”

“Oh no, no, we’ll be back by twelve or one.”

Satyabati looked at him, startled and anxious. “Twelve or one?”

Byomkesh stood up, wiping his mouth, and said lightly, “Don’t worry. When I return, I’ll tell you the tale of Dushyanta and Shakuntala. Come, Ajit.”

Satyabati stood there, her face clouded with worry, as we left.

We did not go to Pandeyji’s house, but headed straight to Dipnarayan’s mansion, as had been arranged. Pandeyji was sitting in an armchair padded with cushions in the outer hall, legs stretched out before him. Seeing us, he sat up straight. Byomkesh asked, “Ratikanta hasn’t returned from Boxer yet?”

Pandeyji replied, “No. The police have been notified. He’ll come straight here when he gets back.”

After that, the three of us sat in silence, smoking cigarettes. When darkness fell, Pandeyji got up and lit a lamp, which illuminated only a portion of the room. The manager, Gangadhar Banshi, poked his head in from outside, then withdrew without a word. Chandni came down the stairs, saw us, and quietly went back up. After a while, a servant brought in three cups of tea. We drank our tea.

The house felt haunted; not a sound, and in the corners of the rooms, indistinct shadowy figures seemed to drift about. The three of us sat like expectant spirits, waiting for something—what, we did not know, shrouded as we were in deep mystery.

--- Page 854 ---
552  
Byomkesh Samagra

At a quarter to eight, Ratikanta arrived. He was dressed head to toe in police uniform, a suppressed excitement glinting in his eyes. He saluted Pandeyji and sat down on the edge of the chair beside him, leaning in to say, “I’ve got proof—it’s Dr. Palit’s doing.”

Pandeyji fixed Ratikanta with a sharp gaze. “You’ve got proof? What proof?”

Ratikanta replied, “The prisoner has confessed. At first, he wouldn’t say a word, but after much questioning, he admitted that Palit bought curare from him.”

“Is that so?” Pandeyji seemed to withdraw into himself.

Ratikanta, eager, said, “Then perhaps now we can arrest Palit?”

“Wait, not so fast. It’s not safe to arrest a man like Dr. Palit on the word of a petty thief. Meanwhile, we’ve gathered some information ourselves—” With this, Pandeyji cast a meaningful glance at Byomkesh. Ratikanta turned his eyes to Byomkesh, his voice rising, “What news?”

“I’ll tell you,” Byomkesh said, scanning the large room with a careful eye before pulling his chair closer to Ratikanta. In the dim light, four heads drew together. They began to speak in hushed tones.

Byomkesh said, “This morning, I interrogated Shakuntala Devi. At first, she tried to mislead me, but in the end, she broke down. She admitted that she knows the culprit—the culprit is her secret lover…” Byomkesh fell silent. Ratikanta gazed at him, unblinking.

Byomkesh sighed. “But there’s a problem—she refuses to reveal the culprit’s name.”

“She won’t say the name?” Ratikanta burst out.

Byomkesh shook his head. “No. Shakuntala is a woman—she has her modesty, her shame, her fear of disgrace. One can’t blame her too much for that. Despite all my efforts, I couldn’t get the name from her lips.”

Ratikanta sat up straight, thought for a moment, and said, “Shall I try once? If I go and question her alone, perhaps she’ll tell me the name.”

Pandeyji shook his head. “It’s no use now—she won’t say a word. But there’s another way—”

“What way?” Ratikanta’s eyes shifted from Pandeyji to Byomkesh.

Byomkesh lowered his voice further. “After much persuasion, Shakuntala has agreed—she’ll write a letter to Pandeyji, revealing the culprit’s name. The arrangement is this: the police posted here will be withdrawn. Only Miss Manna will remain with Shakuntala. No one else will be allowed near her. By a quarter past ten tonight, Miss Manna will leave Shakuntala alone and return to her own quarters. Then Shakuntala will write the letter herself and drop it in the postbox. It’s a local letter—we’ll receive it tomorrow between ten and eleven in the morning.”

For a while, no one spoke. The four heads remained close together. At last, Ratikanta said, “So, in your opinion, Dr. Palit isn’t the culprit?”

Byomkesh replied, “It could still be Dr. Palit—we can’t say anything for certain yet. Or it could be Narmada Shankar. Tomorrow, we’ll know for sure.” With that, he recounted everything that had happened that morning at Narmada Shankar’s house.

--- Page 855 ---
Bohni - Potongo 553

Hearing this, Ratikanta fell silent. Pandeyji glanced at his wristwatch and said, “Let’s call it a day, then. Byomkesh Babu, you should come to my house as well. Ratikanta, you too—let’s all gather and discuss the case together. You’ve been away all day; much has transpired in your absence.”

Byomkesh replied, “But we’ll have to return home early tonight. The missus is terribly cross.”

We stepped outside. Ratikanta called the orderly and instructed him to relieve the guard. Then the four of us climbed into Pandeyji’s motorcar and set off.

The tale of the flame and the moth is drawing to its close. Yet, if you think about it, this story has no end; across the world, through the ages, its repetition plays on. Sometimes the moth burns away slowly, bit by bit; sometimes it is reduced to ashes in a single instant.

After the present game of flame and moth had ended, I asked Byomkesh, “Tell me, Byomkesh, who is the moth here? And who the flame?”

Byomkesh had replied, “Both are flames, both are moths.”

But let that be. I shall not spoil the flavor by revealing what is yet to come. That night, as the clock struck eight, Byomkesh and I took our leave from Pandeyji’s house; Pandeyji and Ratikanta remained, deep in discussion about the case. They were poring over the statement Ratikanta had brought from the prisoner in Buxar.

Outside, the darkness was thick and impenetrable. There were a few streetlights here and there, but they were hardly enough to dispel the night’s gloom. I did not know the streets of Patna well, and on this new moon night, the hope of reaching any particular destination by guesswork seemed a distant dream. We chose a direction at random and stumbled along, tripping as we went. My mind was so scattered that I hadn’t even thought to bring an electric torch. Fortunately, we had not gone far when we heard a faint jingling, a tinkling sound. A hazy light, moving slowly, was approaching us. As it drew near, the outline of an ekka gradually took shape. Byomkesh raised his hand and called out, “Stop! Will you take us for hire?”

The ekka halted. From within the folds of a blanket that swaddled him from head to toe, the ekkawallah’s voice emerged, “No, babu, my horse is tired.”

Byomkesh said, “It’s not far—Dipnarayan Singh’s house. Come, you’ll get a tip.”

The ekkawallah replied, “Come, babu, my stable is in that direction anyway.”

We swung our legs over either side of the ekka and sat down. The ekkawallah cracked his whip, making a sharp ‘tokas tokas’ sound with his mouth. The horse set off, hooves clattering in the night.

Fourteen

Ten minutes later, Byomkesh told him to stop. I climbed down from the ekka and, peering through the misty light, saw that we had arrived at the corner of Dipnarayan Singh’s house, near the letterbox. Byomkesh paid the ekkawallah his fare and a tip.

“Salam, babuji.”

The hazy bubble of light, floating in the sea of darkness, jingled away into the distance.

--- Page 856 ---
We were plunged back into the same darkness from which we had emerged.

“What now? Shall I strike a match?”

Before Byomkesh could reply, a harsh beam of light flared before our eyes; I shielded my face with my hand. Byomkesh spoke, “Who’s there—Sub-Inspector Tiwari?”

“Yes.” Tiwari lowered the torch so its light fell to the ground and came to stand beside us. The faint glow rising from the earth illuminated our three faces. All of us were clad in black; the brass buttons on Tiwari’s black coat glinted in the light.

“How many men do you have with you?”

“Two.” Tiwari swung the torch slightly behind him. Two thin, ghostly police jamadars stood at his back.

Byomkesh said, “Good, two will suffice. Have you told them what to do?”

“Yes.”

“Then let’s climb the trees, one by one. Ajit, you take the tree in front. Sit quietly on a branch—don’t smoke. Don’t come down until you hear the whistle. Tiwari-ji, hand me the torch.”

Taking the torch, Byomkesh shone it up into a mango tree. About five or six yards from the letterbox stood a large mango tree, a thick branch jutting out from the trunk. As I wondered whether there might be an ants’ nest in the tree, I began to climb.

“That’s enough, don’t go any higher.”

I settled myself carefully at the fork of two branches. My days of clambering about in trees were long past; a faint unease crept over me.

Byomkesh asked, “Alright. You remember everything?”

“Yes. The moment I hear the whistle, I’ll rush down like a lovelorn Radha.”

Byomkesh then led the other three along the inside of the wall. Skipping two or three trees, one of the jamadars climbed up another. They moved farther away; I couldn’t see who climbed which tree. Only the shifting beams of electric torches played through the dense foliage before my eyes.

Then even those torches went dark.

I brought my wristwatch close to my face, reading the radium dial—ten minutes past nine. I doubted anything would happen before another hour had passed.

I sat waiting. Luckily, there was no wind; the winter’s bite could not sink its teeth in too deeply. Yet, from time to time, my bones shivered, my teeth chattered. The mango grove was not entirely silent. The leaves seemed restless, whispering among themselves; in the darkness, my hearing had sharpened, and I could make out their voices. Once, above my head, a bird—perhaps an owl—flew off with a harsh screech, perhaps having spotted me in the tree. Glancing up, I saw two or four stars peeking through the gaps in the foliage.

I sat waiting. It was a quarter to ten. Suddenly, all my senses sprang alert. I saw nothing, heard nothing, but felt—someone was passing by my tree, moving inward. Who was it? Pandey-ji? Or—?

A damp, clammy breeze brushed my face. Looking up, I saw the stars dim, then flare bright again. Perhaps, as last night, the sky was beginning to gather mist.

--- Page 857 ---
Bohni-Patanga 555

It has done so.
Yes, it is indeed the mist. The stars have vanished from sight. Dew gathers on the leaves of the trees, coalescing into droplets that fall to the ground—soft sounds rise from all around—top, top, top!
A fierce desire to smoke seized me. I clenched my teeth and suppressed the urge...

The clock showed a quarter past ten. I perched upright on the branch of the tree. From within Dipnarayan’s sleeve, a faint buzzing sound seemed to emanate. Then a motorcar emerged from the lane and drove off in the opposite direction. For a moment, the car’s headlights swirled through the mist, then darkness returned.

Perhaps Miss Manna had gone back to her own house.

Now! For ten minutes I sat taut, nerves and muscles tense, nothing happened. Then suddenly—a flash of light blazed at the khidki door. And almost at once, in rapid succession, three or four pistol shots rang out. I was not prepared for the gunfire; for a moment I was frozen, motionless.

The spell broke with the shrill blast of a police whistle. I leapt down from the branch. I could not judge how far the ground was; a jarring shock ran through my whole body.

I got to my feet and saw three or so torches flicker to life near the khidki. I ran in that direction.

As I ran, I heard another pistol shot. Then my foot caught on a root and I fell hard. I got up and ran again. There was no time to check if my limbs were intact. Where everyone else was already gathered, I arrived in a rush.

Near the khidki door, five men stood encircling a spot. In Pandeyji’s hand was a revolver; Tiwari and two jamadars held torches; Byomkesh stood with his hand on his waist. The beams of three torches converged on a single point. Five pairs of eyes were fixed there as well.

Two bodies lay on the ground; one a woman, the other a man. The woman was Shakuntala, and the man—Ratikanta.

Ratikanta’s blue eyes were wide with astonishment; a pistol lay near his right hand, the fingers of his left clutched a white envelope. Shakuntala’s face was not clearly visible. She was wrapped in a black shawl. By her breast, fresh blood pooled—raw and red as oleander.

Byomkesh bent down and, prying the envelope from Ratikanta’s fingers, slipped it into his own pocket.

Fifteen

Dinner invitation at Pandeyji’s house. The number of guests had increased: Dr. Palit, Miss Manna, Byomkesh, and I. We sat around the table to eat. The main dish among the fare—Kashmiri chicken korma.

Byomkesh placed a piece of meat in his mouth, savoring it with half-closed eyes, then said in a voice thick with emotion, “Pandeyji, I am going to steal.”

Pandeyji raised his brows with a smile. “What will you steal?”

“Your cook.”

--- Page 858 ---
556  
Byomkesh Samagra

Pandeyji burst into laughter and said, “Impossible.”  
“Why impossible?”  
Pandeyji replied, “I am my own cook.”  
“You—so you cooked this ambrosia yourself! Then why bother with a job in the police? Open a hotel, and you’ll be rolling in riches within three days.”  
After a while of laughter and banter, Miss Manna said, “Byomkesh Babu, you have deceived me. That won’t do. You must tell the whole story from beginning to end. Tell me how everything happened—I want to hear it all.”  
Dr. Palit said, “I want to hear it too. These last few days I’ve been half-dead with worry, wondering if I was a suspect. Now, please tell us.”  
Byomkesh said, “Let us finish eating first. I’ll speak after the meal.”  
Having eaten our fill, we stepped outside and sat down. Byomkesh took the stem of the hookah in his hand, and Dr. Palit lit a thick cigar.  
Miss Manna, with a wad of jorda tucked in her cheek, smiled and said, “Now, begin.”  
Byomkesh drew a few slow, deliberate puffs from the hookah, then began to speak, his words unhurried and measured.  
“It was in this very room that I first saw Ratikanta. He had come to invite Pandeyji. Handsome face, blue eyes. He made a light jest at Dipnarayan Singh—called him a ‘great man, a kinsman.’ I didn’t know then how much venom was hidden behind that light jest. I knew nothing at the time, so even the word ‘kinsman’ didn’t prick my ears. Now, of course, I have learned that there was a distant connection between Shakuntala and Ratikanta; both were from Pratapgarh, both from once-prominent but now-fallen families, childhood sweethearts.  
“That night, Ratikanta didn’t learn who I was—Pandeyji had only said, ‘my friend from Calcutta.’ He had no reason to suspect anything. If he had known that the unworthy fellow’s name was Byomkesh Bakshi, who knows what he would have done—perhaps changed his plan altogether. But he was in a bind; there was no time to turn back, the crisis was upon him.  
“As far as I can surmise, the secret romance between Shakuntala and Ratikanta had a past. There were social obstacles to their marriage, so their wild passions found an outlet in clandestine love, deceiving the eyes of society; their reckless, unrestrained hearts took full advantage of modern permissiveness. Yet all was done in secret. They may have lacked moral shame, but they feared public disgrace; besides, ‘stolen love is a hundred times sweeter.’ There is an intense sweetness in hidden love.  
“Then, one day, Dipnarayan saw Shakuntala and fell into the snare of her beauty and youth. Shakuntala saw Dipnarayan’s immense wealth and could not resist temptation. She married him. But she did not let go of Ratikanta. Whether Ratikanta agreed to the marriage, we do not know. Perhaps not entirely, but it was impossible for him to give up Shakuntala. When Shakuntala came to Patna after her marriage, Ratikanta too made arrangements and settled in Patna—perhaps with some help from Dipnarayan himself. Thus, beneath the surface, the old relationship between Ratikanta and Shakuntala continued. The marriage was nothing but a sham.  
“As a ‘kinsman,’ Ratikanta frequented Dipnarayan’s house, but in public he did not display much intimacy with Shakuntala. Their true meetings happened away from prying eyes. Shakuntala would write letters and, late at night, drop them in the mailbox with her own hand; Ratikanta would come on appointed nights, enter through the back door into the courtyard, then climb the iron stairs to the upper floor.”

--- Page 859 ---
Bohni - Patanga 557

Shakuntala would wait by the door, expectant—

“Things were proceeding in this manner when suddenly Mother Nature herself intervened. Just as Dipnarayan fell gravely ill, Shakuntala discovered she was with child. What now? Perhaps others could be deceived, but not Dipnarayan. The two of them conspired: Dipnarayan must be removed quickly; otherwise, their honor, prestige, and royal fortune would be lost, and they would be forced to slink away from respectable society in utter disgrace.

“There is no doubt that this clever plan for murder originated in Ratikanta’s mind. Fate lent its hand as well; a vial of curare had been found with a petty thief. When it came into Ratikanta’s possession, he first set aside a portion of the curare. Then, at the opportune moment—Ratikanta himself broke into the doctor’s dispensary, swapped out the liver injection vial, and left. Afterwards, he went to the house where the feast was being held and spread the news. Everyone assumed it was the work of a common thief.

“That very night, Ratikanta learned my name. Thanks to my translations, my name is not unknown among the Hindi-educated circles. Ratikanta was rattled. But by then, it was too late—the arrow had already left the bow.

“The next morning, the doctor administered the injection, and Dipnarayan died. Ratikanta believed that no one would suspect curare; everyone would think death was due to shock from the liver injection. The doctor himself thought so at first, but when the matter of curare was raised, he grew suspicious. He said, ‘It’s possible.’

“Ratikanta, already shaken, now became even more nervous and made a mistake. This may have been his only error. He thought, curare will surely be found in Dipnarayan’s body; but if no curare is found in the liver vial, suspicion will fall on Dr. Palit for having swapped the vial. Ratikanta had in his possession a harmless liver vial, which he had taken from Dr. Palit’s bag. He sent that harmless vial for analysis.

“When it was discovered that the vial contained no poison, confusion reigned. Poison was found in the body, but not in the medicine—how could this be? After Dipnarayan’s death, only three people had handled the vial—Dr. Palit, Pandeyji, and Ratikanta. Pandeyji and Ratikanta were police officers; therefore, it must have been the doctor’s doing—he had created this tangled situation to confound the police. But what was Dr. Palit’s motive?

“In the meantime, two motives had come to light—money and a secret love affair. The suspicion of a secret affair had been planted in our minds by Dr. Palit himself. If a secret love was the true motive, then the question arose: who was Shakuntala’s Dushyanta? And if money was the motive, then suspicion fell on three people—Debnarayan, Chandni, and Gangadhar Banshi. Shakuntala, too, might have arranged her husband’s murder to avoid disgrace. Any of them could have bribed Dr. Palit handsomely to get the job done. In short, the list of suspects was not small; from Debnarayan to Narmadashankar, even Ghoda Jagannath—each had some interest at stake.

“Ratikanta, however, was determined to pin the blame on Dr. Palit. He went to Buxar and obtained a written confession from a prisoner. We know that the police can extract any confession from such prisoners through threats or inducements. So we saw through Ratikanta’s scheme and smiled inwardly. By then, we had already realized that Ratikanta himself was the culprit.

“Meanwhile, a few minor incidents had occurred. Father and son, Gangadhar and Liladhar, together—”

--- Page 860 ---
BYOMKESH SAMAGRA

He was scheming to swallow twelve thousand rupees. On the other hand, Narmadashankar had been elated at the death of Dipnarayan, thinking he would finally seize the vacant throne of Shakuntala’s heart. He did not know that the throne of Shakuntala’s heart had never been vacant.

Suddenly, all the mysteries were resolved—I discovered who Shakuntala’s Dushyanta was. Shakuntala had painted a picture on the wall. In those days, it was the portrait of her budding love. The first night I saw the painting, I understood nothing; the blue hues of the painting were muted in the blue light. But the next day, when I saw it in daylight, everything became clear in an instant. It was as if a fog had blurred the world, and suddenly the sun burst through. In the painting, Dushyanta’s eyes were blue.

Love is a dangerous thing. Its nature is to reveal itself, to declare, to call out to the world—I love him. Illicit love is even more perilous. When there is no way to express love openly, the heart finds strange disguises to reveal itself. Shakuntala had tried to express her love by painting. The face of Dushyanta in the picture bore no resemblance to Ratikanta, but the eyes were blue. “The wise will seek and find!” Ajit and Pandeyji had both seen the painting, but neither had caught the hint of the blue eyes.

Among all those involved in this affair, only Ratikanta had blue eyes. Therefore, Ratikanta was Shakuntala’s secret lover. Motive and opportunity, intelligence and initiative—by every measure, no one but he could be responsible for Dipnarayan’s death.

But how to catch him? The evidence of blue eyes alone was not enough. The only way was if they met in secret, if I could catch them in such a situation that denial would be impossible.

So I set a trap. I met Shakuntala alone and told her plainly—I know who your Dushyanta is, and I can prove how he killed Dipnarayan. But I am not the police; if you give me one lakh rupees, I will not turn you in. If you refuse, the police will learn everything. Both of you will hang. Shakuntala would not confess, but I saw she was afraid. Then I said—I give you until the end of today to think it over. If you agree to silence me with one lakh rupees, write me a letter tonight, in your own hand, and drop it in the mailbox. The letter need only say one word—Yes. After ten o’clock tonight, I will arrange for Miss Manna to be taken away from here, and there will be no police guards in the corridor. If I do not receive your letter, I will hand all my evidence over to Pandeyji.

Leaving the pale and frightened Shakuntala behind, I departed; Miss Manna took charge of her. Now all that remained was to ensure Shakuntala had no chance to speak with Ratikanta in secret. Then, after consulting with Pandeyji, I arranged the rest. That night, when Ratikanta returned from Buxar, I told him a new story and brought him here to Pandeyji.

Ajit and I left early in the morning and went to the mango grove beside Dipnarayan’s house; Tiwari was there with two men, and we all climbed the trees and hid. Meanwhile, Pandeyji kept Ratikanta detained until half past nine at night, then released him and came to join us. Sitting on the branches in the darkness, we began the patient wait for our prey.

I was on a tree near the back door. Pandeyji came and settled on the tree beside mine.

--- Page 861 ---
Bohni - Patanga 559

We sat, six living souls, in the silent darkness. The clock struck ten. Mist had begun to gather in the sky; drops of water started falling from the leaves with a steady, plopping sound. After that, Miss Manna left for home in her motorcar.

We never noticed when Ratikanta had arrived. He must have come a little late; after being released by Pandeyji, he had gone to his own quarters, fetched his pistol, and then made his way to the mango grove.

We had misjudged Ratikanta’s character somewhat—wherever two or five people are seen working together, there is always one leader, the rest his assistants. In this case, we had assumed Shakuntala was the mastermind, Ratikanta her accomplice. But in truth, it was quite the opposite. Ratikanta’s mind was like that of a savage beast, recognizing no obstacle before his own needs. When he learned that Shakuntala had agreed to reveal the criminal’s name in a letter, he resolved at once to put an end to her. For him, love was never greater than his own life.

We had thought Ratikanta would try to persuade Shakuntala—that if she did not reveal the criminal’s name, no one could catch them, no punishment could befall them. Our plan was to apprehend them at the very moment they discussed these things.

But Ratikanta did not follow that path. He had resolved in his heart to leave no root of harm, to eradicate it completely.

We never realized when Shakuntala, letter in hand, slipped out through the back door; the sound of her footsteps drowned in the steady drip-drip all around. But Ratikanta must have been lying in wait by the door, for he heard her clearly. Suddenly, before our eyes, a torch flared to life, and in that beam we saw Shakuntala’s terrified face. No words passed between them, only the sharp report of a pistol, again and again. Shakuntala crumpled to the ground.

I had a police whistle with me; I blew it with all my might and leapt down from the tree. Pandeyji too jumped down, torch in his left hand, revolver in his right.

Ratikanta had already extinguished his torch. When Pandeyji’s beam fell upon him, he was kneeling, reaching for the letter in Shakuntala’s hand, slipping his pistol into his pocket. Like a wounded tiger, he turned to look at us, then, with lightning speed, drew his pistol from his pocket.

But he had no chance to fire; Pandeyji’s revolver spoke first—

Byomkesh fell silent, and for a moment the room remained steeped in stillness. Dr. Palit’s cheroot had gone out; he relit it. Miss Manna let out a trembling sigh.

“Shakuntala was not a good woman. But—”

Byomkesh said, “Yes. She knew the art of enchantment. Chandni still refuses to believe Shakuntala was guilty—”

I said, “Perhaps it would have been better if we could have caught them alive—”

Pandeyji shook his head. “No, this is better.”

--- Page 862 ---
Stains of Blood

One

The first spring has arrived after the attainment of independence. A gentle breeze has begun to drift in from the south, and on the scattered city trees of Calcutta—those few, urban survivors—there is a flush of reddish new leaves trembling with excitement. I have heard that, at this time, even the glands within the human body are stirred with a fresh surge of sap.

Byomkesh lay sprawled on the taktaposh, reading a book of poetry. I was thinking to myself, Look at him, the poet—dusk is upon us. These days, with the coming of spring, my heart grows strangely wistful. Age is catching up.

As evening approached, Satyabati entered our baithak-khana. I saw that she had done up her hair, wound a garland of jasmine through her bun, and draped herself in a light, saffron-colored sari. It had been a long time since I’d seen her so adorned. She sat down beside the taktaposh, her face bright with a half-smile, and said to Byomkesh, “Why do you spend day and night with your nose buried in books? Come, let’s go out somewhere.”

Byomkesh gave no reply. I asked, “Where do you want to go? To the Maidan?”

Satyabati said, “No, no, outside Calcutta. Somewhere like—Kashmir—or—”

Byomkesh closed his book and slowly sat up, extending his right hand theatrically and, in the pure rhythm of Mandakranta metre, recited:

“The desire to travel is strong within me,
But I have no provisions for the journey.
Chains bind my feet, my mind longs to fly—
Is this the punishment of fate?”

Astonished, I asked, “Where did you get that from?”

“Hmph—why should I tell you?” Byomkesh flopped back down and reopened his book.

When people have nothing to do, they go off on pilgrimages with their uncles; Byomkesh, meanwhile, had immersed himself in the old poets of Bengali literature. He had started with Bharat Chandra and was working his way through every poet, one by one. He had threatened that he wouldn’t even spare the ultra-modern poets. I had grown apprehensive—one day, he might even begin writing poetry himself. Now that rhyme and metre have fallen out of fashion, there’s nothing to stop anyone from composing verse. But the thought of the satyanweshi Byomkesh writing poetry—what a deadly thing that would be—sent a shiver down my spine. It all began when I bought Khoka a copy of ‘Abol Tabol’; that was the source of Byomkesh’s poetic inspiration. Then, becoming a partner in the bookshop had only made matters worse.

Satyabati gave Byomkesh’s big toe a playful twist and said, “Get up, won’t you? Why are you lying down again?”

--- Page 863 ---
Rokter Daag 561

Byomkesh sat up with a start and said, "Do you know how much it costs to go to Kashmir?"

"How much?"

"At least a thousand rupees. Where would I get that kind of money?"

Satyabati, her temper flaring, stood up and said, "I don’t know about all that. Just tell me if you’ll go or not."

"I told you, I don’t have the money."

At that moment, there was a knock at the outer door. What promised to be a most enjoyable marital quarrel was thus abruptly interrupted. Satyabati, having scorched Byomkesh with a few sharp, half-spoken barbs, retreated into the inner rooms.

I lit the lamp and opened the door. The man standing outside appeared, at first glance, to be little more than a youth. Not very tall, slim and wiry, with a fair, handsome face marked by the faintest trace of a moustache. His attire was immaculate, from the deerskin shoes on his feet to the sheer muslin panjabi draped over his frame—everything about him was impeccable.

"Whom do you seek?"

"The satyanweshi, Byomkesh Babu."

"Come in," I said, stepping aside from the door.

The man entered and stood beneath the bright electric light, allowing me a better look at his features. Not as young as I had first thought; there was a subtle ripeness to his complexion. His eyes bore the imprint of worldly experience, with fine shadows etched at their corners, and beneath the outward delicacy of his face, the bones had already begun to harden with age. Yet he could not have been more than twenty-five.

Byomkesh, who had been observing the visitor from beside the takhtposh, now rose and took a seat in a chair. Indicating the chair opposite, he said, "Sit. What brings you to me?"

The man did not answer at once. He sat down, studied us both with careful attention for a moment, and then said, "You’ll do for my purpose."

Byomkesh raised his brows. "Is that so? And what is this purpose?"

The young man drew a bundle of notes from his side pocket and, with an air of indifference, tossed them onto the table before Byomkesh. "If I should die suddenly, you are to investigate the cause of my death. That is the task. Since I may not be able to pay you later, I am giving you your fee in advance. Count it—one thousand rupees."

Byomkesh gazed at the youth with narrowed eyes for a moment, then counted the bundle. Ten crisp notes of one hundred rupees each. Setting the notes to one side of the table, Byomkesh lazily glanced at me; a glimmer of amusement flickered in his eyes. Then, fixing a grave look upon the young man’s face, he said, "I have a few questions for you. Whether I accept your case will depend on your answers."

The youth opened a gold cigarette case and offered it to Byomkesh, who shook his head in refusal. Lighting a cigarette for himself, the young man exhaled a plume of smoke and said, "Ask. But I may not answer every question."

Byomkesh was silent for a moment, then asked in a languid tone, "What is your name?"

A quick smile played across the young man’s lips—a rather captivating smile. He replied, "I haven’t told you that yet. My name is Satyakam Das."

"Satyakam?"

"Yes! Just as you are a satyanweshi, I am Satyakam."

--- Page 864 ---
