“Do you suspect anyone?”
“I do. But I won’t name names.”
“You won’t even try to save your own life?”
Satyakam’s face twisted into a desolate expression as he made to rise. “There’s no use trying, Byomkesh-babu. Well, I’ll take your leave now, I suppose you have no more questions. I have an appointment tonight.”
That this appointment was not of a business nature was evident from the sly smile that played on his lips. As he reached the door, Byomkesh called after him, “If someone murders you, how will I know?”
Satyakam turned and replied, “You’ll read about it in the papers. Besides, you can always make inquiries. I doubt you’ll have to wait long.”
Once Satyakam had departed, I closed the door and sank down onto the divan. Satyabati re-entered, her face alight with laughter. It seemed she had been waiting just outside the door.
“You were worrying about that thousand rupees, and now you’ve got it!”
Byomkesh, with a sour face, handed the notes towards Satyabati and said, “The ant eats sugar, Chintamani supplies it. Well, what else—start making arrangements for our trip to Kashmir.”
He turned to me. “Well, what did you make of the lad?”
I said, “I’ve never seen such a brazen, two-faced scoundrel at such a young age.”
Byomkesh said, “Nor have I. But it’s strange—he doesn’t want to save his own life, but he wants an investigation after his death!”

Two

The next morning, Satyabati said, “If we’re going to Kashmir, where are the quilts and bedding?”
Byomkesh replied, “Why, didn’t we have them last year in Patna?”
Satyabati said, “Those all belong to your elder brothers. Do we have anything of our own? The mild winters of Calcutta are manageable, but for Kashmir, we’ll need at least two English blankets and a beaver coat for me.”
“Hm. Come on, Ajit, let’s go out.”
I asked, “Where are we going?”
He said, “Let’s go to Suchitra Emporium. We’ll kill two birds with one stone.”
I said, “Why not take Satyabati along? She can choose her own things.”
Byomkesh glanced at Satyabati. In a plaintive voice, she said, “I’d love to go, but how can I? The school car will be coming for Khoka.”
Byomkesh said, “You don’t need to come. I’ll pick out your things myself. Don’t worry, you won’t be disappointed.”
Satyabati cast a laughing, sidelong glance at Byomkesh as she went inside, her unwavering faith in his taste evident. In truth, I had always done the shopping for Satyabati’s fashionable things. But now spring had arrived, the month of Phalgun was passing—
The two of us set out. By half past nine, we reached Dharmatala Street and saw that the emporium’s...

--- Page 866 ---
The door stood open, the heavy curtains drawn aside from the immense glass windows. We stepped inside. It was a vast room, its mosaic floor dotted here and there with showcases displaying an array of elegant wares. A handful of customers, most of them upper-class ladies, had already arrived and were browsing. The staff stood at their stations, attending to the whims of the clientele. A distinguished elderly gentleman paced from one end of the room to the other, his watchful gaze sweeping over everything.

As we entered, the elderly gentleman approached and greeted us with respectful courtesy.  
“Please, come in. What may I show you?”

Byomkesh glanced about the room, his voice hesitant. “Just a small thing—two British blankets. Would you have them?”

“Certainly. This way, please.” The gentleman led us aside. “Anything else?”

“And a ladies’ beaver coat.”

“You’ll find both. Here’s the lift—upstairs you’ll get blankets and beaver coats alike.”

In the corner of the room, a small lift was ascending and descending. As we moved to stand before it, a voice called from behind, “I’ll take care of these gentlemen.”

Turning at the familiar voice, we saw Satyakam. Dressed in a silk suit, his appearance was neat and trim—he must have been in this very room all along, but his foreign attire had made him unremarkable to us until now. The elderly gentleman, seeing him, said, “Ah—very well. Take them upstairs, they want to buy British blankets and a beaver coat.” With a brief smile in our direction, he moved off elsewhere.

Byomkesh glanced quickly from Satyakam to the elderly gentleman, and asked softly, “Is he your—?”

Satyakam suppressed a smile. “Partner.”

“In other words—your father!”

Satyakam nodded in assent.

Though I had seen the elderly gentleman before, I had not truly observed him until now. He stood a little distance away, conversing with another customer, yet every so often, his gaze flickered uneasily in our direction. He was a tall, broad-shouldered man of dusky complexion, his jawline firm and pronounced. He looked to be about forty-five, with a touch of grey at his temples. Despite the practiced courtesy of a shopkeeper, there was an ascetic harshness about his face—a certain austerity. I suspected that, beneath the veneer of business, his temperament was rather stern.

At that moment, the lift descended. We stepped into its cage and arrived at the second floor.

Satyakam arched his brows at Byomkesh with a playful gesture. “Are you really here to buy something? Or is this a field investigation?”

“I intend to buy.”

The upper floor was not arranged like the one below; it resembled a storeroom more than a showroom. Yet even here, a few customers wandered about. Satyakam led us to the section for warm clothing. At his signal, an attendant brought out a variety of British blankets for our inspection. Byomkesh, in matters such as these, could hardly tell the difference between chintz and chintamani; it was left to me to select two blankets. The price was steep, but the quality was excellent.

Next, the beaver coats. Coats of every color and size—all of them exorbitantly priced. As we examined them, Satyakam remarked, “Are you thinking about the size?”

--- Page 867 ---
Rokter Dag 565

A beaver coat can be a little loose, it doesn’t matter. Take whichever you like, and if it’s completely unsuitable, I’ll exchange it for you.’

I was drawn to a deep purple coat, but hesitated when I saw the price tag. Satyakam, sensing my predicament, said, ‘Don’t worry about the price. That’s for regular customers. You’ll get it at the purchase price. —Come.’

He led us to the cashier and said, ‘These items are to be given at the purchase rate. Please make out the cash memo.’

‘As you wish,’ said the elderly cashier, writing out the memo. I noticed the price was nearly fifty rupees less than the tag. My spirits lifted; the impression I’d formed of Satyakam last night shifted considerably. No, whatever else he may be, the fellow is not a complete bloodsucker.

At that moment, a young woman appeared on the upper floor. Fair-skinned, youthful, her attire and manner spoke of a life of indulgence and refinement. Satyakam turned his head once to glance at her; his expression changed. With one eye narrowed, he said to us, ‘I suppose you don’t have anything else to buy? In that case—I have a new customer—well then, goodbye.’

Like a bee drawn to the scent of honey, Satyakam flew straight toward the young woman. By the time we had our purchases packed and were about to descend, we saw that Satyakam had thoroughly enchanted her; the young woman, drinking in his every word, followed him wherever he led.

Back at home, we showed Satyabati our purchases. She was utterly delighted and heaped all the praise for our discerning choices upon Byomkesh. Such is the glory of spring!

When I mentioned the reduction in price, Satyabati exclaimed in surprise, ‘Really? What a nice boy Satyakam is!’

Byomkesh exhaled a plume of smoke toward the ceiling and said, ‘A very nice boy! A golden boy! If no one murders him, his shop will be bankrupt soon enough.’

In the evening, Byomkesh took me out for a walk. This time our path led toward Amherst Street. By the time we reached house number 33/34, dusk had fallen thick and heavy.

At this hour of twilight, even the footpaths of Calcutta grow deserted for a brief spell, perhaps waiting for the streetlights to flicker on. We stopped in front of our destination. There were few passersby, only a man wrapped in a shawl pacing the pavement, who moved a little distance away when he saw us.

House number 33/34 did not stand flush with the footpath. First, a small iron gate, then a narrow brick-paved lane like an alley, running twenty or twenty-five feet up to the main entrance. A two-storied house, not very large from the front. On either side of the main door were two windows, and above them on the first floor, two round balconies. No lights were yet burning inside. Standing on the pavement, I thought I saw a woman sitting in one of the upstairs balconies, reading a book or perhaps sewing. Through the wrought-iron railing of the balcony, I caught a faint glimpse.

‘Byomkesh-babu!’

At the sudden call from behind, we both turned. The man we’d seen pacing the pavement, wrapped in his shawl, had come up behind us. A well-built young man,

--- Page 868 ---
566  
Byomkesh Samagra

His hair was cropped short, and his face seemed oddly familiar. Byomkesh asked, “Who are you?”  
The young man replied, “Don’t you recognize me, sir? I came the other day to collect a subscription for Saraswati Puja. My name is Nanda Ghosh, I live in your para.”  
Byomkesh said, “Ah, now I remember. But you’re from the other side of the neighborhood—what brings you here, wandering about at this hour of the evening?”  
“Sir—” One of Nanda Ghosh’s hands slipped out from under his shawl and just as quickly disappeared back inside. Still, I caught a glimpse of it—a vindipal, a heavy iron knuckle-duster. Though small in size, in the hands of a strong man it could be a deadly weapon. Byomkesh eyed Nanda Ghosh with suspicion and said, “What’s your game? Out with it.”  
“My game—sir—” Nanda edged closer and lowered his voice, “I’ll tell you, sir. There’s a fellow living in this house—I’ve come to thrash him.”  
“Is that so! And why do you want to thrash him?”  
“There’s a reason, sir. But what are you doing here? Do you know someone in this house?”  
“I know Satyakam. He’s the one you want to thrash, isn’t it?”  
“Sir—” Nanda seemed a little unsettled. “Are you very close to him?”  
“No, not close. But I’d like to know why you want to beat him up. Has he wronged you in some way?”  
“Wronged—well, that’s a long story, sir. If you want to hear it, come with me. Bhuteshwar’s akhra is nearby, I’ll tell you everything there.”  
“Bhuteshwar’s akhra?”  
“Yes, sir, our wrestling club. It’s just down the lane. Come.”  
“Let’s go.”  
By now, the streetlights had come on. We followed Nanda into a narrow alley, and after a short walk, arrived at a walled courtyard. Beside the courtyard stood a couple of damp, crumbling rooms, their windows glowing with light. The yard itself was almost dark; a few bare-chested youths were doing push-ups, swinging mughurs, and otherwise hardening their bodies in various ways. Nanda led us past them and into one of the rooms.  
A satranji was spread on the floor; an enormous man sat there. Nanda introduced him: this was the wrestling club’s ustad, Bhuteshwar Bag. The name suited him—his skin was ghostly pale, his face tiger-like, and his body as massive as an elephant’s. Not a single hair on his head; he must have been close to sixty. Perhaps in his youth he’d been a goonda or a wrestler, but now, in old age, he ran this gymnasium.  
Nanda said, “Bhuteshwarda, this is Byomkesh Babu, a famous detective. He knows Satyakam.”  
Bhuteshwar turned his tigerish eyes on Byomkesh and asked, “Are you with the police? That boy’s guardian?”  
Byomkesh politely explained that he was not with the police, and had only met Satyakam once. He simply wanted to know why there was a need to beat Satyakam—he had no other motive. Bhuteshwar softened a little and said, “That boy’s a real scoundrel. Some of the gentlemen in our para have complained to us. He harasses the girls. This is a respectable neighborhood—we won’t tolerate such things here.”  
At that moment, a few more burly wrestlers entered the room and sat down around us.

--- Page 869 ---
The Bloodstain 567

He scrutinized us with a hard, unblinking stare. It was clear as daylight that the plan to thrash Satyakam was not the whim of a single man; the entire gymnasium committee had sanctioned it. A chill of unease crept over me regarding our own safety. The situation was precarious—Satyakam’s trap might well snap shut upon us instead.

Byomkesh, however, kept his composure. In a calm voice, he said, “If someone from the para misbehaves, it is the duty of the people of the para to discipline him. Such matters cannot be settled by outsiders. I have no objection if you wish to teach Satyakam a lesson. From what I know of him, a couple of blows across his back might do him some good. Only one thing—don’t go so far as murder! And, do it carefully, so that you don’t get caught.”

Nanda grinned and said, “That’s exactly why I’ve taken the job upon myself, sir. I’ll give him a few knocks and slip away. I’m not from this para— even if they recognize me, they won’t be able to identify me.”

Byomkesh smiled and rose to his feet. “Still, if any trouble arises, let me know. We’ll take our leave now. Namaskar, Bhuteshwar-babu.”

Nanda escorted us to the main road and then returned to the akhra. Byomkesh exhaled deeply and said, “My word, we really stuck our necks into the tiger’s den!”

I said, “But was it right of you to encourage them to beat up Satyakam? You’ve taken his money.”

Byomkesh replied, “If a few blows can save his life, isn’t that better?”

Three

Though I have never worked in an office in my life, for some reason I always wake late on Sunday mornings. My ancestors were government clerks, perhaps the stain of servitude lingers in my blood.

The next day was Sunday. At half past seven, rubbing the sleep from my eyes, I entered the outer room to find Byomkesh holding the newspaper wide open in both hands, staring at it intently. He didn’t turn his gaze at my arrival; it was as if he addressed the newspaper itself, reciting, “O messenger, your tidings are like the dreams of night!”

His demeanor struck me as odd. I asked, “What’s happened?”

He lowered the paper and said, “Satyakam died last night.”

“What! How did he die?”

“I don’t know. Get ready—we have to leave within half an hour.”

I picked up the newspaper. At the bottom of the middle page, a five-line report—

—In the small hours of this morning, Satyakam Das, the well-known proprietor of Suchitra Emporium, Dharmatala, has died under suspicious circumstances. Police have taken up the investigation.

So Satyakam had sensed it—he had foreseen his death. But so soon! The first thing that came to mind was Nanda Ghosh, skulking in the shadows beneath his shawl outside the house last evening—

At half past eight, Byomkesh and I arrived at Amherst Street. A constable stood on the pavement outside the gate; after a bit of scrutiny, he allowed us to enter.

--- Page 870 ---
568  
Byomkesh Samagra

We arrived at the main entrance by way of the brick-paved road. The front door stood open, but there was no one in sight. Nor was there any sound of weeping or lamentation from inside the house. Byomkesh, upon reaching the doorway, halted abruptly and silently pointed to the ground. I saw that, right in front of the door where the brick path ended, there was a stain of blood. Not fresh blood, but a patch the size of a palm, dried and caked into a dark crust.

We exchanged a glance; Byomkesh nodded. Skirting the bloodstained spot, we entered the house.

A wide veranda stretched before us, with two doors on either side. One was locked, the other open; through the open door, a medium-sized office room was visible. In the center of the room stood a large table, and facing it, Ushapati Babu sat alone.

Ushapati Babu sat with both elbows resting on the table, his chin cupped in his palms. As we entered, he raised his sorrow-filled eyes to us and, in a dry, weary voice, asked, “What do you want?”

Byomkesh stepped up to the table and, in a tone of sympathy, said, “Forgive me for disturbing you at such a time. My name is Byomkesh Bakshi—”

Ushapati Babu, becoming slightly more alert, turned his gaze from one to the other, then said, “I’ve seen you somewhere before. Perhaps at Suchitra’s. —What did you say your name was?”

“Byomkesh Bakshi. This is Ajit Bandyopadhyay. —Yesterday we visited your shop—”

It did not seem that Ushapati Babu remembered our names from before, but the natural courtesy of a shopkeeper toward a customer appeared ingrained in his very bones; so, without displaying any impatience, he said, “Is there something you need? I’m a bit—there’s been an accident at home—”

Byomkesh said, “I know. That’s why we’ve come. Satyakam Babu—”

“You knew Satyakam?”

“I met him only the day before yesterday. He came to me with a proposal—”

“What proposal?”

“He proposed that, should he die suddenly, I would investigate the circumstances of his death.”

Ushapati Babu now sat up straight, staring at us for a moment with unblinking eyes, as if suppressing a surge of emotion. Then, in a controlled voice, he said, “Please, have a seat. —So Satyakam understood, after all. But forgive me, I don’t understand why Satyakam came to you. You—your identity—are you with the police? But the police were here last night, they—”

“No, I am not with the police. I am a satyanweshi—a seeker of truth. You could say a private detective.”

“Oh—” Ushapati Babu was silent for a long while, then said, “Did Satyakam tell you whom he suspected?”

“No, he mentioned no names. —If you permit, I would like to investigate.”

“But—the police have already taken charge of the investigation. What more could you do?”

“I don’t know yet if I can do anything at all, but I can try.”

--- Page 871 ---
Rokter Daag 569

Even in the midst of such overwhelming grief, Ushapati Babu had not lost his presence of mind—this became clear to me now.

He said, "You are a private detective. How much must I pay you for your services?"

Byomkesh replied, "You need not pay me anything. My fee has already been settled by Satyakam Babu."

Ushapati Babu fixed Byomkesh with a sharp gaze, then lowered his eyes and murmured, "Oh. Well, investigate if you must. But there is no use, Byomkesh Babu."

"No use? Why not?"

"Satyakam will not return, will he? What is the point of muddying the waters?"

Byomkesh regarded Ushapati Babu with steady eyes for a moment, then spoke in a calm voice, "I understand your feelings. Rest assured, I will not stir up trouble. My only aim is to uncover the truth."

Ushapati Babu let out a weary sigh. "Very well. Tell me what I must do."

Byomkesh said, "I know nothing of how or when Satyakam Babu died last night. Can you tell me?"

Ushapati Babu’s face grew even more drawn. He ran a hand across his chest and said, "Who else is there to tell you but me? Last night, at one o’clock, I was asleep in my own room when a sound woke me. A sudden, sharp noise. It seemed to come from the direction of the main entrance—"

"Forgive me, where is your bedroom?"

Ushapati Babu pointed upward toward the ceiling. "The room above this one. I sleep alone; my wife sleeps in the adjoining room."

"And which room did Satyakam Babu sleep in?"

"Satyakam slept downstairs. That room across the verandah, the one with the locked door—that was his. My wife’s bedroom is directly above his."

"Why did Satyakam Babu sleep downstairs?"

Ushapati Babu did not answer. He gazed absently out the window. From his manner, it was clear that Satyakam slept downstairs for the convenience of coming and going at night without disturbance. There was no fixed hour for his return home.

At that moment, the curtain over the inner doorway was drawn aside and a young woman entered, carrying a glass of sherbet. She stopped short at the sight of us, hesitated, and in an uncertain voice said, "Mama—" but then stood rooted to the spot, silent. The girl was seventeen or eighteen, not beautiful, but well-formed and lively. At present, a shadow of anxiety darkened her face and eyes.

Ushapati Babu turned to her and said, "No need." The girl withdrew.

Byomkesh asked, "Who else lives in your house?"

Ushapati Babu replied, "Besides us, my nephew and niece live here."

"That was your niece?"

"Yes."

"How long have they been with you?"

"Their father died about a year ago. Their mother passed away before that. Since then, I have been their guardian. Apart from us, there is no one else in the house."

"Servants?"

--- Page 872 ---
Byomkesh Samagra

“The old servant, Sahadev, lives in the house. Besides him, there’s a maid and a cook, but they don’t stay at night.”

“I see. Now, tell me about last night’s events.”

Mr. Ushapati ran his palm over his eyes once and said, “Yes. Hearing the noise, I opened the balcony door and stepped outside. It was pitch dark below; I couldn’t see a thing. Then, from near the main door, Sahadev suddenly screamed… I rushed down, and saw Sahadev had opened the door, and—Satyakam was lying in front of it. Lifeless, a bullet had entered from his back.”

“A bullet! From a gun?”

“Yes. Satyakam would return home late every night. Sahadev used to sleep on the veranda, and whenever he heard a knock, he’d get up and open the door. Last night, before he could open it after hearing the knock, someone shot Satyakam from behind.”

“A bullet. I had thought—” Byomkesh paused, then said, “Go on.”

Mr. Ushapati let out a stifled sigh. “What else? I telephoned the police.”

Byomkesh sat for a while, head bowed in thought. Then he looked up and asked, “Who locked Satyakam’s room?”

Ushapati replied, “Whenever Satyakam left the house, he’d lock his room. I believe he did so yesterday as well, and then—”

“I understand. So the room key is with the police?”

“Most likely.”

“The police haven’t opened the room?”

“No.”

“Well, I have nothing more to ask you for now. I’d like to speak to the other members of the household.”

“Tell me whom to call.”

“Is Sahadev in the house?”

“Of course. I’ll call him.”

Mr. Ushapati rose and called Sahadev from near the inner doorway, then returned and sat down.

Sahadev entered. A decrepit old man, little more than skin and bones. A wild shock of white hair on his head, white brows, even the pupils of his eyes had turned pale. Loose skin, slack muscles, and a vacant, almost simple-minded look on his face.

Byomkesh asked, “Your name is Sahadev? How many years have you worked in this house?”

Sahadev did not reply. He stared blankly, first at us, then at Mr. Ushapati. Ushapati said, “He’s been here since my father-in-law’s time—almost thirty-five years.”

Byomkesh turned to Sahadev. “Last night, you—”

Before Byomkesh could finish, Sahadev folded his hands and said, “I know nothing, babu.”

Byomkesh said, “Listen to my question and answer. Last night, when Satyakam-babu knocked at the door, were you awake?”

--- Page 873 ---
Rokter Daag 571

Sahadev, as before, spoke with folded hands, “I don’t know anything, babu.”

Byomkesh pierced him with a sharp gaze and said, “Try to remember. Did you hear any sudden noise at that time?”

“I don’t know anything, babu.”

No matter how many questions Byomkesh asked thereafter, Sahadev gave only one answer—“I don’t know anything, babu.” How much truth lay in this all-encompassing ignorance was hard to guess; in short, even if Sahadev did know something, he would not say it. Byomkesh, growing irritated, said, “You may go. Ushapati-babu, please call your niece now.”

Ushapati-babu said to Sahadev, “Go call Chumki.”

Sahadev left. A short while later, Chumki entered, and with a deliberate firmness, came to stand beside the table. I saw that the shadow of anxiety on her face had deepened; she raised her eyes towards us only to lower them again at once.

Byomkesh spoke in an easy tone, “I hear from your uncle that you have been living in this house for about a year now. Where did you live before?”

Chumki replied in a subdued voice, “In Maniktala.”

“Do you study?”

“I’m in college.”

“And your brother?”

“My brother is in college too.”

“All right, when did you find out last night?”

Chumki took a breath and said slowly, “I was sleeping. My brother came and knocked on the door, calling me. That’s when I woke up.”

“Oh—you sleep with your door locked at night?”

Chumki seemed a little startled, and said, “Yes.”

“Is your bedroom downstairs or upstairs?”

“Downstairs, at the back. My brother’s room is next to mine.”

“Then you didn’t hear the gunshot?”

“No.”

“What did you do after waking up?”

“My brother and I came to this room. Uncle had called the police.”

“And your aunt?”

“I didn’t see her then. Going upstairs from here, I saw she was lying unconscious on the floor of her own room.” Chumki’s eyes brimmed with tears.

Byomkesh said kindly, “All right, you may go now. Send your brother in.”

Chumki had barely left the room when her brother entered; it seemed he had been waiting just outside the door. There was a certain resemblance between the siblings. But the boy’s eyes were of a peculiar kind. Like an owl’s, his gaze held an unwavering, motionless intensity. With great composure, he came to stand beside the table and, without blinking, fixed his eyes on Byomkesh.

The questioning began.

“What is your name?”

“Shitangshu Dutta.”

--- Page 874 ---
572  
Byomkesh Samagra

“How old are you?”  
“Twenty.”  
“Were you awake last night?”  
“Yes.”  
“What were you doing?”  
“I was reading.”  
“What were you reading? Exam material?”  
“No. I was reading Gorky’s ‘Lower Depths.’ I’m in the habit of reading at night.”  
“Oh… Did you hear the sound of a gunshot?”  
“I did. But I didn’t realize it was a gunshot.”  
“And then?”  
“I heard Sahadeb’s scream and went to see.”  
“Afterwards, did you come back and wake your sister?”  
“Yes.”  

Byomkesh sat for a while, his chin resting on his palm. I noticed that Ushapati Babu, too, sat impassively, and I doubted whether any of the questions and answers were reaching his ears. He seemed to have sunk into the dark depths of his own mind.

Byomkesh resumed his questioning.  
“When you sleep at night, do you keep your door closed?”  
“No, it stays open.”  
“Is Chumki’s door closed?”  
“Yes. She’s a girl, after all.”  
“Alright. —After everyone had gone to bed last night, did you go outside the house?”  
“No.”  
“Is there any way to leave the house besides the main door?”  
“Yes. The side door.”  
“Did anyone go out through the side door last night?”  
“No. If they had, I would have known. The side door is right next to my room. When it’s opened, it makes a creaking sound. Besides, at night the side door is locked.”  
“Is that so! Who keeps the key?”  
“Sahadeb.”  
“Hm. Did you know that Satyakam Babu used to return home late at night?”  
“I did.”  
“Could you tell every day when he came home?”  
“Not every day, only sometimes.”  
“Alright, you may go now.”  

Shitanshu gazed at Byomkesh for a moment longer, unblinking, then slowly walked away.

Byomkesh turned to Ushapati Babu and said in a slightly hesitant voice, “Ushapati Babu, may I see your wife now?”  
Ushapati Babu started, “My wife! But she—her condition—”  
“I understand her condition. She doesn’t have to come here, I’ll go to her room and—”

--- Page 875 ---
Rokter Daag 573

“A word or two—”

Byomkesh’s sentence was left unfinished, for at that moment a woman, her hands trembling with agitation, swept aside the curtain and entered the room. There could be no doubt that she was Ushapati Babu’s wife. Fixing her gaze on Byomkesh, she said in a sharp, piercing voice, “Why are you harassing my husband like this? What do you want? Why have you come here?”

We hastily rose to our feet. The lady appeared to be around forty, though her features suggested a younger age. Her complexion was fair, and the traces of beauty had not yet entirely vanished from her face. At this moment, however, grief for her son was overshadowed by an expression of anger. Byomkesh, in an exceedingly gentle tone, replied, “Please forgive me, madam. I have come here only out of a sense of duty—”

She interrupted, “Who invited you here? You have no business here. Please leave us alone. Do not disturb us any further.”

Byomkesh said, “Do you not wish for the truth behind Satyakam Babu’s death to come to light?”

“No, I do not. What’s done is done. Please go, spare us.”

“Very well, I am leaving.”

We glanced at Ushapati Babu. He was staring at his wife in astonishment, as if unable to believe his own eyes and ears. The lady, too, cast a glance at her husband, then swiftly left the room.

Four

We stood outside the main door. Ushapati Babu had followed us, the look of astonishment still lingering on his face. As he began to close the door, he said, “Please forgive us, considering our state of mind. Namaskar.”

The door was nearly shut when Byomkesh said, “What’s that?”

I had not noticed it before, but now, about a hand’s breadth above the lower threshold on the outside of the door, a golden disc gleamed. Ushapati Babu paused in the act of closing the door. The disc was slightly larger than a silver rupee. Byomkesh bent down to examine it, running his finger over it. “A tinsel disc, fastened to the door with a tack,” he said.

Straightening up, he asked Ushapati Babu, “What is this?”

Ushapati Babu replied hesitantly, “I don’t know. I don’t recall seeing it before.”

Byomkesh said, “Someone has put it here recently. If there were children in the house, it would make sense. But—perhaps you should inquire?”

Ushapati Babu called for Sahadev, who, as usual, said, “I know nothing about it, Babu.” Chumki, too, had nothing to say. Shitanshu added, “When I came home yesterday evening, it wasn’t there.”

My mind began to whirl with thoughts. Had the murderer of Satyakam left this as a clue to his identity? A challenge worthy of Harton! Such things are seen in sensational novels. But—

No clue could be found. We took our leave.

Once out on the street, Byomkesh glanced at his watch and said, “It’s not yet ten o’clock. Come on—”

--- Page 876 ---
574  
Byomkesh Samagra

“Let’s take a turn by the thana.”

As we walked towards the police station, Byomkesh asked at one point, “You heard the family’s statement. What did you think?”

That very question had been circling in my own mind. I replied, “None of them seemed particularly grief-stricken.”

Byomkesh said, “There’s a proverb: ‘A little sorrow makes one faint, too much sorrow turns one to stone.’”

I said, “There may be such a proverb, but the behavior of Ushapati Babu and his wife didn’t seem natural at all. Satyakam may not have been a good boy, may well have tormented his parents with his unruliness, all that could be true. Still, he was their son. Their only son. I believe there’s something deeply wrong in this family.”

“Of course. Satyakam himself was a deep flaw. Anyway, did you understand the meaning of the tinsel disc on the door?”

“No. Did you?”

“It could be entirely accidental. But if it’s not—”

Arriving at the thana, we found that the officer in charge, Bhabanibabu, was an acquaintance. An elderly man; he was working with a cross-kent spread open on the table. He didn’t seem particularly pleased to see us. Still, after displaying the necessary courtesies, he said in a low voice, “Why are you mixed up in this again?”

Byomkesh replied, “Got caught up by a twist of fate.”

Bhabanibabu, still speaking softly as before, said, “The boy was a thorough scoundrel. Whoever killed him did the world a favor. Such a man deserves a medal.”

Byomkesh said, “Indeed. Do as you see fit, I have no wish to interfere. I only want to know—”

Bhabanibabu pierced him with a sharp look and said, “Truth-seeking? What do you want to know?”

“The post-mortem report hasn’t come yet, I suppose?”

“No. We might get it by evening.”

“I’ll call you after evening—was death caused by a gunshot?”

“Not a large gun, a pistol or revolver. The bullet entered the left side of the back, but didn’t exit in front. It’s still lodged inside the body. The hole in the back is very small, so it seems to be a pistol or revolver.”

“The hole’s in the back, so whoever fired was behind Satyakam.”

“Yes. Perhaps he was hiding in the bushes inside the gate, and the moment Satyakam stood in front of the main door, he fired, then slipped out through the gate.”

“Hm. Did you know there’s a gymnasium club in this para?”

“I know. It’s not their doing. They might rough someone up, but not kill—they’re all gentlemen’s sons.”

Gentlemen’s sons don’t kill—hearing that from a policeman was novel. But Byomkesh let it pass, and said, “Speaking of gentlemen’s sons, Satyakam’s cousin lives in the house, have you seen him?”

Bhabanibabu smiled a little. “I’ve seen him. He’s known to the police.”

“Really? What has he done?”

--- Page 877 ---
Rokter Daag 575

“The boy was decent enough, but during the last riots, his father was murdered by Muslims. Since then, his nature has changed. We suspect he’s committed at least three murders. Of course, there’s no solid proof.”

“From the look in his eyes, I had a similar suspicion. Do you think he’s involved in this matter?”

“Nothing can be said for certain, Byomkesh Babu. Where someone like Satyakam is concerned, anything is possible. However, as far as I could learn, when the murder took place, he was inside the house. Hearing Sahadev’s scream, his uncle and he reached the front door together. Since Satyakam was shot from behind, it wouldn’t have been possible for him.”

Byomkesh asked, “Would it have been possible to shoot from the roof?”

Bhabanibabu replied, “If the shot had come from the roof, the bullet would have entered from above and gone downward. But the bullet went from the back to the front. Therefore—”

At that moment, the telephone rang. Bhabanibabu exchanged a few words over the phone and then told us, “I have to leave at once. An urgent summons—”

“We’ll be going too.” Byomkesh stood up and said, “By the way, what things were with Satyakam at the time of his death—?”

“They’re in the next room, go and have a look.” Saying this, Bhabanibabu began fastening his belt.

In the adjoining room, several items lay on a table. I immediately recognized the gold cigarette case. Besides that, there was a whisky flask, a leather wallet, a small electric torch, and a few other things. Byomkesh glanced over them and returned. By then, Bhabanibabu had finished buckling his belt and was slipping a pistol from a drawer into the holster at his waist. He said, “Seen everything? Nothing else to look at? Well, I’ll be off.”

Bhabanibabu departed. From his demeanor, it seemed he had no intention of trying to apprehend the culprit. In the end, Satyakam’s death would remain an unsolved mystery.

We too left the house. Byomkesh said, “Since we’ve come this far, let’s go see Bag’s den.”

“Will we find anyone there at this hour?”

“Let’s see. Even if no one else is there, Bag Mashai will surely be in his cave.”

But Bag was not in his cave. When we arrived, we found the door locked. A servant-class man was sitting on the veranda, smoking a bidi. He said, “Looking for Bhutu Sardar? He left for Kashi on the morning train.”

Byomkesh said, “Really! All the way to Kashi! And who are you?”

The man replied, “I’m his servant, sir. I sweep the rooms, wash the clothes, fill the water pots. This morning, when I came to sweep, Sardar was reading the newspaper. I filled the water pot and brought it in, and Sardar was all dressed up, ready to go. He said, ‘I’m off to Kashi, if the boys come in the evening, let them know.’”

There was no doubt left—Bhuteshwar Bag had read the news in the paper and vanished without delay.

We returned home around a quarter past eleven. In front of the closed door, Nanda Ghosh was pacing, waiting anxiously. His face was drawn, his eyes full of uneasy apprehension. Byomkesh knocked on the door and, with a faint smile, asked Nanda, “What news?”

--- Page 878 ---
‘Yes, sir…’ said Nanda, licking his lips.

Putiram came and opened the door. We brought Nanda inside and sat down. Nanda licked his lips a few more times and said, ‘Have you heard about Satyakam?’

Byomkesh, lighting a cigarette, replied, ‘I’ve heard. Where did you hear it?’

Nanda said, ‘This morning I went to a friend’s house across the para, and there I heard that last night someone shot Satyakam dead. But I don’t know anything about it, sir. Yesterday evening, after you all left the akhra, I didn’t go that way again.’

Byomkesh said, ‘Sit down, I have a few questions for you. Among the people you know across the para, does anyone own a pistol or a revolver?’

‘No, sir. Even if someone does, I don’t know about it.’

‘No one in your akhra has one?’

‘I don’t know. But once, a man came to Bhuteshwar to sell a stolen pistol.’

‘A stolen pistol.’

‘Yes, sir. I’ve heard that after the war, it was easy to buy stolen pistols.’

‘Did Bhuteshwar buy it?’

‘I don’t know. He didn’t buy it in front of us.’

‘Alright, let’s leave that. —Satyakam used to chase after girls from respectable families. Can you say how he went about it?’

Nanda remained silent for a while, then said, ‘Sir, Satyakam knew some kind of magic spell—just a few words and he’d have the girls under his sway. Then he’d take them to his shop, give them nice gifts, take them to hotels and feed them—’ He fell silent, embarrassed.

‘I see. The girls weren’t entirely innocent either.’ Byomkesh drew on his cigarette, his face grave, and after a moment said, ‘Even women’s freedom doesn’t come without a price. Well, can you tell me which gentlemen’s daughters Satyakam was close to?’

Nanda grew even more hesitant. ‘I don’t know about everyone, sir, but Akhilbabu from number 73 lodged a complaint at our gymnasium—his daughter Shobhana… Then Rameshwarbabu’s granddaughter—she too fell into Satyakam’s trap for a while, it nearly turned into a terrible scandal. Anyway, she’s married now—’

‘Anyone else?’

‘And—Bhabanibabu’s daughter, Salila—’

‘Which Bhabanibabu?’

‘Bhabanibabu, the inspector at the thana across the para. He locked his daughter up at home. Later, he sent her to her uncle’s house.’

Byomkesh and I exchanged a quick, startled glance. He stood up, stretched, and said to Nanda, ‘Alright, Nanda, you may go for today. We’ll talk again another time. By the way, your ustad has disappeared. Don’t go that way for a few days.’

Nanda licked his lips once more and said, ‘Alright, sir.’

