At four in the afternoon, Ushapati Babu came again. Once more, he arrived unannounced.  
The exhaustion and melancholy of the morning were gone; now his eyes were sharp, alert. He sat down before Byomkesh, fixed him with a piercing gaze for a moment, and said, “You went to see Dhananjay Babu?”  
Byomkesh replied in a calm voice, “Yes, I did.”  
“What did you go to find out?”  
“What I went to find out, I have learned.”  
“What did you go to find out?”  
“I have learned everything, Ushapati Babu. Even the mystery of the brass disc nailed to the door is no longer unknown to me.”  
The intensity of Ushapati Babu’s questioning seemed to falter, as if struck by an unseen blow. Again, he gazed at Byomkesh’s face for a while, then, in a restrained voice, asked, “What you have learned—can you prove it in court?”  
Byomkesh said, “Other than the dates of your marriage and Satyakam’s birth, I doubt anything else can be proven. But I never intended to prove anything, Ushapati Babu. I only wished to know. Satyakam asked me to investigate his death; it is not my duty to hand the culprit over to the police.”  
Ushapati Babu stared at Byomkesh with unwavering eyes; slowly, the expression on his face began to change. The man who, until now, had been ready for battle, suddenly lowered his weapons. In a voice tinged with disbelief, he asked, “You will not tell the police what you have learned?”  
Byomkesh replied, “No. The police do not seek my help—why should I go out of my way to assist them?”  
Ushapati Babu took a handkerchief from his pocket and covered his face with both hands. His body trembled two or three times with suppressed emotion. When he finally uncovered his face, I saw that his entire countenance had changed. The look that comes over the face of a patient, long tormented by illness, when he first senses the hope of recovery—such a look now appeared on Ushapati Babu’s face. He sat in silence for a while, gathering himself, then said in a broken voice, “Byomkesh Babu, would you care to hear why Satyakam’s death became necessary?”  
Byomkesh said, “I will listen. Tell me everything.”  
Ushapati Babu glanced at me with pleading eyes. The meaning of his look was clear: he was willing to bare his soul to Byomkesh, but not in front of anyone else. Byomkesh, understanding his feelings, said to me, “Ajit, would you go to Howrah Station and find out at the Enquiry Office about the arrangements for traveling to Kashmir? There’s trouble in Kashmir these days—it’s best to get information in advance.”  
I felt a twinge of disappointment, but changed my clothes and went out.

