583

Seven

By the time I returned after finishing my work at Howrah Station, dusk was about to fall. The main door was half-closed. Entering, I saw that Ushapati Babu had already left; in the shadowy room, at the far end by the window, Satyabati and Byomkesh were sitting so close their shoulders touched. A gentle southern breeze drifted in through the window. Seeing me, Satyabati shifted a little.

I approached and said, “Well, well, like a pair of doves, you sit here enjoying the Malaya breeze. —Where’s Khoka?”

Satyabati, a little embarrassed, replied, “Putiram has taken Khoka for a walk in the park.”

Byomkesh said, “You see, Ajit, the poets are not liars. There’s good reason for the way they lose themselves at the advent of spring. It’s true, the Malaya breeze strikes hardest at young men and women, but the elders aren’t spared either. I believe, if it hadn’t been spring, Ushapati Babu might not have murdered Satyakam.”

I said, “You don’t say! The poets never wrote about such deadly powers of spring.”

Byomkesh replied, “Even if they didn’t write it plainly, they hinted at it. All power is dangerous; the same fire that gives light can also burn everything to ashes. —But let that be. Tell me, what news from Kashmir?”

I said, “There’s fighting in Kashmir, ordinary people aren’t being allowed in. To go, you need a permit from the Government of India.”

I fetched a chair and sat down on Byomkesh’s other side. Byomkesh said, “Getting a permit won’t be hard. The Government of India and I are on excellent terms these days, at least as long as Vallabhbhai Patel is alive. But the question is, is it wise for all of us to go to Kashmir? Khoka has only just started school, and there’s still time before the summer holidays. I don’t think it’s right to take him out of school.”

Satyabati said, “Why should Khoka go? He’ll stay at home. Thakurpo, can’t you look after Khoka?”

I gazed at Satyabati for a moment and then said, “Ah—so that’s the plan. You two will fly off to Kashmir like a pair of swans, and I’ll be left at home with Khoka. My friend Byomkesh, you’re right, spring is indeed a dangerous season. But, no matter. Go on, wander as you please, I’ll stay at home with Khoka and enjoy myself. To tell the truth, I never had the slightest desire to go to Kashmir. Bengal is my own earthly paradise—janani janmabhumishcha swargadapi gariyasi.” With that, I lit a cigarette.

Satyabati pressed her sari’s edge to her lips to hide a smile. Byomkesh, in a low hum, recited poetry, “Youth is the sweetest time, but it will not last—so drink the nectar of love while you can. —Give me a cigarette.”

Handing him one, I said, “Reading poetry has corrupted your character. But leave that for now—do you mind telling us the tale Ushapati just confided in you?”

Byomkesh said, “Not at all. I was waiting for you. I want both of you to hear it. It’s a tragic story.”

Lighting his cigarette, Byomkesh began—

“Satyakam had come to me with a strange proposition—if I were to die suddenly, you must investigate. He knew who wanted to kill him, but he didn’t tell me the name—”

--- Page 886 ---
No! Even then, the question arose in my mind—why does he refuse to reveal his name? Now I understand, there was a grave reason for his silence; had he spoken, a sordid family scandal would have come to light. He was illegitimate, his mother disgraced—how could he confess such a thing? How many men can bring themselves to utter their own tale of shame? Not everyone is a Satyakam from the age of truth.

Yet he did leave me a clue—his date of birth. But he gave it in such a way that it never once occurred to me that the key to his death lay hidden within that date. He knew, if I began to investigate, the date of birth would serve me well. Satyakam was a conscienceless libertine, but he was not lacking in intelligence.

Let me begin the story from the very start. The thread of this tale stretches back to before Satyakam’s birth. I heard most of it from Ushapati Babu himself, and yet, there is no doubt as to its truth. He did not spare himself; he confessed his own faults and weaknesses with disarming honesty.

In the second decade of the twentieth century, Ramakanta Chowdhury founded Suchitra Emporium. Ramakanta Chowdhury’s only daughter was named Suchitra; the shop bore her name. Chowdhury-moshai was a shrewd businessman—within a few years, his shop was thriving. A new house was built in Dharmatala, business was booming. Chowdhury-moshai’s Suchitra Emporium began to rival the British shops.

In 1925, Ushapati Das joined Suchitra Emporium as a humble shop assistant. He was then twenty-one or twenty-two; a poor orphan, not much educated. But he was good-looking, intelligent. Within a few days, he had mastered the art of selling merchandise, learned the tricks of keeping customers happy. He quickly became popular among his colleagues. Soon, even the proprietor himself took notice. His salary began to rise by a few rupees at a time.

Two years passed. Then, suddenly, one day, Ushapati Babu’s fortune changed dramatically. Ramakanta Chowdhury summoned him to his office and said, “I wish to marry my daughter to you.” The proposal was beyond Ushapati’s wildest dreams—it was as if he had caught the moon in his hands. Like the fairy tale where the beggar marries the princess. Ushapati had seen Suchitra many times before; she often visited the shop. She was sweet-faced, gentle. Ushapati’s heart filled with the fragrance of romance.

Within a month, the wedding took place. It was a grand affair. Ushapati’s colleagues presented him with gifts and congratulated their friend. Until then, Ushapati Babu had lived with his married sister; now, arrangements were made for him to stay at his father-in-law’s house. The in-laws’ house—meaning the Amherst Street residence. Ramakanta Chowdhury was a wealthy man, and a widower; he did not wish to part with his daughter.

But there was a hook hidden in the bait—Ushapati realized it on his wedding night. The fairy tale palace crumbled; he understood why the master of Suchitra Emporium had married his daughter to a poor employee. He did not get to lie upon a bed of flowers; Ushapati Babu spent the entire night sitting in a chair. In the morning, he went to his father-in-law and said—your purpose has been served, now let me go.

Ramakanta Chowdhury, the wily businessman, was perhaps prepared for this; in a gentle tone, he began to explain to his son-in-law—Suchitra is a child, a motherless girl; and with the winds blowing in the country these days, it is difficult to keep daughters in check. Suchitra is a very good girl, only, under the influence of the present times, she made a small mistake. These things are happening in every household nowadays, it’s hard to find the genuine among the fakes, but do outsiders ever know? Everyone lives happily with their wives...

--- Page 887 ---
Rokter Daag 585

She runs the household. If one tries to meddle in this, one’s own face will be blackened with shame. Therefore—

Ushapati, however, did not falter in his words. He said, “Forgive me, I may be poor, but I am the son of a respectable family. I cannot do it.”

Seeing that words alone would not suffice, Ramakanta Chowdhury played his trump card. He drew out a stamped deed from the drawer and said, “From today, you are an eight-anna partner in Suchitra Emporium. Here, see the deed. When I die, whatever I have will all be yours—there is no one else. But from today, you are my partner. In the shop, your authority will be equal to mine.”

Ushapati’s head spun. The princess might be tainted, but here was half a kingdom, handed over just like that. In short, Ushapati finally agreed—he could not resist the lure of so much money, so suddenly. He agreed to stay at his in-laws’ house. But there remained no relationship with his wife. Since that wedding night, when they had exchanged a few words, there had been silence; even their sleeping arrangements were separate. Of course, outsiders knew nothing—the screen of deception remained intact.

Ramakanta’s claim that Suchitra was a good woman was not entirely false. After the Great War, a wave of rebellion had swept through, and the free mingling of the upper classes had spread to every level of society. Suchitra, dazzled by the lights, had perhaps indulged a bit too much. Lacking a guardian, she did not realize she was stepping beyond the bounds. But after a severe shock, she came to her senses. After marriage, she stopped going out, and remained quietly, soberly at home. There were few people in Ramakanta’s house, no relatives—only Ramakanta, Suchitra, and Ushapati. Among the permanent servants was Sahadev; the rest were just maids and menials. Sahadev was not particularly clever, but his devotion to the family was unwavering. Thus, nothing from inside the house ever leaked outside.

A month and a half after the wedding, Ramakanta left for England with his daughter. He gave the excuse that her health was failing, and he was taking her abroad for treatment. Ushapati became the absolute master of the shop and managed all affairs.

Nearly a year later, Ramakanta returned from England. In Suchitra’s arms was a child. Looking at the boy, it was impossible to tell whether he was two months old or five...

And so began Ushapati Babu’s dreary, lifeless existence in the Amherst Street house. No relationship with his wife, only a working relationship with his father-in-law. Ushapati poured his heart into the shop. But can the taste of milk ever be satisfied with water? Inside, his hungry youth cried out in anguish. Meanwhile, Suchitra had withdrawn into herself, shrinking away from the world. Occasionally, Ushapati would see her, and it seemed as if Suchitra had become a severe ascetic. His heart would soften, but he forced himself to remain hard.

Year after year passed in this way, room by room. Satyakam grew up. The unruly blood of his libertine father ran in his veins; as he grew older, the stains of that blood became ever more apparent. All other stains may be washed away, but the stain of blood never fades. Satyakam obeyed no one, did whatever he pleased; but he was dangerously cunning, his mind twisted. He had so completely ensnared his grandfather that, though Ramakanta knew everything, he could say nothing. Suchitra had tried and failed to discipline him, and had given up. Ushapati never involved himself in anything concerning Satyakam, always keeping himself apart... No man can look with affection upon his wife’s illegitimate son. Had Satyakam’s nature and character been good, perhaps Ushapati could have tolerated him, but now his heart was utterly poisoned. Between Suchitra and Ushapati, there remained only a practical...

--- Page 888 ---
586  
Byomkesh Samagra

If there had been any possibility of reconciliation, it vanished completely. Satyakam now stood between Ushapati and Suchitra like the barbed fence of Phani-Monsa, a living barrier.

When Satyakam was nineteen years old, Ramakanta died, leaving his share to Satyakam in his will. It was around this time that Satyakam discovered the secret of his birth. He had been born in England, so there was a birth certificate. Among his grandfather’s papers, he must have found that certificate, and, observing the family situation, pieced together the truth. Outwardly, he was an impeccably polished young man, but inside, he was cunning and consumed by jealousy. His behavior toward Ushapati and Suchitra grew increasingly vicious. One day, he said bluntly to his mother, “With what right do you try to discipline me? I know everything.” To Ushapati he said, “You are not my father—why should I show you any respect?”

Life in the house became unbearable for Ushapati and Suchitra. Meanwhile, at the shop, Satyakam began to play a different game. Now he was a partner in the business, with rights equal to Ushapati’s. He began to exercise those rights to the fullest. In a refined shop like Suchitra’s, there were always more female customers than male; Satyakam would pick out the young and beautiful among them, strike up friendships, sell them expensive goods at a discount, and take them out to dine at hotels. He would withdraw as much money as he pleased from the shop’s funds and squander it with both hands. Drinking, horse racing, gambling at the city’s grandest clubs—these became his daily pursuits.

Within a year of Ramakanta’s death, it became clear that the shop was in trouble; it could not go on like this much longer. When Ushapati Babu tried to intervene, Satyakam retorted, “I’m spending my own money—what’s it to you?” Worse, a rumor began to circulate that it was not safe for women to visit their shop. Customers dwindled. Distraught, Ushapati Babu had no idea what to do.

When the situation had become utterly dire, an incident occurred. One evening, Ushapati returned home for some reason. As he was about to enter his room upstairs, he heard a muffled, anguished moan coming from the adjoining room—his wife’s chamber. Stepping softly, Ushapati approached the door. He saw his wife alone on the floor, banging her head and crying, “Is my atonement still not over? I can’t bear it any longer!”

Ushapati slipped quietly downstairs. Asking Sahadev, he learned that earlier that evening, an elderly lady from the para had come by and heaped insults on Suchitra. Apparently, Satyakam had been taking the woman’s daughter to the cinema and treating her to meals at fancy hotels.

That very day, Ushapati resolved that Satyakam must be removed. Unless he killed him, there would be no escape from any direction. Life could not go on like this.

Ushapati began to prepare. He had one advantage: if Satyakam were murdered, no one would suspect him. To the outside world, Satyakam was his son—no one would believe a father could kill his own child. Satyakam had many enemies; suspicion would fall on them. Still, it had to be done in such a way that no attention would be drawn to him.

Ushapati devised a brilliant plan. He procured a revolver from a familiar goonda. In his youth, he had spent some time among the revolutionaries and was accustomed to handling firearms; he went several times to a mango orchard in Belgharia to brush up his skills. Then he waited for the right opportunity.

--- Page 889 ---
Rokter Daag 587

"Satyakam was a clever boy; he understood Ushapati’s scheme, but could find no way to save himself. If he went to the police, the secret of his birth would be revealed. In the end, in despair, he came to me. Ushapati Babu, of course, did not know this.

"The night Satyakam was murdered was a Saturday. On Saturdays, Satyakam returned home later than on other nights, so Saturday was ideal. Ushapati Babu had prepared a disc of tinfoil; at half past ten, when Sahadev went to the kitchen to eat, he quietly came down and fixed the disc to the door, then slipped back upstairs without a sound. The main door remained closed as before; no one knew that a tinfoil disc had been attached outside. Shuko the maid and the cook had left for home long before.

"Sahadev finished his meal, locked the back door, and then went to the front verandah to make his bed. Upstairs, Ushapati Babu waited in his room with the light off, the door to the front balcony left open.

"After two hours, there was a sound at the gate—Satyakam was coming. Ushapati stepped out onto the balcony and crouched, waiting. The path from the gate to the main door was shrouded in darkness; Satyakam advanced, shining his torch as he walked. He knocked on the main door, and suddenly noticed, near the bottom of the door, something glinting in the torchlight—a disc, like a coin. He bent forward to examine it. Instantly, Ushapati Babu leaned over from the balcony and fired. The revolver bullet pierced Satyakam’s back and lodged in the bone of his chest. Satyakam collapsed face-first right there, his burning torch still aglow in his hand.

"This is the true story of Satyakam’s death. Ushapati Babu had devised such a clever plan that, upon examining the body, it would seem as if someone had shot him from behind; it would be impossible to guess the shot had come from above. If not for the tinfoil disc, even I would not have understood."

Byomkesh fell silent. We too remained quiet for a long while. At last, Satyabati let out a long sigh and asked, "When did you first suspect Ushapati Babu?"

Byomkesh replied, "From the very beginning, I suspected it was the work of someone in the house. If it had been an outsider, why wouldn’t the murderer have named Satyakam? Even then, I felt that behind this premeditated murder lay a deeply hidden family scandal.

"Later, I learned that Ushapati and Suchitra’s married life was not normal. For years, they hadn’t spoken to each other; even their bedrooms were separate. That made me suspicious. I struck up a conversation with Khajanchi Mashay. He was Ushapati Babu’s devoted friend; it was he who had written the wedding felicitation twenty-one years ago. Khajanchi Mashay had preserved the felicitation with great care, as it was his first and only poetic composition. When I held that felicitation in my hand, all doubts vanished. I remembered Satyakam’s date of birth—7th July, 1927. The wedding date was 13th February, 1927. That is, the child was born before five full months had passed since the wedding. It was not hard to understand why the cunning Ramakanta had married his daughter to a poor clerk.

"Satyakam was not Ushapati’s son, so Ushapati had no compunction about killing him. But how did he commit the murder? When I learned that Satyakam had a burning torch in his hand at the time of death, the purpose of the tinfoil disc became clear in an instant. The beam of Satyakam’s torch had fallen on the door, the tinfoil disc had flashed, Satyakam had leaned forward—"

--- Page 890 ---
Leaning in, he had tried to see what was glittering there. That was it—!'

Again, a spell of silence. I offered Byomkesh a cigarette and took one myself; we both began to smoke. The room was now completely shrouded in darkness, the southern breeze quietly circling around us, playing its secret games.

Suddenly Byomkesh spoke, 'Today, as Ushapati Babu was leaving, he grasped my hand and said, "Byomkesh Babu, my wife and I have suffered greatly in life; for twenty-one years, we have lived in the shadow of the crematorium. Now, we wish to forget the past and begin life anew, to find a little happiness. Please, do not muddy the waters any further." I gave Ushapati Babu my word—I will not stir the waters again. Perhaps what I have done is not strictly legal. But there are things greater than the law—justice, righteousness. What do you think? Have I done wrong?'

Satyabati and I replied in unison, 'No.'

--- Page 891 ---
Monimondon

A priceless jeweled necklace has been stolen from the house of the renowned jeweler, Rasamay Sarkar. I had seen the news in the delayed reports column of the morning paper. Around eight o’clock, the telephone rang.

An unfamiliar, urgent voice: “Hello. Is this Byomkesh Babu?”

I replied, “No, I am Ajit. Who is this?”

The telephone said, “My name is Rasamay Sarkar. Could you please call Byomkesh Babu?”

Hearing the name, there was no doubt left—Byomkesh was being summoned to catch a thief.

I said, “He’s in the bathroom; it’ll be a while before he comes out. I saw in the paper that a necklace was stolen from your shop.”

The reply came, “Not from the shop, from the house. —Are you Ajit Bandyopadhyay, Byomkesh Babu’s friend?”

I said, “Yes. If you have anything to say to Byomkesh, you can tell me.”

After a moment’s silence, Rasamay said, “Look, the necklace that’s been stolen is worth fifty-seven thousand rupees. I suspect one of the household servants has stolen it, but there’s no proof. Of course, I have informed the police, but I want Byomkesh Babu. No one but him can recover the necklace.”

I said, “Well then, why don’t you come over? By the time you arrive, Byomkesh will be out of the bathroom.”

Rasamay replied, a little plaintively, “You see, I’m a patient with dropsy, I can’t move about much. It would be much better if you could come instead.”

Those who find themselves in trouble are the ones who come to Byomkesh; he never goes to anyone first. I said, “Very well, I’ll tell Byomkesh.”

Rasamay’s plea became even more insistent, “No, no, don’t just tell him—please, you must come. I’m sending a car for you, you won’t be inconvenienced at all.”

“All right.”

“Thank you, thank you. I’m sending the car right away.”

A few minutes later, a Cadillac pulled up at our door. When Byomkesh emerged from the bathroom, I told him everything and pointed out the car through the window. He neither objected nor hesitated. We climbed into the Cadillac and set off.

Rasamay Sarkar owned half a dozen shops dealing in gold, diamonds, and precious stones in various parts of Calcutta, but his residence was in Bowbazar. In a short while, the car drew up at his door.

--- Page 892 ---
590  
Byomkesh Samagra

Rasamay Sarkar’s house was of the old-fashioned sort, rising three stories straight up from the very edge of the footpath. In the middle, a stairway with an open door led to the upper floors; on either side, rows of shops. The master of the house occupied the top two floors.

The door to the stairs was bolted from within; as soon as our car came to a halt, the door swung open and a young man stepped out. He was stylish, handsome, about twenty-seven or twenty-eight. Bowing, he said, “My name is Manimoy Sarkar. My father is waiting for you upstairs. Please come.”

We began to ascend the stairs. On the second floor were the kitchen, the pantry, the servants’ quarters, and a modest sitting room with a mattress spread on the floor. We passed the second floor and climbed to the third. Here the master of the house lived with his family.

On the third floor, the signs of the master’s wealth were immediately apparent. The heavy door was fitted with an imported lock, silk curtains hung from it, and the floor was covered with a thick carpet. The drawing room was furnished with expensive pieces: a cushioned sofa set arranged around a low Kashmiri wooden table, a bookcase between two windows, Persian tapestry with painted scenes on the walls, and so on. The room, however, was a little untidy. Manimoy led us in and announced, “Father, Byomkesh Babu has arrived.”

I saw Rasamay Sarkar seated in a chair, his right leg stretched out before him, and a married young woman sitting at his feet, warming his toes. Rasamay Babu appeared to be around fifty, heavily built, his fleshy face still quite firm. At the sight of us, he tried to rise quickly but had to sink back down, glancing at Byomkesh and me in turn, then folding his hands together as he addressed Byomkesh, “Come in, Byomkesh Babu. I have become an old man in every sense. Now that you—you both—are here, I feel relieved. Please, sit down, Ajit Babu.”

Without asking, he had already discerned which of us was Byomkesh. There was no doubt that Rasamay Sarkar was a shrewd man.

We sat side by side on the sofa. Byomkesh said, “I see you have gout in your foot. It’s not a dangerous illness, but it can be terribly painful.”

Rasamay replied, “Don’t even mention it. My health is otherwise good, but this gout has crippled me. I used to play football as a boy—once broke the big toe on my right foot. Now it’s reached the point where, if a scrap of cloud as small as a handkerchief appears in the sky, my toe starts to throb. But let that be—Bouma, bring some tea for our guests.”

The young bride had been sitting with her head bowed, tending to her father-in-law’s foot. She was a beautiful woman, but her face bore the shadow of some family misfortune. As she made to rise, Byomkesh said, “No, no, there’s no need for tea, I’ve already had some before coming. Let her continue serving her father-in-law.”

Rasamay smiled a little, and the young woman sat back down. Rasamay said, “Very well, let it be. Moni, bring some cigarettes.”

Manimoy had been standing behind a chair all this while; as he left, Rasamay cast a fond glance at his daughter-in-law and said, “She’s a very good girl, my Bouma. My wife has gone on pilgrimage with our younger son, so she’s managing the whole household now. Of course, I don’t usually make her tend to my feet, but the servant—”

At this point Rasamay broke off, then changed his tone and said, “Enough of such talk, let’s get down to business. You have kindly come here, I won’t waste your precious time. Byomkesh Babu, last night something untoward happened in my house—something that has never happened before. A diamond necklace—”

--- Page 893 ---
Thus Spake Poet Kalidas 675

Phanish said, “No, sir.”

“Then take them to the guest room. See that they have no inconvenience. —You gentlemen, please wash your hands and faces, tea is being prepared.”

The guest room adjoined the drawing room. A large chamber, with two beds. Table, chairs, and other suitable furnishings arranged neatly, with an attached bathroom. Phanish led us to the room and lingered by the door.

The boy struck me as gentle and good-natured. Handsome like his father, but his body and mind had yet to reach full maturity; a trace of boyishness still clung to his mannerisms. He seemed to be about twenty-three or twenty-four.

As we changed our clothes, a few words were exchanged; Phanish answered Byomkesh’s questions shyly. He was his father’s only son, married just a year ago. Every day he accompanied his father to the coal mine to oversee the work. I noticed that, as he replied to Byomkesh, he seemed to be trying to say something else, but hesitated and fell silent out of embarrassment.

What Phanish wished to say remained unheard, for we returned to the sitting room. In the meantime, tea and refreshments had arrived; we took our seats.

At the tea table, however, there were no women to be seen—only the four of us. Yet there were certainly at least two women in the house. It seemed Manish Babu had not entirely renounced swadeshi customs. But in this modern, cosmopolitan age, is a little seclusion such a bad thing?

Having finished our refreshments and lit our cigarettes, a large car pulled up in front of the house. From it descended a middle-aged man. He had the appearance of a gorilla, with eyes ringed in darkness, slow and cunning. If it were possible to read character from a face, I would have said this man was a great villain.

Manish Babu welcomed the newcomer warmly, brought him into the room, and introduced us. “This is Mr. Gobinda Haldar, owner of one of the local coal mines. And these are Mr. Gagan Mitra and Sujit Bandyopadhyay; my friends, visiting from Calcutta.”

Gobinda Babu surveyed us with his Saturnine gaze, then said to Manish Babu, “I came to get the news. Any more trouble at the mine?”

Manish Babu replied gravely, “There’s always trouble. Night before last, another incident. Suddenly, the pump in Number Five Pit stopped working. Luckily, the watchmen were alert, so no great harm was done. Otherwise—”

Gobinda Babu clicked his tongue. Manish Babu said, “You seem to be doing well; all the mischief is reserved for my mine. I can’t understand why these wretches have their eyes fixed on me.”

Gobinda Babu replied, “There was trouble at my mine too, about six months ago. I knew the police would be useless, so I set spies directly. Planted eight men as informers, and within eight or nine days they brought me word of who was behind the mischief. Five ringleaders—had them brought in one day and gave them a good thrashing. Didn’t have to dismiss them; they ran off on their own. Since then, everything’s been quiet.” He gave a characteristic gorilla grin.

Manish Babu said, “I set spies too, but nothing came of it. Let’s leave that aside—” He changed the subject. Conversation drifted to more general matters. Tea and refreshments were brought for Gobinda Babu, which he partook of. Yet his eyes continued to roam about us.

--- Page 894 ---
Byomkesh Samagra

It seemed he did not quite believe that we had come here merely for a holiday.

After about an hour, he rose to leave. Manishbabu accompanied him to the car porch, and so did we. The driver opened the car door. Gobindababu, about to step into the car, turned his head towards Byomkesh with a half-smile and said, “Let’s see you try.” He got in, and the car drove away.

For a while, Manishbabu and we exchanged glances. Then, in a melancholy tone, he said, “Gobinda Haldar is a shrewd man. It’s not easy to pull the wool over his eyes.”

It was eleven by the time we finished dinner and retired for the night. The fatigue of the train journey still weighed on me. As soon as I lay down under the whirring fan, I sank into a deep sleep.

When I awoke the next morning, it was already eight o’clock.

A servant informed us that both the elder and the younger master had left for the colliery at dawn. We hurried through our morning ablutions and entered the outer room, where we found our tea and breakfast arranged on the table—and a young woman standing beside it.

We had not seen any of the women of the house before, so we were a little taken aback. In response to Byomkesh’s questioning glance, the girl lowered her head and said in a soft, hesitant voice, “I am Indira, the daughter-in-law of this house. Please, do eat.”

Phanish’s wife. Dusky-skinned, slender, her face fresh and youthful—she could not have been more than eighteen or nineteen. It was clear at a glance that Indira was a shy girl, unaccustomed to conversing freely with unfamiliar, older men. With no men left in the house, the poor girl had been compelled to come and attend to the guests.

We sat down to eat. Byomkesh said, “Why don’t you sit? Why are you standing?”

Indira perched on the edge of a sofa.

Byomkesh took a sip of tea to moisten his throat, then pulled the breakfast tray towards him. “We woke up late today. Does the master always leave so early?”

“Yes, Father leaves at seven,” she replied.

“And your husband?”

Indira’s head dropped at once. Without raising her eyes, she murmured, “He does too.”

Then, forcing herself to overcome her embarrassment, she added, “They return for lunch at twelve, then leave again at three.”

She glanced at Byomkesh with a faint, conspiratorial smile, but said nothing more. As I ate, I observed Indira. She sat quietly, occasionally casting a swift, sidelong glance at Byomkesh. It seemed to me she had some purpose beyond merely attending to guests. She knew who Byomkesh was—Phanish must have told her—and she wanted to say something to him. She had resolved something in her mind, but shyness held her back. I had noticed a similar hesitation on Phanish’s face last night.

After breakfast, Byomkesh took a final sip of tea, wiped his mouth with a handkerchief, and said in a gentle tone, “Well, what is it you want to say? Go on.”

I had been watching the struggle between resolve and embarrassment on Indira’s face. Now I saw her start in surprise, eyes wide, and, almost without realizing it, she stood up. Then all her...

--- Page 895 ---
A sigh of anxiety escaped in a single breath. “Byomkesh Babu, please save my husband. He is in grave danger.”

Byomkesh rose and sat down on the sofa, gesturing for Indira to sit beside him. “Sit. Tell me, what danger threatens your husband?”

Indira perched nervously on the edge of the sofa, her voice thin and restrained. “I—I can’t arrange all the words properly. If you help, he will tell you himself.”

Byomkesh asked, “Is it something to do with the mine?”

Indira replied, “No, something else. Please don’t tell my father anything. He knows nothing.”

Byomkesh assured her in a calm, soothing tone, “I won’t say a word to anyone. Don’t be afraid.”

“You will help him?”

“I don’t know anything yet. Still, if your husband is innocent, I will certainly help.”

“My husband is innocent.”

“Then have no fear.”

Along the side of the house, at the edge of the garden, stood a row of rooms. After a smile finally broke across Indira’s face, we made our way in that direction, drawing on our cigarettes.

From the front room emerged a middle-aged gentleman. He wore a Farashdanga dhoti and an addi panjabi, his appearance neat and trim. He must have used hair dye, for beneath the black hair, white roots were beginning to show.

Byomkesh said, “My name is Ganan Mitra, and this is Sujit Bandyopadhyay. Guests of Manish Babu.”

The gentleman greeted us with hurried hospitality. “Come in, come in. I heard from the master that you would be arriving. I am Surapati Ghatak, caretaker of this office.”

Surapatibabu did not know our real names. Byomkesh said, “This is the coal mine office, I presume. You are the office-master.”

Surapatibabu replied, “Yes, indeed. There is a small office at the coal mine itself, but this is the main office. Please, come and see.”

We inspected the rooms one by one. In various rooms, clerks worked over ledgers and papers, the clatter of typewriters filled the air—nothing particularly remarkable. After making the rounds, we finally sat down in Surapatibabu’s office.

After some general conversation, Byomkesh hesitated slightly before saying, “Let me tell you, my friend and I are thinking of buying a small coal mine together. Not here, in another district. We’re getting it cheap. But we have no idea how to run a coal mine; that’s why we’ve come to see Manish Babu’s mine. We want to gain some experience in office work, mining work, everything.”

Surapatibabu replied with great enthusiasm, “Of course, of course. That’s no trouble at all. You’ll learn the office work in two days; and mining work isn’t so difficult either. Besides, if you need, I can get you some very good men.”

Byomkesh asked, “What sort of men?”

Surapatibabu said, “Men who know office work, who know colliery work. People I’ve trained myself.”

Byomkesh showed interest. “Really! If we can get capable, experienced men—”

--- Page 896 ---
Byomkesh Samagra

We’ll discuss this with you. I’ll also look into the office work. We’ll be here for a few days now.’

I returned from the office.

At twelve, Phanish and Manish Babu came back from the mine. After their bath and meal, a bell rang somewhere in the house. Then, after a brief rest, the four of us boarded the motorcar and set off for the coal mine.

It was a large car. Phanish took the wheel, while the three of us sat in the back.

The motorcar left the city behind and took to a lonely road. The mine was about three miles away.

Byomkesh said, ‘I met Surapati Babu this morning. How long has he been working for you?’

Manish Babu replied, ‘Nearly twenty years. An experienced man.’

Byomkesh said, ‘I told him we’re looking to buy a coal mine. That we’re here to make inquiries. I didn’t reveal our true identity.’

Manish Babu said, ‘You did the right thing. Surapati is a trustworthy fellow, though, except for the fact that two years ago he took a second wife.’

So that explained Surapati Babu’s dyed hair and dandyish clothes. In middle age, it’s only natural to try and conjure the illusion of youth in the eyes of a young bride.

After a while spent in silence, Byomkesh asked, ‘Has anyone recently offered to buy your mine?’

Manish Babu replied, ‘Not recently, a few years back. A Marwari gentleman. He offered a good price, but I didn’t sell.’

Byomkesh asked a second question, ‘Do you have any dealings with the other mine owners around here?’

Manish Babu said, ‘I can’t claim to be on intimate terms, but I have no open quarrel with anyone.’

‘Is there anyone who, while wearing the mask of civility, secretly wishes you harm?’

‘There might be, but how would I know?’

‘True enough. The man who came last night—Govinda Haldar—what sort of person is he?’

Manish Babu replied in a slow, thoughtful voice, ‘Govinda Haldar is hard to read. Like a slippery fish, impossible to grasp. But Govinda Babu’s younger brother and partner, Aravinda, is a thoroughly bad lot. A drunkard, a gambler, a man of vile character. A few years ago his wife killed herself to escape his torment. Since then, Aravinda has become a notorious ruffian.’

No more was said. We entered the coal mine’s domain.

I have no desire to give a detailed description of the coal mine. Those who haven’t seen one with their own eyes have surely glimpsed it on stage or screen—it’s hardly a feast for the eyes. Especially since, in this tale, the mine’s role is minimal; it serves merely as the dark backdrop to our story. Without a backdrop, the narrative would stand naked, so it must remain.

Coal! The force that drives the machines is itself dragged from the deep womb of the earth by the power of machines; the wheels of civilization turn. Hail, machine. Your picks and shovels tear the earth’s flesh, scatter her entrails! Hail, machine. Enough.

We were introduced to the mine’s manager, Tarapada Babu. An elderly man, within the boundaries of the mine—

--- Page 897 ---
Kohen Kobikalidas 679

Residence; he strikes one as a grave, formidable man. He showed us the various workings of the mine. He even proposed that we descend into the bowels of the earth, but we declined. Sita had ample reason to enter the underworld; we have no such cause.

In the afternoon, we took tea in Tarapada Babu’s office. There we met the mine’s doctor, Jatindra Ghosh, and several other senior staff. No business was discussed; only general conversation drifted about. Needless to say, we remained under our assumed names. At one point, I noticed Byomkesh had struck up quite a rapport with Dr. Ghosh, sitting together in a corner, deep in conversation. Dr. Ghosh is of our own age, and he manages the dispensary and hospital attached to the mine. In his suit and trousers, there is a faint trace of weariness, as if life itself has left its mark.

When evening fell, we boarded the motorcar and set out once more for home.

After dinner that night, Manish Babu went upstairs to sleep, and we retired to our own room. Phanish came with us.

Byomkesh switched on the fan, stretched out on his bed, lit a cigarette, and said to Phanish, “Sit down. What’s going on? Why have you made your wife so anxious?”

Phanish sat in a chair, rubbing his hands nervously, then said in a hesitant voice, “I’d persuaded Indira to tell you herself—I didn’t have the courage—”

“But what is it? From the looks of things, it seems quite serious.”

“Yes, sir, it’s serious. By a twist of fate, I’ve become entangled in a murder case. If Father finds out—”

Byomkesh sat up on the bed. “A murder case?”

Phanish replied in a thin voice, “Yes, sir, it’s a nasty business. The police have started their investigation, and they’ve learned that we—”

“Tell us everything, from the beginning, in order.”

Of course, Phanish could not relate the whole story in an orderly fashion. His tale was tangled and confused; I shall do my best to set it down straight—

There is a club in this town. Out of jest, it has been named the ‘Coal Club’. The membership fee is steep, so only the well-to-do can belong. Phanish is a member. There are many other distinguished members; among them, Mrigendra Moulik, owner of the Uludanga coal mine, Madhumoy Sur of Dhoopipota mine, and Aravinda and Govinda Haldar of the Shimulia mine are especially notable.

In the afternoons, there is tennis and badminton at the club; after dusk, billiards, ping-pong, and cards and dice. Gambling on cards is common, but the club rules prohibit high stakes; so those with a gambler’s blood find little satisfaction. Aravinda Haldar is one of these restless souls. But what can be done? There is no other respectable den of gambling in town.

About a year ago, an elderly gentleman became a member of the club. A man of means, he had set up a moneylending business, a newcomer to the town. He kept a small office in the market district, but lived outside town, in a house by a lonely road. With a face like a vulture, his name was Pranahari Poddar.

--- Page 898 ---
Byomkesh Samagra

Poddar Mahashay would come and sit at the club. There were no other elderly gentlemen of his age there; most were young men, with a few of middle age. Gradually, he made the acquaintance of one or two. But, owing to the difference in age, he did not form any particular intimacy with anyone.

Phonish, Mrigen Moulik, Madhumoy Sur, and Aravinda Haldar—these four had formed a little group at the club. Phonish was the youngest among them, and Aravinda Haldar the eldest. He was about thirty-five; the leader of the group.

One evening, after dusk, they were sitting in a room at the club playing bridge, when Poddar Mahashay came and began to watch their game. Circling the table, he observed who had been dealt what kind of hand. Aravinda asked lazily, “Do you know contract bridge?”

The old man smiled a little and replied, “I do.”

“Will you play?”

“I will. What are the stakes?”

“One rupee a point. Is that all right?”

“That’s fine.”

When the current rubber ended, the players cut the cards and one of them stepped out. Pranahari Poddar took a seat at the table.

It soon became clear that Poddar Mahashay was a most skillful player. But that day, fortune did not favor him; he was not dealt good hands. At the end of the game, the accounts were tallied and it was found he had lost twenty-one rupees. He paid his debt at once.

From then on, Pranaharibabu would join Phonish’s group to play almost every day. Sometimes he lost, sometimes he won; in all circumstances, he remained unruffled. In this way, he became a member of Phonish’s circle.

Several months passed in this manner.

One day last Phalgun, as they sat down to play, Pranaharibabu said, “Don’t you play any other games besides bridge?”

Madhumoy Sur asked, “What sort of games?”

Pranahari replied, “For instance, poker or running flash.”

Mrigen Moulik said, “We know how to play them all. But gambling is not allowed at the club. Bridge isn’t gambling, after all, it’s a game of skill.” He gave a mocking laugh through his nose.

Pranahari said nothing then. When the game ended, he said, “Why don’t you come to my house one day? I’ll teach you a new game.”

No one objected. Aravinda said, “Why not? Where do you live?”

Pranahari replied, “My house is outside the city, on the Uludanga Khoni Road. I live alone—if you come, it’ll be lively. Why not come tomorrow?”

Everyone agreed. Pranahari hailed a taxi and left. He didn’t own a car; he had an arrangement with a taxi and always traveled by it.

The next evening, the four of them rode in Aravinda’s car and arrived at Pranahari’s house. The two-storied house stood on a deserted road, about a mile and a half beyond the city limits; there wasn’t another house within two or three hundred yards.

Pranaharibabu welcomed them with great hospitality, led them into a well-appointed room on the ground floor, and seated them. For a while, they conversed on general topics. Pranaharibabu

--- Page 899 ---
Kohen K Bi Kalidas  
681

A widower, childless; he had previously lived in the city of Cuttack, Orissa. But his heart did not settle there, so he moved here. He has a maidservant with him, who cooks and tends to his needs.

At this moment, the maid entered, carrying a tray of tea. She set the tray down on the table and left, only to return with a plate of cutlets. A strikingly built young woman. Twenty or twenty-two years old; her complexion dusky, but her face beautiful, her eyes filled with the enchantment of a doe. At first glance, one would not take her for a mere servant girl. As she passed among the guests, she left a ripple of restlessness in her wake.

Sipping tea with the hot cutlets, Aravind remarked, “Splendid cutlets she’s fried. Is she your maid?”

Pranharibabu replied, “Yes. I brought Mohini from Orissa. She cooks well.”

After refreshments, the game began. By unanimous agreement, a three-card game of running flush commenced. Everyone had brought extra money; Pranharibabu sat down with five hundred rupees in hand. The game lasted two hours. There were no great wins or losses; one won fifty rupees, another lost a hundred. Pranharibabu, on the whole, ended up losing. It was decided that in three days, the game would be held here again.

But Phanish was not happy. He liked playing cards, true, but he was no gambler. He had a strict father hovering over him; he was not entirely free with money matters. He had been drawn into this gambling circle by the force of the group, but if he tried to leave, he would become a laughingstock. Phanish remained reluctantly bound to the gambling party.

On the second day, the game was livelier than ever. Mohini had prepared chicken fry. The game began as they ate it with tea; midway through, Pranharibabu produced a bottle of imported whisky. Phanish could not tolerate liquor—if he drank, he would vomit—so he abstained. The others drank. Aravind drank the most. The stakes of the game climbed higher and higher. Everyone grew excited, except for Pranharibabu, who remained impassive.

At the end of the game, the tally was made: Aravind had won nearly a thousand rupees, while everyone else had lost. Pranharibabu had won two hundred rupees.

Thereafter, the game was held once or twice a week. Some days one would lose, some days another, the rest would win. Pranharibabu never lost much; overall, he always profited.

Alongside the gambling, another drama had begun to unfold—this one involving Mohini. Madhumoy and Mrigendra may have been secretly drawn to Mohini, but Aravind pursued her openly and shamelessly. On game days, he would arrive at Pranharibabu’s house before anyone else and stand by the kitchen door, flirting with Mohini. It was even suspected that, during the day, in Pranharibabu’s absence, he would visit the house. How intimate Aravind and Mohini had become was impossible to say, but given Mohini’s station, it was unlikely she would spurn the attentions of a man of means.

Thus, five or six weeks passed. Phanish found no peace; he tried to avoid his friends, but could not—Aravind would drag him along. Then, one day, everyone’s eyes were opened. They discovered that Pranharibabu was a seasoned card-sharp, a master of sleight of hand. There was quite a quarrel, and then the guests abandoned the game and left.

--- Page 900 ---
682  
Byomkesh Samagra

It was learned that each of the guests had lost three to four thousand rupees, and all the money had vanished into Pranahari’s coffers. Aravind had lost the most—almost five thousand rupees. Sitting in the club, Aravind fumed, “Let that old vulture show his face—I’ll break his bones to powder.” Madhumoy and Mrigen said nothing, but their expressions made it clear that if they got their hands on Pranahari, they wouldn’t let him off easily either.

But Pranahari Babu was a shrewd man; he never set foot in the club again.

About a week later, Aravind said, “The scoundrel’s gone into hiding. Let’s go to his house and teach him a lesson.”

Phanish objected, “What’s the point? The money’s gone—what’s done is done.”

Aravind replied, “Money is dirt on our hands. But are we to let the bastard cheat us and walk away? What do you say, Mrigen?”

Mrigen said, “He needs to be taught a lesson.”

Madhumoy added, “There’s no one in his house except a woman. Nothing to be afraid of.”

Around eight in the evening, the four of them set out. From the taxi stand not far from the club, they hired a taxi and headed toward Pranahari’s house. It wasn’t wise to take their own car; though the road was lonely, at night many vehicles traveled from the Uludanga Colliery. If anyone saw a familiar car near Pranahari’s house, it would raise suspicion. Besides, chauffeurs aren’t deaf-mutes—they’d gossip. If you’re going to rough someone up, it’s best to have as few witnesses as possible.

They stopped the taxi a hundred yards from Pranahari’s house and got out. The road was deserted. Madhumoy carried a large electric torch, which he flicked on and off as they advanced toward the house, telling the taxi driver to turn the car around and wait.

There was light in the upstairs room. The main door below was open. From the kitchen came the hiss and sputter of cooking—Mohini was preparing dinner. The four slipped in like hunters, silent and alert.

The entrance hall was long, with a staircase on the left leading to the second floor. Here, they paused and conferred in low voices. Then Aravind took the torch from Madhumoy and crept upstairs, treading softly. After a while, he returned and said, “There’s a door at the top of the stairs—a sturdy one. Can’t tell if it’s locked from inside or out. There’s a Yale lock on it.”

After more discussion, they decided to search the ground floor thoroughly. The old man was cunning; perhaps he’d left the light burning upstairs and was hiding somewhere in the darkness below.

Aravind peeked into the kitchen—Mohini stood alone, her back to the door, cooking. No one else was there.

Then, the four began to search the rooms and the open yard at the back, each going their separate ways.

Fifteen minutes later, they all gathered again at the foot of the stairs. No one had found Pranahari. So, the old man must be upstairs. Aravind said, “Come on, let’s try the door again.”

This time, all four climbed the stairs. As soon as they pressed on the door, it swung open.

Light filled the room. In the middle of the floor, Pranahari Poddar lay sprawled on his side. On the right side of his sparse-haired head was a long, bloody gash, as if he had—

--- Page 901 ---
Kohen K Bi Kalidas 683

A parting down the right side of her hair, vermilion smeared along the line. Her face contorted, teeth missing; Pranahari, lying on his deathbed, was making faces at the onlookers.

For a moment, the four of them stood frozen, then, all at once, they tumbled down the stairs in a rush. Out onto the street.

Approaching the taxi, they saw the driver slumped over the steering wheel, fast asleep. Each pressed a finger to his lips, warning the others to silence, then quietly climbed into the car. The driver woke and started the engine.

When the four returned to the club, it was only nine o’clock. They sat together in a secluded corner and conferred: there was no need to tell anyone anything. News of Pranahari’s accidental death would, of course, become public, but there was no evidence that the four of them had visited his house. The taxi driver had waited a hundred yards away. He hadn’t seen them enter Pranahari’s home. Therefore, it was wise to keep the adventure buried in their consciences.

That night, they played cards at the club until half past ten, then returned home, as if nothing had happened. The next day, news of Pranahari’s death spread through the city, but the names of these four were not linked to the murder. On the third day, the police raided Aravinda’s house. How the police had found out, none could say.

But all four were men of great influence in the city; so far, none had been caught. The matter had not become public knowledge. The police were conducting a vigorous investigation, questioning each of them in turn. Anything could happen at any time. Phanish’s situation was dire. On one hand, the charge of murder; on the other, if his strict father were to learn that he gambled and was entangled in a murder case—who could say what he might do, and so on, and so forth.

By the time Phanish finished his tale, it was midnight. Reassuring him, Byomkesh said, “Tell your wife there’s nothing to worry about. I take responsibility for uncovering the truth. Tomorrow we’ll go into the city; we’ll need a car.”

Phanish replied, “I’ll tell the driver—the small car will be at your disposal.”

Phanish departed. We turned off the lights and went to bed. Lying on his own cot, Byomkesh lit a cigarette and drew on it softly.

I asked, “What do you make of it?”

Byomkesh said, “Of the five suspects, I’ve seen only one. Until I see the other four, it’s hard to say anything.”

“Five suspects!”

“Yes. We can’t rule out the maidservant.”

Nothing more was said. Thinking of the strange conclusion to Pranahari Poddar’s life, I drifted off to sleep.

In the morning, I awoke to find Byomkesh seated at the table, writing a letter with utmost concentration. Stretching and yawning, I asked, “Who are you writing to? Satyabati? Barely two days apart and already you’re pining for her?”

Byomkesh, still writing, replied, “Not longing—Bikash.”

“Bikash?”

“Yes—Bikash. Why are you writing to him?”

--- Page 902 ---
684  
Byomkesh Samagra

“I’ve arranged a job for Bikash. A compounder’s position at the colliery’s dispensary. That’s why I’m writing to ask him to come.”  
“I see.”  
Byomkesh returned to his letter. He wanted to bring Bikash here and install him at the colliery, while he himself would remain at a distance, gathering intelligence about the mine. You stay in Dorapani and send a spy to Pola—  
At breakfast, I noticed Indira’s face was much brighter today; the clouds of doubt and anxiety had parted, and sunlight shimmered through. Phanish had told her of Byomkesh’s reassurances.  
Again, it was just the two of us at breakfast; both masters had long since left for work. Byomkesh, chewing on his toast, glanced sidelong at Indira and said, “You’re quite the child at heart.”  
Indira lowered her eyes in embarrassment; but soon enough, worry and fear returned to them. This girl’s mind was never free of anxiety for her husband; Byomkesh comforted her, saying, “Don’t worry, everything will turn out fine. We’re heading out now.”  
Indira looked up and asked, “Where are you going?”  
Byomkesh replied, “Oh, here and there. We’ll probably be back by noon. If your husband asks, tell him we’ve gone to see the city.”  
When breakfast was over, we rose. The motor driver came to announce the car was ready at the door.  
Once inside, Byomkesh instructed the driver, “First, to the post office.”  
At the post office, he affixed an express delivery ticket to his letter and handed it over, then returned to the car and said, “Now to the police station. The main station.”  
At the police station’s grand entrance, a constable stood guard. When Byomkesh requested an audience with the chief inspector, the constable produced a slip of paper and said, “Write your name and business—I’ll send word in.”  
Byomkesh wrote on the paper, “Gagan Mitra. Regarding Manish Chakraborty’s colliery.”  
A short while later, the constable returned and said, “Come in.”  
Inside, in a room, the uniformed inspector sat at his desk. As we entered, he looked up, then sprang to his feet and grasped Byomkesh’s hand, exclaiming, “What’s this! Since when have you become Gagan Mitra?”  
I recognized the voice at once—Promod Barat. A few years ago, we’d grown close for a time over the Golap Colony affair. A policeman’s life is a wandering one; in his wanderings, he’d become chief inspector at the city’s main station. His pitch-dark features had grown a bit heavier over the years, but the sharpness of his face had not dulled in the least.  
He welcomed us warmly and seated us. For a while, we reminisced about the past, then Byomkesh explained the reason for our visit to this city. Listening, Promodbabu said, “Hmm, the Phuljhuri Colliery case is in our files, but we couldn’t do much. These matters aren’t well suited to police work; we have to operate with too many men, and secrecy is impossible. But you—you’ll manage.”  
Byomkesh said, “Do you remember Bikash Dutta? I’ve called him here; he’ll stay at the colliery and look for leads.”  
Promodbabu replied, “Of course I remember Bikash. Smart fellow. If there’s anything I can do—”

--- Page 903 ---
Kahen Kabi Kalidas 685

Byomkesh said, “I haven’t come to you for that matter, Pramodbabu. Recently, there’s been a murder here—a certain old man named Pranahari Poddar—”

“You’ve heard about that too?”

“How could I not? The son of the man whose house we’re guests in is one of your suspects.”

Pramod Barat twisted his mouth into a pained expression and said, “I’m in a real fix, Byomkeshbabu. The four people under suspicion are all big shots in this city, men of immense influence. I have to tread very carefully. There are no witnesses, no solid proof—everything is circumstantial evidence. If I mistakenly arrest any one of them, it’ll be my own neck on the line.”

Byomkesh asked, “Among these four, whom do you suspect most?”

Pramodbabu pondered for a moment and said, “All four have equal motive, all four had the same opportunity. Still, I feel this is Aravind Halder’s doing.”

“Do you think the four of them could have acted together, committed the murder as a group?”

“No.”

“There was a maid in the house—have you considered her?”

“I have. She had the most opportunity, but I couldn’t find a motive.”

“Hm. Tell me everything you know—perhaps I can help you.”

“You’ll help? Thank you. To have your help would be a stroke of luck, Byomkeshbabu.”

Thereafter, what Pramod Barat recounted was this—

On the night Pranahari Poddar died, at around ten o’clock, a truck was coming from the direction of the Uludanga Colliery. The truck driver suddenly stopped his vehicle, because a woman was standing in the middle of the road, waving her hands and signaling him to halt. When the truck stopped, the woman ran up and said, “Quick, call the police! Someone has murdered the owner of this house.”

The truck driver went to the thana and gave the news. Within half an hour, Inspector Barat arrived at the scene with his men. The girl was still standing by the roadside, eyes wild with distress. Her name was Mohini, the only maid in Pranahari’s household—there were no other servants.

Inspector Barat went up to the second floor and saw the corpse; his subordinates searched the house thoroughly. There was no one else in the house. Questioning Mohini, they learned that she slept in a small room beside the kitchen on the ground floor; the master slept in an upstairs room. That evening, after returning from the city, he had sat in the downstairs room and taken tea, then gone upstairs. Mohini had begun cooking. Usually, after nine o’clock, the master would come down for dinner, but tonight he did not. Half an hour later, when Mohini went upstairs to call him, she found the master lying dead on the floor.

After sending the body for post-mortem, Barat questioned Mohini again. In response, she said that after evening, no one came to the house; for some time, four gentlemen had been coming at night to play cards. On the days they were expected, the master would bring fish, meat, minced meat, and such from the city; Mohini would cook and serve them. Tonight, the gentlemen had not come, so there was no special cooking. All four were young men, not old like the master. They arrived by motorcar; from their dress and manner, they seemed wealthy. Mohini did not know their names. Tonight, while she was cooking, she could not say whether anyone had come to the house. If anyone did come, Pranahari would meet them downstairs; he never took visitors upstairs.

--- Page 904 ---
He would not take anyone along. The master of the house had not come downstairs today; if he had, Mohini would have heard the sound of conversation.

Finishing his interrogation, Barot said, “What will you do now? Do you know anyone in the city?”

Mohini replied, “No, I don’t know anyone here.”

Barot said, “Then you should come with me. The constable from the street will stay at the station; tomorrow we can make some arrangements. You are a woman—how can you stay alone in this house?”

Mohini said, “I can manage! I’ll lock myself in my room. I won’t be afraid.”

So that was the arrangement. Barot left, posting a constable on guard.

Investigating about Pranahari, Pramod Babu learned that Pranahari had been a member of the Coal Club. Going there, he found out that Pranahari used to play cards regularly with four other members. The matter became somewhat clearer; it could be surmised that these four used to go to Pranahari’s house to play cards.

Pramod Babu interrogated the four separately. They admitted that they sometimes went to Pranahari’s house to play cards, but they firmly denied having gone there on the night of Pranahari’s death.

Barot then questioned the four men’s chauffeurs. Three of the drivers said that their employers had not gone to Pranahari’s house by car that night. Only one said that at around eight o’clock, the gentlemen had left the club together, but had gone on foot instead of by car, and had returned about an hour later. He did not know where they had gone together.

Barot then made inquiries among the taxi drivers—there were about fifty taxis in the city. At last, one driver pointed out another, saying, “He went out on hire that night—ask him.” The second driver then said that on the night in question, he had taken four passengers to the road by the Uludanga coal mine. Barot brought the driver to the Coal Club and discreetly showed him the four men. The driver identified them.

After that, Barot interrogated the four men repeatedly, but they steadfastly denied everything. The situation now stood thus: apart from the testimony of a single taxi driver, there was no other witness; in such circumstances, it was not possible to arrest four prominent citizens of the city on a charge of murder.

Finishing his account, Barot said, “I have told you all that I have been able to find out. However, perhaps I should mention one irrelevant detail. The elder brother of one of the accused, Govinda Haldar, came to offer me a bribe of five thousand rupees.”

“Is that so?”

“Yes. A very cunning man. He called me aside and signaled that if I hushed up the matter, I would receive five thousand rupees as a reward.”

I glanced at the clock—it was half past nine.

Byomkesh said, “Do you have any urgent work right now? I’d like to see the scene of the crime.”

Barot said, “Very well, let’s go.”

Byomkesh asked, “Is the girl still there?”

Barot replied, “Of course. She has nowhere else to go—she’s still in that house.”

--- Page 905 ---
Kahen Kabikalidas 687

The three of us stepped outside; Pramod Babu joined us in our car. As the car began to move, Byomkesh said to the driver, “Take us to the house where the gentlemen used to play cards.”

The driver’s impassive face betrayed no emotion; he drove as instructed.

Ten minutes later, the motorcar halted before Pranahari Poddar’s house. No one was at the front entrance. The house looked somewhat bare—no boundary wall surrounded it, and it stood a few yards back from the road, exposed and unadorned. The main door was open.

Barat frowned, glancing around, and said, “Where has that wretched constable gone?”

Barat led the way; we followed him into the house. From the direction of the kitchen came the sound of voices. Advancing that way, we saw the uniformed guard, twirling his moustache, standing at the kitchen doorway, engaged in a flirtatious conversation with the maid. At the sight of us, he froze as if turned to wood.

Barat glared at him with bloodshot eyes; the guard snapped to attention and saluted like an automaton. Barat said, “Go outside. What are you doing here, leaving the front door open?”

Barat’s question was purely rhetorical. Even the densest of men could guess what the guard had been doing here. What does a bee do near a pot of honey?

The guard saluted again and left. Barat then cast a suspicious glance into the kitchen. Mohini, seated on the floor, had been cutting vegetables; she rose swiftly and looked at Barat with questioning eyes.

The dark-skinned girl’s entire being—her face, her eyes, her gestures—was woven with an enchanting, magical allure, the irresistible pull of youth in full bloom. Had her complexion been fair, she would have been called a rare beauty. Yet, even in her darkness, there was a midnight-cool intoxication that could ensnare the mind.

But Pramod Barat was a hard, unyielding man. He said, “Where did you get fresh vegetables?”

Mohini replied, “The guard brought them. He brings me his own share of vegetables, I cook for him, and it serves my needs as well.”

Barat made a guttural sound in his throat and said, “Hmm, I see the guard is a man of great charity.”

Whether Mohini caught the sarcasm or not, she asked, “Do you need me for something, Inspector Babu?”

Pramod Babu said, “Stay here. We’ll call you in a little while.”

“All right.”

We turned back toward the main door. As we walked, Byomkesh said with a faint smile, “You made a small mistake, Inspector Barat. You should have posted an old guard here.”

Barat replied, “Byomkesh Babu, you don’t know these fellows. The older the guards get, the more their appetites grow.”

Byomkesh laughed, “And what about the usurious moneylenders?”

Barat shot a quick glance at Byomkesh, then said in a low voice, “I can’t quite figure that out, Byomkesh Babu. But the situation is suspicious. Why don’t you question the girl, see if she had any sort of… arrangement with the old man?”

“I will.”

Beside the main door, we ascended the stairs leading up. At the top of the staircase—

--- Page 906 ---
688  
Byomkesh Samagra

A sturdy, heavy door, fitted with a Yale lock. Compared to the other doors in the house, this one seemed new. Perhaps Pranahari Poddar had installed this new door after renting the house.

Borat took a key from his pocket and unlocked the door. We entered the dark room. Then, as Borat opened a window, brilliant sunlight flooded in.

The room had two windows and two doors. One door faced the staircase, the other was set in the back wall. The room was roughly fifteen feet square, by my estimation. There was little in the way of furniture: a bedstead with bedding, and against the wall at the head of the bed, a massive iron safe. From a wall-mounted rack hung Pranahari’s used clothes. Pranahari was never short of money, but his way of life was exceedingly simple. With the iron safe at his head and a Yale lock on the door, he would sleep on the dingy bedding of his bedstead.

Byomkesh cast his inquisitive eyes about the room and asked, “Where was the body?”

Pointing to the floor about four feet from the stairway door, Borat said, “Here.”

Byomkesh bent down to examine the spot and remarked, “I don’t see much blood. Just a few scattered drops.”

Borat replied, “Was there ever any blood on that old man? His body was like a dried-out bamboo pole.”

Byomkesh said, “Of course, when the skull is fractured, there isn’t much bleeding. Was the murder weapon found?”

“No. There was no weapon in the room. Nor was anything found in the house that could be considered a murder weapon. We searched far and wide around the house, but there was no trace of it.”

“I see. You must have opened the safe. What did you find?”

“The safe key was on Poddar’s waist. When we opened the safe, we found an account ledger bound in khadi cloth, and ten thousand rupees in cash.”

“Ten thousand rupees!”

“Yes. The old man was a moneylender, so perhaps he kept cash on hand.”

“Hm. Did he have money in the bank?”

“He did. And still does. I don’t know who will get it. It’s no small sum—about a hundred and fifty thousand.”

“Is that so! Have any relatives been notified?”

“Apparently, there are none. If there were, they’d have flocked here like a pack of vultures.”

“I’ve heard the old man had an office in the city. Did you find anything there?”

“The ‘office’ was a room like a rat’s hole. There were a few ledgers and papers, but from those it seems the moneylending business wasn’t doing well.”

Byomkesh, lost in thought, half to himself, murmured, “The moneylending business wasn’t doing well, yet there’s a hundred and fifty thousand in the bank and ten thousand in the safe…” Rousing himself from his reverie, he asked, “What’s beyond that other door?”

Borat answered, “Bathing room and such.”

This door too was new and sturdy. Pranahari Poddar had fortified the room like a fortress, for there were valuables in the safe.

Byomkesh opened the door. In the narrow room, light filtered in through a small ventilator in the back wall, and beneath the ventilator was a narrow door. Apart from an empty bucket and a tin mug, there was nothing else in the room.

--- Page 907 ---
Kahen Kobi Kalidas  
689

Nothing.

A narrow door, bolted above and below. Byomkesh slid back the bolts and pushed the panel ajar. Peering through, I saw a slender iron ladder descending all the way to the ground. The sweeper’s alley; the second entrance into Pranahari’s fortress.

Byomkesh asked Barat, “When you first came that night, were these two doors shut?”

Barat replied, “Yes, both were closed. Only the door to the stairs in front was open.”

Byomkesh said, “Come, let’s go downstairs now. Let’s ask the girl a few questions.”

We sat in the downstairs room, arranged like a drawing-room, where they used to play cards. Mohini stood before us, resting her hand on the back of a chair; there was no sign of fear or anxiety on her face, her demeanor composed and reserved.

In my mind, I compared Pranahari’s bare sleeping chamber with this well-appointed drawing-room. Byomkesh asked Mohini, “How long have you worked for Pranaharibabu?”

Mohini replied, “More than two years.”

“Were you with him since he was in Cuttack?”

“Yes, sir.”

“Does Pranaharibabu have any relatives?”

“I don’t know. I’ve never seen any.”

“How much do you earn?”

“In Cuttack, it was ten rupees a month, plus food and lodging. After coming here, he increased my salary by five rupees.”

“What kind of man was Pranaharibabu?”

After a brief pause, Mohini said, “He was my employer, a good man.” In other words: he was my master, I will not speak ill of him—draw your own conclusions.

Byomkesh asked, “Was he miserly?”

Mohini remained silent. Byomkesh fixed his gaze on her and said, “What sort of relationship did you have with him?”

Mohini looked up at Byomkesh with a hint of surprise; at the corner of her lips, a fleeting glimmer of mischief. Then, in a calm voice, she said, “It was good. He was fond of me.”

Byomkesh said, “Hmm. Did he have any vices involving women?”

“No, sir. He was an old man, had none of those faults. Only a passion for cards. He would sit alone and play.”

“All right. Now tell me about yourself. Pranaharibabu has been murdered, yet you remain alone in this house—why?”

“Where would I go? I have no one in this city.”

“Why don’t you return home?”

“I will. But the Daroga Babu has ordered that I must not leave until the murder is solved.”

“Who do you have at home?”

“My old mother and father.”

--- Page 908 ---
690  
Byomkesh Samagra

“And your husband?”  
Mohini glanced up swiftly, then lowered her eyes again, offering no reply.  
“You are married, surely?”  
Mohini nodded silently.  
“Where is your husband?”  
Mohini raised her head and answered slowly, “My husband left home and never returned.”  
Byomkesh fixed his gaze upon her and lit a cigarette. “How long has it been since your husband deserted you?”  
“Three years.”  
“What did your husband do?”  
“He worked in a mill, in a factory.”  
“Why did he become a vagabond?”  
Mohini’s lips curved ever so slightly; she cast a quick, restless glance at Byomkesh and said, “I don’t know.”  
Listening to their exchange and observing Mohini, I wondered—what sort of woman was she? Virtuous, or wayward? Among women of her class, fidelity and chastity are not held in high regard. Pressed by worldly needs, their lives often stray down crooked, perilous paths. Yet Mohini did not seem to belong to that common class of servant girls. There was something different about her. Alongside the restlessness and coquettishness of youth, she possessed a firmness and courage of character. If this woman were to become corrupted, she would do so knowingly and deliberately, not out of mere necessity.  
Byomkesh took two long drags on his cigarette and said, “The four gentlemen who used to come here to play cards—you’ve seen them a few times, haven’t you?”  
Mohini’s eyes darted once to the south, then to the left; her lips parted for a moment, as if she was about to smile but stopped herself. Then she said, “Yes, I’ve seen them a few times.” She understood the direction of Byomkesh’s questioning.  
Byomkesh asked, “Can you tell what sort of men they were?”  
The unspoken smile now blossomed fully. Tilting her head slightly, Mohini replied, “Can you judge a man’s nature by his face, babu? Still, one of them was the most childish, and the kindest. The other three—” She broke off.  
Byomkesh said, “Yes, what about the other three?”  
With a smile and a click of her tongue, Mohini said, “I don’t know, babu.”  
Mohini had a certain power—by saying ‘I don’t know’ she could reveal a great deal.  
Byomkesh flicked the burnt end of his cigarette out the window and asked, “Did any of them come here at times other than to play cards?”  
Mohini glanced toward the wooden cot and said, “One of them used to come. He would arrive after the master left for the office in the morning.”  
“Who was he?”  
“I don’t know his name, babu. Dark, rather stout, and very good at idle talk.”  
Barat muttered under his breath, “Aravind Halder.”  
Byomkesh said to Mohini, “So he used to come to see you, then?”

--- Page 909 ---
Kohen K Bi Kalidas 691

Mohini merely shook her head.

Byomkesh asked, “Did he make any proposition?”

Mohini’s gaze suddenly hardened; in a sharp voice, she replied, “He came to give me a gold ring, came to give me a silk saree.”

“Did you accept them?”

“No. My honor is not so cheap.”

Byomkesh observed her intently for a while, then said, “Alright, that’s enough for today. If needed, I’ll question you again later. —You’re from Orissa, but I see you speak clear Bengali.”

Mohini’s tone softened this time. She said, “Babu, I have worked in Bengali households since childhood.”

On the way back, I pondered: the naive and decent man Mohini described was surely Phanish. Among the other three, Aravinda Haldar was a two-eared scoundrel. And the remaining two? Perhaps not so shameless, but greed lurked in their hearts; they drink water while pretending to drown. Mohini had said her honor was not so cheap. But what is the price of her honor? Does its value rise and fall in proportion to beauty and youth? Or is there some other measure? Only an expert could answer such a question.

In front of the thana, Barat got down.

Byomkesh said, “I’ll come again this afternoon. I must meet the civil surgeon—the one who performed the autopsy.”

Barat replied, “You’ll come. I’ll arrange a time with the civil surgeon. The PM report is ready, of course.”

Byomkesh said, “I’ll look at the PM report as well.”

Barat said, “Alright. I’ll fix the appointment between four and four-thirty.”

We returned home—it was already twelve o’clock. After a while, Manish Babu and the others came back. Raising his brows, Manish Babu looked at Byomkesh, who said, “You needn’t worry, I’m doing what needs to be done. I’ve met the police. Arrangements have been made; I’ll tell you everything later.”

Satisfied, Manish Babu went off to bathe. Phanish hovered about us, eager and restless. Byomkesh smiled and said, “You too can rest easy, things have moved forward. I’ll go out again in the afternoon.”

At three o’clock, father and son set out again for work. We went to Surapati Ghatak’s office. Surapati Babu seated us in his office room and began to regale us with various details about running a coal mine. Then, two young men appeared at the doorway. Clad in khaddar, with calm, gentle faces, their expressions combined intelligence with humility. Surapati Babu said, “Ah, you’ve come! Gagan Babu, these are the ones I spoke to you about. They’re brothers, Vishwanath and Jagannath. I’ve taught them the work myself. They’re young, but thoroughly skilled.”

Byomkesh said, “Very good. You have no objection to leaving this job and going elsewhere?”

Vishwanath and Jagannath shook their heads—no objection. Surapati Babu said, “But I can’t let both of them go at once, that would harm my work. You may take one of them, whichever you prefer.”

“So be it,” said Byomkesh, taking a notebook from his pocket and writing down both their names and particulars.

--- Page 910 ---
692  
Byomkesh Samagra

He took it and said, “At the proper time, I will write to you.”  
Biswanath and Jagannath offered their farewells and departed. Byomkesh said to Surapati Babu,  
“I like both of them. I will take whichever one you wish to give.”  
Surapati Babu, pleased, replied, “Both brothers are equally capable men; whichever you choose, you won’t be disappointed.”  
Seeing that it was almost four o’clock, we rose to leave.  
Barat was in his office. He said, “The Civil Surgeon will see you at half past four. Here is the post-mortem report.”  
Byomkesh glanced over the report and returned it. Then we set off towards the hospital. The Civil Surgeon’s office was at the hospital.  
The Civil Surgeon, Birajmohan Ghoshal, was seated in his office, drawing on his hookah. An elderly man, his complexion fair as a schoolboy’s, with a handsome face—upon seeing us, he burst into loud laughter. He said,  
“I have found out your real name, Byomkesh Babu. Inspector Barat tried to pull a fast one on me, but it didn’t work.” With that, he laughed again, heartily.  
Byomkesh replied with humility, “Circumstances forced the five Pandavas to adopt aliases; I am but a simple man. I have come here on a confidential matter, so I had to keep a low profile.”  
“Don’t worry, nothing will escape my lips. Please, sit.”  
For a while, light conversation and laughter ensued. Dr. Ghoshal was a cheerful soul; a lifetime spent among corpses had not dulled his spontaneous, booming laughter.  
At last, we turned to business. Byomkesh said, “I have read the post-mortem report on Pranahari Poddar. I would like to hear anything extra you might have to say. The man was old, frail—did he have any strength left in his body?”  
Biraj Babu replied, “Physical strength—”  
“I mean—virility. A man’s youth can last to a great age; there are cases of men fathering children at a hundred. Was Pranahari Poddar’s body still capable in that sense?”  
Biraj Babu burst out laughing again. “Oh—that’s what you want to know? Why be shy with a doctor? No, there was nothing left in Pranahari Poddar’s body, not a drop—dry as a stick.” He drew twice on his hookah and said, “I have noticed, those who worry about money day and night, don’t keep such things for long. Pranahari Poddar was a usurious moneylender, after all.”  
It seemed Byomkesh was a little disappointed. He furrowed his brow for a moment, then said,  
“Very well, let that be. Now, tell me about the murder weapon. Apart from that one blow to the skull, was there any other sign of injury?”  
“No.”  
“One blow caused death?”  
“Yes.”  
“What kind of weapon was it?”  
Biraj Babu drew on his hookah for a while. “It’s hard to say what kind of weapon it was. It was something long, long and heavy. Not sharp like a cutlass, nor blunt like a police baton—”  
Byomkesh said, “Could it have been an electric torch?”  
“An electric torch?” Biraj Babu shook his head. “No, that wouldn’t leave such a clean, cut mark.”

--- Page 911 ---
Said K Bikali Das 693

“Take this, for instance—if you strike the head hard with the blunt edge of a katari, that is, the spine of the blade, it could fracture the skull in just that way.”

“A kitchen ladle, a heavy pestle—?”

“No, something heavier than that.”

Byomkesh sat for a while, his cheek resting on his hand. Then he said, “It’s the weapon that’s troubling me. I can’t quite believe that those we suspect would commit murder with a dao or katari. Still, it’s not entirely impossible. Now, answer me one more question. Did the assailant strike from the front, or from behind?”

Birajbabu replied at once, “From the front. The bone is broken from the forehead to the middle of the head—the bones at the back are untouched.”

“Is it absolutely impossible to have struck from behind?”

Birajbabu considered, then said, “If Poddar had been sitting in a chair, it would have been possible. Not if he was standing. Unless, of course, the assailant was ten feet tall—”

Byomkesh stood up, smiling. “If there was a man of such stature here, we’d have noticed. Well, I’ll take my leave for today. Namaskar.”

Back at the thana, Barat said, “And now? Would you like to see the remaining three suspects?”

Byomkesh replied, “Of course. Will they be at home now?”

Barat said, “No, at this hour they’ll be at the club, playing games.”

“Then let it be for now. If I go to the club with you, the prey will be startled. By the way, could you give me Poddar’s account book? I’d like to go through it, see if anything turns up.”

“It’s at the office—take it. Anything else?”

“And—one more thing. We know nothing of Pranahari Poddar’s past. He was in Cuttack about a year and a half ago. Can we get any information from the Cuttack police department?”

Barat said, “I’ve already checked with the Cuttack police—Pranahari Poddar has no police record. But if you want to know about him in general, I know an officer there—Inspector Pattnayak. He’s been in Cuttack for several years—I can write to him.”

“Do that. Send Inspector Pattnayak a telegram, so we get news as soon as possible. I’ll take my leave now; I’ll be back again tomorrow morning.”

After dinner, Manishbabu went upstairs, and we retired to our own rooms. I turned on the fan above my head and began to prepare for sleep, but Byomkesh did not lie down. He took Pranahari’s account book and sat at the table. It was a long, folded ledger, bound in cloth, with accounts kept in the native style.

Byomkesh, starting from the beginning, slowly turned the pages. I sat at the edge of the bed, nearly finished with my cigarette, when Phanish appeared and stood at the doorway.

Byomkesh looked up at him, then did something strange. On the table before him was a glass paperweight—he swiftly picked it up and tossed it at Phanish.

Phanish caught it deftly; otherwise, it would have shattered on the floor. Byomkesh smiled and called out, “Come in, Phanish.”

--- Page 912 ---
Phanish approached, his face bewildered and astonished. Byomkesh took the glass sphere from his hand and said, “I see you’re surprised. It’s nothing—just testing your reflexes. Sit down, I have a few questions.”

Phanish sat on the chair in front. Byomkesh asked, “You don’t go to the club these days, do you?”

Phanish replied, “Not since that incident. I haven’t gone back.”

Byomkesh said, “Why not? If you suddenly stop going, it draws people’s attention.”

Phanish said, “Alright, I’ll go from tomorrow.”

“We’ll go too. There’s no objection to bringing guests, I hope?”

“No. But—do you have some business at the club?”

“I want to observe your three friends from a distance. Now, tell me—when you all went to thrash Pranahari Poddar that day, did any of you carry weapons?”

“No weapons. But Madhumoy-babu had a long torch, one with a big head. And Mriganka-babu had a cane.”

“What kind of cane? Thick, or flimsy?”

“Flimsy. The kind they call a swagger cane.”

“Hm. You had nothing in your hand?”

“No.”

“And Aravinda Haldar?”

“No.”

“Would it have been possible to hide an iron rod or something similar in your clothes?”

“No. It was summer, everyone was wearing light clothes—dhotis and panjabis. If anyone had something like that, it would have been noticed.”

“Hm—” Byomkesh lit a cigarette and drew on it for a while, then said, “I can’t find a direction. Go on, get some sleep. Can you recite poetry? Tell your wife—Never lose hope, O mind, it will surely come to pass.”

Phanish left, embarrassed. I went to bed. Byomkesh studied his notebook for a while longer, then turned off the light and lay down.

In the darkness, I asked, “You’re quoting poetry quite a bit—did you get anything out of today?”

The answer came, “I’ve discovered three facts. One—the person who killed Pranahari Poddar was not motivated by money; two—he is ambidextrous; three—for a woman like Mohini, anyone could commit murder. Now go to sleep.”

In the morning, I woke to find Byomkesh already seated with the account book. After breakfast, we set out as usual. Byomkesh took the account book along.

At the thana, Inspector Barat greeted us with a smile. “Finished with the account book already?”

Byomkesh replied, “There’s only a year and a half’s worth of accounts in this book—meaning Pranahari started a new ledger after coming here.”

Barat asked, “Did you find anything?”

Byomkesh said, “Nothing that sheds light on the murder. But there’s a small detail that bothers me.”

--- Page 913 ---
“What’s the matter?”

“Pranahari had an arrangement with a taxi driver—he would bring him from home every day and drop him back again. Surely there was a monthly payment for this service. But in the ledger, I see the exact opposite. Here, look at the book.” Byomkesh opened the ledger and showed it to me.

On each page, side by side, were columns for credits and expenses. In the expenses column, entries went down to a single paisa or two, but most days the credit column was empty. Occasionally, there was a note of interest deposited by some debtor. Byomkesh pointed with his finger, “Look here, on the 3rd of Magh, under credits, it says: Taxi driver, thirty-five rupees. It’s the same every month. But under expenses, there’s no mention of any payment for the taxi.”

“Perhaps, by mistake, the expense was written in the credit column.”

“Every month, the same mistake?”

“Hm. What do you think?”

“I can’t say. There’s no record here of gambling winnings or losses either. Doesn’t it all seem a bit mysterious to you?”

“It certainly does. What can be done about it?”

Byomkesh thought for a moment and said, “If we could find the taxi driver who used to ferry Pranahari, we could question him. Do you know him?”

Barat replied, “No, I never thought to look for him. Let’s do one thing—let’s send for Bhuban Das. He should be able to help.”

“Bhuban Das?”

“The taxi driver who took those four to Pranahari’s house that night—his name is Bhubaneshwar Das.”

“Oh—can we find him?”

“The taxi stand is nearby. I’ll send for him.”

Fifteen minutes later, Bhubaneshwar Das arrived, saluted, and stood before us. He was of medium build, dressed in khaki trousers and shirt, a cap on his head like a guard’s. Age—about thirty or thirty-two. His eyes were reddish, his face grave. I wondered if the man was under the influence.

Barat nodded at Byomkesh, signaling him to proceed. Byomkesh looked Bhuban Das up and down, then began his questions.

“Your name is Bhuban Das. You were in the military?”

“Yes, sir.”

“A sepoy?”

“No, sir, a truck driver.”

“How long have you been driving a taxi?”

“Three or four years.”

“All three or four years here?”

“No, sir, I’ve been here about a year and a half. Before that, I was in Calcutta.”

“Where is your home?”

“Medinipur district, Bhagabanpur village.”

“You took four people to Pranahari Poddar’s house that day?”

“Not to the house, sir—some distance away from it.”

“All right. While they were in your taxi, did they talk among themselves?”

Bhuban Das hesitated a moment, then said, “They did. I didn’t pay attention to everything they said.”

--- Page 914 ---
Byomkesh asked, “Do you remember anything?”

Bhuban Das fell silent for a moment, then replied, “I think they were talking about some woman. They were speaking in hushed voices—I couldn’t hear clearly.”

Byomkesh said, “All right, let it be. Tell me, did any of your four passengers have a weapon in hand?”

“One of them had a cane.”

“And none of the others had anything?”

“I didn’t notice.”

“Do you take intoxicants?”

“No, sir,” Bhuban Das replied, casting a sidelong, sardonic glance at Inspector Barat.

“Where do you live in the city?”

“I have no home. At night, I sleep in the car.”

“The car is your own?”

“Yes, sir.”

“You must know other taxi drivers in the city.”

“I do, but I don’t mingle much.”

“Can you tell us whose taxi Pranahari Poddar used to take to and from the city?”

It seemed a glint of amusement flickered in Bhuban Das’s bloodshot eyes. Yet he answered in a grave tone, “Sir, it was my taxi.”

We stared at him in surprise. Then Barat demanded sharply, “Why didn’t you tell me this before?”

Bhuban replied, “You never asked, sir.”

Byomkesh suppressed a smile and drew a cigarette from his pocket. My experience with the fraternity of taxi drivers is not vast, but I have observed that they are a taciturn breed, sparing with words unless a quarrel over fare arises—then it is a different matter.

Byomkesh said, “So you knew Pranahari Poddar from before?”

“Yes, sir.”

“What kind of man was he?”

“He was a good man, sir, never once failed to pay his fare.” To Bhuban, this was the ultimate mark of virtue.

“He paid you cash every day?”

“No, sir, we had a monthly arrangement.”

“How much was the monthly fare?”

“Thirty-five rupees.”

Byomkesh exchanged a glance with Barat, then said to Bhuban, “Tell me everything you know about Pranahari Poddar.”

Bhuban said, “I don’t know much, sir. He had an office in the city. About a year ago, he called me and proposed hiring the taxi on a monthly basis—I agreed. Since then, I would bring him from home in the morning and drop him back in the evening. At the start of each Bengali month, he would summon me to his office and settle the fare. That’s all I know about him.”

“You agreed to a monthly wage of only thirty-five rupees? Was there any profit in it?”

--- Page 915 ---
Kohen Kabikalidas 697

“There was a small profit. Since I was a regular renter, I agreed.”

Byomkesh sat with his eyes closed for a while, then asked, “Do you know if Pranahari Babu had any dealings with other taxi drivers?”

Bhuban replied, “Sir, I do not know.”

Byomkesh sighed and said, “Alright, you may go now. If you remember anything about Pranahari, let the Inspector know.”

“Yes, sir.” Bhuban Das saluted and left.

The three of us sat in silence for a while. Then Barat said, “We’ve found nothing at all! Perhaps it’s just a mistake in the ledger—an expense entered as a deposit.”

Byomkesh said, “Or perhaps a coded deposit and expense.”

Lifting his brows, Barat asked, “What do you mean by a coded deposit and expense?”

Byomkesh replied, “Suppose Pranahari Poddar was blackmailing someone. Bhuban Das may think him a good man, but we know he was a cunning fellow. Suppose he was collecting seventy rupees a month through blackmail, but he couldn’t show that money in his accounts. On the other hand, he had to pay the taxi driver thirty-five rupees a month. So, in his ledger, Pranahari made a coded entry: from the seventy rupees, he subtracted thirty-five, and deposited the remaining thirty-five. He couldn’t write the real name of the person he was blackmailing, so he wrote the taxi driver’s name instead. Do you follow?”

Barat said, “I follow. Not impossible. Pranahari’s mind was certainly devious, but yours is even more so.”

Byomkesh stood up, smiling. “Well, I’ll take my leave for today. If we could find out whom Pranahari was blackmailing, perhaps we’d have a lead on the murder. But nothing of that sort turned up among his papers, did it?”

“No. The few documents we found gave no hint of illegal activity. —Will you be coming by this afternoon?”

Byomkesh said, “No, I won’t trouble you again today. I’m going to the Coal Club with Phanish.”

The Coal Club building stood surrounded by a sprawling tract of land. In front, a garden and a parking lawn for motorcars; on either side, spaces for badminton, tennis, and other games. Though the house was single-storied, it contained many large rooms. In the central hall stood a billiards table; in other rooms, some had ping-pong tables, some four or five tables and chairs for cards. In yet another room, the floor was spread with rugs, where chess and dice games were held. At the back of the house were two smaller rooms: one the manager’s office, the other for refreshments—snacks, and a variety of soft and hot drinks prepared for the members.

When we arrived at the club, there was still plenty of daylight. Many members had already gathered. Outside, a game was underway on the tennis court; four were playing, the rest sat on chairs by the court, watching. Phanish led us in that direction.

After standing for a while and watching the game, Byomkesh whispered in Phanish’s ear, “Any of your friends among them?”

Phanish replied, “That one playing there, in the blue vest and white trousers, that’s Mrigen—”

--- Page 916 ---
698  
Byomkesh Samagra

Moulik.’

Though Mrigen Moulik’s frame was somewhat lean, his appearance was unmistakably that of an athlete. There was a slyness in his sporting manner, yet he played tennis well enough. His backhand was forceful; his net play, too, was impressive.

Watching the game, Byomkesh remarked, “The other two aren’t here?”

Phanish replied, “No. Come, let’s go inside.”

At that moment, a voice called from behind, “Byomkesh Babu—oh, pardon me—Gagan Babu, isn’t it!”

Turning, I saw our old acquaintance, Govinda Haldar, gazing at Byomkesh with his familiar gorilla-like, genial grin.

Byomkesh, however, did not smile. Fixing Govinda Babu with a steady gaze, he said, “I see you’ve discovered my real name. How did you find out?”

Govinda Babu replied, “I suspected from the very first meeting. Then, putting two and two together, it all made sense. Gagan—Byomkesh, Sajit—Ajit.”

Byomkesh said, “We didn’t do a good job with the aliases, it was a half-baked effort. But is it wise to broadcast our real names?”

Govinda Babu said, “I’m not broadcasting anything. The name just slipped out. In any case, it’s a pleasure to have you visit our club. Is there a particular purpose?”

Byomkesh replied, “What do you think?”

Govinda Babu’s slow eyes drifted once to Phanish, then returned to Byomkesh’s face. “You’re a man of business; I can’t believe you’d be out amusing yourself for no reason. So you’re here on business. But what business? Investigating the mystery of the coal mine?”

Byomkesh repeated, “What do you think?”

Govinda Babu’s eyes narrowed until they became two tiny points. “Then I’ve guessed correctly. Look, you’re a sharp man, but I’ll warn you anyway—don’t go digging for worms and end up unearthing a snake.” His narrowed gaze flickered once more to Phanish, then he walked to the edge of the tennis court and sat down in a chair.

A shadow of anxiety passed over Phanish’s face; in a faltering voice, he said, “Govinda Babu is Aravinda Babu’s elder brother. If he tells Father—”

Byomkesh said, “Don’t worry, Govinda Babu won’t say a word. He loves his own scoundrel of a younger brother. Come, let’s go inside.”

On the front veranda, a table was laid with daily newspapers and weeklies. We sat there. Phanish summoned a liveried servant and ordered three glasses of chilled sharbat.

As we sipped the ice-cold sharbat, I felt a pleasant dizziness creeping in. Outside, the tennis match ended. The club members began coming in, snatches of conversation drifting to our ears. Inside the house, electric lights blazed in every room. From the table-tennis room came the sharp clack of play. Suddenly, a member’s voice rang out—Hey, bearer!

A living tableau of affluent, genteel life.

When the sharbat was finished, we lit cigarettes and entered the house. In the central hall, two silent players were absorbed in a languid game of billiards; on the vast table, three balls played hide-and-seek like children. —Here, there was no one of interest to us.

--- Page 917 ---
Said the Poet Kalidas 699

No one. From there, we went to the table-tennis room; standing by the door, I watched a game of half-volley in progress. With a sharp, rhythmic clatter, the ball darted back and forth across the table—a restless, white bubble. There was no one of interest to us in this room either.

From the room with the French sofas, bursts of raucous laughter drifted out now and then. Inside, a game of dice was underway; four players sat around the board in a square. One of them, rubbing the dice between both hands, addressed them in a coaxing tone, “Pasha! Twelve-five-seventeen! Show me twelve-five-seventeen just once! I’ll strike so hard, your insides will spill out.” He threw the dice. A roar of triumph erupted from the opposing side—“Three kora! Three kora!”

We withdrew from the doorway and entered the card room.

Not all the tables in the card room were occupied yet; at one table, a solitary man played patience, at another, three players, lacking a fourth, passed the time with a truncated game. At one table, a game of chaturanga was in progress; four players, deeply absorbed, studied their cards. One of them declared, “Three hearts.” Contract bridge.

Phanish whispered, “The one who bid is Madhumoy Sur, and his partner is Aravind Halder.”

Byomkesh did not approach the table, but stood at a distance, gazing in that direction. Even without an introduction, it was clear that Aravind Halder was the younger brother of Govinda Halder. The same gorilla-like features, only younger. Madhumoy Sur was a dapper, fastidious man; the lack of character in his face was compensated for by his immaculate, tailored panjabi and diamond buttons.

The game had begun, one of the opposing team had become dummy. It was a slam, and no one’s attention strayed elsewhere.

After watching for five minutes, Byomkesh signaled, and we stepped outside. He did not seem satisfied with the possible suspects he had observed; in a dry tone, he said, “We’ve seen what there was to see. Let’s go home now.”

On our way back in the car, I asked, “What did you see?”

Byomkesh replied, “I saw three men, their surroundings, the way they moved their hands and feet. —Phanish, tomorrow morning we’ll visit their homes. It’s necessary to make their acquaintance. I don’t expect to get more than what we have today, but still—”

“So you did get something today?”

“I did. Though it’s negative evidence.”

The next morning, Phanish did not go to the coal mine with his father; Manish Babu went alone. Phanish drove us himself. Starting the car, he asked, “Where shall we go first?”

Byomkesh said, “I have no preference for any of them. Let’s visit the one whose house is nearest.”

“In that case, let’s go to Mrigen Babu’s house.”

Mrigen Moulik’s house was exceedingly elegant, a testament to the owner’s refined taste. Our car passed through the garden and stopped at the portico. We saw Mrigen Moulik reclining in an easy chair in front of the house, reading the newspaper, dressed in loose pajamas and a silk dressing gown. As we got out of the car, he folded the paper and looked up at us. No smile of welcome appeared on his face; rather, his expression darkened. When we approached, he said in a harsh voice, “What do you want?”

--- Page 918 ---
We halted, startled. Phanish said, “Mrigenbabu, these are my father’s friends, come from Calcutta—”

Casting a look of fierce contempt at Phanish, Mrigen said, “I know. Which one is Byomkesh Bakshi?”

Phanish faltered. Byomkesh replied, “I am Byomkesh Bakshi. I had a few words for you.”

Mrigen twisted his face in utter disdain and said, “Nothing will be accomplished here. You may leave.” With that, he tucked the paper under his arm and strode into the house.

We looked at each other. Phanish’s face had turned the color of vermilion from humiliation, while a wounded smile played on Byomkesh’s lips. He said, “I see Govinda Haldar has warned the suspects.”

Phanish said, “Let’s go home.”

Byomkesh shook his head. “No, since we’re out, we’ll finish our work before returning. Don’t be ashamed, Phanish. Those who seek the truth must abandon shame, hatred, and fear. Come, let’s go to Madhumoy Sur’s house next.”

As we made our way to the car, I said, “But why? What does this behavior mean? If Mrigen Maulik is innocent, what is he afraid of?”

Byomkesh said, “They think I’m on Phanish’s side, that I want to save him and implicate them.”

Madhumoy Sur’s house had the air of late afternoon, the garden devoid of any charm. On the front veranda, Madhumoy Sur lay on a mat, clad in a gamchha, while a burly young servant massaged his body with oil. Madhumoy’s frame was not particularly muscular, but he had a solid little paunch! Seeing us, he sat up.

Phanish began, his voice thin and hesitant, “Madhumoybabu, forgive us, this is your bathing hour—”

Madhumoy paid no heed to his words, blinking at us several times, then said in a singsong tone, “Why have you come to me? I know nothing about Pranahari Poddar’s death. If anyone claims I went to his house the night he died, that is a lie. I don’t know if anyone else went, but I did not.”

With that, Madhumoy Sur prepared to recline again.

Byomkesh said, “But the taxi driver identified you.”

Madhumoy replied, “The taxi driver is a liar. —Now, please, namaskar.”

Byomkesh quickly asked, “Do you own a torch?”

Madhumoy said, “I have five torches. Now, please, namaskar.”

As we headed toward Aravinda Haldar’s house, Byomkesh remarked, “Madhumoy knew we would come, had rehearsed what to say. Whatever else, he’s more civil than Mrigen Maulik. How sweetly he said—‘Now, please, namaskar.’ In the words of Nimchand Dutta—‘The boy is not ill-mannered.’”

Aravinda Haldar and Govinda Haldar lived in the same house, but in separate wings. Aravinda was reclining in his baithak-khana on a mattress covered with a Persian rug, a fat pillow under his head, smoking a cigarette. Seeing us, he propped himself up on his elbow. His eyes were bloodshot, dark

--- Page 919 ---
Kahen Kabi Kalidas  
701

The unshaven bristle of his beard lent a harshness to his face. He scrutinized us one by one, then said, “Come in, Phanish.”

Phanish, his face ashen, murmured, “These gentlemen—”

Aravind interrupted, “I know. Please, have a seat.” He pushed forward a tin of cigarettes.

We were unprepared for such civility, and so, for a moment, we hesitated. Byomkesh settled himself on the edge of the takhtposh, and we followed suit. Aravind spoke in an easy tone, “Last night I overindulged a bit. Still haven’t quite shaken off the stupor. —Hey, Gadadhar.”

A servant brought in a glass of some drink. Aravind drained it in a single gulp, handed the glass back, and said, “What can I get you gentlemen? Tea? Sherbet? Beer?”

Byomkesh replied in a courteous voice, “Thank you. We require nothing, Aravindbabu; if we might simply have a few words with you, we’d consider ourselves obliged.”

Aravind said, “Of course! Ask what you will. But let me make one thing clear from the outset. I don’t know what Phanish has told you, but on the night Pranahari Poddar died, I did not go to his house.”

Byomkesh was silent for a moment, then said, “Aravindbabu, I have no ulterior motive. It is not my business to implicate an innocent man in a murder case; I am a satyanweshi—a seeker of truth. Of course, if you are guilty—”

Aravind said, “I am innocent. On the night Pranahari died, I did not go within a mile of his house. With that understood, ask whatever you wish.”

Byomkesh said, “Very well, let us leave that matter aside. But before Pranahari’s death, you visited his house several times.”

Aravind replied, “Yes, I did. The four of us used to go there to gamble.”

Byomkesh said, “Apart from those gambling sessions, you also visited his house alone on several occasions.”

A sly, lecherous grin flickered across Aravind’s face. He said, “Yes, I did.”

“For what purpose?”

With shameless candor, baring his teeth, Aravind said, “To see Mohini. To get close to her.”

Byomkesh asked, with a hint of irony, “But you didn’t succeed, did you?”

The smile vanished from Aravind’s face. He stared at Byomkesh with wide eyes. “Didn’t succeed—what do you mean?”

Byomkesh said, “I think you understand what I mean. Are you suggesting that—?”

Aravind suddenly burst out laughing, loud and coarse, then checked himself and said, “Byomkeshbabu, you may be a great detective, but you know nothing of the ways of the world. Mohini is a trifling woman, a dasibadi. If you throw enough money, there’s nothing you can’t buy.”

Byomkesh asked, “How much money did you throw?”

Aravind raised two fingers. “Two thousand rupees.”

“You gave Mohini two thousand rupees? Isn’t that a bit steep for a dasibadi?”

“I didn’t give it to Mohini. I gave it to Mohini’s broker. To Pranahari Poddar.” Aravind’s words dripped with venom.

Byomkesh was silent for a while, then said, “Very well, let’s leave that aside. What kind of man was Pranahari Poddar?”

--- Page 920 ---
702  
Byomkesh Samagra

Aravind said in a flat voice, “He was a chamar, a money-fiend. He was just like any other common man.”  
Aravind’s opinion of the common man is not particularly high. Byomkesh asked, “Did Pranahari Poddar cheat you out of a lot of money at cards?”  
Aravind replied with disdain, “He won, we lost. Whether he cheated or not, I cannot say.”  
“Then why did you go to thrash him?”  
Aravind opened his mouth to answer, then stopped, scrutinizing Byomkesh carefully before saying, “Who told you I went to thrash him? Let those who went speak for themselves. I didn’t go to beat anyone.”  
I cast a sidelong glance at Phanish. He was listening with his head bowed, then looked up once at Aravind, before lowering his head again.  
Byomkesh sighed and stood up, saying slowly, “Even from what you’ve told us, there are discrepancies. Your account doesn’t match Mohini’s. Perhaps you’re telling the truth. Well then, namaskar. Tell your elder brother that it’s not safe to go bribing the police—it only increases suspicion. Not all police officers are corrupt.”  

Thus, three days passed for us in near idleness, the mystery of Pranahari Poddar’s death hanging in the air like Trishanku, suspended in the void. No new information came to light; we had to make do with the scant details we had gathered earlier. Even the letter from Inspector Borat’s friend Pattnayak in Cuttack, concerning Pranahari’s past, shed no light on the murder. Pranahari Poddar was a professional gambler, but had never been caught by the police. He had spent a couple of years in Cuttack, but where he had come from before that was unknown. He had no dependents, no occupation. In his own house, he would gather the wayward sons of a few wealthy men for gambling sessions. Gradually, these wayward youths realized that Pranahari was cheating at cards and draining their blood. They then conspired to give him a sound thrashing. But before the plan could be carried out, one day Pranahari Poddar vanished. There was a young maidservant working in his house; she too disappeared. It was suspected that the old Pranahari had an illicit relationship with the maid.  
From Pattnayak’s letter, only one thing became clear: there was a definite pattern to Pranahari’s professional life.  
Byomkesh’s mind was unsettled. In Indira’s eyes, anxiety and apprehension were thickening once again. Phanish was restless. Manish Babu, a man of grave temperament, too seemed to be growing a little impatient. The nameless miscreants of the coal mine were still at large.  
Bikash Dutta had arrived and joined the hospital at the coal mine. One afternoon, we had gone to the coal mine with Manish Babu; Byomkesh, under the pretext of inspecting the hospital, had met Bikash and offered him some advice.  
In these three days, only one noteworthy incident occurred, which deserves mention.  
Byomkesh, gradually growing weary of lying in bed and pacing the room, said to me last evening, “Come, let’s take a walk outside.”  
The road was deserted, the lights not very bright, the air pleasantly cool. Now and then a passerby, an occasional motorcar passed by. Suddenly, Byomkesh said, “What’s the point of investigating the murder of a third-rate fellow like Pranahari Poddar? Whoever killed him did the right thing—he deserves a gold—”

--- Page 921 ---
Kohen Kabikalidas  
703

“They deserve a medal.”

I said, “Even to give them a gold medal, we’d need to know who they are.”

Byomkesh paced a while longer, then said, “We’ll have to visit Mohini again. There’s something I haven’t asked her yet.”

It was at this moment that I first noticed the bicycle. We were pacing along the edge of the road, when I saw, about fifty yards ahead, a bicycle approaching. The cycle bore no light; in the streetlamp’s glow, the rider appeared only as a vague silhouette. A solar topee was pulled low, shadowing his face. As we watched, the bicycle drew up to us, and the rider tossed a pale object at our feet before pedaling away at speed, vanishing into the darkness.

Byomkesh seized my arm and pulled me back with lightning speed. We retreated ten paces and turned, staring fixedly at the whitish object. But nothing happened. The thing, about the size of a tennis ball, lay inert on the ground. It never occurred to me that it could be a bomb; but seeing Byomkesh’s tense posture, my heart began to thud in my chest.

Byomkesh said, “Ajit, run to the house and fetch a torch, quickly.”

He stood his ground while I hurried back to the house. Phanish and Manish Babu, having heard the commotion, came out with me.

“What’s the matter?”

Byomkesh said, “Don’t come any closer. It’s probably nothing, but better to be careful. Ajit, hand me the torch.”

Taking the torch, he shone its beam on the object lying on the ground. I craned my neck to see—a paper-wrapped parcel was slowly coming undone. Byomkesh stepped closer, observed it a moment, then picked it up. Smiling, he said, “A lump of coal, wrapped in paper.”

Manish Babu exclaimed, “Coal—!”

Byomkesh replied, “The coal isn’t important—the paper is. Let’s go inside and have a look.”

Standing under the bright light in the drawing room, Byomkesh carefully unwrapped the parcel. He placed the lump of coal on the table, then held the crumpled paper up to the light, gripping it by both edges. The paper was the size of an ordinary letter, and on it, in large, ink-black letters, two lines were written: “Byomkesh Bakshi, if you do not leave the city at once, you will not return alive.”

“How dreadful—they know your name,” said Manish Babu, reaching out. “Let me see that paper.”

Byomkesh shook his head. “No, best not to touch it. There may be fingerprints.”

Holding the paper carefully, Byomkesh went to the bedroom. I followed. On the table lay an illustrated British magazine; he opened it and tucked the paper carefully inside. I said, “Which side is this from? Of course, seeing the coal, it seems the coal mine people have found out.”

Byomkesh replied, “That could be a ruse. Govinda Haldar knows I’m here about the coal mine.”

Returning to the drawing room, I saw that Surapati Ghatak, the office manager, had arrived and was speaking with the master of the house.

--- Page 922 ---
Byomkesh Samagra

Most likely, they were discussing some office matter. Seeing us, he greeted us politely with a respectful namaskar.

When he had finished his conversation and departed, Byomkesh said to Manishbabu, “You haven’t said anything to Surapati Babu, have you?”

Manishbabu replied, “No. —But those scoundrels are frightened.”

Byomkesh said, “If they weren’t afraid, they wouldn’t have tried to scare me.”

Manishbabu, pleased, said, “I don’t know what you’re up to behind the scenes, but you’re certainly doing something—those rascals are rattled. —Anyway, you weren’t alarmed when you received the letter, were you?”

Byomkesh smiled faintly and said, “Not too much. Still, tonight I’ll sleep with the door locked.”

In the morning, Phanish dropped us off at the thana and said, “I need to go to the market for a bit—Indira needs something. I’ll be back within the hour. Will that be all right?”

“No. We’ll be here for about an hour.”

Phanish drove off in the motorcar, and we entered the thana.

Promodbabu was seated at his desk, busy with papers. Byomkesh placed the illustrated British magazine before him and said, “There’s a slip of paper inside—there might be fingerprints on it. Do you have a fingerprint expert?”

Lifting the pages of the magazine and seeing the slip, Barat said, “Of course we do. What’s the matter?”

Byomkesh recounted the events of the previous night. Hearing this, Barat said, “It seems to be about the coal mine. I’ll arrange for it right away. You’ll have the report by this afternoon.”

He called someone and sent the magazine and the slip of paper off to the experts. Then he said, “You haven’t come for three days—any news from your end?”

Byomkesh said, “As before, so now—no new developments. But there’s something that bothers me.”

“What is it?”

“Mohini was paid fifteen rupees a month by Pranahari. But in the account book, there’s no mention of Mohini’s salary.”

Barat pondered and said, “Hmm. I see plenty of discrepancies in Pranahari’s accounts. What will you do now?”

Byomkesh said, “I’d like to question Mohini. She’s still here, isn’t she?”

Barat replied, “She’s very much here, hasn’t budged an inch. I can’t let her go either, not until this case is settled—”

“In that case, let us go see her.”

“Go ahead.”

“No, no, you have other work—please stay. Ajit and I will go. Will we find that young constable of yours there?”

Barat smiled, “You certainly will.”

We left the thana. Phanish hadn’t returned yet, so we made our way toward the taxi stand.

Not far from the thana, by the roadside, under the vast shade of a pakur tree, was the spot where taxis waited. As we walked in that direction, I said, “Byomkesh, what is the connection between Pranahari and the coal mine affair?”

--- Page 923 ---
Kohen K Bi Kalidas  
705

“Is there any connection?”  
He replied, “Nothing at all. I am the only link.”  
As we approached the taxi stand, we saw that beneath the trees stood just a single taxi, and by the roadside, a massive black motorcar was parked with its rear facing us. The taxi driver, Bhuban Das, was standing by the window of the black car, conversing with its chauffeur. As we drew a little closer, the black motorcar drove away. Bhuban Das returned to his own taxi.

Byomkesh, frowning deeply, said, “Did you recognize whose car that was? Govinda Haldar’s. I saw the number on the first day.”  
“What does Govinda Haldar want with a taxi driver?”  
“Perhaps he’s trying to break a witness. Let’s see.”

When we reached the taxi, Bhuban was taking a jack out of the boot, preparing to set it under the wheel. Seeing us, he saluted and said, “Need a taxi, sir?”  
Byomkesh said, “Yes, we need to go to Pranharibabu’s house. There’s a woman there I need to speak to.”  
Bhuban glanced at Byomkesh from the corner of his eye, scratched his head, and said, “I’ll be a little late, sir. Got a flat tire, need to change the wheel.”  
Byomkesh suddenly asked, “What was Govinda Haldar talking to you about?”  
Bhuban started, “Sir?—He—he knows me, so he stopped for a chat. Very good man.” With that, he began spinning the jack furiously, lifting the car’s wheel off the ground.

I looked at Byomkesh’s face—he was standing as if struck by a trance, his eyes fixed on Bhuban, but in his mind’s eye he was seeing something else. I called, “Byomkesh!”  
He turned his head toward me and muttered, “Ajit, what do you get if you add fifteen and thirty-five?”  
I said, “Fifty. What nonsense are you talking?”  
He said, “Come.” And turned toward the thana. After a little distance, I glanced back—Bhuban was staring at us intently.

When we arrived at the thana, Inspector Barat looked up and said, “What’s this, you didn’t leave?”  
Byomkesh said, “Pramodbabu, is there a quiet spot in your station? I need to sit alone and think for a bit.”  
A snake recognizes the scent of its own kind. Barat immediately stood up and said, “Come with me.”  
At the back of the station was a covered veranda, deserted, with a few chairs scattered about. Byomkesh stretched out in an easy chair and lit a cigarette. Barat smiled faintly and withdrew.

Within half an hour, having finished five or six cigarettes, Byomkesh said, “Let’s go. It’s done.”  
I asked, “What’s done?”  
He said, “The inner eye has opened, the truth is revealed. Come.”  
We went to Barat’s office; as we stood by his desk, he looked up, his face eager.  
Byomkesh said, “Pramodbabu, in which bank does Pranharibabu keep his money?”

--- Page 924 ---
Barat said, “At the Central Bank. Why?”

Byomkesh asked, “Do you know if they have a safe deposit vault there?”

“I believe they do.”

Glancing at his wristwatch, Byomkesh said, “The bank should be open by now. —Let’s go.”

Without further questions, Barat rose to his feet. Stepping outside, we saw that Phanish had returned and was preparing to alight from the car. Byomkesh called out, “Don’t get down, Phanish. You’ll have to drop us at the Central Bank.”

The bank stood in the heart of the city, guarded at the entrance by an armed sentry. Before getting out of the car, Byomkesh said to Phanish, “Phanish, you go home. We’ll be late returning. —By the way, where is your wife’s parental home?”

Phanish turned his head in surprise and replied, “In Nabadwip.”

Byomkesh said, “Hmm. Then she must know how to make malpo. Tell her we’ll have malpo this evening.”

We got out; Phanish drove off with a hint of disappointment. He had realized that the mystery of Pranahari’s death was nearing its end.

Barat led us to the bank manager’s office; he was already acquainted with the manager. He said, “We’re here regarding Pranahari Poddar. Does your bank have a safe deposit vault?”

The manager replied, “Yes, we do.”

Barat looked at Byomkesh, who asked, “Did Pranahari Poddar rent a vault?”

The manager called for an employee and asked him; the man replied, “Yes, he did.”

Byomkesh said, “We’d like to see what’s in his safe deposit box.”

The manager hesitated. “But that’s against bank policy. Unless, of course, you have a warrant—”

Barat said, “Pranahari Poddar has been murdered. The police have a warrant to search all his documents and papers.”

The manager thought for a moment, then said, “Very well. Do you have the key?”

“The key?”

“Each safe deposit box has two keys; one is kept by the renter, the other remains with the bank. Without both, the box cannot be opened.”

Byomkesh exchanged glances with Barat. Barat asked, “Surely there’s a duplicate key?”

The manager replied, “There is. But I can’t give it to you without an order from the bank’s directors. It will take four or five days to obtain permission.”

Byomkesh said to Barat, “Let’s go and search Pranahari’s chest once more. The key must be somewhere in that room.”

Barat stood up and told the manager, “We’ll be back. If we can’t find the key, we’ll make a formal request.”

We returned to the thana, and from there, taking two more men, arrived at Pranahari’s house in the police car.

Today, the young constable was sitting on a stool in front of the house; seeing us, he stood up ceremoniously.

--- Page 925 ---
Kahen Kabi Kalidas  
707

He saluted.

Standing at the doorway, Byomkesh said to Barot, “Let me ask Mohini a few questions. In the meantime, you all search the upstairs room. I believe it won’t be too hard to find the key. It might even be in the safe itself—you simply didn’t look for anything hidden, so you didn’t find it. At that time, you didn’t know Pranahari had a safe-deposit.”

The police party ascended the stairs. Byomkesh and I walked to the front of the kitchen and stood there.

Mohini, her back turned to the door, was cooking. At the sound of our footsteps, she turned her head. At the sight of us, her eyes widened in sudden fear, but then, taking the pan off the stove, she wiped her hands on her sari and came to stand by the doorway.

“Do you need something, Babu?” The fleeting terror had already left her face.

Byomkesh said, “I see you’re still here. Why haven’t you gone back to your village?”

Mohini replied, “What can I do, Babu? How can I leave if the police won’t let me?”

Byomkesh asked, “Have you informed your parents or your husband?”

Mohini lowered her eyes for a moment, then said, “I don’t know where my husband is. I haven’t informed my parents. They’re old people—what good would it do to trouble them?”

Byomkesh said, “That’s true. Now, tell me—on the night Pranaharibabu was murdered, when he didn’t come down for dinner, did you go to his room?”

Mohini nodded. “Yes, Babu.”

“Was the light on in his room?”

“Yes, Babu.”

“Did you see the back door of the room—the bathroom door—open?”

“No, Babu.” A shadow of anxiety flickered in Mohini’s eyes.

“The door was closed?”

For a moment, Mohini hesitated, then said, “I didn’t notice anything, Babu. When I saw Karta-babu lying dead, I just ran away.”

“You didn’t close the bathroom door yourself?”

“No, Babu.”

“Hm.” Byomkesh frowned slightly. “Pranaharibabu paid you fifteen rupees a month?”

“Yes, Babu.”

“Did he pay you on time every month?”

When a person thinks one thing and says another, you can see it in their face. In just that way, Mohini replied absentmindedly, “My wages were kept with Motababu. If I needed a rupee or two, I’d ask him for it.”

Glancing at Byomkesh, I saw a faint smile on his lips. He said, “Seems your wages have died with him. Now, my last question: do you know any ‘nyata’ men?”

Mohini looked surprised. “Nyata men? What do you mean?”

Byomkesh said, “Don’t you know what ‘nyata’ means? Someone who uses their left hand more than their right.”

Mohini suddenly placed her hand on her chest and said, “No, Babu, I don’t know anyone like that.”

--- Page 926 ---
Mohini remained standing, while we ascended to Pranahari’s bedchamber.

The key had been found. There was no need for much searching; in the narrow space between the chest and the wall, the key had been stuck to the back of the chest with wax. Barat said, “Here it is.”

It was a long key, its number engraved upon it. Byomkesh examined it and said, “Let’s go to the bank again.”

At the bank, the key was presented to the manager. This time he did not protest; he himself rose and led us to the vault. Beneath the bank building, underground, was a room whose three walls were lined with row upon row of steel compartments, each with its own door.

With two keys, the door to Pranahari’s compartment was opened. Inside, there was no money, no jewelry—only a few old letters and a bundle of mortgage bonds.

The letters were neither written to Pranahari nor by him. They were addressed to unknown persons, written by unknown men or women. It seemed likely that Pranahari had used these letters as weapons to bleed their authors dry.

Byomkesh had no use for the letters; he took the bonds and came back upstairs. Seated in the manager’s office, he glanced through the bonds one by one. Then, holding up a particular bond, he said to Barat, “Here is your culprit.”

The bond was written in proper legal language: the moneylender Pranahari Poddar of Bhagabanpur had loaned two and a half thousand rupees to Bhubaneswar Das, mortgaging his property as security. The terms of repayment were also stated: fifty rupees in cash; Pranahari would use the motorcar at a monthly rent of twenty-five rupees; thus, seventy-five rupees would be paid monthly.

Barat raised his eyebrows and looked at Byomkesh. Byomkesh said, “My work is done. Now it’s up to you.”

Barat said, “But the proof of murder?”

“There is proof. Whether it will stand in court, I cannot say. Now we’ll return home—it’s half past one.”

“Let me see you home.”

There was little conversation as we rode in the police car. Once, Barat asked, “Shall I arrest Bhuban, then?”

Byomkesh replied, “Do. If he confesses, all the knots will be untangled.”

The car dropped us at the gate of our house and drove away. Barat called out, “I’ll come in the afternoon.”

Around five in the afternoon, as we were sitting down to malpua with kheer, Inspector Barat arrived.

Manish Babu had gone to the coal mine; Phanish was at home. Indira had been with us until now, but on seeing Barat, she withdrew inside. After learning the identity of the culprit, my head had been in a whirl, but now I was beginning to regain my composure.

Inspector Barat’s face was drawn, his mind distracted; he was still wearing the same uniform as in the morning, or so it seemed. He entered, unsmiling, and from his pocket produced an envelope.

--- Page 927 ---
Kohen K Bikali Das  
709

He handed it over to Byomkesh and said, “Here you are—the fingerprints have been obtained.”  
Byomkesh, without opening the envelope, slipped it into his pocket. Looking at Barot, he remarked, “I see you haven’t had your lunch today.”  
Barot shook his head. “How could I? Your suspect has escaped.”  
Byomkesh nodded as if he had anticipated this. Then, inviting Barot to sit, he turned his gaze to Phanish. Phanish quickly retreated into the inner rooms. Barot, leaning back, spoke in a weary voice, “Not just the suspect—Mohini has fled as well. The two of them got into a taxi and vanished. The constable was stationed at Pranahari’s house, but he had no orders to detain Mohini. Bhuban Das arrived in a taxi, honked from the street, Mohini came out and got in. The two of them drove away.”  
Phanish returned with a plate of food and set it before Barot, who began to eat in a dispirited manner. We, too, turned our attention to the malpoa. Silence reigned as we ate.  
Having finished the Vaishnavite repast and about to light a cigarette, I saw a man of the Adali type enter from outside. Gandhi cap on his head, clad in khaddar chapkan and pyjamas—at first glance, I did not recognize him. He snatched the cap from his head and flung it to the floor. Then, in a robust voice, he declared, “I’ve caught the scoundrels, sir!”  
It was Bikash Dutta. The moment he removed his cap, his true identity was revealed. Byomkesh greeted him warmly. “Come in, Bikash. So, you’ve finished the job?”  
“I have, sir. They were about to crack my skull, and that’s when they got caught.” Bikash sprawled on a sofa, stretching his arms and legs, and said firmly, “Both of them are rascals.”  
“Both rascals—who do you mean?”  
Before Bikash could reply, Surapati Ghatak entered the room. Despite his fashionable attire, he had the air of a drenched cat, his eyes sharp and wary like a feline’s. He swiftly surveyed the room with keen, smooth glances, then said in a humble tone, “Is the master in? I’d like to—”  
Byomkesh said, “Come in, Surapatibabu.”  
Bikash suddenly sat upright, scrutinizing Surapatibabu with intense eyes. “His name is Surapati Ghatak? The big boss from the big office?”  
Byomkesh replied, “Yes. Why do you ask?”  
Pointing his forefinger at Surapatibabu, Bikash said, “I was talking about his two brothers-in-law, sir. Bishwanath and Jagannath Ray. They’re the ones stirring up trouble in the coal mine.”  
Fear flickered in Surapati’s eyes. In a thin voice, he stammered, “What? What? I—I know nothing…”  
Barot slowly turned his gaze towards him, fixing him with an unblinking stare. Byomkesh said, “Surapatibabu, the two young men you tried to foist upon us—are they your brothers-in-law?”  
Surapatibabu replied, “Well—what if they are?”  
Byomkesh said, “Nothing at all. Last night, I received a letter with fingerprints from three people. We’d like to compare them, to see if you’re among the three. Inspector Barot, please take Surapatibabu’s fingerprints. Once we compare, we’ll know how deep he is in this conspiracy. Phanish, do we have a rubber stamp ink pad in the house?”

--- Page 928 ---
Surapati Babu was retreating, step by cautious step, toward the door. As he neared it, he spun around, attempting to make his escape. By coincidence, at that very moment, Manish Babu was entering the room; the two nearly collided, but managed to steady themselves at the last instant. Then, with the speed of a startled horse, Surapati Ghatak fled.

Manish Babu had just returned from the coal mine. Entering the room, he looked about in astonishment and confusion. We all stood up. He asked, “What is happening here? Inspector Barat—why did Surapati leap away like that?”

Barat replied, “Please, have a seat. The people who were causing trouble in your mine have been apprehended.”

Manish Babu exclaimed, “They’ve been caught?”

Byomkesh said, “Yes, indeed. This young man’s name is Bikash Dutta—he is my assistant. After consulting with Inspector Barat, I sent Bikash to the mine disguised as a hospital orderly. He caught them.”

Manish Babu asked, “Who—who are they?”

Byomkesh replied, “Surapati Ghatak and his two brothers-in-law.”

“What! Surapati!” Manish Babu collapsed into a chair. “But—Surapati! He’s worked in my office for twenty years! That he would do such a thing!”

We all sat down again. Byomkesh said, “Manish Babu, when a man marries a second time, he often falls under the sway of his wife. Surapati Babu, it seems, has fallen under the sway of his brothers-in-law. There’s not much difference.”

Manish Babu said, “But why? Why would they wish me harm?”

Byomkesh replied, “That has not yet been discovered. But it shouldn’t be difficult. I suspect the Marwari who wanted to buy your mine is pulling the strings from behind the scenes. Or perhaps it’s someone else. If Surapati Babu is pressed, the truth will come out.”

“But—do you have any direct evidence against Surapati?”

“Not yet. But the way he bolted at the mention of taking his fingerprints—his conscience is not clear.”

Manish Babu sighed and stood up. It seemed he was less astonished than deeply wounded. He said, “Please, remain seated. Phani, come with me. I must make some arrangements in the office. And about Surapati—” He looked questioningly at Barat.

Barat said, “I’ll take care of Surapati.”

Manish Babu left for the office with his son.

The four of us remained seated for a while. At last, Byomkesh said lazily, “You’ve surely issued a warrant for Bhuban?”

Barat replied, “That’s taken up my whole day.”

Byomkesh asked, “No promising news?”

Barat said, “We’ve received word from a railway station forty miles away—a driverless, numberless taxi has been found there. I’ve sent men to investigate. It may be Bhuban’s taxi; perhaps he abandoned it there and boarded a train.”

“Who knows whether he’s gone to Bombay or Madras!”

“Hm. I’ll be off now.”

“Very well, go ahead. It is your duty to catch the culprit; I know you’ll do your best. Still, if you fail to catch them, I won’t be unhappy.”

--- Page 929 ---
Kalidas the Poet Speaks 711

Inspector Birat smiled a little.

After dinner, Manish Babu had gone to retire for the night; Phanish slipped quietly into our room. Tonight, arrangements had been made for three of us to sleep here—a camp cot had been spread out for Bikash.

All three of us were present in the room, lying on our beds and smoking cigarettes; Bikash was recounting the tale of how he had nabbed those scoundrels. Seeing Phanish, Byomkesh propped himself up on his elbow against the pillow.

“Come in, Phanish.”

Phanish dragged a chair over to Byomkesh’s bedside and sat down, speaking in a tone of reproach, “You’re leaving tomorrow?”

Byomkesh replied, “Yes, considering the threats from those gentlemen, it’s best to make a quick exit. If you ever come to Calcutta with your wife, you must visit us. Satyabati would be very fond of your wife.” With that, he smiled, as if recalling some old memory.

Phanish nodded in agreement, then said slowly, “I’d like to hear the story.”

Byomkesh sat up on the bed, pulling the pillow onto his lap, and said, “You want to hear the story—the story of Pranahari? Very well, I’ll tell it; but remember, it will remain a story, not the unvarnished truth from start to finish. It will be much like a historical novel.”

Phanish raised his eyebrows in question. Byomkesh explained, “Don’t you see? Those who write historical novels don’t write history directly; they take a handful of characters and events from history and build their own tale upon that framework. The story I’m about to tell you will be much the same. I don’t know every detail, but from what I do know, I’ve pieced together the whole narrative; in this story, imagination and truth are equal partners. —Do you wish to hear it?”

Phanish said, “Please, go on.”

Byomkesh lit a fresh cigarette and began his tale— 

Let me begin with Bhuvaneshwar Das. Even after hearing his name, it never occurred to me that he wasn’t a Bengali, but an Oriya. Those who live at the confluence of Bengal and Orissa can speak both languages fluently; it’s impossible to tell whether they are Bengali or Oriya. Had I realized this, the problem would have been solved much earlier. For Mohini was a girl from Orissa. Two and two make four.

Mohini was Bhuvaneshwar’s wife. They belonged to that class of people who earn their living through hard labor, man and woman alike. Bhuvan worked at a motor repair shop in Cuttack. Mohini served as a maid in a Bengali household. And the two of them loved each other with all their hearts. This love is the main thread of our story.

Bhuvan had high ambitions; he did not like Mohini’s life as a domestic servant. Working in the motor shop, he managed to secure a job as a truck driver in the military, and left; he told Mohini—he would earn money, buy a taxi, and she would never have to work again.

For two years, there was no sign of Bhuvan. In the meantime, Mohini found employment in the house of Pranahari Poddar in Cuttack; she worked there during the day, and at night returned to her parents.

Pranahari was a man possessed by money! As miserly as he was greedy. He had grown old chasing money all his life, amassing a fortune through trickery, fraud, and blackmail, yet his hunger for wealth was never sated. He had no lust for women, or perhaps, in his old age, that desire had—

--- Page 930 ---
Time had passed. But when Mohini came to work at his house, seeing her sparked a wicked idea in Pranahari’s mind—he saw a new avenue for making money. The wayward sons of wealthy men came to his house to gamble; if he could parade a woman like Mohini before their eyes—

Mohini’s body possessed a fierce sexual allure, and seeing this, Pranahari formed a mistaken impression of her character. He began to fleece the rich young men, dropping Mohini’s name as bait. But Mohini would not allow herself to be touched. For some time, things went on like this, but the rich youths grew restless—having poured in their money, why should they give up? They began to plot to beat Pranahari.

Pranahari realized that if he didn’t get out of Cuttack soon, he would be thrashed. But he needed Mohini too—where else would he find such a tempting lure? He proposed to Mohini that she come away with him. Mohini had no objection; her husband was abroad, she had to earn her living as a maidservant, and to her, Cuttack was the same as any other place. She agreed to go with Pranahari for one and a half times her usual wage.

But before they could leave Cuttack, Bhuban returned. Bhuban had saved some money from his job, but it was not enough to buy a taxi. Husband and wife consulted together, and then Bhuban went to Pranahari.

Bhuban spoke to Pranahari about the money; he had some, but if he could get another two and a half thousand rupees, he could buy his own taxi. Pranahari thought it over—if he lent the money, both Bhuban and Mohini would be in his grasp; Mohini would have to obey his orders. He agreed. A registered deed was drawn up, with terms for repayment—the fifteen rupees of Mohini’s wages could be deducted, Bhuban would pay thirty-five rupees a month from his taxi earnings, and Pranahari would pay twenty-five rupees for using the taxi as he needed; thus, seventy-five rupees would be repaid each month.

Everyone was pleased. Bhuban bought the taxi. The three of them came to the coal town. Then, after getting the lay of the land, Pranahari began his usual games.

The Coal Club was the haunt of the wealthy, and Pranahari cast his line there. Four big fish—ruhi and katla—rose to his bait. He brought them home.

During the gambling sessions, everyone saw Mohini as well. One man, in particular, took special notice of her—Aravind Halder, a debauched libertine, who became maddened with desire. Pranahari was exploiting all four men in the games, but he squeezed Aravind Halder the hardest. He made it clear to Aravind that one could not eat the grass by jumping over the horse.

Having received permission from Pranahari, Aravind Halder began to visit Mohini at all hours. But Mohini was a strong woman—she was not what she appeared at first glance. She understood Aravind’s intentions, but did not drive him away with harsh words. She spoke to him cordially, perhaps even joined in his jokes and laughter, but she never accepted his gifts. Pranahari had probably signaled something to Mohini; Mohini complied as much as one could by hints alone. Pranahari was a sly old bird—he never said anything to Mohini outright; he thought hints would suffice. After all, Mohini was a woman of the lower classes.

After some time and effort, Aravind realized this was a hard fortress to breach. Meanwhile, they had lost a lot of money at cards. Then one day, Pranahari’s cheating was exposed. The gambling stopped.

It was natural for all those who had lost money to be angry, but Aravind was the angriest of all. For he had not only been cheated at cards—he had been cheated in another matter as well. Cheated, and—

--- Page 931 ---
Kahen K Bikali Das 713

He had been humiliated. That is why, one day, he set out with his three companions to thrash Pranahari.

By a twist of fate, the taxi they took to go beat up Pranahari belonged to Bhuban Das. On the way, as they rode in the taxi, Aravind must have let slip the shadow that Mohini cast upon his heart. Listening to his words, Bhuban realized that Pranahari had sold his wife for two thousand rupees.

Bhuban had no home in the coal town; nor did Pranahari allow Bhuban to stay at his house. Yet I believe that whenever Bhuban found the chance, he would slip away in secret to spend the night with Mohini. Husband and wife would talk; perhaps Mohini had signaled to her husband—the old man is not a good person. Bhuban, in his heart, despised Pranahari. The love between debtor and moneylender is a rare thing indeed. But Bhuban was a cautious man; he would say—once the debt is paid, the taxi will be entirely mine, then we’ll take the car and leave, and we’ll have nothing more to do with the old man.

Bhuban could never have imagined that Pranahari was such a devil. But when he heard that Pranahari had sold his wife for two thousand rupees, murder seized his mind. There are many wealthy lechers in this world who cast their eyes on other men’s wives; Bhuban bore them no grudge. But that old devil—he would kill him.

And the opportunity for murder fell into his hands, just like that. Near Pranahari’s house, the four passengers got out. Bhuban turned the taxi around and parked it; then he too got out. In his hand was the motor’s spanner.

Bhuban had come to Pranahari’s house two, three times a day and night; he knew there was a sweeper’s stair at the back of the house leading up. Cloaked in darkness, he slipped around to the back, climbed the stairs, and knocked at the door.

Pranahari had shut both doors and was in his own room; perhaps he had no inkling that four men had come to thrash him. But he was a wary man; on hearing the knock, he went to the bathroom. Then, when he learned it was Bhuban, he opened the door. For he had no suspicion of Bhuban.

The two of them entered the bedroom and stood face to face.

I do not know if any words passed between them. In Bhuban’s left hand was the spanner; suddenly, he raised it and struck Pranahari a blow to the head. Pranahari had no time to open his mouth; at once he fell, and died.

Bhuban then carefully opened the front door. Perhaps his plan was—if there was no sound from the front, he would go down the front stairs, leaving the back door closed. But at the front, it seems, the other four were standing at the foot of the stairs, deep in consultation. So Bhuban, leaving the front door ajar, slipped away the way he had come. He took the spanner with him. Now the situation was this: the front door was open, the back door was open. It was impossible to say which way Pranahari’s assailant had entered.

The first time, Aravind had found Pranahari’s door closed; the second time, the four of them went up and found the door open, and Pranahari Poddar had departed this world. They fled in a clatter of footsteps. Returning to the taxi, they found the driver sleeping with his head on the steering wheel. They woke the driver and returned to the city.

Meanwhile, Mohini was cooking, knowing nothing of what had transpired. The sounds of her cooking drowned out any noise from afar. When her cooking was done and she saw that the old man was not coming down to eat, she went upstairs. She found Pranahari lying dead, both the front and back doors open.

Aravind did not come to her mind. It was Bhuban she thought of. Where there is love, there is always...

--- Page 932 ---
714  
Byomkesh Samagra

Suspicion. Bhuvan had been warned by her—she had signaled to him that the old man was not to be trusted. Outwardly, Bhuvan appeared calm and collected, but within him simmered a fierce, concealed pride. He would never tolerate an insult to his wife. Mohini, that girl, was remarkably astute. Even at the sight of a corpse, she did not lose her head; she instantly determined her course of action. Whoever the murderer was, the police must not catch him. Mohini was certain the killer had entered and exited through the bathroom door. She had no doubt about it. She locked the two back doors from the inside, then sent word to the police through the truck driver. What a formidable woman—she did not overdo a single thing. She did exactly as much as was needed to mislead the police.

Mohini lied to us many times, but never unnecessarily. Bhuvan was the same. I am convinced that on the very night of the murder, at some point, Bhuvan returned and told Mohini everything, and from then on, he visited her frequently. That is why Mohini did not want to leave the house after the murder. It was absolutely necessary for her to maintain contact with Bhuvan.

At any rate, when I entered the stage, the police’s suspicious gaze had already settled on the four accused. Motive and opportunity—both were present in abundance. Either these four had conspired together to commit the murder, or one of them had done it, throwing dust in the eyes of the others. I had no reason to disagree with the police; yet, the idea of a collective murder was hard to digest. The suspects were not dacoits from central India—they were so-called civilized members of society. They would not band together to kill.

But one among them could have murdered, deceiving the other three. The question was: who? The greatest suspicion fell upon Aravind Halder. He had not only lost at gambling, but had been cheated in another matter as well—one so shameful that he could never admit it to anyone. The shame of a libertine is a strange thing; he feels it only when, after spending two thousand rupees, he fails to obtain the object of his shameless desire.

As I began my investigation, a particular question troubled me. The first thing that came to mind was—where had the murder weapon gone? Dr. Ghoshal had described the weapon in detail, but no such object was found. If someone from Aravind’s group had brought the weapon, Phanish and Bhuvan could not have failed to notice. Therefore, they had not brought it, nor had they taken it away. Then where did it come from, and where did it go?

Secondly, from Dr. Ghoshal’s statement, it was clear to me that the murderer was left-handed. Think about it—there wasn’t even a chair in Pranahari’s bedroom; it was not believable that he would be sitting at the edge of the bed with his back turned to the assailant. The attacker had struck him face-to-face, the blow landing on the right side of his head, along the parting of his hair. Therefore, the assailant was left-handed, his left hand dominant.

I set out to find which of the four accused was left-handed. At the Coal Club, I saw Mrigen Maulik playing tennis with his right hand, Madhumoy Sur and Aravind Halder dealing and playing cards with their right hands. Then, when I tossed a glass paperweight to Phanish, he caught it with his right hand as well. None of them were left-handed.

But even if not left-handed, one of them could be ambidextrous. So I could not rule them out entirely. There were no other suspects. Mohini had not committed the murder; if she had wanted to kill Pranahari, she would have poisoned him. She had no motive.

I was at a loss, unable to find a direction—when, in a single moment, everything became clear.

--- Page 933 ---
As if a bolt of lightning had flashed across a cloud-shrouded, pitch-dark night. I saw Bhuban crouched by his taxi, a jack wedged beneath the wheel, turning it with his left hand!

We all knew that on the night of the murder, Bhubaneshwar Das, the taxi driver, had been present at the scene. Yet, not once had he crossed our minds. This is what G. K. Chesterton called the Invisible Man.

The problem of the weapon was solved in an instant. Bhuban had killed Pranahari with a spanner; the murder weapon matched Dr. Ghoshal’s description exactly.

Bhuban has fled with his wife. A shrewd man—he realized I had seen through him. Where they will set up their hideout, I cannot say; there are so many places—Madras, Bombay. I hope Pramodbabu never finds Bhuban. For if he does, he certainly won’t be awarding him a gold medal.

There is nothing more to say. If I have left anything out, you can guess at it yourselves. Bhuban and Mohini will remain fugitives for life, unless they are caught. The cruel greed of Pranahari Poddar has destroyed two lives—that is the greatest tragedy in this tale.

--- Page 934 ---
The Invisible Triangle

I first heard this story from the lips of Police Inspector Ramanimohan Sanyal. Byomkesh and I had traveled to a large city in the West on confidential government business, and it was there that we became acquainted with Ramanibabu. The tangle of red tape was delaying our official work, so we too sat idly in the dak-bungalow, like men with nothing to do. Almost every evening after dusk, Ramanibabu would come to our quarters; there would be conversation, a little adda. His appearance, too, was of the Ramanimohan type—exceedingly sweet and charming. But that was merely his disguise. In truth, he was a most clever and astute officer of the police department. He was actually younger than us, not more than forty. Yet, owing to a natural affinity, whenever he visited, the adda would come alive.

It had certainly occurred to us that his frequent visits could hardly be explained by disinterested goodwill; we waited, hoping that his real purpose would reveal itself in due course.

Then, one day, he told us the story. Not quite a story, in fact, but a sequence of events from a murder case. Yet, by joining these scattered incidents, one could construct a coherent tale.

After finishing his account, Ramanibabu said, “Byomkeshbabu, I know who committed the murder, I know why it was done; but still, I cannot send the man to the gallows. There is no proof. The only way is a confession—to make the accused admit his guilt with his own mouth. You’re a man of many schemes and stratagems—can’t you devise a plan to trap him?”

Byomkesh smiled and said, “I’ll think about it.”

The story had intrigued me; perhaps it had left its mark on Byomkesh’s mind as well. That night, after Ramanibabu had departed, Byomkesh said, “With the material Ramanibabu has given, can’t you write a story?”

I replied, “I can. The material is good. If only I can weave in the psychology of the characters, it will become a real story.”

Byomkesh said, “Then write it. But there’s one condition: you cannot alter the events under the pretext of embellishing the story.”

“There’ll be no need to change anything.”

It took me two days to write the story. When it was finished, I gave it to Byomkesh. After reading it, he said, “It seems just right. Let’s have Ramanibabu read it and see what he says.”

That night, when Ramanibabu came, we gave him the story to read. After reading it, he looked at me with delighted eyes—“There you have it! The psychology blends seamlessly with the events. But—”

Below, I present the story—

--- Page 935 ---
Nengti said, “She’s my uncle’s friend’s daughter. Used to live in East Bengal. Her parents were killed in the Hindu-Muslim riots; the girl barely escaped with her life. My uncle gave her shelter, let her stay in the house. Her situation is much like mine.”

Inwardly, I offered my respects to Nengti’s uncle, Santosh Samaddar. Though I had never met him in person, I knew of him through Nengti. He was a renowned figure; his political and business exploits were well known throughout Bangladesh. We were also familiar with his family circumstances. In fact, the story I am about to recount is a family affair of Santosh Babu himself.

What I learned about the members of this family, both before and after the incident, I shall record here in brief. It was a sudden death that brought us into contact with this prosperous household. And again, it was a sudden death that drew the final curtain on the last act of this drama. I haven’t seen Nengti in many days—but let that be.

Santosh Samaddar was a wealthy businessman, closely involved in the independence movement in the political sphere. A little way south from Chowringhee, on a side street, stood his sprawling two-storied, garden-surrounded house. Yet Santosh Babu was seldom at home; he spent his days absorbed in business matters and political meetings, returning only after dusk. Even then, on Saturday evenings he was never to be found at home; after finishing his office work, he would go to the house of a kirtan singer to listen to music, and from there, on Monday mornings, he would head straight to the office. At that time, he was about forty-eight years old.

His wife, Chameli Samaddar, was two or three years younger than he. A tall, slender, nervous woman, she had been involved with the revolutionaries in her youth. After marrying Santosh Babu, she spent several years quietly fulfilling her domestic duties and had given birth to twin sons. Gradually, however, she developed obsessive tendencies; her nature grew sharp and fault-finding. Fish and meat disappeared from the household, and apart from sharing the same roof, all other relations with her husband ceased. Thus passed the last ten or twelve years.

Their twin sons were Jugalchand and Udaychand. Twenty years old, both attended college. Though twins, the brothers were utterly different in appearance and character; Jugalchand was slim and delicate-featured, while Udaychand was burly and rough-hewn. Jugalchand was calm by temperament, good at his studies, secretly wrote poetry. Uday was arrogant and unruly, quarreled with everyone, and, defying his mother’s wishes, would go to hotels to eat chicken. Sreemati Chameli could not control him, but perhaps, in her heart, she loved him a little more than his brother.

Besides these four, three others lived in the house. First, Nengti and her younger sister Chingri. Two years ago, their parents had died together of cholera, leaving Nengti and Chingri orphans. Sreemati Chameli was not their actual maternal aunt, but she had brought them into her home; they had been there ever since. I have already introduced Nengti; Chingri was three or four years younger. She was petite, but on the whole, pretty; even at her age, she had become clever and adept at household work. Owing to her aunt’s obsession with cleanliness, she spent most of her time in the scullery—Chingri ran the household. Jugalchand had nicknamed her “Kucho Chingri”—tiny shrimp.

The third person living in the house was Rabibarma. His full name was probably Rabindranath Barman, but he was better known as Rabibarma. Tall and skeletal in build; the shape of his face, the slant of his eyes, and the scantiness of his moustache and beard marked him as a native of the Tripura region.

--- Page 936 ---
756  
Byomkesh Samagra

I had my suspicions; he appeared to be around forty. He was one of Santosh Babu’s employees, the secretary responsible for all his political activities. Having no family of his own, he lived in Santosh Babu’s house, had become one of the household; when necessary, he even looked after the domestic affairs.

Into this circle of seven, the day an exquisitely beautiful young woman arrived, none saw the sly smile that flickered across the face of the god of death. Nengti had been the first to bring word of the young woman’s arrival; since then, every time he visited, he was absorbed in discussing her, describing the powerful currents of attraction and repulsion that had begun to surge within the family. Listening to him, I felt as though a calamity was gathering over Nengti’s household, but I never imagined it would take such a deadly form.

It was about six months after the young woman’s arrival. Durga Puja was over, and preparations for Kali Puja had begun. One evening, after dusk, Byomkesh sat in our baithak-khana, reading the Ramayana under the lamplight, utterly absorbed. Rajshekhar Basu Mahashoy had trimmed and polished the original Valmiki Ramayana, making it crisp and lucid; Byomkesh, in those saltless hours of idle days, was trying to devour it with his help. I lay sprawled on the takhtposh, lost in lazy, scattered thoughts. From the few pieces I’d read in the recent Sharadiya magazines, it seemed to me that Bengali writers had forgotten how to write Bengali; just as we have gained independence in the realm of the nation, so too in language—unfettered, ungoverned autocracy... The human mind today is running wild, brazenly trespassing on land, water, and sky... This morning I saw in the newspaper that a plane from Chattogram to Calcutta had crashed into the sea... a Pakistan Airlines plane—not a single soul survived, a long list of the dead has been published... We have become skyfarers, our feet no longer touch the earth... Poet Satyen Dutta wrote about airplanes, ‘udgat-pakha jādrel pipīlikā’—‘a fierce-winged ant’—a most striking metaphor.

“Do you know the name of Parvati’s brother?”

I sat up on the takhtposh. Byomkesh had set aside the Ramayana and was lighting a cigarette. I said, “Parvati’s brother? Which Parvati?”

Byomkesh exhaled smoke and said, “Mahadev’s Parvati, Parvati the daughter of the Himalayas.”

“Oh, I see. Did Parvati have a brother?”

“She did.” Byomkesh raised his forefinger and began to speak in a lecturing tone, “His name was Mainak Parvat. In those days, mountains had wings, they would fly about, settling wherever they pleased atop cities and settlements. You can imagine the state of those cities and settlements. Seeing this, Indra, king of the gods, grew angry, took up his thunderbolt. Wherever there were mountains on earth, he burned off all their wings with his thunder. Only Himalaya’s son Mainak escaped; he fled to Setubandh Rameshwar and hid beneath the sea. Since then, Mainak lies under the ocean, sometimes poking out his nose, then diving back down. Rather like a fugitive criminal.”

I asked, “Indra was such a great god, yet he couldn’t catch Mainak?”

Byomkesh replied, “Indra was indeed king of the gods, but he was not a satyanweshi. Besides, he was a notorious drunkard and libertine.”

I was pondering how, despite being a notorious drunkard and libertine, Indra became king of the gods, when suddenly, from the next room, the telephone rang out with a bright, tinkling sound. It had been ages since I’d heard such a sweet noise; my heart instantly leapt with excitement. Surely, someone was in trouble—

--- Page 937 ---
MAGNA MOINA K  
757

Byomkesh had been sought out for refuge. Before Byomkesh could rise from his chair, I leapt forward and picked up the telephone. It would not be right to keep a desperate supplicant waiting.

Hearing Nengti’s voice on the line, I was momentarily taken aback; but after listening to her message, I felt invigorated once more. Nengti said, “Ajit-babu, please bring Byomkesh-da quickly. Hena Mallik is dead.”

Hena Mallik—the exquisitely beautiful young woman. Excited, I exclaimed, “Dead? What happened?”

Nengti replied, “She fell from the roof on the third floor and died. The police have arrived. Mesomoshai isn’t home—it’s Saturday—please come at once.”

Byomkesh came over and took the telephone from my hand. “Who is this, Nengti? What’s happened?”

He listened for a while, then said, “Alright—we’ll see—” and hung up.

We returned to the baithak-khana. The clock showed it was seven thirty-five. Byomkesh, brows furrowed, began to pace the room. I watched him for a moment and then asked, “Are you wondering whether to go?”

He said, “I’m wondering whether we ought to go. The master of the house hasn’t called us; perhaps it’s nothing but an accident. In that case, is it proper to go just because Nengti has summoned us?”

I considered and said, “The master isn’t home. The others are just youngsters. The police have arrived. Perhaps Nengti’s aunt asked her to send word to us. If we go as family friends in this situation, would it be so wrong?”

Byomkesh frowned a little longer, then said, “That’s true. Come, let’s go.”

We reached Santosh-babu’s house around half past eight. No one was at the gatehouse. The house itself was barely visible in the darkness; only on the outer wall, I noticed a bamboo scaffolding tied up. Perhaps repairs and whitewashing were underway before Dewali.

Upon entering, we found ourselves in a large, well-appointed hall. Four or five powerful electric bulbs overhead lit the room as bright as day. In the center stood a low, round table, surrounded by several chairs and sofas. As we entered, we saw eight or ten men present, most of them in police uniform.

No one noticed our arrival. An inspector sat at the table, bent over, writing something in a diary, while the others stood around him, all eyes fixed on the inspector. Excluding the police, only four others were present; among them, I recognized Nengti. Of the remaining three, one was obviously the secretary, Ravi Verma—his Mongolian features made that clear. The other two were young men, so they must be Yugalchand and Udaychand. Both had the stunned, limp look of those still numb from shock, the true reaction yet to set in.

We stood by the doorway. Byomkesh cast his gaze once around the room. On the left, there was no furniture, only a telephone atop a tall side table in the far corner; in the center, several men gathered around the round table; to the right, near the wall, a white sheet-covered form lay on the floor—a body. Beyond that, on the lowest step of the staircase leading to the upper floor, two women sat pressed close together—surely Sreemati Chameli and Chingri. Their eyes were filled with unmitigated terror; they did not look at the shrouded corpse, but stared fixedly at the group of people in the middle of the room.

Byomkesh, too, took in everything at a glance and moved forward in that direction, coming to stand before the table.

--- Page 938 ---
Byomkesh suddenly exclaimed, “Aré! Who is this?”

The Inspector looked up from his diary; the others turned their heads to see. Closing his diary, the Inspector came forward to stand before Byomkesh, extending his hand as he said, “Byomkesh! Where have you come from?”

Byomkesh shook his hand, but did not answer the question; instead, he introduced me. I learned that his name was Atulkṛiṣṇa Ray, known in short as A. K. Ray. He had been Byomkesh’s classmate in college, now stationed in Calcutta. Though I had not met him before, he and Byomkesh saw each other from time to time. Later I would come to know, he was a jovial man, but during work he was grave and sparing of words.

Byomkesh asked, “What’s the matter? I heard a girl has died?”

“Yes.” After a moment’s thoughtful silence, A. K. Ray said, “Come, I’ll tell you.”

We stepped a little away from the group, and A. K. Ray recounted the incident in brief. He was now the officer-in-charge of the local thana. At ten minutes to seven this evening, he received a telephone call reporting an accidental death at Santosh Babu’s house; the call had come from the secretary, Ravi Verma. A. K. Ray had arrived at once with his men. On the western side of the house, where the fence had not yet been built, right near the foundation, the body of a young woman had been found. A. K. Ray had brought the police doctor along; after examination, the doctor declared that the cause of death was a broken neck from a fall from a height, the time of death estimated at about an hour before, that is, around six-thirty. A. K. Ray then went up to the open terrace on the third floor and found, in the middle of the roof, a small mat laid out, beside it a pair of feminine slippers. On inquiry, he learned that the girl would come up to the terrace every day at sunset and sit there. There was no doubt she had gone up today as well, and had fallen from the terrace to her death.

Finishing his account, A. K. Ray asked again, “But who informed you?”

Byomkesh pointed towards Nengti and said, “That boy. His name is Nengti Dutta. He comes to see me from time to time. I suppose he panicked and called me.”

Nengti was standing a little distance away, looking in our direction. A. K. Ray observed him intently for a moment and said, “Hmm. So, what do you want to do now?”

Byomkesh replied, “What can I do? I’ve come merely as a family friend, not as a satyanweshi. What do you think? Accidental death?”

A. K. Ray said, “It appears to be an accident. However—” He left the sentence unfinished, a hint of a smile flickering in his eyes.

Byomkesh nodded. “Have you taken everyone’s statements?”

A. K. Ray replied, “Yes! Only the master of the house is yet to be found. No one seems to know where he is. I hear he’s never home on weekends.” Again, that smile glimmered in his eyes.

Byomkesh said, “When you’ve prepared copies of the statements, will you give me one?”

A. K. Ray replied, “Certainly. You’ll have it by tomorrow afternoon. Do you want to see the body?”

Byomkesh said, “I can have a look. Why not?”

A. K. Ray led us to where the shrouded corpse lay, lifted the edge of the sheet and drew it aside. In the harsh, brilliant light, I saw the dead Hena Mallik.

She had indeed been beautiful, Nengti had not lied. Her complexion was like milk tinged with alta, her hair thick and jet black—

--- Page 939 ---
Disheveled as she was, it only seemed to lend her face a more intimate charm, as if her brows had been painted with a delicate brush. Her eyes were half-closed, the deep blue irises barely visible, her body brimming with the bold exuberance of youth. Death had stolen only her soul, leaving not a single mark upon her body, unable to rob even a fraction of her youthful beauty. The thought that this body would, within two days, be reduced to ashes was almost unbearable.

We gazed, spellbound, when suddenly a sound behind us made us turn our heads. Jugal and Uday had come to stand behind us. Uday’s eyes were bloodshot, both fists clenched. He turned to Jugal and roared, “Jugal, you killed Hena.”

Jugal lifted his fiery eyes to Uday and replied in a thin, hard voice, “I—killed Hena! Liar! You killed her.”

Had there been a moment’s delay, I believe a silent, deadly battle would have erupted between the two brothers. But the two women sitting on the stairs did not allow it. Sreemati Chameli and Chingri rushed forward together and stood between them. Chameli, pressing her hands against Uday’s chest, pushed him back, her sharp, broken voice ringing out, “Wretched boy! What are you saying? Go away from here, go to your own room. No one killed Hena, she fell from the roof and died herself.”

Meanwhile, Chingri clutched Jugal’s hand and pleaded in a hurried, anxious tone, “Dada, I beg you, come away, don’t stay here. Come to your bedroom—please!”

The quarrel was quelled, but neither of the two left the room; with bloodshot eyes, they continued to glare at each other.

Neither Byomkesh nor the police made any attempt to intervene in this sudden outburst. They stood by, entirely detached, watching. From their demeanor, it seemed they were waiting to see if, in the heat of the quarrel, some hidden truth might be revealed. But when the fight broke off midway, the inspector went to Uday and asked, “Just now you accused your brother of killing Hena. Is there any basis for this accusation?”

Uday did not answer, remaining stubbornly silent. Sreemati Chameli shot a piercing glance at the inspector, but before he could say anything, the atmosphere in the room seemed to change as if by magic.

A middle-aged gentleman had appeared at the main door. Of medium height, somewhat heavyset but not fat; his face lacked softness, but there was a firmness to it. His attire was a touch elegant—a collared panjabi and a pleated dhuti, beneath which peeked white leather Vidyasagari slippers. I had seen his photograph countless times in the newspapers; thus, I had no trouble recognizing Santosh Samaddar. Yet what I had never gleaned from those pictures I now sensed: the man possessed a powerful presence. Wherever he was, he was the center; no one else could command attention.

At the sight of him, Sreemati Chameli made no comment, but hurried up the stairs. A moment later, Uday too went upstairs. The rest of us remained as we were.

When I saw Santosh Babu, he was standing by the door, his unwavering gaze fixed on the body lying on the floor. Without looking at anyone, he walked over to the dead woman’s feet. As he stared, the muscles in his face grew taut, his eyes clouded with mist, then cleared again. Without addressing anyone, he spoke slowly, “So, father, mother, daughter—all lost to untimely deaths! What a strange fate.”

--- Page 940 ---
No one had expected that he would be overwhelmed with grief at the death of his dependent friend’s daughter, yet I was not prepared for this unyielding composure either; it struck me as a little too dry and hard. Nevertheless, he lifted his gaze from the corpse and turned his eyes to each of us in turn.

Byomkesh, a bit awkwardly, cleared his throat and said, “You might call us uninvited guests. My name is Byomkesh Bakshi, and this is my friend Ajit Bandyopadhyay. You do not know us, but Nengti—”

Santoshbabu said, “I do not know you, but I know your names. Did Nengti bring you here?” He turned his eyes to Nengti.

Nengti, who had been standing behind, replied in a nervous voice, “I—Masima was very frightened—”

“You did well to inform Byomkeshbabu. In times of trouble, one always thinks of a friend first.” There was a hint of warmth in his tone as he turned to Byomkesh and said, “Nengti is your friend, I suppose?”

Byomkesh replied, “You could say so.”

Santoshbabu said, “Very good, very good.” Then, turning to A. K. Ray, he asked, “Is your work done?”

A. K. Ray replied, “All our work is finished; as soon as the body is dispatched, we’ll be leaving.”

Perhaps the thought had not occurred to Santoshbabu; he paused and said, “That’s right. There must be a post-mortem.” He glanced briefly at the corpse and said, “I have nothing to say. Do what you must.”

He started towards the stairs, but A. K. Ray called after him, “If you don’t mind, I’d like to ask you a few questions.”

Santoshbabu stopped and said, “Why should I mind? Please, be seated. I’ll be back in a moment. Robi, show them to the dining room. And Chingri, arrange for tea and refreshments for them.”

He went upstairs. Robi Varma came forward and said, “Please come with me.”

A. K. Ray stationed an officer by the body and followed Robi Varma; we went along with him.

For the reader’s convenience, a plan of the house is provided here.

Santoshbabu’s dining room was quite large; a long table could seat twelve or fourteen people at once. We took our seats on the chairs. I noticed that Jugalchand, Nengti, and Chingri had not come with us. Robi Varma did not sit down; he remained standing by the door, awaiting his master’s return.

Byomkesh had taken the chair next to A. K. Ray and asked, “Have you seen Hena Mallik’s room?”

A. K. Ray replied, “I’ve seen it, more or less. Just a very ordinary bedroom. Not much furniture either.”

Byomkesh asked, “Any letters or papers?”

A. K. Ray replied, “I haven’t had a proper look yet. I’ll check again before I leave. Would you like to see it?”

“I would.”

At that moment, the officer who had been guarding the body came over and whispered into A. K. Ray’s ear—

--- Page 941 ---
Magamaenak  
761  
Pantry, kitchen, servants’ quarters  
Telephone  
Hall  
Staircase  
Dining room  
Bathroom  
Corpse  
Ravi Varma  
Rohan  

In a low voice, he said, “The van has arrived, shall we send the body off?”  
A. K. Ray replied, “Go ahead.”  
The officer left. We sat in silence. Ravi Varma stood by the open door, gazing unblinking toward the hall. Through his eyes, it was as if we ourselves witnessed the removal of the corpse. For a fleeting moment, a hungry, unsated longing flickered in his Mongolian eyes, only to vanish at once. That brief glance revealed that Secretary Ravi Varma’s feelings toward Hena were not entirely devoid of attachment.

Afterwards, Santosh Babu entered and seated himself at the head of the table. He had shed his elegant attire for plain, everyday clothes. Sitting down, he said, “Ravi, bring some cigarettes.”  
Ravi Varma hurried off to fetch them. Santosh Babu looked at A. K. Ray and said, “Perhaps you wish to ask me about Hena? Regrettably, I don’t know much about her. I did give the girl shelter, but never really had the chance to know her well. For one, I’m rarely at home, and besides, Hena was not a particularly sociable girl. In any case—”  
Ravi Varma returned with a tin of cigarettes and a box of matches, placing them before Santosh Babu. He opened the tin and offered it to us. “Please.”  
As Byomkesh reached for a cigarette, he smiled slightly and said, “I’d heard smoking was forbidden in this house.”  
Santosh Babu frowned ever so slightly and replied, “Not for you gentlemen.” He took a cigarette himself, struck a match, and extended it toward us.  
“Now, ask whatever questions you wish.”

--- Page 942 ---
762  
Byomkesh Samagra

A.K. Ray signaled to the writer-jamadar, who produced a notebook and pencil. The questioning then began.

Question: What is Hena’s father’s name?  
Answer: Kamal Mallik.  
Question: Was Kamal Mallik your friend?  
Answer: Yes. I had known him for nearly fifteen years. Because of my business, I have had to travel all over India, and I still do. I first met Kamal Mallik in Dhaka, and gradually we became close.  
Question: So you saw Hena even before she came to Calcutta?  
Answer: Many times. I have known her since she was three or four years old.  
Question: Did taking her in create any disturbance in your household?  
Santoshbabu paused for a moment, then said, “My wife was displeased. She is a stickler for cleanliness; Hena is a girl from Pakistan, she has no sense of propriety—using that as an excuse, my wife refused to let Hena eat from our kitchen. There’s a hotel nearby, so I arranged for Hena’s meals to be brought from there.”  
Question: No one else objected?  
Answer: No one else would dare object.  
Question: Did Hena have any interaction with anyone in the house?  
Answer: There was no restriction on interaction. But Hena was not a sociable girl, and perhaps she had not yet recovered from the shock of her parents’ deaths. So she kept to herself, rarely leaving her room.  
Question: Did you know she used to go up to the terrace on the third floor every evening?  
Answer: I didn’t know before; I learned of it today.  
Question: From whom did you learn this?  
Answer: From the person who telephoned me with the news of her death.  
Question: Who informed you of her death?  
Santoshbabu rested his hand on his cheek for a moment, then looked up and said, “That’s just it—I didn’t notice who it was. No one even knows the address of where I was staying.” Suddenly, he turned a sharp gaze toward Robi Verma and said, “Robi.”  
Robi Verma replied in a deep voice, “No, sir, I didn’t make the call.”  
We exchanged glances. A.K. Ray asked, “Didn’t you recognize the voice on the telephone?”  
Santoshbabu said, “After receiving the news, no other question even occurred to me. But—”  
A.K. Ray now asked the inevitable question, “Where were you?”  
A faint flush crept into Santoshbabu’s face. He looked at each of us in turn and said, “Is it absolutely necessary to know that?”  
A.K. Ray seemed a little uncomfortable; it was evident from his demeanor. He said awkwardly, “Forgive me, but I am still not entirely certain about the circumstances of Hena Mallik’s death. It is quite possible that she accidentally fell from the terrace, but we cannot entirely rule out the possibility that someone pushed her. That is why we need to know everything.”  
Santoshbabu raised his brows and looked at A.K. Ray for a while, then said, “Hena—”

--- Page 943 ---
MAGNA MOINA K 763

“Is it possible that someone pushed him?”  
A.K. Ray replied, “Yes, it is.”  
Santosh Babu raised his voice slightly, “But who would kill him? And why?”  
A.K. Ray shook his head, “That I do not know yet. But we must investigate every possibility.”  
Santosh Babu sat for a while, his hand on his cheek, then suddenly straightened up; surveying us all with a stern gaze, he said in a hard tone, “Very well, let me tell you where I was. But this is a secret of my life—let there be no talk about it.”  
“There will be no talk. Whatever you say will remain off the record.” A.K. Ray gestured to the other police officers; they rose and went into the hall, and the writer-jamadar closed his notebook and left as well. Byomkesh made as if to rise, saying, “Then perhaps we too should go to the next room.”  
Santosh Babu raised his hand and said firmly, “No, you stay. In fact, it is good that you are here—I now appoint you to safeguard my family’s interests.”  
Byomkesh sat down again. Santosh Babu lit another cigarette and drew on it gently, while we waited in silence.  
From near the prawn door, Chingri craned his neck and asked, “Shall I bring in the tea?”  
Santosh Babu said, “Yes, bring it.”  
Chingri entered the room, followed by two servants carrying trays of food and tea. As Chingri set the cups and plates before us, he cast a wide-eyed glance at Byomkesh. He must have heard of Byomkesh from Nengti. In his gaze there was not only curiosity, but something else—something harder to define. Perhaps, inwardly, he was afraid.  
Santosh Babu said, “Serve those outside as well.”  
Chingri left with the servants for the hall, and Ravi Varma quietly closed the door behind them. We turned our attention to the refreshments. Santosh Babu took only a single cup of tea, and after a tentative sip, began to speak without looking at us:  
“I am not a man of spotless character, but for that I blame no one but myself. Among my many flaws, one is that I am fond of listening to kirtan.”  
We looked up at him. In politics, Santosh Babu was a renowned orator; there was no doubt that he would relate his secret with a moving gravity. Indeed, the variety of his preamble drew our undivided attention.  
After another sip of tea, he took a long drag on his cigarette, then, dropping the burnt stub into the cup and pushing it aside, he began:  
“You may have heard of the kirtan-singer Sukumari. Singing is her profession—she performs at gatherings for money. Ten years ago, I heard her sing and was enchanted. My married life was not happy, and I became attracted to Sukumari. She was then twenty-two or twenty-three. For some time, I visited her house in secret, and we became close. But all sorts of people came to her house—some to listen, some to book her for performances. I realized it was not safe for me to keep going there.  
“You know that my life is closely entwined with politics. In the struggle for independence—”

--- Page 944 ---
764  
Byomkesh Samagra

I have fought, I have gone to jail, I have been beaten by police batons, I have given countless sums to revolutionaries, and during the Partition, I have acted as a messenger between both sides. In the realm of politics, I have a reputation, I have influence. And naturally, I have enemies as well. If my adversaries ever get the chance to spread scandal in my name, my fame and position will be ruined. After much deliberation, I took a certain step: I rented a small house under an assumed name. My intention was to take Sukumari there, to end her public life as a singer. But Sukumari would not agree to this. In the end, it was decided that she would remain in her own home and continue her music business, but twice a week—on Saturdays and Sundays—she would come and stay at my rented secret house. I would come and go in such a way that no one would know.

For the past ten years, this arrangement has continued. On Saturday afternoons, after finishing my office work, I go there, and then on Monday morning, I go straight from there to the office. That is what happened today as well; around half-past three, I arrived there. Then—at eight in the evening, I received a telephone call and left immediately.' A dry sarcasm flickered across his face. 'That is my alibi.'

Digesting the sting of his irony, A.K.R. said in a gentle tone, 'Thank you. Pardon my impertinence, but if I may ask just a couple more questions, I will release you. Is there any servant or attendant at the rented house?'

Santoshbabu replied, 'No, I have deliberately kept no servant. Every Saturday at noon, Sukumari comes from her own house to the rented one and tidies up the rooms. I arrive in the afternoon. Then, after I leave for the office on Monday, she locks up and returns to her own home. The house remains closed for the rest of the week.'

Question: Why have you kept a telephone there?

Answer: Not for myself, but for Sukumari. When she is at the rented house, she wants to keep in touch with her own home. But it is a private number; you will not find it in the directory.

Question: Did you tell the number to your secretary?

Answer: No.

Question: Is it possible that anyone else knows?

Answer: No one could possibly know. I have told no one, and Sukumari will not tell anyone either.

Question: Do you trust her?

Answer: I do. I give her a thousand rupees a month. She is no fool; she would not cut off her own feet.

Question: When you received the telephone call today, what were you doing?

Answer: I was listening to kirtan. Sukumari was singing the verses of Chandidas.

A.K.R. turned his eyes to Byomkesh; Byomkesh shook his head silently, meaning, there were no further questions. Then A.K.R. rose and said, 'That will be all for today. Forgive us for troubling you. Will you be—going back again tonight?'

'No, I will not return; I shall stay at home.' A glint of amusement played in Santoshbabu’s grave eyes as he said, 'You will not find my house by setting a spy after me.'

A.K.R. clicked his tongue and said, 'No, no, nothing of the sort! I have not the slightest curiosity about your secret residence. What you have told us is sufficient for our investigation. Only—if we could meet Srimati Sukumari once, that would be helpful.'

'You will find her at her own address.' Santoshbabu handed over Sukumari’s address and stood up. 'It is ten o’clock. Your work is probably not yet finished; stay as long as you need.'

--- Page 945 ---
M G MOINAK  
765

“Byomkesh-babu, will you stay with the Inspector on my behalf?”

“Certainly,” Byomkesh replied, rising to his feet.

Santosh-babu said, “Well then, I’ll go and take some rest. I’m feeling a bit fatigued.”

He strode out of the room with firm steps. There was no sign of weariness in his body. Perhaps it was fatigue of the mind. After such a calamity in the house—Santosh-babu had revealed his secrets with neither evasion nor subterfuge, made no effort to justify himself. When one must speak the profound truths of life, it is best to speak plainly. Yet, his ruthless honesty could not help but trouble me. He was a seasoned businessman and a shrewd politician; perhaps his character would have been better without that dark stain.

A.K. Ray asked Byomkesh, “And now?”

Byomkesh said, “Come, let’s take a look at Hena’s room.”

“Let’s go. Shall we go up to the roof as well?”

“We shall. Since we’re here, let’s see all that needs to be seen.”

Near the round table in the hall, the remaining police officers were conversing in low voices. Except for Ravi Verma, none of the household members were present. Hena’s room lay diagonally across from the dining room, at the far end of the hall. [See plan.] The door to Hena’s room was slightly ajar, a light burning within. The three of us entered. Ravi Verma followed close behind.

The room was quite large. On the main road side, a large arched window; on the east wall, another ordinary window. In front of this window stood a table and chair, beside them a bookshelf. On the other side of the room, a narrow, simple cot was made up; beneath it, two large suitcases were visible. In the corner of the north wall, a narrow door led to an adjoining bathroom. The room was free of clutter, and so appeared rather neat. Perhaps Hena herself was of a tidy disposition.

Standing in the center of the room, Byomkesh looked around and asked, “Was the door open?”

A.K. Ray replied, “No, it was locked. In the deceased’s hand was a leather handbag, and inside it we found a key ring. Here it is.” He produced a bunch of keys from his pocket.

Taking the keys in hand, Byomkesh said, “So Hena locked her room before going up to the roof.”

A.K. Ray said, “That’s how it appears.”

Ravi Verma made a sound like a cough, his fist before his mouth. When Byomkesh turned to him, he said, “Hena never stepped out of her room without locking the door. Never left it open, not even for a moment.”

Byomkesh asked, “Is that so? Was it always this way, or did something prompt it?”

“Always this way.”

Byomkesh said nothing further. Examining the key ring, he said, “I see there are five keys. One must be for the door lock. And the others?”

A.K. Ray replied, “Two of the others are for the suitcases. The remaining two—we haven’t identified what they open.”

Byomkesh examined each key in turn and said, “One of them has a number engraved on it—”

--- Page 946 ---
766  
Byomkesh Samagra

remains—number 7. See if this key fits anywhere.”  
A.K.R. examined the key and said, “No. Of the two locks whose keys are missing, this is one.”  
“There aren’t any padlocks on the table drawers?”  
“There are. But all the drawers are open. The keys are missing.”  
“Hm. What do you think?”  
The two men exchanged glances for a moment. Finally, A.K.R. said, “Hard to say. Often the lock goes missing, but the key remains on the ring.”  
Byomkesh looked at Rabivarma and asked, “Can you say anything?”  
Rabivarma shook his head. “I know nothing about this room. This is the first time I’ve entered it.”  
Byomkesh made a sound in his throat, handed the bunch of keys back to A.K.R., and went to stand before the table.  

On one side stood a table with drawers, covered in red baize, with nothing atop it but a couple of books. Then his eyes fell upon a single red rose lying on the red baize.  
There was no vase in the room, and the rose had been left there so carelessly, it seemed strange.  
Byomkesh did not touch the flower. He leaned forward to examine it closely, then raised his eyes to the open window at the head of the table and said, “Fresh flower. Are there roses in the garden?” The view beyond the window was lost in darkness.  
Rabivarma replied, “Yes.”  
Byomkesh turned to A.K.R. and asked, “What do you think about the rose? Why is it lying on the table like this?”  
A.K.R. silently pointed outside the window.  
Byomkesh nodded in agreement. “That’s what I think too. When Hena was not in the room, someone plucked the rose from the garden and tossed it onto the table through the bars of the window.”  
All our eyes turned to Rabivarma, the same question in each gaze—who could have thrown it?  
But Rabivarma avoided our questioning eyes, glancing here and there, and finally said, “I don’t know anything.”  
Byomkesh sighed and began opening the drawers one by one. I went to stand before the bookshelf.  

Two rows of books. In the first row: Rabindranath’s *Sanchayita*, Satyen Dutta’s *Kabyasanchayan*, Nazrul’s *Sanchita*, and a few novels by modern writers. The second row held many cheap editions of English novels. Hena read foreign mysteries and thrillers too.  
“Ajit, look.”  
I turned to see Byomkesh had taken a photograph from a drawer and was staring at it intently. Mounted on cardboard, postcard-sized—a portrait of a woman. I glanced at it and exclaimed, “Hena’s photo.”  
Byomkesh shook his head. “No. The photo is several years old, see how yellowed it is, yet the woman can’t be less than twenty-five. It can’t be Hena, perhaps Hena’s mother. Now I see where Hena got her beauty.”  
I had never seen Hena alive; I had guessed her beauty from her corpse. Now, looking at this photo, it felt as though I was seeing her in life. Not just beauty—an inexhaustible vitality.

--- Page 947 ---
A strange radiance seemed to emanate from every corner.

Byomkesh handed the photograph to A. K. Ray and said, “Keep this. We’ll have to ask Santosh Babu whether this is a picture of Hena’s mother.”

A. K. Ray took the photograph, running his eyes over it, while Rabibarma craned his neck to get a look. The man’s face betrayed nothing, but there was a keen curiosity burning within him.

A. K. Ray slipped the photo into his pocket and asked, “Did you find anything else in the drawer?”

“No. Just a few coins—nothing of note. Rabibabu, do you know if Hena ever received any letters?”

Rabibarma replied, “I’m not at home when the post arrives. Nengti or Chingri might know.”

Without another word, Byomkesh approached the bookshelf. He glanced over the spines, then took down the volume of Sanchayita. As soon as he opened the cover, a piece of pink paper, folded neatly, came into view. Four lines of handwriting adorned the page. Byomkesh held the slip between two fingers, examining it, while Rabibarma, like a crane, stretched his neck to see.

But Byomkesh did not let him read the note; he swiftly slipped the paper into his pocket. Rabibarma’s expression did not change, but it was clear that his soul was restless with longing to read those words.

Byomkesh began opening the other books one by one, while A. K. Ray and I stood on either side, watching his every move. Since he could not see what we were doing behind him, Rabibarma grew increasingly agitated with curiosity. What was the source of such intense interest?

Nothing else was found among the Bengali books on the upper shelf. On the first page of each, in a neat, feminine hand, was written—Hena Mallik.

There were no papers in the English books on the lower shelf either, but Byomkesh drew our attention to one detail. On the title pages of several books, a rubber stamp bore the name of a bookseller from Dhaka. A. K. Ray raised his brows at Byomkesh, and I did the same. But Byomkesh said nothing; perhaps Rabibarma’s presence restrained him.

When the inspection of the books was done, Byomkesh said, “Let’s see what’s in the suitcases. Open them, will you?”

A. K. Ray took out a bunch of keys and unlocked both suitcases. Inside, various types of women’s clothing were neatly arranged in layers. Saris, skirts, ghagras, dupattas, kameezes, pyjamas—garments of every kind. All of them expensive. Byomkesh sifted through them, turning them over, then sighed and said, “No, nothing useful here. You’ve checked the bathroom, haven’t you?”

A. K. Ray replied, “Yes. Nothing noteworthy.”

“I’ll take a look myself.” Byomkesh entered the bathroom. Two or three minutes later, he returned and said, “Come, let’s go up to the roof.”

A few steps from the door, the staircase began—a broad, elegant flight of stairs. Byomkesh turned on the bottom step and said, “Rabibabu, you needn’t come with us. We can inspect the roof ourselves.” There was such firmness in his tone that Rabibarma did not protest, remaining at the foot of the stairs. We ascended.

--- Page 948 ---
The staircase, brushing past the first floor, wound its way up to the second. As we rounded the bend, I took in the second floor at a glance. Above the hall below was another hall, identical in every respect, and at the front corners, two rooms. The only difference was that, unlike the ground floor, there were no doors on the back wall; instead, on the second floor, three doors stood in a row. In other words, above the kitchen, pantry, and such below, were several bedrooms, their doors opening into the upper hall.

At the top of the stairs, on the third floor, stood a closed door. A.K. Ray slid back the latch and opened it, then pressed a switch by the doorway, flooding the roof with light. Under the glare of the floodlights, the vast expanse of the terrace was revealed.

The three of us stepped onto the roof. Byomkesh immediately examined the door and remarked, “I see the door can be locked from both sides—there’s a latch inside, a chain outside. A.K. Ray, when you came up to the roof, was the door closed?”

A.K. Ray replied, “No, it was open from both sides.”

The electric lights blazed, and by now, the waning moon of the dark fortnight had risen above the horizon. We walked to the center of the terrace.

The roof was enormous—one could easily lay out a fine tennis court up here. Around the edge ran a solid parapet, and along its top, bamboo poles jutted up from outside, except on the eastern side, where the scaffolding was missing—perhaps repairs had just been completed there. Beyond the roof, some twenty or twenty-five feet away at the edge of the garden, a row of tall silver pines stood at regular intervals, encircling the house like silent sentinels. From the terrace, their upper branches looked like the spires of a temple.

Byomkesh slowly turned his head, taking in the entire scene, then let his gaze return to the terrace itself. There was nothing else on the roof, save for a mat, laid a little to the west of center, and beside it, a pair of women’s rubber slippers.

A picture floated before my mind’s eye: Hena had come up to the terrace, spread out the mat, slipped off her slippers, and sat down. And then—?

Byomkesh approached this mute tableau and stood before it, gazing at it for a while. Then he raised his head and asked, “Where did Hena fall?”

Pointing toward the side where the scaffolding was missing, A.K. Ray replied, “This way.”

The three of us walked to the edge of the eastern parapet. The moon hung before us. Twenty-five feet away, the row of pines whispered in the gentle breeze, as if murmuring in low voices about Hena’s untimely death. If only they could speak the language of men, perhaps we would have the testimony of eyewitnesses. “She fell right there!” A.K. Ray pointed downward.

We leaned over and looked. In the shadow of the pines, nothing was clearly visible. The parapet reached up to my waist and was a foot wide. Hena was certainly shorter than I; even if she had leaned over for some reason, it seemed unlikely she would have toppled over the parapet.

Byomkesh, too, was silently taking measurements in his mind. He turned to A.K. Ray and said, “Hmm. The parapet is about four feet high. How tall was Hena?”

A.K. Ray, reading Byomkesh’s thoughts, replied, “About five feet three inches. But even so, it’s not impossible.”

“I didn’t say it was impossible.” Byomkesh slowly walked along the edge of the parapet. The stars had built a ladder from the ground to the roof; a sturdy man could easily climb up here using the scaffolding.

--- Page 949 ---
MAGNA MOINA K 769

After finishing his inspection of the roof, Byomkesh said in a slightly disappointed tone, “It’s quite late. Let’s leave it at this for tonight. —Should we seal Hena’s room?”

A.K. Ray replied, “I don’t see any need to seal it. Hena didn’t die in that room. Besides, both of us have already searched it thoroughly.”

Byomkesh said nothing more. A.K. Ray switched off the light and started down the stairs; we followed behind him.

We descended in silence. As we reached the first floor and were about to turn the corner, a muffled, sharp voice reached our ears from the side—“You will keep quiet, you will not say a word.”

I turned my head sharply and saw, at the far end of the hall on the first floor, Rabivarma and Mrs. Chameli standing face to face. Rabivarma, noticing us, seemed to silently signal Mrs. Chameli, who turned to look in our direction. Then, her eyes flashing with sharp, intolerant annoyance, she strode quickly into a room at the back.

Once downstairs, Byomkesh cast a sidelong, sardonic glance at A.K. Ray and said, “Did you hear that?”

A.K. Ray nodded slightly and said, “Come, let me drop you both home in the police van.”

The next morning, Sunday, at seven o’clock, Byomkesh and I had just sat down with our cups of tea when Nengti burst into the room with a clatter and exclaimed, “Byomkesh-da, something terrible has happened!”

Byomkesh raised an eyebrow and said, “Something terrible?”

Nengti said, “Yes. Give me a cigarette.”

Byomkesh handed him one. Lighting it, Nengti took two or three deep drags and said, “Last night, someone set fire to Hena’s room and burned it down.”

I exclaimed, “What! The house burned down?”

Nengti replied, “Not the house, only Hena’s room. The bed, bedding, table, wardrobe—nothing remains, everything has turned to ashes.”

Byomkesh remained silent for a while, then asked, “When did you all find out?”

Nengti said, “How would we know at night? We sleep upstairs. Rabivarma sleeps downstairs, but even he didn’t know anything. It was only discovered early this morning.”

“And then?”

“What else? The whole house is in an uproar. I slipped away to come and tell you.”

“Hm. Who could have set the fire—someone from the house, or an outsider?”

“How would I know? At night, all the doors and windows on the ground floor are kept shut.”

“When you saw it in the morning, were Hena’s room’s windows open?”

“The doors and windows are all burned to charcoal—there’s no way to tell if they were open or closed. But—” Nengti broke off.

Byomkesh asked, “But what?”

Nengti’s cigarette was half-smoked; carefully extinguishing the rest, he put it in his pocket and said, “There used to be a tin of petrol kept under the stairs. It’s empty now.”

“That means—” Byomkesh left his sentence unfinished, sinking into thought.

Nengti got up and said, “I’d better run. If Mashima finds out I’m not at home,

--- Page 950 ---
Byomkesh Samagra

There will be no escape.’

Byomkesh raised his head and said, ‘Sit down. I have a couple of questions for you.’

Reluctantly, Nengti sat and said, ‘What more do you want to ask? I’ve told you everything I know. Now it’s up to you to use your brains and figure out who committed the murder.’

Byomkesh said, ‘There’s still no proof that it was a murder. But let that be. Where were you at the time when Hena fell from the roof?’

Nengti replied, ‘I wasn’t in the house, I’d gone out for a smoke.’

‘How do you know that it was exactly when you went out for a smoke that Hena fell from the roof?’

‘Listen. Just before half past five, as I was leaving the house, I noticed Hena’s door was slightly ajar. I saw her sitting on the bed, knitting a woolen vest with a needle. When I came back half an hour later, the house was in an uproar—Hena’s body had just been found.’

‘Who found the body?’

‘Ravivarma.’

‘When you left, was there anyone else in the hall?’

‘Uday-da was there, no one else.’

‘When you came back after your smoke, was everyone in the house present?’

Nengti thought for a moment and said, ‘Everyone except Mesomoshai was present.’

Byomkesh was silent for a while, thinking, then said, ‘One more thing. Did Hena ever receive any letters?’

Nengti replied firmly, ‘No, she didn’t. Whenever letters arrive, morning or evening, I take them from the peon. Not a single letter has come for Hena till today.’

‘Did Hena have any contact with anyone outside?’

Nengti began to shake his head, then stopped, and looked at Byomkesh with furrowed brows.

Byomkesh said, ‘What is it?’

Nengti said, ‘I forgot to mention this, Byomkesh-da. Perhaps it seemed trivial, that’s why it slipped my mind—’

Byomkesh said, ‘Trivial or not, tell me.’

Nengti began slowly, thinking as he spoke, ‘About ten or twelve days after Hena arrived, this started happening. There isn’t much traffic on our street—hardly any cars or motors—quiet, upper-class para. One afternoon, a taxi drove slowly past our house, and inside, a man was playing a mouth organ. You know what a mouth organ is, right? Looks like a spectacle case, you rub it on your lips and it makes a loud, shrill sound—’

‘I know. Go on.’

‘The taxi went by, and two or three minutes later, it passed again from the opposite direction, the mouth organ still playing as it went by our house. Within ten or fifteen minutes of this, Hena locked her room and left. The first time, I didn’t realize the connection—’

‘Did you see the man playing the mouth organ?’

‘I did. He was wearing a coat and trousers.’

‘And then?’

‘Then, for ten or twelve days, nothing—Hena didn’t leave the house. One day, I was upstairs—’

--- Page 951 ---
Magna Mainak                                     771

I was standing by the window of the hall, gazing out onto the street, when I saw a taxi approaching. As it neared the house, a mouth-organ began to play from inside; but as soon as it passed the house, the music stopped. A little while later, the taxi returned, and once again, right in front of the house, it let out a peppy “peppo peppo” tune before driving off. I began to wonder, what’s going on—why play the mouth-organ right in front of our house? Just then, I saw Hena step out of the house and head in the same direction the taxi had gone. Suddenly, I understood—someone signals to Hena, and at once she goes out to meet them.

“When would Hena return?”

“She’d be back in about an hour.”

“Do you know where she went?”

“How would I know? Once, I tried following her. About a hundred yards from the house, the taxi was waiting by the roadside. Hena slipped into it, and the taxi drove away.”

“Hm. When was the last time Hena went out?”

“Ten or twelve days ago. —Well, Byomkesh-da, I’ll take my leave now, it’s gotten quite late. I’ll come again when I can.”

“All right, do come.”

After Nengti had left, Byomkesh sat in silence for a long while. At last, I broke the quiet and said, “What do you make of it?”

Byomkesh, distractedly lighting a cigarette, replied, “The business with the mouth-organ seems fishy, but one thing is clear—Hena was not entirely alone in Calcutta. Anyway, we can be certain about Hena’s death now; it wasn’t an accident, someone murdered her. Now the question is—who is Morgamainak?”

I said, “Surely the one who set the fire in the room.”

Byomkesh didn’t seem satisfied with that. Shaking his head, he said, “Could be, or maybe not. Think about it. Someone killed Hena, but we don’t know the motive. It could be sexual jealousy, or something else entirely. But the person who set the fire—their intention is clear: there was something dangerous in Hena’s room that they wanted to destroy. We searched the room thoroughly enough, but found nothing dangerous. If we searched again, maybe we’d find the deadly object! So, better to burn it to ashes.”

“What could this dangerous thing be?”

“Perhaps a piece of paper. If it were something bigger, we’d have found it.”

Suddenly, I remembered and said, “Byomkesh, that scrap of pink paper! What’s written on it?”

Byomkesh took the scrap out of the drawer and handed it to me. “A poem. Read it and see if it’s poetry at all.”

I read the poem—

The glimmer of your smile  
Stabbed my heart like a knife  
Your lips, so red, can punish  
The innocent, without cause.

I said, “Not bad—rather like some bizarre Sanskrit poetry. Who wrote it?”

--- Page 952 ---
Byomkesh said, “There’s only one poet in their household—Yugal.”

In the afternoon, A. K. Ray himself arrived, carrying a copy of the deposition.  
Today, his demeanor was entirely different. He joked with us, shared a few amusing stories, and proved that Byomkesh, instead of joining the police, was chasing wild buffaloes on an empty stomach. He partook of refreshments appropriate to the hour; then, at last, he turned to business. Handing the file of the deposition to Byomkesh, he said, “Here you go, have a read. But it won’t be of any use to you.”

Raising his brows, Byomkesh asked, “Why won’t it be of use?”

A. K. Ray replied, “According to the police department, Hena’s death is nothing but an accident. Further investigation is pointless.”

Byomkesh gazed at him for a moment, then said, “Have you heard about the fire?”

A. K. Ray answered, “I have. That was a coincidence. There’s no evidence that anyone set the fire deliberately.”

Byomkesh fixed him with a sharp look, then turned his gaze to the window.

At last, he said, “So, the task Santoshbabu gave me is finished. There’s no longer any need to protect his family’s interests.”

A. K. Ray laughed. “No. You can assure him the police won’t trouble his family any further. —By the way, when I heard about the fire, I went to Santoshbabu’s house. He was there. Yesterday, the photograph found in Hena’s drawer—I showed it to him. He said it was a picture of Hena’s mother.”

Byomkesh nodded. “What did the post-mortem reveal?”

A. K. Ray replied, “Nothing more than what one would expect in such a case. One of the ribs was broken and pierced the heart, causing instant death. No other complications.”

“Time of death?”

“Between five-thirty and six.”

Then, after a few more jokes, A. K. Ray clapped Byomkesh on the back and took his leave. Byomkesh sat in silence for a long while.

I asked, “Is A. K. Ray a very clever man?”

Byomkesh looked at me and said, “His intelligence is second to none.”

I said, “His only fault is being with the police.”

“Yes, his only fault is being with the police.” Byomkesh picked up the file of depositions.

Half an hour later, having finished reading, he handed the file to me and said, “There’s nothing special, but you can take a look. —I’ll step out for a bit.”

“Where are you going?”

“It’s been a while since I visited the shop. I’ll go see what Prabhat is up to.”

He left. I opened the file and began to read the depositions—

Rabindranath Barman. Age 39. One of Santosh Samaddar’s secretaries. Lives at Santoshbabu’s house. Salary: 350 rupees.  
Today is Saturday. After the master left for the office, I returned around half-past three...

