The old newspapers were always stacked in one place; at the end of the month, Putiram would sell them off. I fetched that day’s paper and handed it to Byomkesh, who eagerly flipped through the pages, searching for the account of the air disaster.

I asked, “What are you looking at?”

Without lifting his gaze from the paper, he replied, “Dakota plane. A run from Singapore to Cairo. All the officers were Muslim, except for one English pilot. Among the passengers, there were people of every nationality—”

Slowly, Byomkesh folded the paper and sat gazing vacantly at the rafters above.

After that, the burst of activity that had seized us seemed to die away as suddenly as a gust of wind. For two days, there was no sign or sound. Only Bikash called once to say they were still on the hunt. Santoshbabu and Ravi Varma continued their routine—going to the office and returning home; Jugal and Uday went to college and came back; one afternoon, Uday had gone out to play hockey. There was nothing else of note.

On the third day—Thursday—life returned to us once more, as if a dying lamp had suddenly flared up again.

That morning, Nengti arrived. There was a hint of unease in his manner. Byomkesh handed him a cigarette and asked, “Any news?”

Nengti didn’t answer immediately. Lighting the cigarette, he squinted at Byomkesh, then said, “Byomkesh-da, have you put a spy on Uday-da?”

Byomkesh raised an eyebrow. “Who told you that?”

“Uday-da said so. He claims some scrawny kid is tailing him everywhere.”

“Is Udaydi very frightened?”

“Uday-da isn’t the sort to get scared. He’s strutting about as if it’s a badge of honor. But Masima is frightened.”

Byomkesh looked up sharply. “Is that so? But why is she afraid?”

Nengti shook his head. “I don’t know. Yesterday, Uday-da was boasting to Masima—‘You know, Ma, the police detectives are after me.’ Hearing that, Masima’s face went pale. She’s always been a restless soul, but since then she’s been even more agitated. This morning she told me, ‘Go fetch Byomkesh-babu, I want to speak with him.’”

“She wants to speak with me?”

“Yes. —Byomkesh-da, have you found any clues?”

Byomkesh paused for a moment, then said, “I have found something.”

Nengti’s eyes widened. “You have?”

“Perhaps I have, but it’s not the time to speak of it yet. Come, let’s hear what your Masima has to say. Get up, Ajit.”

When we arrived at Sambhashbabu’s house, we found the hall on the ground floor abuzz with noise and commotion.

--- Page 968 ---
788  
Byomkesh Samagra

The game was in full swing. Chingri, wielding a palm-leaf fan, was chasing after Jugal, intent on striking him, while Uday, clutching Chingri’s long braid as though it were a horse’s rein, tried to rein her in, calling out, “Haat, horse—haat, haat!” Chingri protested, “Why did you undo my hair?” All three burst into peals of laughter, darting about the room, their faces alight with mischievous glee.

Our arrival brought their revelry to an abrupt halt. For a moment, the trio stood awkwardly, caught off guard; then, with a blush, Chingri fled up the stairs, while Jugal and Uday followed at a more measured pace.

Nengti showed us to our seats and went to inform Masima of our arrival. I whispered quietly to Byomkesh, “See how close they’ve become, like brothers?”

Byomkesh gave a faint, grave smile. “This is what youth is,” he replied.

Nengti soon returned and announced, “Masima is calling you upstairs.”

We ascended to the upper floor, but found the hall empty. Those who had just come up must have retreated to their respective rooms. Nengti knocked on the panel of a half-closed door. A voice called from within, “Come in.”

Nengti pushed open the door and ushered us inside.

The room was spacious for a bedroom; a double-bed occupied one side, but could not diminish the sense of space. It was here, perhaps, that Sreemati Chameli slept, with Chingri beside her. Besides the bed, there was a wardrobe, a clothes-stand, a dressing table, and two comfortable armchairs. On the wall hung a vivid painting of Ma Kali, her tongue lolling. Through two large windows, one could see a row of pines at the back of the house.

There were two women in the room. First, Sreemati Chameli: she had bathed and donned a silk sari, a rudraksha garland at her throat, a large vermilion mark like a silver coin on her brow, her face grave, her eyes burning with a strange, suppressed excitement. Second, Chingri. Her playful restlessness had vanished. She stood by the window, craning her neck, staring at us with wide, startled eyes.

Sreemati Chameli said, “Nengti, Chingri, go outside. I wish to speak to them alone.”

Chingri was reluctant to leave; she moved at a snail’s pace from the window to the door, but Nengti frowned deeply and signaled her with a nod. The two girls left the room, and Nengti pulled the door to.

Sreemati Chameli gestured to the chairs. “Please, sit down.” Her manner of speaking was clipped, as if she were weighing every word with utmost caution.

Byomkesh replied, “Please, you sit as well.”

There were only two chairs in the room; a third person would have to perch on the edge of the bed. Sreemati Chameli glanced at the bed, her lips curling slightly in distaste. “I won’t sit. My worship is not yet done. Please, you sit.”

As I took my seat, I mused: once, she had been a revolutionary, wielding firearms; surely, she had no such fastidiousness then. How circumstances change the mind.

Once we were seated, Sreemati Chameli began to speak, her words slow and deliberate, counting out each syllable. She spoke of ordinary household matters, none of which could possibly interest Byomkesh; it seemed she was afraid, and was circling around the real subject, delaying it with small talk.

After listening intently for a while, Byomkesh raised his head and said, “Please, do not be anxious. I am a friend of this family. Santoshbabu has entrusted me with the protection of everyone’s interests—”

--- Page 969 ---
...have done. If you know anything about Hena’s death, you can tell me openly.’

Shrimati Chameli hesitated for a moment, then regarded Byomkesh with a new, searching gaze and said, ‘You’re not with the police, are you?’

Byomkesh replied, ‘No, I have no connection with the police.’

‘But—but—you know the police have set detectives after my sons!’

I realized Shrimati Chameli did not know that the police had already withdrawn from this case. With her conversation with Santoshbabu at an end, who would have told her?

Byomkesh remained silent. Shrimati Chameli’s voice rose, sharp and indignant, ‘Is this not an injustice? My sons are innocent. Yet they must be shadowed by spies?’

Byomkesh answered in a calm tone, ‘Perhaps spies have been set on them precisely because people want to know if they are innocent.’

‘I have said a thousand times that my sons are innocent, yet they do not believe me!’

‘But how do you know they are innocent? Please don’t take offense—you are their mother, it is natural for you to believe in their innocence. But for outsiders, it is not so. In their eyes, everyone is equal.’

A shadow of inner speculation flickered in Shrimati Chameli’s eyes. She stepped forward and, in a hushed voice, said suddenly, ‘Byomkeshbabu, I know how Hena died. I saw it with my own eyes.’

Byomkesh started, his eyes widening. ‘You saw it with your own eyes?’

‘Yes.’ Shrimati Chameli spoke in a single breath, ‘That day, after Chingri went to the bathroom, I came outside and saw Hena heading up to the roof on the third floor. Everyone knows I cannot tolerate Hena, and Hena herself is afraid of me. I thought, if I seize this opportunity to go up to the roof and give her a piece of my mind, perhaps she will leave my house, and my sons will be safe.’

‘So you were anxious about your sons’ safety? Well, then what happened?’

‘I too climbed the stairs to the third-floor roof. The moment she saw me, Hena panicked, ran toward the parapet, and crashed against it. Then, unable to regain her balance, she toppled over and fell down. Perhaps, seeing me, she was afraid I would harm her!’

Byomkesh gazed at her intently and asked, ‘Why did you never mention this before?’

Shrimati Chameli made a restless gesture with her mouth and said, ‘Would anyone have believed me if I had? On the contrary, they would have suspected that I pushed Hena over.’

Byomkesh bowed his head for a moment, then looked up again. ‘True enough. Tell me, when you went up to the roof by the stairs, did you see Uday?’

Shrimati Chameli replied, her voice tinged with unease, ‘No, Uday was not there.’

‘Did you see anyone else?’

‘No, no one.’

‘The stair door, the door to the roof—they were open, I suppose?’

‘Yes, they were open.’

‘When you saw Hena, what was she doing?’

‘She was standing in the middle of the roof.’

‘Was there anything in her hand?’

‘I didn’t notice.’

--- Page 970 ---
Byomkesh exhaled deeply and stood up. He said, “I suppose you have nothing more to say. Very well, then—we’ll take our leave. You may try telling the police your story.”

Sreemati Chameli stood there, rigid and unmoving, as we took our leave.

By the time we returned home, it was already afternoon.

The intricate fiction Sreemati Chameli had spun to save her sons had only left Byomkesh more bewildered and despondent. He stretched himself out on the taktaposh, and in a voice rough with agitation, said, “Nothing is happening—nothing at all. It’s all a sham, all deception. Everyone is trying to pull the wool over my eyes.”

I said, “But why are you so invested, Byomkesh? The police have given up, Santoshbabu has lost interest—so why are you wearing yourself out for nothing?”

Byomkesh replied, his voice weary, “Do you know what the trouble is? I am a satyanweshi—a seeker of truth. Until I know the real story, I cannot find peace. Damn it! If only I had another case at hand right now, perhaps I could forget—”

At that moment, the postman arrived at the front door.

An envelope, registered and insured. The sender’s name—Office of the Government of Orissa. My curiosity was piqued—what could this be? Byomkesh opened the envelope and took out a typewritten letter.

The Chief Secretary of the Government of Orissa had written—

Dear Sir,  
By order of the Honorable Chief Minister, I write this letter to you. The work you have previously done for the Central Government and the Bombay Government is well known to us. Recently, some mysterious incidents have begun to occur in the offices of the Orissa Government. Valuable and highly confidential documents have been disappearing from the department, but the culprit remains at large. In this matter, the Government of Orissa seeks your assistance. We would be obliged if you could come to Cuttack at once and take charge of the investigation. Any delay may result in loss to the state.  
We would appreciate it if you could inform us by telegram when you will arrive. Enclosed please find a cheque for Rs 500 towards your expenses.  
With thanks and regards, etc.

Byomkesh, his face alight with cheer, handed me the letter and the cheque. “It seems my reputation has now reached the corridors of government,” he said.

After reading the letter, I looked up to see him pacing, hands clasped behind his back. I asked, “So, you got what you wanted. Will you go?”

“It’s a matter of national interest. Of course I’ll go.”

“When will you leave?”

He paused in his pacing and said, “Ajit, finish your meal, deposit the cheque at the bank, and send a telegram to Cuttack. We are leaving at once.”

I asked, “And when, exactly, is ‘at once’?”

He smiled and replied, “Within today or tomorrow.”

It was almost dusk when Bikash arrived and said, “There’s news, sir.”

I had quite forgotten that Bikash, along with Gupikesto, Babui, and Chiching—four young men—worked as informants for Byomkesh. My mind had already raced ahead to Cuttack.

--- Page 971 ---
We three sat close together on the takhtposh, shoulders nearly touching. Bikash said, “That Chinaman is giving us a lot of trouble, sir.”

“Chinaman?”

“Yes, your Ravi Verma. His nose, mouth, eyes—just like a Chinaman’s. I’m sure he sneaks around eating cockroaches.”

“Not impossible. Go on, how is he troubling you?”

“I’ve been tailing the man for days, but not once does he stray from his routine! No, it’s always home to office, office to home. I was getting worn out, sir. Then today—”

“What happened today?”

“Usually he leaves the office at five, but today he left at half past four. Didn’t go home, got on a bus to Bowbazar. I got on too. The man’s conscience is clearly guilty, kept glancing back over his shoulder. I kept my head down. Near Sealdah, Ravi Verma suddenly hopped off the bus. I followed.

“You may have noticed there’s a hotel there—called the Indo-Pak Hotel. Three stories, but a bit dingy. Mostly people coming and going from Pakistan stay there. There’s a restaurant on the ground floor, chicken and mutton always cooking, and rooms to stay in upstairs. Ravi Verma slipped into the hotel.

“There was a racket in the restaurant, but Ravi Verma didn’t go that way. He crept up a dark staircase at the side.

“I followed. Where the stairs turn from the second to the third floor, there’s a narrow corridor like an alley, lined with doors on both sides. A smoky bulb hung overhead. From the bend in the stairs, I peeked and saw Ravi Verma unlocking a door at the far end with a key. But the door wouldn’t open. Then he picked another key from his bunch and tried again, but still the lock wouldn’t budge.

“At that moment, footsteps sounded above—two or three tenants were coming down the stairs. I pretended as if I lived on the second floor, took out my own keys, and stood in front of a door. Ravi Verma quickly stuffed his keys in his pocket and hurried down. I took a good look at his door and followed him.

“After that, Ravi Verma went straight home. I saw him safely back.”

Byomkesh’s eyes grew distant, lost in thought.

“Indo-Pak Hotel—Hena was from Pakistan—Ravi Verma—” Raising his gaze to Bikash, Byomkesh asked, “Did you notice the room number?”

Bikash replied, “Yes, sir; there was a brass number above the door—number 7.”

“Number 7!” Byomkesh’s eyes flashed with sudden light.

“Yes, sir, number 7.”

Byomkesh closed his eyes again, sinking into meditation. I too felt I had heard of this number seven somewhere, but for the life of me, I could not recall where. Bikash whispered to me, “Is there something special about number 7, sir?”

Byomkesh opened his eyes, placed a hand on Bikash’s shoulder, and said, “You’re the most useful man among us. But your work isn’t done yet. Go back to the Indo-Pak Hotel, keep watch in front of room number 7. We’ll be there within half an hour.”

“All right, sir.” Bikash departed.

--- Page 972 ---
Byomkesh went into the adjoining room and picked up the phone. Once the connection was established, he said, “Is that E.K. Ray? This is Byomkesh... I need a favor... You have Henna’s bunch of keys, don’t you?... Good, good. We’re coming to you right now, I need that bunch... I’ll explain in person... If you can come along with us, that would be best... Very well, we’re on our way.”

Replacing the receiver, he said, “Come, I think the mystery of Henna will be solved tonight.”

It was only then that I remembered—one of the keys on Henna’s bunch bore the number 7.

We bundled E.K. Ray into a taxi and arrived in front of the Indo-Pak Hotel at around eight o’clock. On the way, we spoke only once; E.K. Ray said, “But remember, I’m not the police now, just your friend.”

Byomkesh replied, “That’s fine.”

At the head of the staircase on the second floor, Bikash was leaning against the railing. On seeing us, he straightened up. Byomkesh quietly asked him, “Any news from room number seven?”

Bikash replied, “All’s well. No one else has come.”

“Do you know where the hotel manager stays?”

“In that room.” Bikash pointed to the room ahead.

Byomkesh turned his gaze to E.K. Ray; a silent exchange passed between their eyes. E.K. Ray nodded and knocked on the closed door of the manager’s room.

A middle-aged man opened the door. Behind him, we glimpsed a well-lit room furnished with a table and chairs. No one else was present, save for a bottle displayed on the table.

E.K. Ray asked, “Are you the hotel manager?”

The manager, his eyes bleary, surveyed E.K. Ray’s attire and replied, “Yes, sir, please come in.” As he bowed low in greeting, he nearly lost his balance and would have fallen had E.K. Ray not caught him. “I’m not here on police business,” E.K. Ray said. “I’ve come to have a word with you.”

A thick, syrupy laugh gurgled from the manager’s throat. E.K. Ray glanced back at Byomkesh, signaled with his eyes, then took Henna’s key ring from his pocket and handed it to him. After that, he entered the room and closed the door behind him. The three of us remained outside.

Byomkesh said, “Now for number seven.”

At the far end of the corridor was room number seven. In the dim glow of a flickering bulb, Byomkesh selected the right key from the ring and inserted it into the lock. Instantly, the lock clicked open. There could be no doubt now that this was the key to this room, and that Henna had frequented it.

The room was dark. As we stepped inside, it felt as if a black, savage beast lay in wait in the corner, ready to leap at our throats the moment we set foot within. Byomkesh said, “Wait, let me get a match.”

But Bikash had already entered, groped along the wall by the door, and pressed the switch. With a sudden flare, the room was flooded with light. We went in and closed the door behind us.

The stale, damp air of the closed room carried a musty odor. There was only one window, shut tight. The room was almost bare; against one wall lay a naked iron cot, and against another, a medium-sized Godrej steel almirah. Nothing else.

Byomkesh’s gaze immediately went to the steel almirah. From the key ring, he—

--- Page 973 ---
Magna Mainak 793

As soon as the key was turned in the lock, the almirah swung open. Bikash and I leaned over Byomkesh’s shoulder to peer inside.

There were three shelves in the almirah. The lower two were empty; on the top shelf rested a single book and, beside it, a file bound in rexine, shaped like a ledger. At the back, a dark object was barely visible. Byomkesh reached in and drew it out. It turned out to be a broken mouth-organ.

Byomkesh examined the mouth-organ, turning it over in his hands, then set it back in its place. Next, he picked up the book. It was a quarto-sized Bengali volume, bound in faded cloth, the title on the cover inscribed in imitation of Persian script—Rubaiyat-e-Omar Khayyam. Byomkesh flipped through the pages and stopped at the dedication. Written there: “To Srimati Meena ‘Matahari’, beloved.” The name of the giver, inscribed below, made me start. The name was intimately familiar.

Handing me the book, Byomkesh took up the rexine-bound file, opened it cautiously, then snapped it shut almost at once. From what little I glimpsed, it seemed to contain several letters written in Bengali script.

Byomkesh said, “Come, our work here is done.” I saw his eyes were gleaming with excitement. Once outside the room, he locked the door and said, “Now we can meet with the manager.”

In the manager’s room, the adda was in full swing; an empty bottle rolled lazily on the table. A.K. Ray sat with a half-filled glass in hand, while the manager, palms pressed together in supplication, was urging him, “Just one more sip, sir, only a sip. I swear on my own head!”

As we entered, Byomkesh and A.K. Ray exchanged glances; Byomkesh gave a slight nod. A.K. Ray made as if to rise and said, “Well then, for today—”

The manager, beaming, protested, “How can that be! The gentlemen have come; you can’t leave without a drink. I’ll open another bottle.”

He moved toward the almirah, but Byomkesh stopped him. “No, no, not today. Some other time. Tell me, Manager Babu, who stays in Room 7?”

“Room 7?” The manager squinted, thinking for a moment. “Oh, Room 7. A Pakistani gentleman has rented it. Quite an amusing fellow. Pays his rent every month, but in the whole month, he comes at most three days. Stays for half an hour, then leaves. Very amusing man.”

“Does anyone come with him?”

A sly glint flickered in the manager’s eyes. “A fairy comes with him.”

“What is the gentleman’s name?”

“Name!” The manager racked his brains. “I’ve forgotten the name. But no matter, it’s in the register.”

He opened a bound ledger and said, “Just as I thought, where could he go? Here it is—the gentleman’s name: Dhaka, Pakistan, Omar Shirazi.” He looked up, triumphant.

Byomkesh said, “Omar Shirazi. Thank you—thank you very much. We’ll take our leave now, but we’ll be back another day.”

Sidestepping the manager’s renewed offer to open another bottle, we descended the stairs. Byomkesh clapped Bikash on the back and said, “Your work here is done. Go home now. Come again soon.”

--- Page 974 ---
794  
Byomkesh Samagra

Bikash departed. We hailed a taxi and set off towards the A.K. Ray police station, to see him home before returning to our own quarters.

On the way, A.K. Ray remarked, “The manager is the very embodiment of courtesy, but stubborn as a mule—he wouldn’t let me go without having a peg. He was angling to ply me with more. —Anyway, did you get your work done?”

Byomkesh handed him back the key and replied, “Yes. Aren’t you curious to know what I found?”

A.K. Ray shook his head firmly. “No.”

Back home, the first thing Byomkesh did was to carefully lock away Omar Khayyam’s poetry and the file of letters in a drawer. I asked, “I know Omar Khayyam, but who is this Omar Shirazi fellow?”

The old newspaper was still lying on the table; Byomkesh unfolded it and showed me. In the list of those killed in the Pakistani plane crash was the name—Omar Shirazi, navigator.

After dinner, Byomkesh sat down with the file of letters.

The next day, at nine in the morning, we arrived at Santosh Babu’s office.

Santosh Babu had just arrived and taken his seat. Seeing Byomkesh, he exclaimed in astonishment, “What’s this! You’re still here?”

Byomkesh regarded him steadily and said, “I had thought of setting a trap for you, but it seems there’s no need. Yes, I have received the invitation from the Orissa government, but I haven’t left yet. May I sit?” Without waiting for permission, he took a chair. I sat as well.

A careless word had slipped out. For a moment, Santosh Babu’s face flushed red. Then, regaining his composure, he said, “The Orissa government!”

A faint smile played on Byomkesh’s lips as he said, “At your recommendation, the Orissa government has summoned me; of course, they don’t know your true intentions. But let’s leave that aside. Santosh Babu, I have discovered who pushed Hena Mallik from the terrace.”

I was watching Santosh Babu closely and saw his face turn ashen, while his eyes grew sharp and venomous, like those of a serpent. In that instant, it became clear what a dangerous man he was—cornered, he was as perilous as a wildcat at bay. Clenching his teeth, he said, “Who pushed her?”

Byomkesh replied in an even tone, “You did.”

Santosh Babu’s voice sounded as if someone were choking him. “Can you prove it?”

Byomkesh shook his head calmly. “No. But it can be proved that you had a motive.”

“Is that so? Do you realize I could sue you for defamation and have you thrown in jail?”

“You don’t have the courage to bring a case against me, Santosh Babu. There’s no point in blustering. Listen, you engaged me to protect your family’s interests, and that is what I have done. For whatever reason, the police have closed the investigation into Hena’s death; I have no further duty in that matter. But everyone has the right to know the truth.”

--- Page 975 ---
Magna Mainak 795

“I know. I have learned the truth.”

Santoshbabu gazed at him for a while. It was impossible to decipher how many kinds of thoughts flashed like lightning through his eyes. At last he spoke, “What truth have you learned?”

Byomkesh said, “What you were searching for, but could not find—the very thing for which you set your house on fire—I have found it. A book—Rubaiyat-e-Omar Khayyam—and a few letters.”

The veins in Santoshbabu’s temples swelled. He looked at Byomkesh with helpless, venomous eyes and said, “What do you want? Money?”

Byomkesh replied in a dry voice, “You could not bribe me, Santoshbabu, not even with all the money you possess. You have betrayed, you have committed treason, and you must face punishment.”

Santoshbabu stared at him, speechless, the veins in his temples throbbing.

“You were in love with Meena, Henna’s mother. During the Partition, you used to go to Dhaka, meet Meena. You knew Meena was a spy for the opposing side, and yet you revealed your own party’s secrets to her. Not only did you confide in her, you wrote letters, disclosing every plan and counsel of your party. Because of that, we lost at every step, lost the land that was rightfully ours.

“Meena kept your letters. Then, suddenly, she died, and the letters came into her daughter Henna’s hands. Henna had an accomplice—Omar Shirazi. Together, they conspired, and then Henna came and pressed you, began to blackmail you.”

Santoshbabu’s eyes turned blood-red. Suddenly, he reached out his hand and cried, “I’ll give you a lakh of rupees—return those letters to me!”

Byomkesh stood up, and immediately Santoshbabu did the same, glaring with bloodshot, terrifying eyes. “You won’t?”

Byomkesh shook his head, then said quietly, “From tomorrow, a prominent daily newspaper will publish facsimiles of your letters, one by one. Be prepared.”

Santoshbabu, his eyes blazing, collapsed into his chair. Byomkesh signaled to me, and we made our way toward the door.

A voice called from behind, “Byomkeshbabu!”

We turned and stood before Santoshbabu. He sat with both elbows on the table, his face buried in his hands. After a minute, he lowered his hands; his face was expressionless. He said, “Will you give me one day? I have a speech at Park Circus Maath this evening at five—”

Byomkesh fixed him with a grave look and replied in a slow, deliberate voice, “You have one day. Tomorrow morning your letters will not appear in the newspaper. But let me warn you—hiring goons to have me killed will do you no good. You will never get hold of those letters. In due time, they will be published.”

“Thank you.”

All day, Byomkesh lay on the taktaposh, counting the rafters, silent. At four in the afternoon, when tea arrived, he sat up and drank his tea, then said, “Come, let’s go out.”

“Where are we going?”

--- Page 976 ---
796  
Byomkesh Samagra

“To listen to Santosh Babu’s lecture.”  
Thus, we set out. It is only natural, I suppose, to feel curious about what sort of speech a man would give when the sword is hanging over his head.  
A stage had been erected on the Park Circus grounds, upon which sat a row of dignitaries, the Chief Secretary among them. Before them, a vast crowd had gathered. The meeting had been called to bring some grave political matter to the attention of the public. We took our place at the back of the crowd.  
First, the Prime Minister rose—he was the chairman. Standing before the microphone, he introduced the subject at hand. Then, one by one, the speakers took the stage. Each delivered impassioned speeches, laced with a touch of reasoned argument. Carried away by their eloquence, I found myself drifting on a tide of words.  
At last, Santosh Babu stepped up to the microphone. The resolute gravity on his face signaled the seriousness of the matter. He stood in silence for a moment, then began to speak, slowly and deliberately.  
The man had the gift of oratory. There was no bombast, no sentimentality—only an irresistible logic that drew the entire audience into his grasp. Gradually, the rhythm of his speech quickened, his voice resonating with a hidden passion, like the beat of a mridangam. And when, at the end of his address, he uttered “Bande Mataram” in a ringing voice, the crowd responded with a spontaneous chorus of victory cries.  
Finishing his speech, Santosh Babu returned to his seat. My gaze remained fixed on him; I saw him take a small tin from his pocket and put something in his mouth. I thought, perhaps, it was a penicillin tablet.  
Meanwhile, the Chief Secretary had come forward and begun his closing remarks. The audience was beginning to stir, preparing to leave, when suddenly a commotion broke out on the stage. Instantly, my eyes darted in that direction; I saw Santosh Babu slumped in his seat, those nearby leaning over him in alarm. The Prime Minister broke off his speech and rushed to his side. A wave of anxious murmurs swept through the crowd.  
Five minutes later, the Prime Minister returned to the microphone and, his voice heavy with emotion, announced, “With deepest sorrow, I must inform you that our dear friend, the nation’s beloved son, Santosh Samaddar, has left this world—”  
His voice broke as he continued. Byomkesh seized my arm and tugged, saying, “Come. The curtain has fallen on the fifth act.”  

Once outside the park, Byomkesh lit a cigarette and said, “Let’s walk.”  
It was quite a distance, but neither of us felt like taking a tram or bus. I lit a cigarette as well and replied, “Let’s.”  
As we walked side by side, Byomkesh began to speak—  
“Santosh Babu was a man of talent, but he was not a man of character. There’s an English saying—‘A sailor has a wife in every port.’ Santosh Babu was much the same. His work took him to Madras, Bombay, Delhi—everywhere. I believe he had a sweetheart in every city. Even in old age, he never outgrew that affliction.  
“In Calcutta, he had Sukumari; in Dhaka, there was Meena. Meena was a Muslim by faith. In every country, in every civilized society, there exists a class of women who outwardly appear—”

--- Page 977 ---
Magnamainak 797

Refined and decorous, yet beneath the surface she ran the trade of a courtesan. In Western countries, such women are called—demi-mondaines. Meena was a demi-mondaine. Whether she had a husband, I do not know; perhaps there was someone of the shadowy, witness-Gopal sort, a certain Kamal Mallik. The name Kamal Mallik is Hindu, but if you say Kamal Mallik, it becomes a Muslim name. Hena Mallik is the same. The surname Mallik exists among Hindus, but in truth, it is a Muslim title.

You have seen Meena’s photograph—she was a woman of rare beauty. She had influence in the upper echelons of society. She had ensnared Santoshbabu in her web of enchantment; whenever he visited Dhaka, he would meet with Meena. Perhaps she sang him ghazals.

Then came Independence, and with it, the war of Partition. None can forget how brutal that conflict was. At this time, Santoshbabu was a prominent leader of our group. When envoys were needed between the two sides, Santoshbabu was appointed as our emissary. He began to travel frequently between Calcutta and Dhaka. Naturally, he continued to meet Meena.

Santoshbabu, then drowning in Meena’s love, began to divulge even the most confidential secrets of his party to her. Though Meena was a woman of pleasure, she still cared for her own side’s interests, and she passed those secrets to their proper destination. Consider the situation: a political war of intrigue was raging, we knew nothing of their secret intentions, while they knew all of ours. The outcome was inevitable.

Santoshbabu, in his besotted state, did not stop at revealing secrets verbally to Meena; when he was in Calcutta, he wrote her letters containing confidential information. I do not know the reason for this betrayal—perhaps personal jealousy against another national leader. But that he knowingly sent Meena these reports is beyond doubt. On the dedication page of a copy of the Rubaiyat of Omar Khayyam, he had written—Meena Mata Hari. He knew Meena was a spy for the opposing side.

In any case, the struggle of Partition ended one day. Several years passed. Meena had carefully preserved Santoshbabu’s letters; she had not destroyed them. What her intentions were, I cannot say—perhaps she thought if Santoshbabu ever tried to break free, the letters would prove useful. But suddenly, one day, Meena died. Perhaps it was an accident.

Meena had a daughter—Hena. When her mother died, she had come of age. Among her mother’s papers, she found Santoshbabu’s letters. Hena surely had a few suitors; her closest friend among them was Omar Shirazi. Shirazi worked for an airline company, a navigator on airplanes. He flew from country to country; his route stretched from Singapore to Cairo. Every ten or twelve days, his plane would land at Dum Dum.

Hena told Omar Shirazi about the letters, and together they decided to blackmail Santoshbabu. They came to Calcutta and made their intentions clear to him. Hena settled herself in his house. They had reasoned that Santoshbabu’s home was the safest place for Hena. Santoshbabu was caught in a snare. Even if he wished, he could not kill Hena—if he did, Omar Shirazi would expose his secrets. He began to pay the blackmail money.

Santoshbabu could not discover where Hena had hidden the incriminating letters, though he suspected she had concealed them in her own room in his house. But

--- Page 978 ---
Byomkesh Samagra

Since it was his own house, there was no convenience in searching it. Hena always stayed in her own room, only going up to the roof once in the evening to perform her namaz. Even then, she locked the door behind her.

“Omar Shirazi had rented a room in the Indo-Pak Hotel. All their evidence, their letters and documents, were hidden in that room. It seems there was an arrangement—Santoshbabu would give Hena money every week. I don’t know how much, but it could be found out by examining Santoshbabu’s bank records. In any case, Hena would wait for Shirazi with the money. At the appointed time, Shirazi would arrive and signal her by playing his mouth-organ. Then the two of them would go to the Indo-Pak Hotel. There, Hena would hand over the money to Shirazi, and Shirazi would leave for Pakistan with it. That, in short, was their method.”

I asked, “He took Indian currency?”

Byomkesh replied, “He took the money, or perhaps bought gold with it, or deposited it in some Calcutta bank. But I believe he simply took the money.”

“Go on.”

“Hena was not the orphaned daughter of Santoshbabu’s friend—she was bleeding him dry. Only one person suspected this. Rabivarma. He was Santoshbabu’s secretary, and a terribly cunning, slippery fellow. Whether he knew Hena beforehand is uncertain, but at some point, he followed her and discovered the Indo-Pak Hotel. He realized that Room 7 contained dangerous documents. He managed to procure a bunch of keys and tried to enter Room 7 at an opportune moment. But he couldn’t open the door. Most likely, his plan was to get hold of those documents and blackmail Santoshbabu himself. But his scheme failed. After Hena’s death, he tried once more. Bikash was on his tail and caught him in the act. If Bikash hadn’t seen him in front of Room 7 at the Indo-Pak Hotel, Santoshbabu would never have been caught.”

I said, “One thing. Isn’t it possible that Santoshbabu himself employed Rabivarma to recover those documents?”

Byomkesh replied, “No. If Santoshbabu were involved, he wouldn’t have acted like a petty thief. He’d have bribed the manager handsomely and got the job done. Anyway, let’s not get ahead of ourselves. Santoshbabu was caught in a vice, suffering for six months, with no end in sight. Then, something happened. One morning, opening the newspaper, Santoshbabu saw that a Pakistani plane had crashed into the sea. Among the dead was the name—Omar Shirazi.

“That was it. Santoshbabu saw his path to salvation. Hena didn’t read the newspaper, so she didn’t know yet; before she could find out, she had to be eliminated. He knew Hena went to the roof every evening for namaz. The house was under repair, so it was easy to climb up from outside. He made up his mind—he would kill Hena in such a way that it would look like an accident.

“It was a Saturday. In the afternoon, he went to Sukumari, consulted with her, and arranged his alibi. He had to plan everything meticulously.”

I said, “I didn’t realize Sukumari had lied so blatantly to us.”

Byomkesh replied, “Yes. Sukumari was deeply attached to Santoshbabu, or else she wouldn’t have entangled herself in a murder case for his sake. If anyone would mourn Santoshbabu’s death, it would be Sukumari.

“Santoshbabu entered his own house through the side gate, climbed up using the scaffolding—”

--- Page 979 ---
Magna Mainak  
799

Henna was perhaps at that moment spreading a mat, preparing to face west and begin her prayers, when she saw Santoshbabu coming up. It did not take her long to understand his intention; frightened, she ran towards the eastern side of the roof to escape. But where could she go? As she neared the parapet, Santoshbabu rushed up from behind and shoved her—she fell from the roof to the ground below.

Santoshbabu unlocked the roof gate and, just as he had come, climbed down the ladder and left. The reason for unlocking the gate—so that even if someone suspected Henna’s death to be murder, it would remain uncertain from which direction the assailant had come up to the roof.

No one knew Santoshbabu had come to the house; having completed his grim task, he returned to Sukumari. But there was still one thing left to do.

The letters were surely in Henna’s room. The police hadn’t found them, but they might later. In the dead of night, when all were asleep, he quietly came down and searched Henna’s room. But he could not find the letters. Then, pouring petrol, he set the room ablaze. Immolation, with all the evidence.

Byomkesh fell silent. After a while, as we walked on in silence, I asked, “Who telephoned Santoshbabu?”

He replied, “No one. That was pure fabrication. Even after returning to his secret bower, Santoshbabu could not feel secure—what if Henna hadn’t died by chance? Besides, the letters had to be removed from Henna’s room. So he invented an anonymous informant; coached Sukumari about the telephone call, and returned home.”

“An extraordinary actor, this Santoshbabu.”

“Yes. An extraordinary speaker, an extraordinary actor—they are all of the same breed.”

“Tell me, Byomkesh, why did Santoshbabu appoint you to safeguard his family’s interests? Why didn’t he send you away right at the start?”

“Nengti had committed a misdeed and called me. If Santoshbabu had dismissed me, everyone would have suspected him. So, posing as a saint, he engaged me to protect his family’s interests. Later, of course, he tried to get rid of me, but by then, kamli nehi chhordti.”

“When did you first suspect him?”

“When I heard about the fire in the room, I realized it had been set to destroy some combustible material. What kind of combustible? Surely something not easily found. Naturally, documents came to mind. What sort of documents? The kind used for blackmail. So, was Henna blackmailing someone? Whom? Jugol and Uday could be ruled out; that left Ravivarma and Santoshbabu. But Ravivarma is a minor figure—one couldn’t extract much money by blackmailing him. On the other hand, Santoshbabu is wealthy, travels frequently to East Bengal, had allowed Henna to stay in his own house. Perhaps even the police’s indifference was due to his influence. He was also behind the attempt to send me away from Calcutta to Cuttack. So, in every way, he was the most suitable candidate. —By the way, tomorrow morning I must send a telegram to Cuttack, to find out if they still want me.”

“All right. What will you do with Santoshbabu’s letters?”

“I’ll burn them. Their purpose is served. Santoshbabu has atoned; there’s no profit in ruining his reputation. Let Magna Mainak remain submerged.”

--- Page 980 ---
When I returned home, I found Nengti sitting there. He had procured tea and cigarettes from Satyabati and was enjoying them in utter comfort. He had not yet heard the news of Santosh Babu’s death. At the sight of Byomkesh, he raised his eyebrows and exclaimed impatiently, “Well, have you still not caught Hena’s murderer?”

Byomkesh replied in an irritable tone, “I have. What are you doing here?”

Nengti, startled and incredulous, blurted out, “You have?”

Byomkesh said, “Yes. Or have you?”

Nengti stammered, “I—I knew from the very beginning.”

“From the very beginning! How did you know? Did you deduce it? Tell me the name of the killer, let’s hear it.”

Nengti replied in a faltering voice, “Mesomoshai.”

Byomkesh stared at him for a moment in astonished disbelief, then asked, “You know! How did you find out?”

Nengti spoke quickly, his voice dazed, “I saw it with my own eyes, Byomkesh-da. I was up in the pine tree behind the house, smoking a cigarette. Just then, Hena came onto the roof, about to lay down her mat and sit, when suddenly Mesomoshai climbed up from the west side by the water pipe. The moment Hena saw him, she ran towards the east, and he chased after her, pushed her, and threw her off the roof.”

Byomkesh fixed him with a hard gaze. “Why didn’t you say anything all this time?”

Nengti replied in a plaintive voice, “How could I, Byomkesh-da? He is our provider—how could I hand him over to the police? Still, I informed you, because I knew, if anyone could catch the culprit, it would be you.”

Byomkesh’s face softened. He placed a hand on Nengti’s shoulder and said, “Nengti, go home quickly. Santosh Babu has died.”

--- Page 981 ---
Dushtachakra

Dr. Suresh Rakshit said, “You simply must come, Byomkesh Babu. Given the patient’s condition, unless you go and reassure him, it will be difficult to save him.”

Dr. Suresh was around forty, a little thin and gaunt, his expensive and brand-new English suit somehow ill-suited to his frame. Yet his manner was brisk, his demeanor intelligent. This morning, he had come to see Byomkesh with a most unusual proposal.

It goes without saying that Byomkesh had no desire to visit a patient. With half-closed eyes, he looked at Dr. Rakshit and asked, “What is the illness?”

The doctor replied, “Paralysis—meaning, a stroke. The attack happened nearly three months ago. He survived the initial shock, but his blood pressure is dangerously high. His mind, however, remains clear. He sent me to you, hoping you might kindly come just once. If he finds some hope, perhaps he might survive.”

Byomkesh asked, “You are treating him?”

The doctor replied, “Yes, indeed. I am a tenant on the ground floor of his house, and also his personal physician. The man is immensely wealthy, a moneylender by trade. You may have heard the name Bishu Pal.”

Byomkesh said, “What name did you say—Shishupal?”

The doctor smiled. “Bishu Pal. Though some do call him Shishupal. I don’t know why. He is a usurer, certainly, but not a fiend for money. Especially these past three months, bedridden, he has become utterly helpless.”

Byomkesh said, “But what can I do? I am no doctor.”

The doctor said, “Let me tell you the real reason. Bishu Pal has a client named Abhay Ghoshal. The man is a scoundrel. In a time of trouble, he borrowed a large sum from Bishu Pal, and now refuses to repay it. Bishu Pal pressed him hard for the money, and in response, Abhay Ghoshal allegedly threatened him—said that if Bishu Pal demanded repayment, he would kill him. Immediately after, Bishu Pal suffered his stroke, and has been bedridden these three months. Of course, his life is not in danger, and with careful treatment, he may yet recover and walk again. But that is not the main issue. What matters is that he is now convinced Abhay Ghoshal will murder him—he believes that even if he forgives the debt, he will still be killed. You need not treat him; Bishu Pal simply wishes to unburden his heart to you. If you could offer him some advice, it might help.”

Byomkesh, a little absent-minded, said, “If someone is truly determined to play the role of Shishupal’s slayer, even Shiva himself could not prevent it. Still, since the gentleman so desires my company—”

--- Page 982 ---
Byomkesh Samagra

Since you are so anxious, I shall go. Whether it be in this world or the next, it is always better to leave things in the hands of the great ones. What do you say, Ajit?'

I replied, 'Of course.'

Dr. Rakshit stood up, smiling, and said, 'Thank you. Here is our address. When will you come?' He took out a card and handed it to Byomkesh.

Byomkesh glanced at the card and extended it towards me. I saw that Bishu Pal’s residence was not far, in a lane off Amherst Street. Byomkesh said, 'I’ll come around five this evening. —Very well, see you then.'

After the doctor left, Byomkesh, still smiling, said, 'This is the first time I’ve been asked to comfort a paralysed patient.'

The lane was serpentine, winding and twisting like a mountain river. On both sides stood three- and four-storey houses. However narrow the lane, I have noticed the houses are never small; lacking space to expand sideways, they grow long instead.

Beside a three-storey house, I saw Dr. Suresh Rakshit’s nameplate and understood this was Bishu Pal’s residence. Dr. Rakshit, having spotted us from the window, came out and said, 'Come in.'

The house was of the old style; on one side, a narrow corridor like a tunnel ran inward, with a door on one side and two windows on the other. Through the door, the doctor’s consulting room was visible: a spotless, gleaming chamber. But there was no crowd of patients. A middle-aged compounder stood by the door. Dr. Rakshit did not take us into the consulting room, but said, 'You’ll have to climb the stairs. Bishu Babu lives on the third floor.'

Byomkesh asked, 'Very well. Do you live in this house as well, or is it only your practice?'

The doctor replied, 'There are three rooms. I use two for my practice, and live in one. I’m alone, so it’s no trouble.'

There were three rooms on the second floor as well. These rooms served as offices. Not the kind with desks and chairs, but offices in the Marwari style, with mattresses spread on the floor. Many clerks sat, pens scratching away.

Byomkesh asked, 'What is this?'

The doctor replied, 'Bishu Babu’s office. A vast business—many princes and potentates have their fortunes tied to him.'

Byomkesh said nothing more. I thought to myself, Bishu Pal is not just Shishupal, but Jarasandha as well.

At the head of the stairs to the third floor, a Gurkha, armed for battle, sat on a stool with a musket in hand; hearing footsteps, he stood up and cast a sidelong glance at us. The doctor said, 'Thik hai.' At that, the Gurkha saluted and stepped aside.

A few paces down the corridor, a closed door. The doctor knocked. From inside, a woman’s voice asked, 'Who is it?'

The doctor replied, 'It’s me, Dr. Rakshit. Please open the door.'

The door opened a crack. I glimpsed the thin face and anxious eyes of an elderly, widowed lady. She looked at each of us in turn, seemed reassured, and opened the door fully. We entered a shadowy room.

--- Page 983 ---
No trace could be found. Are you very busy?'
'No.'
'Then could you come over here once? It’s not far from your house, just five minutes away. The house is called Benimadhab.'
'I know it.'
'When will you come?'
'Immediately.'

Two

Benimadhab Chakraborty had amassed a fortune as a contractor for the government’s military department. His sprawling three-storied house on Sadar Road in South Calcutta was but a modest testament to that wealth.

Benimadhab was a man of shrewd intelligence. Years of contracting had stripped him of faith in the honesty of mankind. Yet, for all that, his heart had not grown narrow. He accepted the faults of his family—and his own—with a smile.

Benimadhab’s household was not large. He had become a widower soon after reaching manhood; his wife left behind a son and a daughter. When they came of age, Benimadhab arranged their marriages. The son, Ajay, grew into a thorough good-for-nothing; after squandering some of his father’s money in failed business ventures, he returned to rest on his father’s shoulders. Benimadhab made no further attempt to employ him. He himself spent most of his time away from home; Ajay lived on the second floor with his wife Arati and their children, Makaranda and Laboni. Benimadhab provided for their household expenses.

He had married off his daughter well; his son-in-law, Gangadhar, had inherited property. But, emboldened by his wealthy father-in-law, Gangadhar lost everything at the races. His daughter, Gayatri, returned to her father in tears. Benimadhab took in his daughter, son-in-law, and granddaughter Jhillika; just as he gave his son a monthly allowance, he allotted the same for his daughter.

Benimadhab’s house, as I have said, was three stories high. The top floor had only three rooms, the rest was a vast terrace. Benimadhab kept this floor for himself; if he was away, it remained locked. The second floor had eight rooms and a continuous veranda in front; here, Benimadhab arranged for his son Ajay and daughter Gayatri to live side by side. Their kitchens, of course, were separate. There was no harmony between the two households, but neither dared quarrel openly. Benimadhab was fond of his children, but he was a man of gravity, and knew how to be strict.

On the ground floor was a massive hall, furnished in the English style like a drawing-room: a low round table in the center, two sofas and several heavy, cushioned chairs around it, and a few more stiff-backed chairs lined up against the wall. But the room was rarely used; only on the rare occasion when a visitor called would a guest be seated there. The remaining five rooms, though designated for guests and visitors, were locked up most of the time.

But they did not remain locked for long. Benimadhab had two younger maternal cousins, both long since deceased...

--- Page 984 ---
Beni Sanhar  
927

...had gone; their two sons, Sanat Ganguli and Nikhil Haldar—cousins by their mothers—worked in Calcutta. They did not have good lodgings, so Benimadhab brought them into his own house. They stayed in two rooms on the ground floor.

So it appears: Benimadhab’s sons, daughters, grandchildren, and two nephews—seven dependents in all. There were no male servants in the house; two maids came during the day to do the chores and left by evening. The atmosphere was utterly monotonous. The brothers-in-law were of almost the same age, forty-three and forty-four; but there was no real intimacy between them, and their personalities were quite different. Ajay was handsome and something of a dandy; he never stepped out of the house without a starched dhuti-panjabi and polished pump shoes. He strongly objected to going to the Gariahat market every morning; most days, his wife Arati went instead. Ajay went to the club in the evenings; he had a deep passion for amateur theatre. He enjoyed acting. The club itself was for amateur dramatics—they staged four or five plays every year.

Gangadhar’s appearance was almost ascetic; his face and body were lean, more bone than flesh. His gaze was sharp. Having squandered his own property and come to live off his father-in-law, he had become extremely grave and taciturn. He never left the house during the day; after dusk, he would go out for a walk with a stick in hand. When he returned an hour or so later, the smell of liquor would waft from his mouth.

Between the sisters-in-law, there was a surface cordiality—visits and gossip—but given the chance, neither would hesitate to take a swipe at the other. Gayatri might go to the next flat and say, “Boudi, what have you cooked today?”

Arati would recite her menu and ask, “And what did you cook, dear?”

Gayatri replied, “What cooking! I was about to put the meat on after draining the rice water, when I found there was no garam masala! You know your brother-in-law can’t eat just greens and rice. He can do without fish, but he must have meat every day. So I came to check if you had any garam masala in your pantry. Otherwise, I’ll have to send the maid to the market again.”

Arati said, “Of course I have some, here you go.”

Handing over the garam masala, Arati smiled and said, “There’s nothing wrong with Nandai loving meat, but honestly, he could do without that other thing.”

Gayatri’s gaze hardened at once. “What thing?”

Arati put on her most innocent face. “Your brother was saying the other evening he ran into Nandai, and there was a strong smell of liquor on his breath. Maybe it’s an old habit he can’t shake, but if Father hears of it—”

Gayatri’s hard gaze turned sly, and with a crooked smile she said, “If Father hears of it, it’ll be because you tell him, Boudi. But would that be wise? You keep a dance master for your daughter, nothing wrong with that, but is it right for Laboni to go out to the cinema with the master till midnight? Laboni’s no little girl—if she gets into some scandal, will Father be pleased?” With a swirl of her sari end, Gayatri left.

Tit for tat, embrace for embrace.

Laboni was a pretty girl; slender and tall, her figure suited for dance. She was a bit restless by nature, her schooling had ended before she finished the upper grades, and she had a wild passion for dance. Seeing his daughter’s inclination, Ajay had arranged for a dance master for her. The master was a young man from a respectable family, named Parag Laha; twice a week he came to teach Laboni dance...

--- Page 985 ---
Byomkesh Samagra

Lavani would practice her dance right before her parents’ eyes. Occasionally, Parag would say, “A new foreign film on dance and music is playing. I’ve bought two tickets for the night show. Shall I take Lavani? She could learn a lot from it.”

At first, Arati would refuse. Parag would reply, “Very well, I’ll take another student instead.”

Gradually, her objections softened. Lavani began going to the cinema with Parag; late at night, Parag would escort her home.

With time, everything becomes familiar, even the unusual.

Lavani’s elder brother Makrand was enrolled in college. But his studies were nominal; his name was on the rolls, nothing more. His horizon was filled with political squabbles, and he only set foot in college when party affairs demanded it. He was good-looking, but his face and eyes bore a fierce, hungry dissatisfaction. He rarely stayed at home; his connection to the household was limited to meals and sleep. From time to time, money for Arati’s household expenses would vanish; Arati understood who had taken it, but remained silent for fear of discord. Makrand harbored a deep resentment for his theatre-loving father, and Ajay too disapproved of his son’s ways; the two avoided each other, as if Makrand were an unwelcome guest in his own parents’ home.

In the adjacent flat lived a small family: only a daughter, Jhilli. Jhilli was Lavani’s age, not as beautiful, but much better at her studies. She was a reserved girl, newly admitted to college, attending classes regularly, diligent in her work, and helping her mother with household chores whenever she could. Her calm face betrayed nothing of her inner world.

Such was the general state of affairs on the second floor.

Downstairs, in two rooms, lived Sanat and Nikhil. Sanat was over thirty; Nikhil, under thirty. Both could be called men of presence, but their characters were entirely different. Sanat was reserved and taciturn, never speaking without thought. Nikhil, on the other hand, was quick-tongued, lively, and fond of jest. Both worked as journalists. Sanat was a press photographer. Nikhil worked as a junior news editor in the editorial department of a newspaper. He was a nocturnal creature—“When the gods sleep, our day begins; when the gods wake, our night falls.” They were kin to those who once tempted Rishyashringa.

Neither had married. Nikhil’s reason was simple: his earnings were insufficient to support a household. Sanat, however, had no such excuse. He earned well; his reason for living in his maternal uncle’s house was not lack of means, but lack of a suitable home. To seek the root of his aversion to marriage, one would have to consult a secret album. In that album were many lively photographs of enchanting young women. Seeing those pictures, one might suspect that, though Sanat was unmarried, he was no celibate. Yet he was an exceedingly cautious man. If, instead of marriage, he had adopted the ways of a honeybee, it was all done without anyone’s knowledge.

These seven people were the permanent residents of the house. Benimadhab would come once or twice a year, stay for a couple of days, and then return to Delhi. Delhi was the center of his professional world.

Suddenly, at the age of sixty-seven, Benimadhab’s health broke down. He had always been robust, capable of tremendous exertion. But after the death of his beloved servant and long-time companion, Rambhajan, he could not remain upright for long. Within three months, he wound up his business. He had no need for money; he had worked out of sheer habit, right up to old age. Now, closing his Delhi office, he returned to Calcutta. With him came a new...

--- Page 986 ---
Beni Sanghar  
929

The servant Meghraj.  
After the death of Rambhajan, Benimadhab kept Meghraj as his personal attendant. Meghraj had once been a sepoy in the Indian Army; during the Sino-Indian war, he was wounded and lost a leg up to the knee. The Indian Army provided him with an artificial limb and discharged him with a modest pension. He managed to secure a job as a doorman at Benimadhab’s Delhi office, which put bread on his table; after Rambhajan’s death, Benimadhab brought him on as his personal servant. Meghraj was a man of utmost loyalty and a strict disposition; he took charge of all the affairs of Benimadhab’s solitary household, from shaving his master’s beard to polishing his shoes. He was about forty, with a robust build. Because of the artificial leg, he walked with a slight limp.

Thus, Benimadhab came and settled in his Calcutta house. The upper floor had all the necessary amenities for daily life, except for a fridge and a telephone. Within a few days, both fridge and telephone were installed. In the meantime, his daughter Gayatri came with a request—“Baba, this time let me take care of your meals. Earlier, whenever you came, you would eat at Dada’s place. Are we nobody to you?”

Benimadhab replied, “But I am not alone—Meghraj is here.”

“Is Meghraj the name of your new servant? Ah, poor old Rambhajan is gone. Well, I’ll feed Meghraj too.”

After some thought, Benimadhab said, “Very well, but that will increase your expenses. I’m raising your monthly allowance by another hundred and fifty rupees.”

Gayatri laughed and said, “As you wish.” Perhaps this was her intention all along; she used to receive seven hundred and fifty rupees a month, now it stood at nine hundred.

No sooner had he arrived in Calcutta than Benimadhab summoned his old friend, Dr. Abinash Sen. Dr. Abinash Sen was a renowned physician, a few years younger than Benimadhab. One day, he took Benimadhab to his clinic and conducted a thorough examination—X-rays, ECG, and other mechanical tests. Then Dr. Sen said, “Look, you have no serious illness; what you are experiencing is simply the natural, all-encompassing decline of old age. I won’t prescribe you any medicines, only a monthly injection to keep your glands invigorated. The truth is, you have worked far too hard for your age. From now on, complete rest: read books, listen to the radio, take a stroll every evening.”

Benimadhab received the injection and returned home in good spirits.

And so the days passed. Gayatri would arrange his plate herself and feed her father. The others came and went. Makaranda rarely visited, and even when he did, he stayed only a couple of minutes. The granddaughters stayed, chatting with Benimadhab; the old man was pleased to hear that Jhillie was doing well in her studies, and he was not displeased to learn that Laboni was learning to dance. Though advanced in years, he was not old-fashioned. When every girl is learning to dance, why shouldn’t his granddaughter?

Some twenty or twenty-five days went by, and then, quite suddenly, Benimadhab fell ill—dysentery, abdominal pain. Dr. Sen came, examined him, and said, “You’ve overindulged in food; you must be careful with your diet.”

Everyone in the house was present. Gayatri said with a long face, “But Doctor, I haven’t given Baba anything to eat that could have made him ill.”

The doctor said nothing, merely wrote out a prescription and dietary instructions, and took his leave.

--- Page 987 ---
He paused—“I’ll call and check on you from time to time.”

After the doctor left, Benimadhab turned to Arati and said, “Daughter-in-law, I leave the responsibility of preparing my diet to you.”

Arati, suppressing her triumph, replied, “Yes, Father.”

Within three or four days, Benimadhab recovered; his stomach settled. He abandoned his bland diet and began eating regular food. Arati continued to cook for him.

But Benimadhab’s mind was not at peace. All his life he had spent his days in the company of various people, engaged in various pursuits; now, his life was stripped of all variety. In the morning, Meghraj shaved his beard, he bathed, had his tea, and sat down with the newspaper. That occupied him for about an hour. Then he would turn on the radio and listen to music for a while. The music didn’t please him for long; he would switch off the radio and leaf through books and magazines.

One day, memories of old friends in Calcutta surfaced. He searched the telephone directory for their names, called some and reached a few, others not; for a while, they reminisced about the old days. After eleven, Arati would arrive with his plate of rice. After the meal, he would spend an hour lying on his bed, dozing in the afternoon.

In the evening, Jhilli or Laboni would come, and he would chat with them for a while. He would say to Laboni, “Let’s see how well you’ve learned to dance.”

Laboni would reply, “I haven’t learned well yet, Grandpa. When I do, I’ll show you.”

Benimadhab would ask, “Is your teacher any good?”

Laboni, flushed with excitement, would say, “Very good. So good that—” Embarrassed, she would stop midway.

Benimadhab would ask, “How old is your teacher?”

“How should I know! Must be twenty-six or twenty-seven. I have to go, Mother’s calling.” Laboni would hurry away.

After sunset, Benimadhab would pace the open terrace for a long time. Sometimes he wished to go to Rabindra Sarobar, to stroll among people for a while; but climbing three flights of stairs was too much for him, so his exercise was limited to the rooftop.

At nine at night, after finishing his meal, he would retire to bed. Such was his daily routine. Meghraj was always close at hand; sometimes inside the room, sometimes just outside the door. Once Benimadhab lay down, Meghraj would go downstairs, eat his dinner, then return, gently close Benimadhab’s door, and spread his bedding outside, standing guard through the night.

Thus the days passed. One day, after a telephone conversation with a professor friend, Benimadhab’s face grew grave. He set down the receiver and sat thinking for a while, then called Meghraj and said, “Go downstairs and fetch Makrand.”

A few minutes later, Makrand appeared. Summoned by the servant, he was not pleased; his face bore a look of displeasure as he gazed at his grandfather, question in his eyes. Benimadhab regarded his tall, robust figure for a while, then asked, “You’ve started college—how are your studies going?”

Makrand’s face darkened with a deep frown. “They’re going, more or less.”

Benimadhab said, “I’ve heard you don’t attend classes, that you roam about in a gang doing politics. Is that true?”

Makrand replied insolently, “Who told you that?”

Benimadhab said sharply, “Who told me is none of your concern. Is it true or not?”

--- Page 988 ---
Beni Sanhar 931

“Yes, it’s true.” Makrand’s eyes reddened as he stared at his grandfather.

“Indeed!” Sparks of anger flashed in Benimadhab’s eyes as well. “You have learned insolence. —Meghraj!”

Meghraj had been standing outside the door; he entered the room. Benimadhab pointed a finger and commanded, “Grab this boy by the ear, slap him across the face, and then throw him out by the scruff of his neck.”

Meghraj had once been a sepoy; he was a servant of orders. As instructed, he seized Makrand by the ear and struck him on the cheek. However much impudence Makrand harbored in his heart, he lacked both the courage and the physical strength to grapple with Meghraj. Stumbling from the blow, he was pushed out of the room.

The matter could no longer be concealed. Ajay and Arati rushed in and begged Benimadhab’s forgiveness. Benimadhab remained grave, and at last said, “There is but one boy in the family, and he has grown wild and insolent. The fault is yours—you do not know how to discipline a child.”

The affair did not escalate further.

Then, one afternoon, Sanat came to visit his uncle. Sanat and Nikhil would sometimes come and sit with their uncle, inquire after his well-being with due respect, and then depart. Today, Sanat had brought his camera, and said, “Uncle, I’d like to take your photograph.”

Benimadhab smiled and replied, “I am an old man—what use is there in taking my picture?”

Sanat said, “I’ll keep it in my album.”

“But the light is fading now; can you take a picture in this light?”

“I can. I’ve brought a flash bulb.”

“Very well, go ahead.” Benimadhab settled into a leaning chair.

Sanat was preparing to take the photograph when Nikhil entered. Sanat moved about, searching for the right angle, and at last took the picture from a particular vantage; the bulb flashed once and went out.

Nikhil said, “Sanatda, when the photograph is ready, give me a copy. I’ll print it in the paper. Uncle has retired from work and settled in Calcutta; the news should be published.”

Benimadhab was inwardly pleased with his nephews.

The next day, Sanat brought the photograph to show Benimadhab. The picture had come out well; Benimadhab’s age-worn face, through the artist’s skill, had acquired a gentle and serene expression. There was no doubt that Sanat was a deft artist.

Benimadhab said, “It’s very good. If you have it framed and hung somewhere, that will do.”

Sanat replied, “I’ll enlarge it, frame it, and bring it over. I’ve given Nikhil a copy—he’ll print it in the paper.”

And so, Benimadhab’s idle, languid days passed. Sanat had the large photograph framed and hung it on the wall of the room. His picture and a brief introduction appeared in the newspaper. In such circumstances, a man in his old age should find peace and comfort. But peace and comfort did not come to Benimadhab’s heart. He could not enjoy continuous closeness with the families of his son and daughter. “Perhaps this is what happens to those who, forgetting the taste of family life, have walked alone for so many years.”

On the other hand, there was no happiness in his son’s or daughter’s families either. Gayatri’s temper was always irritable. Gangadhar spent his days sitting alone, playing solitaire; at dusk he would slip out quietly, only to return before it grew too late. Ajay would once spend many late nights at the club, lost in adda or rehearsals; this had been his chief indulgence in life. Now he was required to return home by nine o’clock, for the master’s order was that after nine, the main door would remain closed.

--- Page 989 ---
No. If he returned home after nine and pushed at the door, the sound would carry up to the third floor, and that was not desirable. Everyone kept an eye and an ear alert toward the third floor. Even though Arati was always trying to please her father-in-law, she could not be at ease.

Only two girls on the second floor, Laboni and Jhilli, and Sanat and Nikhil on the ground floor, were truly at ease. Jhilli and Laboni were only eighteen; their sense of the world was not yet mature. The situation was different for Sanat and Nikhil; their uncle had indeed allowed them to stay in his house, but they had no expectations of money from him. There was no possibility that Benimadhab would learn of Sanat’s secret nocturnal excursions. Nikhil was not guilty of such things; moreover, for the past few months, he had been absorbed in something new.

One day, before Benimadhab had settled in Calcutta, Nikhil suddenly received a letter by post, an envelope. There was no one who would write to him, so he was a little surprised as he opened it. On a single sheet of paper, just two lines were written—

I am a girl. I love you.—

There was no name beneath the letter.

Nikhil stared at it like a fool for a while. Then a delighted smile spread across his face. A girl loved him! Well, well! What a joke!

But who was the girl?

Nikhil examined the postmark on the envelope; the seal was smudged, but it was clear the letter had been posted in Calcutta. A girl from Calcutta. Who could it be? And why write a letter? There were so many easier ways to confess love. Was she too shy to say it aloud, so she wrote instead? But why not sign her name?

Nikhil knew many girls. There were at least ten unmarried women working in his office. Besides, there were his friends’ sisters. The girls were fond of him for his lively, mischievous nature; whenever they saw him, their faces lit up with smiles. But he couldn’t believe any of them loved him in secret. And none of them were so bashful.

Standing on the veranda, letter in hand, Nikhil was pondering all this when he heard Laboni’s voice from behind—“Well, Nikhil Kaka, whose letter are you reading?”

Nikhil turned around. Jhilli and Laboni had come down from the second floor; they were carrying a few books. They often went together to the library to exchange books.

Waving the letter in the air, Nikhil said, “Whose letter! A young lady has written to me.” He puffed out his chest as he spoke.

Laboni said, “A young lady wrote to you! What did she write?”

Nikhil replied, “Oh yes, it’s a serious matter, a grave affair. She wrote that she loves me.”

Laboni and Jhilli looked at each other in astonishment, then burst out laughing. Laboni said, “Why are you making up stories, Nikhil Kaka? What young lady would love you?”

Nikhil rolled his eyes and said, “Why, can’t any young lady love me? Have you seen my looks?”

“We have. Now tell us whose letter it is.”

“I told you, a young lady’s letter!”

Jhilli asked, “What’s the young lady’s name?”

Nikhil scratched his head and said, “Name! I don’t know. There’s no name in the letter.”

Jhilli and Laboni laughed again. Laboni said, “We don’t believe a word you say.”

--- Page 990 ---
“I’m sure it’s from a creditor,” I said.

“A creditor’s letter? Well, here, see for yourself.” Nikhil thrust the letter under our noses.

Both of us read it. Laboni said, “Hmm. But even after reading it, I can’t believe that a girl has confessed her love to you. I think someone’s just pulling your leg.”

Nikhil, a little heated, replied, “Go on, what would you know about these things? These are deep matters. The world is full of traps of love—haven’t you ever heard that?”

“I have.” Jhillika and Laboni walked away, stifling their laughter.

After that, whenever Nikhil met a girl, he would look at her with eager, searching eyes. But he received no response. His mind grew ever more restless. Who was this girl? Surely someone he knew. But why this game of hide-and-seek?

About a month later, a second letter arrived. This one was a little longer—

I am a girl. I love you. Can’t you recognize me?

Receiving the letter, Nikhil’s heart was set ablaze. Laboni and Jhillika were not around, but he could not keep it to himself. On a sudden impulse, he went to Sanat’s room.

Sanat’s room was rather large; within this single chamber were gathered all the necessities of his solitary life. On one side, a thickly padded mattress was spread over the bed; the wooden frame was adorned with intricate jali work. On the other side, in front of the window, stood a desk with drawers, upon which were arranged various implements of photography: three hand-held cameras, one of them a cine-camera. There was also a mirrored wardrobe in the room. Everything was neat and spotless—one could tell at a glance that Sanat was a methodical and fastidious man.

When Nikhil entered, Sanat was seated at the desk, examining the mechanism of a camera. He glanced up briefly, then returned to his task. Nikhil, his face grave, said, “Sanatda, it’s a serious matter.”

Sanat looked up sharply once, then said, “What serious matter could possibly happen in your life? Got dysentery?”

Nikhil replied, “Not dysentery—a girl has fallen in love with me.”

This time Sanat stared at him for quite a while. At last he said, “Not dysentery, I see—it’s your head that’s gone wrong. There isn’t a girl in Bengal who’d fall in love with you.”

Nikhil said, “You don’t believe me? Here, look at this letter. I got another one about a month ago.”

Sanat glanced over the letter and handed it back, asking, “Don’t you know who the girl is?”

“No, that’s the problem.”

Sanat was silent for a moment, then said, “I see. Among your acquaintances, is there any dark, ugly girl?”

Nikhil laughed. “Most of them are dark and ugly, Sanatda.”

Sanat said, “Then it’s one of those dark, ugly girls who’s writing anonymous letters and creating a mystery. She’s trying to provoke you. If you have any sense, you’ll steer clear of them.”

But Nikhil had no power to steer clear. Besides, he bore no aversion toward dark, ugly girls. He believed that dark, ugly girls made the best wives. With redoubled zeal, he began searching for his nameless admirer.

Then Benimadhab arrived, and the atmosphere of the house changed. But to Nikhil, regularly—

--- Page 991 ---
Letters began to arrive. In the third letter, it was written—  
I love you. Haven’t you recognized me yet? But I am not a beautiful girl.  
Nikhil thought, Sanat-da was right. But he did not relent. An unexpected romance had entered his life; he had no power to dismiss it as trivial.

Meanwhile, Benimadhab spent nearly three weeks enjoying his daughter-in-law’s cooking and felt quite well. Then, one night, deep in the hours, his sleep was broken; a terrible pain gripped his stomach. Writhing in agony, he called out for Meghraj. Benimadhab sat clutching his belly with both hands and said, “Meghraj, quickly call Dr. Sen, tell him I am dying of stomach pain, he must come at once.”

Meghraj made the call, and within half an hour Dr. Sen arrived. After questioning and examining him, he began treatment. But the inflammation in the stomach was not easily subdued; after a struggle that lasted until five in the morning, the pain finally abated. Benimadhab lay on the bed, his body limp, staring wide-eyed at the doctor—“Doctor, can you tell me why this happened?”

The doctor paused, his face grave, then reluctantly replied, “It’s hard to say for certain. It could be an allergy, it could be colic, or—”

“Or—?”

“Or the effect of poison. —I suggest you stay at my nursing home for a few days. You’ll receive both treatment and proper diet.”

But Benimadhab had no faith in nursing homes; he believed that those who entered a nursing home never returned. He replied as firmly as he could, “No, doctor, I’ll stay at home.”

The doctor stood up. “Very well, I’ll take my leave now. If there’s any trouble again, inform me at once. For today and tomorrow, eat only curd.”

Meghraj accompanied the doctor downstairs and opened the main door for him; the doctor departed. Meghraj closed the door and came back upstairs. The other members of the household were still asleep, unaware of the doctor’s visit.

Lying in bed, Benimadhab stared into emptiness, deep in thought. Difficult, impenetrable thoughts. Fear greater than the love for one’s own children. Not once, but twice this had happened… His son and daughter are waiting for him to die… growing impatient seeing that he hasn’t died yet! But could his son, daughter, son-in-law, or daughter-in-law do such a thing? Why not—money is the only real thing in this world, everything else is a sham. Even the doctor suspects…

At seven in the morning, Benimadhab sat up in bed, and Meghraj shaved his beard. Then, handing him some money, he said, “Go, buy some curd from the market. Good curd, one seer.”

Taking the money, Meghraj left. He was a soldier, obeyed orders, never spoke. Nothing could be read from his face.

At half past seven, Arati arrived, the maid following her with a tray of tea and breakfast. The moment she entered, Arati started in surprise; Benimadhab was sitting on the bed, staring fixedly at her. In a faint voice, she said, “Baba—”

Benimadhab spoke slowly, “Bouma, take the food away. From today, I will arrange my own meals.”

A shadow of fear crossed Arati’s face. “Why, Baba?”

--- Page 992 ---
Beni Number 935

Benimadhab recounted the events of the previous night. Hearing it, Arati left with a darkened face.  
The matter soon spread from the maid’s lips. Upon hearing it, Gayatri rushed to her father—  
“Baba, I knew you wouldn’t be able to stomach boudi’s cooking. From today, I’ll cook again.”  
Benimadhab looked his daughter up and down and replied in a stern tone, “No—”

At three in the afternoon, having resolved his course of action, he sat up in bed and called out, “Meghraj!”  
Meghraj appeared, “Yes, sir?”  
Benimadhab asked, “Are you married?”  
Meghraj raised his brows and stared for a moment, as if trying to grasp the import of the question—“Yes, sir, I am.”  
“Any children?”  
“No, sir.”  
“Your wife can cook, I presume?”  
“Yes, sir, she can.”  
“Good. Now listen to my proposal. Go to your village and bring your wife here. There’s a vacant room downstairs; you two will stay there. Your wife will cook for me. I’ll increase your salary accordingly. Tomorrow morning, you’ll fly to Delhi—bring your wife back as soon as possible; I’ll cover all your travel expenses, including the plane fare. Well?”  
“Yes, sir.”  
“Good; now I’m at ease. But until you return, I’ll need provisions. Here’s some money—go to the market and buy two more seers of curd, some firm sandesh, a couple of large loaves of bread, butter, marmalade, tinned milk, grapes, apples—bring all these, they’ll keep in the fridge. You go to the market; meanwhile, I’ll arrange your air ticket over the telephone.”

The next day, Meghraj departed. Benimadhab was left alone. Thanks to the curd and other wholesome fare, his stomach recovered within two or three days. For leisure, he chatted on the telephone with Dr. Sen and other friends. No one came or went in the house. The door remained perpetually closed.

On the fourth day, Meghraj returned—with his wife.  
She wore a colorful sari, her face veiled. Meghraj brought her into Benimadhab’s room and lifted the veil from her face. Benimadhab saw a sweet, smiling visage. Her complexion was dusky, her kohl-lined eyes brimming with the intoxication of youth. Compared to Meghraj, she was much younger—no more than twenty or twenty-two. With both hands, she touched Benimadhab’s feet and then her own head.

Benimadhab, pleased, said, “Very good, very good. What is your name?”  
She replied, “Medini.”  
Thus began Benimadhab’s independent household. Meghraj and Medini were assigned a room in the corner downstairs. Upstairs, a room had been converted into a kitchen, utensils had arrived; in the mornings, Medini would come up from her room below and prepare Benimadhab’s tea and toast. Meanwhile, Meghraj would return from the Gariahat market with groceries. The cooking commenced—meals for three. After eating, Medini would return to her room downstairs, Meghraj—

--- Page 993 ---
Byomkesh Samagra

Upstairs, someone stands guard. From the afternoon, the rituals of tea and cooking commence once more; at eight o’clock, when everyone has finished their supper, Medini descends for the night; Benimadhab, content, retires to bed, while Meghraj, after drawing the door nearly shut, spreads his bedding right in front of it.

Such is their daily routine.

Medini has no duties at noon, and in those idle hours she has become acquainted with the rest of the household. All are drawn to her, especially the men! Her conduct is modest, yet without timidity; her words are lively, never brazen. Everyone feels at ease in her presence. Since her arrival, a new vitality seems to have infused the house. Gayatri and Aarti, who had previously harbored some aversion toward Medini, have gradually shed much of their reluctance. Only Makaranda remains entirely oblivious to Medini; whenever she is free, he is never at home.

Slowly, a sense of normalcy returned to the house. Benimadhab now felt himself much safer. Yet the blow his mind had suffered had not quite healed. In the depths of night, his sleep would break. Lying in the darkness, he would think—my own son, my own daughter, wish for my death. Is it possible, or is it merely my baseless suspicion? After lying awake for a long time, he would silently rise from bed, quietly crack open the half-closed door, and see Meghraj sleeping, guarding the entrance. Reassured, he would return to bed.

Medini’s arrival brought another convenience. According to Calcutta custom, the main door always remains closed, opened only for comings and goings. Earlier, if someone arrived from outside, there would be much banging and shouting at the door; now, that is no longer necessary. Medini’s room is right beside the main entrance, and at night, if anyone knocks, Medini comes and opens the door.

The great poet Kalidasa once wrote—one cannot tell, seeing the placid surface of a lake, that fierce aquatic beasts prowl its depths.

A month passed. In the meantime, several small but noteworthy incidents occurred in the house—Nikhil once again received a letter from his mysterious lady—“I love you. You know how to laugh, how to make others laugh. No one in our house ever laughs. Will you marry me?” Receiving the letter, Nikhil was nearly beside himself with delight; clutching it, he pranced about his room like a madman, then went to Sanat’s room. Nikhil’s room was much like Sanat’s in size and shape, but far more disorderly. The bedding on the taktaposh was twisted into knots, a thick layer of dust coated the table. One glance revealed—a housewife’s touch was sorely needed here.

Sanat was just then heading out with his camera. Nikhil said, “What’s this, Sanat-da, all dressed up—where are you off to?”

Sanat replied, “There’s a party at the Grand Hotel. What’s that in your hand?”

Nikhil held up the letter and said, “Another letter! Read it. Even if this girl is dark and ugly, blind or lame, I’ll marry her all the same.”

Sanat read the letter and said, “Hmm, sounds like she might be blind and lame. Well, marry her if you want—who’s stopping you? But first you’ll have to find the girl!”

Sanat locked his own room. As he passed Meghraj’s door, he saw Medini was inside. Sanat paused and said, “Medini, I’ll be late tonight. I’m going to a party to take photographs, can’t say when I’ll be back. If I knock at the door, please open it for me.”

--- Page 994 ---
Beni Sanghar

Medini stood by her own door. By now, she could understand Bengali quite well, though she could not speak it. Lowering her eyes, she replied softly, “Ji.”

After Sanat had left, Nikhil came and stood before Medini. He said, “Medini, tum janta hai, ektho larki hamko bhalobasa mein gir gaya. Ham uske shadi karega.”

A glint of mischief danced in Medini’s eyes. She pressed her laughter behind the edge of her sari and quietly closed the door.

Since Medini’s arrival, Gangadhar’s mind had grown restless. It was a dangerous age; youth, before departing, was biting back with a venomous sting. When Gangadhar went out in the afternoons, his gaze would linger toward Medini’s door. If, by chance, their eyes met, he would not look away, but stare fixedly; Medini, leaning against the threshold, would lower her gaze and accept his attention in silence. She was accustomed to the greedy gaze of men.

Ajay’s demeanor was rather different. He seemed to look upon Medini with a kind of paternal affection; he would sit with her on the mat and chat, ask after her village. Medini spoke simply, smiling inwardly.

At first, Makaranda did not see Medini for several days. Once, he did not return home for three or four days; it was learned that the police had arrested him for throwing stones at a police van. On the fourth night, released at half past ten, he knocked at the main door. Medini opened it. Makaranda’s face was gaunt, his shirt torn, hair wild; he fixed Medini with a piercing stare and asked in a harsh voice, “Who are you?”

“I am Medini.”

“Oh—the wife of Meghraj.” Twisting his face into a sneer, he looked her up and down, then climbed the stairs without another word. Medini knew who Makaranda was; she suppressed a smile and returned to her room.

Three months passed, and when Beni Madhab’s stomach gave him no further trouble, he became certain there was nothing wrong with his digestion, his strength to digest remained intact. His suspicions toward his daughter-in-law and daughter hardened into certainty. Then, one night, he awoke from a nightmare—someone was twisting a knife into his throat.

After that, he could not sleep again. The rest of the night passed in anxious thought, his mind tangled in the fear of death and worldly concerns.

The next day, at half past ten, he telephoned his solicitor. “Sudhanshu Babu, I wish to make a will. I cannot move about much; could you come over?”

Beni Madhab was an old client, a man of means from Malda. Sudhanshu Babu replied, “I’ll come in the afternoon.”

That afternoon, Sudhanshu Babu arrived. With the door closed, the two of them discussed the terms of the will for nearly an hour and a half; Sudhanshu Babu took many notes. At last, he said, “The day after tomorrow, I’ll bring the prepared will. You can read and sign it. I’ll bring two witnesses as well.”

After dusk, Sanat and Nikhil came to sit with Beni Madhab, inquiring after his health. Medini was cooking in the next room; Beni Madhab served his nephews tea and fried potatoes.

After they had left, Beni Madhab called Meghraj and said, “Go upstairs and call everyone down.”

