Everyone was present on the upper floor except Makrand, who came running as soon as he was summoned. Jhillī and Lābani came too. Benimadhab was seated by the side of his cot; he called his two granddaughters and made them sit on either side of him. Then, casting a grave glance at his sons, daughters-in-law, daughter, and son-in-law, he said in a solemn tone, “I have arranged to make a will. I wanted to inform you of the arrangements beforehand.”

Everyone looked on, faces tense with apprehension. Benimadhab began to speak slowly, “After my death, you will not receive my liquid assets in hand. I have arranged for annuities; just as you receive monthly allowances now, so shall you continue to do. I have fixed the amount with care, so that under no circumstances should you suffer financial hardship. As for the house, as long as you live, you will enjoy it equally, but you may not sell it.”

The four of them stood, faces clouded with disappointment. Placing his hands on the shoulders of his two granddaughters, Benimadhab said, “For Jhillī and Lābani, I have already taken out endowment insurance; when they turn twenty-one, each will receive fifty thousand rupees. Besides, I have decided to see them married myself. You need not worry about your daughters’ marriages. For Lābani, there is already a good match—a young man, a lieutenant in the military. For Jhillī, I have not yet found a suitable groom, but as soon as I do, I shall marry them both off together.” A hint of contentment flickered across his face, but it vanished as quickly as it came; frowning, he added, “I had thought to leave Makrand a separate fifty thousand as well, but he has grown most insolent and ill-mannered—I shall leave him nothing.”

Benimadhab fell silent, and his listeners too remained quiet; not a word from anyone. At last, Gangadhar cleared his throat and said indistinctly, “It is your property to dispose of as you wish; we have nothing to say. Only, the value of money changes—today it is one thing, tomorrow another—”

Gayatri interrupted her husband, speaking in a heavy voice, “Father, whatever you give, we shall accept with bowed heads. Has the will been signed?”

Benimadhab did not look at anyone; turning his face away, he said, “I have had the lawyer draw up the will; it will be signed tomorrow or the day after. Yes, there is one condition I have not told you. The will shall contain a clause: if I die an unnatural death, none of you will receive a single paisa—everything will go to Calcutta University.”

At these words, everyone stood stunned for a moment, then slowly filed out of the room.

Night descended. At the usual hour, Benimadhab finished his supper and retired to bed. Meghraj and Medini dined in the adjoining room; Meghraj bolted the front door, spread his bedding, and lay down as a guard, while Medini went to her own room.

Upstairs, a heavy silence hung in the air. Lābani’s dance master had come, but no one in the house had any taste for dance that night. Parag and Lābani spoke quietly in secret, then slipped away, silent as shadows, to see a film. Whether anyone noticed their departure is doubtful.

Nikhil had left for work soon after dusk; he was a nocturnal man, working through the night and returning at dawn.

Around nine o’clock, Sanat set out with his camera, pausing before Medini’s door to say, “Medini, I’m going to Bardhaman; there’s a music and dance soirée there tomorrow morning. I’ll return sometime in the afternoon. You need not wait up to open the door for me tonight.” He smiled a little as he spoke.

Medini met his gaze for a moment and replied, “Yes, sir.”

Sanat departed. Almost at once, Makrand arrived from outside, and in a harsh tone said to Medini—

--- Page 996 ---
Beni Sanghar 939

He said, "Bolt the door. If anyone comes looking for me tonight, tell them I’m not at home." Without waiting for a reply, he went upstairs. Medini slid the bolt across the main door.

Then the mysterious curtain of night descended upon the house.

At dawn the next morning, as Medini went to open the main door, she noticed the door was merely pulled to, not bolted. She frowned in puzzlement, then cracked the door open a little; outside, Nikhil was visible, returning after finishing his work. As soon as their eyes met, he grinned and said, "You people are starting your work, mine is done. Now I’ll sleep soundly."

Nikhil went off to his own room. Medini left the door ajar, since the maid would soon come to work on the second floor. Then she climbed the stairs to the third floor to prepare her master’s tea.

Barely a minute had passed when a piercing, desperate cry rang out from the third floor, followed by a heavy thud. Nikhil, in his room, had just taken off his shirt and was about to remove his vest; at the sound of the scream, he rushed upstairs just as he was. The others on the second floor came running too, and together they reached the third floor almost at once. Then, in front of Benimadhab’s door, they froze at the horrific sight.

Meghraj lay on the bed, face up, his throat slashed from one side to the other; thick pools of blood had gathered on the pillow and the bed. Medini had collapsed in a faint at his feet.

For a few moments, no one could utter a word. Then Nikhil cried out, "Uncle—uncle, are you alive?"

Gayatri, Arati, and Jhilli burst into tears, while Ajay and Gangadhar stood as if turned to stone; it was as though none of them had the strength to move. Nikhil then stepped over Meghraj and pushed at the closed door. The door swung open; through the open doorway, they saw Benimadhab lying on the bed, a dark pool of blood congealed beneath his throat. Just as Meghraj’s throat had been cut with a weapon, Benimadhab had been murdered in exactly the same way, with the same weapon.

A cacophony of weeping broke out. Nikhil stood motionless for a moment, then rushed into the room and grabbed the telephone. First he called his newspaper office, then the police station.

Three

Byomkesh arrived at the scene to find police guarding the main door. A constable, seeing Byomkesh, saluted and said, "Inspector sahib is in the downstairs baithak-khana."

In the spacious drawing room, Inspector Rakhal Sarkar and two sub-inspectors were present; they were gathered around the center table, discussing a file. As soon as Byomkesh entered, Rakhalbabu came over to him, gave a rueful smile, and said, "We’re entangled, Byomkesh-da. You’ve named it well—Beni Sanghar. I’ve heard the real meaning of ‘Beni Sanghar’ is the untying of a braid; women first plait their hair, then twist the braid into a bun. This case is much the same; such a tangled, intricate knot that untying this braid has become a formidable task. The motive for the murders is clear, and the number of suspects is limited to five; yet we can’t pinpoint exactly which one did it."

"Come, let’s sit." The two of them squeezed into chairs side by side. "Now, tell me everything."

--- Page 997 ---
Rakhallababu recounted to Byomkesh all the information he had gathered since yesterday. Several facts emerged through their questions and answers. Byomkesh asked, “What’s the motive?”

“The old man’s vast wealth. He gave his son and daughter a monthly allowance, but it never satisfied them. Dr. Abinash Sen suspects that the daughter and daughter-in-law were trying to poison the old man. If that’s true, then there’s a conspiracy between the son and the son-in-law. In these times, nothing is impossible.”

“What was the purpose of killing Meghraj?”

“Meghraj used to lay his bedding in front of Benimadhab’s door at night. The door would be left ajar, so that if Benimadhab called, he could enter immediately. Therefore, it was impossible to enter the room without first getting rid of him. If someone tried to step over him, he would wake up. That’s why he had to be killed first.”

“The murder weapon hasn’t been found?”

“No. But the post-mortem revealed that the weapon was extremely sharp. Both victims were killed with the same weapon. With a single stroke, their throats were slit wide open.”

“Has the time of death been determined?”

“Roughly between midnight and three in the morning.”

“Hmm. Who are the five suspects?”

“Ajay and his wife Arati, Gayatri and her husband Gangadhar, and Ajay’s son Makrand. Makrand once slapped Meghraj on Benimadhab’s orders. Jhillie can be ruled out—she’s just a child, she has no motive.”

“What does the boy Makrand do?”

“He’s caught up in politics. He’s enrolled in college, that’s all. That night, he came home around nine, and after that, no one knows when he left again. He’s been missing ever since. I’ve issued a warrant for his arrest.”

“Who’s at home now?”

“Ajay, Arati, Gangadhar, Gayatri, Jhillie, Nikhil Roy, Sanat Ganguly, and Meghraj’s widow Medini. Ajay’s daughter Laboni went to see a film with her dance teacher that night and hasn’t returned. Nikhil and Sanat went out for work that night and came back the next day. I’ve forbidden anyone in the house from leaving.”

“You’ve taken everyone’s fingerprints, I presume.”

“Yes, I have.”

“Did you find anything in the house search?”

“Nothing suspicious.”

“Good. Now let me see the statement records.”

“Here they are.” Rakhallababu lifted a file from the table and handed it to Byomkesh. At that moment, a constable appeared at the main door to announce that a gentleman named Sudhanshu Bagchi, a solicitor, wished to see them. Rakhallababu said, “Bring him in.”

Sudhanshubabu entered the room, a neatly folded newspaper in hand. Looking at Rakhallababu, he said, “I am Benimadhabbabu’s solicitor. As soon as I opened the paper today—”

“Please, have a seat.”

Sudhanshubabu sat down on a chair. Rakhallababu stood before him, and Byomkesh stepped forward as well.

