“No fingerprints were found.”

Byomkesh grasped the handle and opened the fridge. The interior light flickered on; the fridge was running. Inside were all manner of fruits, rows of eggs, fish, meat, bottles of milk. Byomkesh closed the door again.

On the back wall of the room hung a long mirror. Beneath it, on a shelf, lay a comb, a brush, hair oil, and shaving implements. The peculiarity of the shaving kit was this: the razor was not a safety razor, but an old-fashioned folding straight razor. Byomkesh carefully picked up the sheathed blade and asked, “Have you looked at the razor itself?”

Rakhalbabu’s eyes widened a little. “No. Benimadhab never shaved himself. Meghraj used to shave him every morning.”

Byomkesh, with great care, drew the razor from its sheath, held it between two fingers, and carried it to the window, turning it this way and that under the light. Then, in a tone of surprise, he exclaimed, “Remarkable!”

He handed the razor to Rakhalbabu. “Look—there are no fingerprints anywhere.”

Rakhalbabu examined the blade minutely, then returned it to Byomkesh, gazing at his face. The two men’s eyes remained locked for some time. Then Byomkesh slid the razor back into its sheath and slipped it into his own pocket. “I’m taking this with me.”

“What will you do with it?”

“Shave.”

There was nothing of interest in the other two rooms upstairs. Yet Byomkesh wandered through them, casting a careful eye. Then he descended to the ground floor and said to Rakhalbabu, “I’ll be going now. I’ll return in the afternoon. Give me the statement file—I’ll read it at home.”

Rakhalbabu said, “I have to go to the thana for a bit. Come, I’ll drop you off. What do you think of all this?”

Byomkesh smiled faintly. “Kshurasya dhara nishita duratyaya—”

Rakhalbabu handed him the statement file, and together they left in the police van. Two sub-inspectors and several lower-ranking officers remained stationed at the house.

At three in the afternoon, Byomkesh returned. Rakhalbabu had already come back; Byomkesh returned the razor and the statement file, then sat down in a chair. Rakhalbabu asked, “How was the shave?”

Byomkesh shook his head. “Not good.”

“And the statements?”

“Medini’s statement is the most informative. I want to question her further.”

“Very well, I’ll send for her. She’s in her own room.”

But before Medini could be summoned, two plainclothes policemen entered the room with Makrand in tow. Makrand’s clothes were torn, his body and face smeared with dust, his eyes bloodshot like hibiscus blooms. It was clear he had not surrendered to the police without a fight. One of the plainclothesmen said, “We’ve caught Makrand Chakraborty, sir.”

Rakhalbabu scrutinized Makrand from head to toe. “So this is Makrand Chakraborty! Where did you catch him?”

“At the racecourse. He was gambling, sir. Had a lot of money in his pockets. Here.”

A wad of five and ten-rupee notes. Rakhalbabu counted—nearly a hundred and seventy-five rupees.

He began his interrogation. “Your name is Makrand Chakraborty?”

