The two plainclothes policemen escorted Makrand upstairs and then left. Rakhalbabu said, ‘Shall I send for Medini?’

Byomkesh stood up and said, ‘No, let’s go to her room ourselves. Too many interruptions here.’

They knocked on Medini’s door and entered to find her lying on a mat spread on the floor. Seeing Byomkesh and Rakhalbabu, she sat up. She wore a grey sari, no vermilion on her forehead, no ornaments on her hands, neck, or ears. Her face was a little puffy; the marks of grief had not yet faded, though the restlessness of mourning was gone. She rose slowly to her feet and looked at the two men with questioning eyes.

Rakhalbabu said kindly, ‘Medini, this is my friend. He wants to ask you a few more questions, in addition to those I’ve already asked.’

Medini replied in a broken, subdued voice, ‘Yes.’

Byomkesh gazed steadily at Medini’s face, and after a while, asked, ‘How long ago did you marry Meghraj?’

Medini answered in a faint voice, ‘Five years ago.’

‘Were you his first wife?’

‘No, there was someone before me. She died.’

‘Hmm.’ Byomkesh looked around the room. Among the furniture were a taktaposh, a wooden wardrobe, and a standing coat rack. Under the taktaposh, two large trunks were visible.

