She went over and dragged a trunk out from under the cot, took a key from the end of her sari, knelt, and began to unlock the trunk. Meanwhile, Byomkesh wandered about the room, finally coming to stand by the window. The toilette box was resting atop the window ledge. Byomkesh hesitated a moment, then lifted the lid. Inside were the usual feminine toiletries and trinkets; in one corner of the mirror was a photograph of Medini. The picture was about the size of half a postcard; Medini sat by the edge of a bed. An utterly domestic scene, but Medini’s open, radiant smile struck Byomkesh like a thorn. Looking at her now, it was impossible to imagine she could ever smile like that. Byomkesh closed the box in silence.

When Medini returned with the papers from the trunk, Byomkesh was speaking in a low voice to Rakhal-babu. Taking the papers from Medini, he read them carefully. Rakhal-babu read along with him. Then, returning the papers to Medini, Byomkesh said, “Keep these safe, we may need them later. Come, Rakhal.”

Once outside the room, Rakhal-babu cast a sidelong glance at Byomkesh. “Well, what do you think?”

Byomkesh replied, “Very good. Now I’d like to see the rest of the household, one by one. Is everyone home?”

“Everyone’s here, except for Ajay’s daughter Laboni. On the night of the murder, Laboni went out that evening with her dance master; we haven’t found her yet. Of the others, whom would you like to see first?”

“I have no preference. Let’s begin with the ground floor.”

