Rakhalbabu knocked on Nikhil’s door. Nikhil came and opened it, standing there with lathered cheeks and a safety razor in hand. He grinned through the foam—“Come in, Inspector Babu.”

Rakhalbabu entered the room with Byomkesh and asked, “Shaving in the evening?”

Nikhil replied, “I’m a night owl, you see, so I shave in the evening. Those who work during the day shave in the morning.” Then, with a note of urgency, he said, “Inspector Babu, let me go for just one hour, let me visit the office once. I swear on my life, I won’t run away. If you don’t trust me, send two constables with me.”

Rakhalbabu laughed. “Why are you so eager to go to the office? You seem comfortable here.”

Nikhil said, “No, Inspector Babu, I’m not comfortable. The addiction to work pulls me towards the office, I can’t sleep at night. Besides—”

“Besides what?”

Nikhil, a little sheepishly, said, “There are many unmarried girls working at the office. I’m searching for one among them—if I find her, I’ll marry her.”

“The matter seems rather mysterious.”

“It would, wouldn’t it? It’s a deeply mysterious affair.”

“If it’s so mysterious, then seek his help. This is Sri Byomkesh Bakshi.”

The shaving foam on Nikhil’s cheeks had begun to dry and flake off. He gaped at Byomkesh—“Eh, you’re the satyanweshi Byomkesh Bakshi! I hadn’t noticed all this time.” With the safety razor still in hand, he folded his palms and said, “You must solve my mystery, Byomkeshbabu. Otherwise, I have no hope left.”

“Tell me everything.”

Nikhil rattled off his secret in a single breath. After listening, Byomkesh said, “Let me see the letters.”

Nikhil went to the bed, fetched a few envelopes from beneath the pillow, and handed them to Byomkesh. Byomkesh opened the envelopes, read the letters one by one, then put them back and slipped them into his own pocket. “I’ll keep these. Let’s see if I can find any clues. For now, stay in this house—I’ll make inquiries at your office. By the way, do you have a raincoat?”

“Raincoat—waterproof? Yes, I have one. Why do you ask?”

“Let me see it.”

Nikhil went into the adjoining bathroom and returned with an old khaki-colored raincoat. Byomkesh handed it to Rakhalbabu and said, “We’ll take this with us as well. When did you last use it?”

Nikhil, scratching his head as if he understood nothing, replied, “Last monsoon, about five or six months ago. What trick are you playing, that you’ll find—her address from my waterproof?”

Byomkesh only smiled, lips pressed together, and said, “I see you shave with a safety razor.”

“What else should I shave with?”

“Quite right. Now that you’ve started shaving, the old straight razor has gone out of fashion. —Very well.”

