Sanat opened the almirah and took out a plastic-wrapped parcel. Inside the wrapping was a neatly folded, elegant, transparent raincoat. Rakhalbabu took it, drew it out of the wrapping, and held it up at its full length. It was an expensive raincoat, almost new. He folded it again, placed it back in the wrapping, and said, “I’m taking this with me. You’ll get it back in two days. I’ll give you a receipt.”

Sanat replied in a listless, discontented voice, “What’s the point of a receipt? This is your kingdom—do as you please.”

Byomkesh suddenly asked, “I noticed in your statement that on the night Benimadhab-babu was murdered, you had gone to Burdwan. Which train did you take?”

Sanat said, “The ten-thirty night train.”

“Why did you take the night train instead of the morning one?”

“If I’d taken the morning train, I wouldn’t have reached on time. There was a gathering in the morning.”

“Do you have a place to stay in Burdwan?”

“No, I spent the night sitting on a bench at the station.”

“You must have had tea at the tea stall?”

“I don’t drink tea.”

“So, there’s no witness or evidence that you took the ten-thirty train to Burdwan?”

Sanat’s brows arched again. “What’s the need for witnesses and evidence? Do you suspect that I killed my uncle?”

Byomkesh, a little discomfited, said, “Not at all. But we need to be certain about everyone.”

Sanat replied in a dry voice, “Go and look for alibis among those who stood to gain from my uncle’s death. That will be more useful.”

“True enough. Come on, Rakhal, let’s go upstairs now.”

First, in the drawing room, Rakhalbabu handed Sanat’s raincoat to the sub-inspector and said, “Ticket it—Sanat Ganguli.” Then he led Byomkesh upstairs.

Ajay was sitting in the front room, having a meager breakfast with tea, and stood up with a worried face as they entered. He wore the unkempt garb of mourning, with stubble sprouting on his face. Rakhalbabu said gravely, “A letter has arrived for you.” He took a postcard from his pocket and handed it to Ajay.

Byomkesh watched Ajay intently; he saw, as Ajay read the letter, a rapid play of various emotions flicker across his face: anxiety—astonishment—relief—exultation. Relief, above all. For a man of Ajay’s temperament, perhaps this was only natural; if a daughter’s marriage could be settled without expense or trouble, joy would be the expected response.

But when he finally raised his head, there was a sorrowful, plaintive look on his face, something of the stage about it. He heaved a deep sigh. “My daughter! Inspector-babu, my only daughter has run away and married someone. The boys and girls of today are so cruel, so selfish—they don’t think of their parents. Well, what’s done is done. Still, if only she had married within our caste. Well, if it’s for the best, so be it.” He sighed theatrically again.

Perhaps to bring the performance to a close, Rakhalbabu said, “This is Byomkesh Bakshi. You’ve probably heard the name.”

Ajay sprang to his feet and stared wide-eyed; his expression was one of fear.

--- Page 1005 ---
Whether it was astonishment or joy that showed on his face, it was hard to tell. Then, in a voice trembling with emotion, he exclaimed, “Never heard your name! What are you saying, sir, never heard your name! Please, please, come in—what good fortune is mine. Byomkesh Babu has come; now, at last, the mystery of my father’s death will be solved.” Turning towards the inner doorway, he raised his voice and called out, “Listen, dear, bring two cups of tea quickly. —Please, sit, sit, I’ll see to it myself.” With that, he vanished swiftly into the inner rooms.

Byomkesh, amused by such effusive hospitality, glanced at Rakhalbabu and suppressed a smile; the two of them settled side by side into the chairs.

A few moments later, Ajay returned, followed by Arati, her head half-covered with her sari’s pallu. In her hands was a tray bearing two cups of tea and some biscuits. Her face was drawn with fear; she set the tray before Byomkesh and turned to leave, but Ajay said, “Hey, why are you going? Speak to Byomkesh Babu.”

Arati halted, turning towards Byomkesh, but no words came from her lips. Noticing her discomfort, Byomkesh said kindly, “No, no, let her go about her work. I have nothing to ask her.”

Arati left. Ajay, a bit embarrassed, said, “My wife is very shy, but we are both your admirers—” He was about to say more, but Byomkesh interrupted, “Your son Makrand is at home, isn’t he?”

Ajay, startled, replied, “Of course. Shall I call him?”

Byomkesh said, “That may not be necessary. He’s in college, so during the monsoon he must need a raincoat. I’d like to see his raincoat.”

Ajay thought for a moment and said, “About a year and a half ago, I bought him a waterproof. It must be here somewhere, I’ll check.”

Ajay went inside. Byomkesh and Rakhalbabu lifted their teacups.

Five minutes later, Ajay returned, his face downcast. “I couldn’t find the waterproof. I asked Makrand—he said he didn’t know.”

Byomkesh set down his cup and dabbed his mouth with a handkerchief. “Do you have a waterproof of your own?”

“Yes. Shall I bring it?”

“And your wife’s and daughter’s waterproofs?”

“There’s only one ladies’ waterproof for the women.”

“Would you kindly bring those two? We’ll take them with us. You’ll have them back in a few days.”

“You’ll take them? Very well, I’ll fetch them.”

Ajay went inside and returned, draping two waterproofs over his arms. Rakhalbabu folded them neatly and tucked them under his arm, saying, “Well, we’ll take our leave now. Thank you for the tea.”

Ajay, looking a bit sheepish, said to Byomkesh, “You’re leaving? There’s a request I haven’t had the courage to make—”

“What is it?”

“I’d like to take your photograph. I have a camera—if you permit, may I take one? I’ll enlarge it and hang it in my room.”

Byomkesh laughed. “You want to take my photograph! Well—why not? No one has ever wanted to enlarge my picture and hang it in their home before.”

Ajay hurried off and returned with his camera—a simple box camera. He said, “There’s still enough—”

