No. There is one thing to remember about Makrand—on Benimadhab’s orders, Meghraj had slapped him across the face; that is to say, he bore a deep grudge against both of them. That night, he returned home at nine o’clock, and then, at some point in the dead of night, he slipped out of the house—no one knows exactly when. When he was caught at the racecourse, there was nearly two hundred rupees in his pocket. He refuses to say where he got so much money.

‘Anyway, let me tell you why I collected the raincoats. Those who plot to slit a sleeping man’s throat know that, while this is a silent way to kill, there is a great risk of the murderer’s own clothes getting drenched in blood. Bloodstains are not easily washed out; the marks remain. That’s why, in Western countries, when committing murder, the killer dons a raincoat; any blood that gets on the tela of the raincoat can be washed off easily. Anyone who has read Western crime thrillers knows this. We sent the raincoats, each tagged with its owner’s name, to the laboratory for scientific examination.

