An hour had passed. Having dispatched the two accused into custody, Rakhalbabu had lifted the siege from the house. Outside, evening was thickening into dusk. Rakhalbabu entered Sanat’s room, unlocked the almirah, and began to open the albums one by one, leafing through them. Byomkesh wandered the room, lost in thought, drawing on his cigarette.

At last, Rakhalbabu selected an album and sat down before the table, absorbed in the photographs. On each page, a portrait of a scantily clad young woman. As a hunter hangs a tiger’s skin on the wall as a trophy, so too, in his own way, had Sanat done with these women. When the album was finished, Rakhalbabu sighed, lit a cigarette, and said, “There is a seed of madness in Sanat Ganguly’s blood, but there is no doubt he was a connoisseur of pleasure.”

Byomkesh came over, flipped through the pages, then remarked, “Shri Shankaracharya said, ‘Woman is the gateway to hell.’ Sanat opened many such gates, and so, in the end, his descent into hell became inevitable.”

“But it is astonishing to think Sanat would commit such a dreadful act for a woman like Medini.”

“Rakhal, do not underestimate a woman like Medini. In every age, women of this kind have been born—sometimes in the houses of the rich, sometimes among the poor—solely to bring about the ruin of men. Draupadi was such a woman—the root of the Kurukshetra war lies with her. Helen of the Iliad was the same. Even in this age, there is no shortage of such women. Not all of them are without character, but there is something in them that can inflame a man—especially a depraved man like Sanat—drive him to madness, strip him of all sense. Alexandre Dumas once said something profound—cherchez la femme: wherever such things occur, look for the woman, at the root there is always a woman.”

“True enough.” Rakhalbabu stood up. “So it seems neither Benimadhab’s daughter nor his daughter-in-law tried to poison him; it was the old man’s worn-out stomach to blame. Come, let’s be off. Evening has fallen, and my soul cries out for a cup of hot tea.”

“Come to my house, we can have a proper tea.”

“An excellent suggestion.”

Stepping outside, Rakhalbabu locked the door, then, heading toward the main entrance, he stopped short. He saw Jhilli descending the stairs, followed by the maid carrying a large tray laden with tea things and plates of kachuri and nimki. Byomkesh said, “Rakhal, the gods have heard your soul’s cry. Come, let’s sit in the drawing room.”

Rakhalbabu, ever cautious, said, “Wait, let’s not trust until we see with our own eyes.”

Jhilli approached them and said shyly, “Mother has sent tea and snacks for you.”

“You see?” They all entered the drawing room. The maid set the tray on the table and left; Jhilli was about to follow her, but Byomkesh said, “Jhilli, we are very tired; pour us our tea, let us sit and eat.”

Jhilli returned, poured tea from the pot, and set the plates of snacks before them. Byomkesh, eyes half-closed, chewed his kachuri and watched as Jhilli tiptoed toward the door.

“Jhilli, listen, don’t go. I have something to say to you.”

Jhilli, startled, stopped, then slowly returned to stand beside Byomkesh. Byomkesh glanced meaningfully at Rakhalbabu; Rakhalbabu lounged idly,

--- Page 1015 ---
Byomkesh Samagra

Finishing his cup of tea, he hummed a snatch of a song and stepped out of the room.

Jhillī remained standing beside Byomkesh. The turmoil in her heart was not betrayed by her face. Byomkesh gave a short, quiet laugh and said, “Technically, Nikhil is your uncle, true, but the relation is quite distant. Legally, there is nothing to prevent a marriage.”

In the shadowy gloom of the room, it was impossible to see, but Jhillī’s face had flushed. Then, in a faint voice, she asked, “How did you know?”

Byomkesh replied, “Silly girl! Every single letter bore the imprint of your fingers. Now, go and sit in that chair in the corner. There’s more to be discussed.”

Like a timid mouse, Jhillī vanished into the dark corner of the room. It was as though, apart from Byomkesh, no one else remained.

Outside, the sound of two pairs of shoes could be heard. Rakhalbabu returned, bringing Nikhil with him.

“Rakhal, turn on the light.”

The switch was by the door. Rakhalbabu pressed it, and several bright bulbs flared to life. Without looking anywhere else, Nikhil came and sat beside Byomkesh, gazing at him with affectionate eyes. He said, “Byomkesh-da, you’re a magician. Sanat-da is my cousin, I’ve known him all my life, but I could never have imagined he was such a man.”

Byomkesh said, “Nikhil, if one could read a man’s mind from his face, there would be no need for law, courts, police, or truth-seekers like me; you too could have known, just by looking, which girl was sending you those anonymous letters.”

“True, true indeed.” Nikhil edged even closer to Byomkesh, and, like a conspirator, whispered, “Have you figured something out?”

Byomkesh smiled. “First, tell me—if you were to find out who the girl is, what would you do?”

Nikhil’s eyes shone with excitement. “What would I do? I’d marry her. Blind, lame, Kaffir, or Habshi—I’d marry her, no matter what.”

Byomkesh said, “Then the search is over. Jhillī, come here.”

Startled, Nikhil looked toward the door. In the corner of the room, there was no sign or sound from Jhillī; she was hiding behind a chair. Nikhil turned back to Byomkesh, his voice trembling with excitement. “Whom are you calling?”

“Let me show you—” Byomkesh got up, took Jhillī by the hand, and drew her out, bringing her to Nikhil. “Here is your cicada! You can’t see a cicada, only hear its song. But we have caught her.”

Nikhil’s hair stood on end, his eyes seemed about to pop out of his head. He threw up both hands and cried out, “Eh! Jhillī—Jhillī writes me letters! Jhillī loves me! But—but she’s my niece!”

Byomkesh laughed. “No need to worry, no need to worry. Jhillī is a clever girl—she hasn’t given her heart to the wrong man. Your relation is such that there’s nothing to prevent a marriage.”

Jhillī’s head was bowed, a timid smile flickering at the corner of her lips. Gradually, a broad grin spread across Nikhil’s face. He said, “Ah, what extraordinary girls there are these days, Byomkesh-da! She had me dancing to her tune. Well, I’ll have my turn too. Once the wedding’s done—”

At that moment, Gangadhar appeared at the door, stick in hand, perhaps on his evening round of duties.

--- Page 1016 ---
Beni Sanhar 959

He was about to leave. Hearing voices from inside the room, he entered. In that brief moment, his temper had flared up again to its highest pitch. Fixing his gaze on Rakhalbabu, he said in a harsh tone, “Your work here is done. Why are you still here?”

Before Rakhalbabu could reply, his eyes fell on Jhilli. Instantly, his brows knotted in a terrifying frown as he barked, “Jhilli! What are you doing here among the men?”

At the sight of her father, Jhilli had frozen like a statue; now she started in fright and tried to hide behind Byomkesh. Gangadhar said, “Impudent girl! You’ve grown too fond of men’s company. I’ll thrash you till you’re red.”

Suddenly, Nikhil seemed to lose his composure. With a leap, he stood before Gangadhar and said, “Mind your words. I will marry Jhilli.”

For a moment, Gangadhar was taken aback, then he shrieked at the top of his lungs, “What! You’ll marry my daughter, you wretched printing press ghost! I’ll break your bones!” He began brandishing his stick.

At this point, Gayatri entered the room, her eyes blazing as she looked around. “What’s going on? What’s all this shouting about?”

Gangadhar paid her no heed, shouting, “Get out of my house! Such insolence from you! You want to marry my daughter!”

Jhilli ran to her mother, threw her arms around her neck, and whispered, “Ma, if you object, I’ll take poison and die.” Faced with the extremity of the situation, words finally came to Jhilli’s lips.

Gayatri looked at Nikhil closely, as if she had never seen him before. Nikhil went and touched her feet. He said, “Didi, Jhilli wants to marry me— I mean, I want to marry Jhilli. Byomkesh-da said there’s no bar in the relationship.”

Gayatri asked Byomkesh, “Is it true there’s no bar in the relationship?”

Byomkesh replied, “No, they’re not first cousins. There’s no bar.”

Gangadhar raised his voice even higher, shouting, “I don’t want to hear it, I won’t listen to anything. Get out of my house, all of you— get out this instant—”

Gayatri cut him off sharply, “Enough from you. This house is not yours, it’s mine. I’ve spoken to Sudhanshubabu; father died before making a will, so by law, half of all his property is mine, half of this house is mine. —You can go wherever you were going. I’ll do what needs to be done.”

Gangadhar deflated like a pricked toy balloon, then, head bowed, left the room.

Gayatri freed herself from Jhilli’s embrace, took her by the hand, and sat on the sofa. Looking at Nikhil, she commanded like a judge, “Now tell me what mischief you two have been up to.”

Nikhil said, “I don’t know anything, Didi, ask her. Byomkesh-da, where are the letters?”

Byomkesh took the letters from his pocket and handed them over, saying, “Rakhal, come, it’s time for us to go. Gayatri Devi, thank you so much for the tea. Nikhil, you are truly fortunate to have found such a bride—few are so lucky. Jhilli, you are not less fortunate either. In life, the rarest thing is that rare smile, and you have found it. May waves of laughter ripple through your lives. —Come, Rakhal.”

--- Page 1017 ---
The Iron Biscuit

Komolbabu said, “I live in the neighborhood, on the edge of Hindusthan Park. I’ve seen you many times, wanted to introduce myself, but never had the courage. Today, I’ve found a pretext, so I thought I’d use this excuse to finally make your acquaintance. A small problem has arisen in my life—”

“A problem!” Byomkesh pushed his cigarette case forward and said, “Go on, go on, it’s been ages since I’ve set eyes on such a thing.”

It was a summer Sunday morning. We were sitting in Byomkesh’s Keyatala house, talking.

Komolbabu’s appearance was much like that of Narugopal, but his face bore an alert, intelligent expression. Smiling, he took a cigarette and lit it, then began his story. “My name is Komolkṛishna Das. There’s a branch of the Bharat Central Bank nearby, and I’m the cashier there. About a year and a half ago, I was transferred here from Purulia.

“Upon arriving in Calcutta, I ran into trouble; I couldn’t find a place to stay anywhere. At last, a man let me have a room on the ground floor of his house. I didn’t bring my family—my wife and daughter stayed behind in Purulia, and I moved in alone.

“The landlord’s name was Akshay Mondal. The house was two-storied; two rooms below, two above, with separate entrances. Akshay Mondal lived alone upstairs, but people came and went to see him. He was a sweet-tongued man, but I could never figure out what he did for a living. Occasionally, he would come to my room for a chat, but he never invited me upstairs. He didn’t mix with the other neighbors, either. At our bank, he had an active account.

“Anyway, about three months passed like this, and then, one holiday evening, I was invited to dinner at a colleague’s house. I returned late that night. When I got home, I saw Akshay Mondal coming down from upstairs, locking the stairway door, with two suitcases at his feet. I asked, ‘What’s this? Where are you off to at this hour?’

“Seeing me, Akshay Mondal seemed startled; then, picking up both suitcases, he came over and stood before me, and said in a slightly grave voice, ‘Komolbabu, I have to leave suddenly. I don’t know when I’ll be back.’

“I noticed his eyes were bloodshot. I said, ‘Really? Where are you going?’

“A shadow of a smile flickered across his face. He said, ‘Far away. Well then, goodbye.’

“I stood there, astonished. He walked a few steps, then stopped, turned back, and said, ‘Komolbabu, you’re a good man, you work at a bank; let me tell you something before I go. If I don’t return within seven days, you are to take possession of my entire house. I have an account at your bank—every month, deposit a hundred and fifty rupees rent into my account. —Goodbye.’”

